The anti-de Sitter proof uses the Taylor coefficients of the given branch. Actual weighted remainder bounds connect those coefficients to the mass and area. The quadratic coefficient is a nonnegative quadratic form with a kernel of dimension four. A fourth-order argument treats its nonradial directions, while radial directions satisfy an exact mass conservation identity. This part is independent of the asymptotically flat elliptic construction.

===== 1.3 Relation to earlier work <relation-to-earlier-work>

Penrose proposed the mass–horizon comparison in the context of gravitational collapse and cosmic censorship #cite(label("Penrose1973")). For time-symmetric data, Huisken and Ilmanen proved the connected-horizon inequality by weak inverse mean curvature flow, and Bray proved the total-area bound for possibly disconnected horizons by conformal flow #cite(label("HuiskenIlmanen2001")) #cite(label("Bray2001")). Bray and Lee extended the numerical Riemannian inequality to spatial dimensions below eight; spin enters their higher-dimensional equality statement #cite(label("BrayLee2009")). Ben-Dov’s counterexample distinguishes apparent-horizon area from minimum enclosing area #cite(label("BenDov2004")). Carrasco and Mars constructed counterexamples for outermost generalized apparent horizons #cite(label("CarrascoMars2010")). Bray and Khuri developed a generalized Jang reduction, including spherical existence results and conditional nonspherical constructions #cite(label("BrayKhuri2010")) #cite(label("BrayKhuri2011")).

Allen, Bryden, Kazaras, and Khuri obtained a universal suboptimal mass–area bound in three dimensions under their decay and horizon assumptions #cite(label("AllenBrydenKazarasKhuri2025")). Khuri and Kunduri proved the sharp inequality for $S U \( n + 1 \)$-invariant cohomogeneity-one data in spatial dimensions $2 \( n + 1 \)$ #cite(label("KhuriKunduri2025")). The inequalities here have a different scope: the three- and four-dimensional results impose no such symmetry, and use their specified minimum enclosing quantities.

The stationary field arising from equality is related to the variational study of ADM mass. Hirsch and Huang proved a strong maximum principle for the norm of a causal Killing field and studied mass minimizers with fixed Bartnik boundary data #cite(label("HirschHuang2025")). Those boundary data and regularity hypotheses differ from the area variations used here. Once static vacuum geometry is obtained, the conformal-doubling method of Bunting and Masood-ul-Alam provides the classical model for the classification #cite(label("BuntingMasoodUlAlam1987")). We prove the completeness and low-regularity zero-mass statement needed for our double. Higher-dimensional static-vacuum uniqueness was developed by Gibbons, Ida, and Shiromizu #cite(label("GibbonsIdaShiromizu2002")). Here the static geometry and its complete base must first be recovered from the original initial data. The charged and anti-de Sitter parts give the comparisons with earlier results specific to those settings where their hypotheses can be stated precisely.

===== 1.4 Reading conventions <reading-conventions>

The five parts are ordered by their mathematical dependencies. The three-dimensional numerical theorem precedes the global extension, rigidity, and charged application. The four-dimensional part states its own normalizations and contains both the numerical construction and original-data equality proof. The anti-de Sitter part is independent and uses its own asymptotic geometry. The entry points are Sections 1, 1, 1, 1, and 2, respectively. Within each analytic construction the geometry and strictification are fixed before the deformation parameters. Uniform polynomial estimates and regularity estimates at a fixed parameter have distinct roles; the proofs specify the order of exhaustion and limits where each is used.

== 1 Three-dimensional initial data <three-dimensional-initial-data>

==== 1.1 Three-dimensional exterior data and the mass inequality <n3:intro:section>

===== 1.1.1 Initial data, normals, and enclosing area <initial-data-normals-and-enclosing-area>

We work in three spatial dimensions, with zero cosmological constant and $G = c = 1$. For a Riemannian metric $g$ and a symmetric covariant two-tensor $K$, define the constraint densities by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.1)")
 $ 16 pi mu = R_g + \( "tr"_g K \)^2 - \| K \|_g^2 \, #h(2em) 8 pi J = "div"_g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] K - \( "tr"_g K \) g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:intro:constraints>]

Here $J$ is a covector field. The dominant energy condition is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.2)")
 $ mu gt.eq \| J \|_g . $ <n3:intro:dec>]

All initial data considered here are smooth, including at a compact boundary when one is present.

On an asymptotically flat end with Euclidean coordinates $x$ and $r = \| x \|$, our basic assumptions are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.3)")
 $ g_(i j) - delta_(i j) = O_2 \( r^(- q) \) \, #h(2em) K_(i j) = O_1 \( r^(- 1 - q) \) \, #h(2em) q > 1 / 2 . $ <n3:intro:decay>]

The notation $O_j \( r^(- a) \)$ includes the corresponding coordinate derivative bounds through order $j$. We assume that $mu$ and $\| J \|_g$ are integrable and that the following ADM limits exist and are finite:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.4)")
 $ E & = frac(1, 16 pi) lim_(r arrow.r oo) integral_(S_r) \( partial_j g_(i j) - partial_i g_(j j) \) n^i thin upright(d) A_delta \,\
P_i & = frac(1, 8 pi) lim_(r arrow.r oo) integral_(S_r) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] K_(i j) - \( "tr"_g K \) g_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] n^j thin upright(d) A_delta . $ <n3:intro:energy>]

The normal and area form in these definitions are Euclidean. Whenever $E > \| P \|_delta$, write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.5)")
 $ m = sqrt(E^2 - \| P \|_delta^2) . $ <n3:intro:rest-mass>]

For a two-sided surface $Sigma$ with specified unit normal $nu$, we use

$ H_Sigma = "div"_Sigma nu \, #h(2em) theta_(+) \( Sigma \) = H_Sigma + "tr"_Sigma K . $

Thus Euclidean spheres have positive mean curvature for the normal toward infinity. A future marginally outer trapped surface, abbreviated MOTS, satisfies $theta_(+) = 0$. A weakly future outer trapped surface satisfies $theta_(+) lt.eq 0$. Our spacetime sign convention is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.6)")
 $ K \( Y \, Z \) = upright(bold(g)) \( upright(bold(nabla))_Y n \, Z \) \, $ <n3:intro:k-sign>]

where $n$ is the future unit timelike normal. Equivalently, in Gaussian normal coordinates the spatial metric has normal derivative $2 K$.

#prodef([Definition 1.1.])[
  (Exterior and enclosing area).  An exterior is a connected orientable smooth three-manifold $Omega$ with nonempty compact smooth boundary, complete as a metric space with its boundary included, and with exactly one end, which is asymptotically flat. An enclosing cut is a compact smooth embedded surface in $overline(Omega)$ separating the entire inner boundary from the sufficiently distant part of that end. Equivalently, it bounds the side containing the distant end; the other side is filled up to the inner obstacle. A cut may have several components and may coincide with the inner boundary. Its normal points toward the end. We put
  $ a_g \( partial Omega \) = inf { \| Gamma \|_g : Gamma upright(" is an enclosing cut") } . $
  When using perimeter compactness, we take the closure of this class among filled inner sets and include the area of any frontier coinciding with the obstacle. Smooth outward approximation gives the same infimum. Enclosing cuts and minimizing sets are taken with bounded complementary pockets filled. Auxiliary perimeter arguments may use competitors containing the inner obstacle before filling: filling a bounded pocket deletes its frontier, cannot increase perimeter, and leaves the enclosing infimum unchanged.
] <n3:intro:exterior-class>

The definition permits coincidence because first variations of the minimum need not be realized by a cut lying strictly outside the obstacle. It permits disconnected cuts because neither the minimizing hull nor an intermediate trapped boundary need be connected. The perimeter formulation and its compactness properties are established in Section 2.

===== 1.1.2 The one-ended exterior inequality <the-one-ended-exterior-inequality>

#thm([Theorem 1.2.])[
  (Minimum-enclosing-area inequality).  #emph[Let $\( Omega \, g \, K \)$ be an exterior as in Definition 1. Assume Equations (1.1)–(1.3), integrability of $mu$ and $\| J \|_g$, and the finite ADM limits (1.4)–(1.4). Orient $partial Omega$ by the normal into $Omega$, and suppose $theta_(+) \( partial Omega \) lt.eq 0$. If $E > \| P \|_delta$, then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.7)")
 $ sqrt(E^2 - \| P \|_delta^2) gt.eq sqrt(frac(a_g \( partial Omega \), 16 pi)) . $ <n3:intro:penrose>]

 No extension of the data across $partial Omega$ is required.]
] <n3:intro:exterior-inequality>

==== 1.2 Exterior approximation and reduction to a rest end <n3:reduction:section>

We reduce the invariant-mass inequality to an energy inequality on a controlled rest end. We replace the distant end, bend the replacement inside Schwarzschild spacetime, and repair its constraint deficit with a conformal change of vanishing mass cost. Throughout, $\( N \, g \, K \)$ is a smooth exterior with one asymptotically flat end and compact smooth boundary $B$. The normal $nu$ on $B$ points into $N$. Completeness means completeness up to this boundary. We assume

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.8)")
 $ g - delta = O_2 \( r^(- q) \) \, #h(2em) K = O_1 \( r^(- 1 - q) \) \, #h(2em) q > 1 / 2 \, $ <n3:reduction:original-decay>]

and the integrability and dominant energy condition of Theorem 2. The symbol $a_g \( B \)$ denotes the filled enclosing perimeter infimum specified there. None of the results in this section requires the boundary to be connected or outermost.

===== 1.2.1 A strict conformal direction <a-strict-conformal-direction>

#thm([Lemma 1.3.])[
  (Conformal constraint formulas).  #emph[For a smooth function $f$, set 
$ g_f = e^(4 f) g \, #h(2em) K_f = e^(2 f) K . $

 The corresponding constraint densities satisfy 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.9)")
 $ 16 pi e^(4 f) mu_f & = 16 pi mu - 8 Delta_g f - 8 \| d f \|_g^2 \,\
8 pi J_f & = e^(- 2 f) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 8 pi J + 4 K \( nabla f \, dot.op \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:reduction:conformal-energy>]

 Here the second identity is an identity of covectors. Consequently, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.10)")
 $ 16 pi e^(4 f) \( mu_f - \| J_f \|_(g_f) \) gt.eq  & 16 pi \( mu - \| J \|_g \)\
 & + 8 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] - Delta_g f - \| d f \|_g^2 - \| K \|_g \| d f \|_g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:reduction:conformal-dec>]

 On the fixed boundary, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.11)")
 $ theta_(+ \, f) = e^(- 2 f) \( theta_(+) + 4 partial_nu f \) . $ <n3:reduction:conformal-expansion>]

 Equivalently, if $u = e^f > 0$, the last summand in Equation (1.10) is $8 u^(- 1) \( - Delta_g u - \| K \|_g \| d u \|_g \)$.]
] <n3:reduction:conformal-formulas>

#proof[
  The scalar-curvature transformation in dimension three is $e^(4 f) "Scal"_(g_f) = "Scal"_g - 8 Delta_g f - 8 \| d f \|^2$. Both quadratic terms in $K$ scale by $e^(- 4 f)$, which proves Equation (1.9). Put $pi = K - \( "tr"_g K \) g$. Then $pi_f = e^(2 f) pi$. For a covariant symmetric tensor $T$, the connection difference for $tilde(g) = Omega^2 g$ gives, in dimension three,
  $ tilde(nabla)^(\, i) \( Omega T \)_(i j) = Omega^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] nabla^i T_(i j) + 2 T_(i j) nabla^i log Omega - \( "tr"_g T \) partial_j log Omega #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Take $Omega = e^(2 f)$ and use $"tr"_g pi = - 2 "tr"_g K$. The terms containing $"tr"_g K$ cancel, leaving Equation (1.9). Taking its norm introduces another factor $e^(- 2 f)$. The triangle inequality, with the factor two between the constraint normalizations $16 pi$ and $8 pi$, yields Equation (1.10). Finally, $nu_f = e^(- 2 f) nu$, $H_f = e^(- 2 f) \( H + 4 partial_nu f \)$, and $"tr"_(B \, g_f) K_f = e^(- 2 f) "tr"_(B \, g) K$. This proves Equation (1.11). The last assertion follows from $Delta log u + \| d log u \|^2 = u^(- 1) Delta u$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.4.])[
  (Strict direction under the original decay).  #emph[Choose 
$ 0 < delta < beta < min \( q \, 1 \) . $

 There are smooth positive functions $w$ and $phi.alt$ on $N$, with $w = r^(- 3 - delta)$ on the end, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.12)")
 $ & partial_nu phi.alt = - 1 quad upright("on ") B \, & phi.alt & = O_2 \( r^(- 1) \) \,\
 & - Delta_g phi.alt - \| K \|_g \| d phi.alt \|_g gt.eq w \, & frac(- Delta_g phi.alt, w) & arrow.r 1 . $ <n3:reduction:strict-boundary>]

 The Euclidean normal flux 
$ L_phi.alt = lim_(r arrow.r oo) integral_(S_r) partial_r phi.alt thin thin upright(d) A_delta $

 is finite and negative. For every finite $s gt.eq 0$, set 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.13)")
 $ u_s = 1 + s phi.alt \, #h(2em) g_s = u_s^4 g \, #h(2em) K_s = u_s^2 K . $ <n3:reduction:linear-family>]

 These data are complete up to $B$ and satisfy DEC, strictly for $s > 0$. If $theta_(+) lt.eq 0$ on $B$, their boundary expansion remains nonpositive and is strictly negative for $s > 0$. Their decay exponent can be taken to be $min \( q \, 1 \) > 1 \/ 2$, their constraint densities are integrable, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.14)")
 $ E_s = E + s A_phi.alt \, #h(2em) P_s = P \, #h(2em) A_phi.alt = - frac(L_phi.alt, 2 pi) > 0 . $ <n3:reduction:strict-charges>]

 Their full enclosing infima satisfy 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.15)")
 $ a_g \( B \) lt.eq a_(g_s) \( B \) lt.eq \( 1 + s parallel phi.alt parallel_oo \)^4 a_g \( B \) . $ <n3:reduction:strict-areas>]

 In particular $E_s arrow.r E$ and $a_(g_s) \( B \) arrow.r a_g \( B \)$ as $s arrow.b 0$. No sign assumption on the ADM vector is needed.]
  #emph[If $K$ is compactly supported and the metric has symbol estimates of all orders with $g - delta = O \( r^(- 1) \)$, the function can instead be chosen with $phi.alt = O_k \( r^(- 1) \)$ for every $k$, while retaining Equations (1.12) and (1.12).]
] <n3:reduction:strict-direction>

#proof[
  Choose smooth $b gt.eq \| K \|_g$, positive everywhere and equal to $b_0 r^(- 1 - beta)$ sufficiently far out, where $b_0 > 0$ is chosen large enough. This is possible because $beta < q$. Choose a smooth positive regularizer $zeta$, equal to $r^(- 2)$ on the end, and extend $w$ smoothly and positively to $N$. We solve
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.16)")
   $ - Delta_g phi.alt = b sqrt(\| d phi.alt \|_g^2 + zeta^2) + w \, #h(2em) partial_nu phi.alt = - 1 \, #h(2em) phi.alt arrow.r 0 . $ <n3:reduction:drift-poisson>]
  The regularizer makes this equation smooth even at critical points.
  First truncate at a large coordinate sphere $S_R$ and prescribe $phi.alt = 0$ there. The Neumann and Dirichlet conditions occur on disjoint boundary components. For $0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1$, replace $b$ in Equation (1.16) by $t b$. At $t = 0$, the mixed Laplacian is invertible: its Dirichlet face gives the Poincare inequality, and the weak solution obtained from its coercive quadratic form is smooth by boundary regularity. At any solution, the linearization is a Laplacian with a smooth drift of norm at most $b$ and with the homogeneous mixed boundary conditions. The strong maximum principle and the Hopf boundary principle make its kernel zero. It is a Fredholm perturbation of the mixed Laplacian of index zero, and is therefore invertible. The boundary estimates used here are the ordinary elliptic estimates for Dirichlet and normal Neumann conditions: positive definiteness of $g$ verifies their principal complementing condition, and the disjoint boundary components can be covered separately #cite(label("AgmonDouglisNirenberg1959")). The maximum principles and local elliptic estimates below are used in their usual uniformly elliptic forms #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")).
  To close continuation at $t = 1$, first work on a fixed truncation. The elliptic $W^(2 \, p)$ estimate and gradient interpolation give
  $ parallel phi.alt parallel_(W^(2 \, p)) lt.eq C_p #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + parallel phi.alt parallel_(L^p) + parallel d phi.alt parallel_(L^p) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq 1 / 2 parallel phi.alt parallel_(W^(2 \, p)) + C'_p \( 1 + parallel phi.alt parallel_(L^p) \) . $
  The constants include the fixed boundary data and are uniform in $t$. If the supremum norms of solutions on this truncation were unbounded, divide them by their supremum norms. The preceding estimate with $p > 3$, compactness, and the equation give a nonzero limit $v$ with homogeneous mixed data satisfying
  $ - Delta_g v = t_(*) b \| d v \|_g . $
  This can be written $Delta_g v + A dot.op d v = 0$ with a bounded measurable drift: take $A = t_(*) b nabla v \/ \| d v \|$ off the critical set and zero on it. The strong and Hopf maximum principles exclude a nonzero solution with the homogeneous mixed conditions. Thus the supremum norms are bounded. The $W^(2 \, p)$ estimate, Schauder estimates, and the smooth equation now give the bounds needed for closedness of continuation. Existence on each truncation follows.
  Every such solution is nonnegative. A negative minimum cannot be interior because the right side of Equation (1.16) is positive. At an inner-boundary minimum, the outward-domain derivative would be negative by the Hopf principle, whereas the prescribed derivative in that direction is $1$. The outer value is zero.
  It remains to make these bounds independent of $R$. For fixed sufficiently large $A$ and then sufficiently large $r_0$, the positive radial function
  $ v_0 \( r \) = r^(- 1) \( 1 - A r^(- delta) \) $
  satisfies, on $r gt.eq r_0$,
  $ - Delta_g v_0 - b \| d v_0 \|_g & = A delta \( 1 + delta \) r^(- 3 - delta) + O \( r^(- 3 - q) \) + O \( r^(- 3 - beta) \) gt.eq c_0 w . $
  The constants are fixed independently of the truncation. Since $b zeta = o \( w \)$, a sufficiently large multiple of $v_0$ is a supersolution of the regularized equation. The comparison principle gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.17)")
   $ 0 lt.eq phi.alt \( x \) lt.eq C \( 1 + M_R \) v_0 \( r \) \, #h(2em) M_R = sup_(N ∩ { r lt.eq r_0 }) phi.alt \, quad r_0 lt.eq r lt.eq R . $ <n3:reduction:core-barrier>]
  The notation for the compact core includes all points outside the end chart. Were $M_R$ unbounded along expanding truncations, the normalized solutions $phi.alt \/ M_R$ would have locally uniform $W^(2 \, p)$ bounds, including at $B$, by the preceding local estimates and Equation (1.17). A subsequence would converge locally in $C^1$ to a nonnegative function with maximum one on the core, homogeneous inner Neumann data, and a bounded-drift homogeneous equation. Equation (1.17) makes its value tend to zero at infinity. It consequently attains a positive maximum, contradicting the strong or Hopf principle for that homogeneous equation. This proves the uniform core bound. The contradiction uses the homogeneous bounded-drift equation.
  Local compactness and diagonal extraction give a smooth solution of Equation (1.16), with $phi.alt = O \( r^(- 1) \)$. The claimed derivative count can be checked directly on annuli. Put
  $ phi.alt_rho \( y \) = rho phi.alt \( rho y \) \, #h(2em) g_rho \( y \) = g \( rho y \) \, #h(2em) 1 < \| y \| < 4 . $
  On smaller fixed annuli the equation is
  $ - Delta_(g_rho) phi.alt_rho = rho b \( rho y \) sqrt(\| d phi.alt_rho \|_(g_rho)^2 + \( rho^2 zeta \( rho y \) \)^2) + rho^3 w \( rho y \) . $
  Its coefficients have uniform $C^(1 \, 1)$ bounds from the two derivatives in Equation (1.8); the scaled drift is $O \( rho^(- beta) \)$ and the scaled source is $O \( rho^(- delta) \)$. The bounded zeroth-order norm, interior $W^(2 \, p)$ estimates, and gradient interpolation first give uniform $C^(1 \, alpha)$ bounds for some $alpha > 0$. The right side is then uniformly $C^alpha$. Schauder estimates give uniform $C^(2 \, alpha)$ bounds on smaller annuli. This proves $phi.alt = O_2 \( r^(- 1) \)$ without using a third derivative of $g$ or a second derivative of $K$.
  The right side of Equation (1.16) is $w + O \( r^(- 3 - beta) \)$. It is integrable and proves Equation (1.12). The divergence theorem, with outward-domain normal $- nu$ on $B$, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.18)")
   $ lim_(r arrow.r oo) integral_(S_r) partial_(n_g) phi.alt thin thin upright(d) A_g = - \| B \|_g - integral_N #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] b sqrt(\| d phi.alt \|_g^2 + zeta^2) + w #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] thin thin upright(d) V_g . $ <n3:reduction:phi-flux>]
  The Euclidean flux differs by $O \( r^(- q) \)$ and has the same finite limit.
  For every fixed finite $s gt.eq 0$, the factor $u_s$ in Equation (1.13) is bounded and at least one, so the transformed data are complete up to $B$. The $u$-form of Lemma 3 gives
  $ 16 pi u_s^4 \( mu_s - \| J_s \|_(g_s) \) & gt.eq 16 pi \( mu - \| J \|_g \) + frac(8 s, u_s) \( - Delta_g phi.alt - \| K \|_g \| d phi.alt \|_g \) gt.eq frac(8 s, u_s) w \,\
theta_(+ \, s) & = u_s^(- 2) (theta_(+) - frac(4 s, u_s)) . $
  This proves the asserted signs for all finite $s$, without a smallness restriction.
  Since $u_s - 1 = O_2 \( r^(- 1) \)$, the transformed decay exponent is $min \( q \, 1 \) > 1 \/ 2$. The exact energy transformation is
  $ 16 pi u_s^4 mu_s = 16 pi mu - 8 s u_s^(- 1) Delta_g phi.alt . $
  Here $Delta_g phi.alt = O \( r^(- 3 - delta) \)$, and the additional current term is bounded by a fixed-$s$ constant times $\| K \|_g \| d phi.alt \|_g = O \( r^(- 3 - q) \)$. Both are integrable in dimension three. The bounded positive conformal factor also preserves integrability in the transformed volume measure.
  The leading metric change is $4 s phi.alt delta$, whose ADM energy flux is $- 8 s integral_(S_r) partial_r phi.alt thin thin upright(d) A_delta$ before division by $16 pi$. All remaining metric flux terms are $O_s \( r^(- q) \) + O_s \( r^(- 1) \)$. Writing $pi = K - \( "tr"_g K \) g$, one has $pi_s = u_s^2 pi$, so the change in its Euclidean momentum flux is $O_s \( r^2 r^(- 1) r^(- 1 - q) \) = O_s \( r^(- q) \)$. This proves Equation (1.14); Equation (1.18) gives $A_phi.alt > 0$. No $1 \/ r$ coefficient expansion for $phi.alt$ is needed. Finally, the cut class is unchanged and every cut has area $integral_Gamma u_s^4 thin thin upright(d) A_g$. Bounding $u_s$ between $1$ and $1 + s parallel phi.alt parallel_oo$ and taking infima proves Equation (1.15), including coincident and disconnected cuts.
  For the final assertion, take $b$ nonnegative, compactly supported, and at least $\| K \|$; the proof is unchanged. The end equation is then simply $- Delta_g phi.alt = w$. Once the two-derivative estimate has been established, successive annular elliptic estimates use the assumed symbol bounds for $g$ and $w$ to give $phi.alt = O_k \( r^(- 1) \)$ for every $k$. ◻
]

===== 1.2.2 Compactness of enclosing minimizers <compactness-of-enclosing-minimizers>

The end replacement will change the metric on receding annuli. The next lemma keeps area-minimizing enclosures in a fixed compact set, where local metric convergence controls their areas.

#thm([Lemma 1.5.])[
  (Enclosing areas under receding changes).  #emph[Let $g_j$ be smooth metrics on the fixed exterior $N$, converging to $g$ locally uniformly up to $B$. Suppose that in a fixed chart outside a compact set they satisfy, with constants independent of $j$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.19)")
 $ c delta lt.eq g_j lt.eq C delta \, #h(2em) r \| partial g_j \|_delta lt.eq C . $ <n3:reduction:area-geometry>]

 Suppose also that each $g_j$ has an asymptotically flat end, possibly in a different chart, and admits a fixed compact enclosing competitor with uniformly bounded area. Then minimizing filled enclosures exist, their boundaries lie in one fixed compact subset of $N$, and 
$ a_(g_j) \( B \) arrow.r a_g \( B \) . $

 The perimeter and smooth enclosing infima agree. Each minimizing boundary is $C^(1 \, 1)$, and is smooth minimal away from contact with $B$.]
] <n3:reduction:area-compactness>

#proof[
  For purposes of perimeter minimization only, extend the metrics through a collar of $B$ and attach a fixed compact filling. The filling need not satisfy any constraint or curvature condition. Extend $g_j$ so that the extensions converge uniformly on this fixed collar and filling. A filled enclosure is then a bounded set containing the prescribed inner obstacle.
  For each fixed metric, sufficiently large spheres in its asymptotically flat chart have strictly positive outward mean curvature. Clipping a bounded enclosure at any such sphere cannot increase perimeter. To see this, integrate the divergence of the outward unit normal field of the sphere foliation over the part of the enclosure outside the sphere. Its divergence is positive. Its flux through the exterior boundary is at most the area of that boundary, whereas its flux on the cutting sphere is the negative of the new cutting area. Thus the discarded boundary area is at least the inserted area. Approximation gives the same statement for finite-perimeter sets. The direct method on the resulting bounded domain now gives a minimizer. The clipping observation makes this a minimizer against every bounded competitor, not merely those inside the chosen truncation.
  Off the obstacle the boundary is locally perimeter minimizing. If it contains a point of radius $r$ sufficiently large, the coordinate ball of radius $c_1 r$ about that point avoids the obstacle. After scaling this ball to unit size, Equation (1.19) gives uniform ellipticity and uniform first-derivative bounds for its area integrand. The local perimeter density estimate gives
  $ "Area"_(g_j) \( partial F_j ∩ B_(c_1 r) \) gt.eq c_2 r^2 . $
  Here $c_1 \, c_2 > 0$ are independent of $j$ and $r$. One may obtain this estimate from the relative isoperimetric inequality and the comparison which removes, or fills, the set in concentric balls; integration of the resulting differential inequality gives the area lower bound. The fixed competitor bounds the left side above independently of $j$. Consequently the minimizing boundaries lie in a fixed compact set.
  Perimeter compactness now gives a limiting filled enclosure. Uniform metric convergence on that compact set and lower semicontinuity imply
  $ a_g \( B \) lt.eq liminf_j a_(g_j) \( B \) . $
  Conversely, using a fixed enclosure with $g$-area arbitrarily close to $a_g \( B \)$ gives the reverse upper-limit inequality.
  The local obstacle regularity theorem for pure area minimization with a $C^2$ obstacle gives $C^(1 \, 1)$ regularity, and smoothness off contact, in dimension three; its local estimates require only the appropriate bounded geometry of the smooth metric #cite(label("HuiskenIlmanen2001")) Regularity Theorem 1.3. This applies to the fixed smooth obstacle and our smooth one-sided metrics after the collar extension. Finally, push a minimizing boundary slightly away from its contact set by a smooth vector field agreeing there with the outward obstacle normal. In a small obstacle collar its flow increases the signed distance to the obstacle. The resulting $C^1$ embedded boundary is a positive distance from the obstacle. Smooth local graph approximation, within that distance, preserves enclosure and changes area by an arbitrarily small amount. This proves equality of the smooth and perimeter infima and also justifies using smooth fixed competitors in the upper-limit argument. ◻
]

===== 1.2.3 Schwarzschild reference charges <schwarzschild-reference-charges>

We next construct the vacuum end used in the replacement. Its charges must agree with those of the original data before we bend it to a rest slice.

#thm([Lemma 1.6.])[
  (A reference end with prescribed timelike charges).  #emph[Let $E > \| P \|$ and $m = \( E^2 - \| P \|^2 \)^(1 \/ 2)$. There is a spacelike plane near spatial infinity of Schwarzschild spacetime of mass $m$ whose induced data, in asymptotically Euclidean coordinates on the plane, have ADM charges $\( E \, P \)$. The reference data satisfy 
$ g_(*) - delta = O_k \( r^(- 1) \) \, #h(2em) K_(*) = O_k \( r^(- 2) \) $

 for every derivative order $k$.]
] <n3:reduction:boosted-reference>

#proof[
  In static isotropic coordinates $\( T \, y \)$ the Schwarzschild metric is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.20)")
   $ - (frac(1 - m \/ \( 2 r \), 1 + m \/ \( 2 r \)))^2 thin upright(d) T^2 + (1 + frac(m, 2 r))^4 delta_(i j) thin upright(d) y^i thin upright(d) y^j \, #h(2em) r = \| y \| . $ <n3:reduction:schwarzschild-spacetime>]
  Its perturbation $h$ from Minkowski space has symbol decay $r^(- 1)$ on every fixed spacelike cone. Constant Lorentz transformations preserve this decay. We verify the transformation of the ADM charges directly.
  In inertial coordinates for the background Minkowski metric $eta$, put
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.21)")
   $ cal(F)_(ell a b) =  & partial_a h_(ell b) - partial_ell h_(a b)\
 & + eta_(ell b) \( partial^d h_(a d) - partial_a "tr"_eta h \) - eta_(a b) \( partial^d h_(ell d) - partial_ell "tr"_eta h \) . $ <n3:reduction:superpotential>]
  This tensor is antisymmetric in its first two slots, and differentiation of the displayed formula gives $partial^ell cal(F)_(ell a b) = 2 G_(a b)^(upright("lin"))$. The exact Einstein tensor vanishes; its terms beyond the linearization are $O \( r^(- 4) \)$. Thus this divergence is $O \( r^(- 4) \)$.
  Use an inertial frame in which the plane under consideration is $x^0 = 0$. With $eta = "diag" \( - 1 \, 1 \, 1 \, 1 \)$, direct substitution in Equation (1.21) gives
  $ cal(F)_(i 00) & = partial_j h_(i j) - partial_i h_(j j) \,\
cal(F)_(i 0 k) & = 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] K_(i k)^(upright("lin")) - delta_(i k) "tr" K^(upright("lin")) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + partial_k h_(0 i) - delta_(i k) partial_j h_(0 j) \, $
  where
  $ K_(i k)^(upright("lin")) = 1 / 2 \( partial_0 h_(i k) - partial_i h_(0 k) - partial_k h_(0 i) \) . $
  The convention is the normal rate of the spatial metric divided by two. For fixed $k$, the final vector has zero flux through every closed sphere. Indeed it equals
  $ partial_j \( delta_(j k) h_(0 i) - delta_(i k) h_(0 j) \) \, $
  the divergence of a tensor antisymmetric in $i \, j$. This zero-flux identity is local to a neighborhood of the sphere: extend the potential smoothly inside before applying the divergence theorem, if necessary. The nonlinear errors in the induced metric and second fundamental form give vanishing flux errors. Therefore the limiting fluxes of $cal(F)_(i 0 b)$ are $16 pi \( E \, P \)$.
  These fluxes are the integrals of the dual two-form of the first two slots of $cal(F)$, with its translation slot held fixed. They therefore transform tensorially under a constant Lorentz change of frame. Independence of the limiting spacelike cut follows directly from Stokes’ theorem: two large cuts on planes of slopes bounded strictly below one can be joined within a fixed spacelike cone by a three-dimensional cylinder of volume $O \( R^3 \)$, all of whose points have radius comparable to $R$. Its divergence error is $O \( R^(- 4) \)$, so the flux difference is $O \( R^(- 1) \)$. Replacing the resulting ellipsoidal cuts by coordinate spheres on either plane gives the same estimate. The limiting translation charges consequently transform by the Lorentz representation. The static plane has charges $\( m \, 0 \)$, as follows by inserting Equation (1.20) into the ADM integral. Its Lorentz orbit is precisely the set of future timelike vectors of norm $m$, proving the assertion.
  More explicitly the plane can be written $T = v dot.op y$, $\| v \| < 1$. Writing $y = A x$, where $A = \( I - v ⊗ v \)^(- 1 \/ 2)$, makes the induced Minkowski metric in the $x$ coordinates equal to $delta$. The displayed decay then follows from the symbol estimates in Equation (1.20) and the formula for the second fundamental form. These coordinates will be used when identifying the reference end with the given end. ◻
]

===== 1.2.4 Moments and compact linear corrections <moments-and-compact-linear-corrections>

All operators in the next two lemmas are Euclidean. For symmetric covariant tensors define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.22)")
 $ cal(L) h = partial_i partial_j h_(i j) - Delta "tr" h \, #h(2em) \( cal(D) k \)_i = partial_j \( k_(i j) - \( "tr" k \) delta_(i j) \) . $ <n3:reduction:linear-operators>]

The formal adjoint kernel of $cal(L)$ consists of affine functions; the formal adjoint kernel of $cal(D)$ consists of Euclidean Killing fields. Orthogonality to these kernels is the compatibility condition for the compact inverses below.

#thm([Lemma 1.7.])[
  (Compact scalar and symmetric divergence inverses).  #emph[Let $cal(A) subset bb(R)^3$ be a fixed connected open annulus and let $Q subset.double cal(A)$. Smooth sources supported in $Q$ admit the following compact corrections, supported in a fixed larger compact subset of $cal(A)$.]
  + #emph[If $integral f = 0$ and $integral x^i f = 0$ for $i = 1 \, 2 \, 3$, there is a smooth symmetric tensor $h$ with $cal(L) h = f$ and $parallel h parallel_(W^(k + 2 \, p)) lt.eq C_(k \, p) parallel f parallel_(W^(k \, p))$.]
  + #emph[If $integral V dot.op Y = 0$ for every Euclidean Killing field $Y$, there is a smooth symmetric tensor $k$ with $cal(D) k = V$ and $parallel k parallel_(W^(k_0 + 1 \, p)) lt.eq C_(k_0 \, p) parallel V parallel_(W^(k_0 \, p))$.]
  #emph[The estimates hold for integers $k \, k_0 gt.eq 0$ and $1 < p < oo$.]
] <n3:reduction:compact-inverses>

#proof[
  We first record the ordinary divergence inverse being used. On a ball, the regularized Bogovskii operator solves $"div" u = f$ for a mean-zero compactly supported source, preserves compact support, and gains one derivative in $W^(k \, p)$; see #cite(label("CostabelMcIntosh2010")) §3.1, Equation (3.13), Theorem 3.2, Corollary 3.4, and Remark 3.5. To apply it on the annulus, cover $Q$ by finitely many balls compactly contained in $cal(A)$, adding finitely many overlapping balls so that the overlap graph is connected. Partition the source among the balls. On a spanning tree of the overlap graph, pass each piece’s integral to its parent by subtracting and adding that integral times a fixed unit-integral bump in the overlap. Starting at the leaves leaves every piece with zero integral; the root does also because the total integral vanishes. The decomposition has bounded Sobolev norms because all cutoffs and bumps are fixed. The sum of the ball inverses gives a compact inverse on the annulus. For sources in a fixed compact set its output support lies in another fixed compact set. This construction may be repeated twice using slightly enlarged compact sets still contained in $cal(A)$.
  For the scalar assertion, solve $partial_i u_i = f$ compactly. Integration by parts gives
  $ integral u_i = - integral x^i f = 0 . $
  Apply the divergence inverse to each component of $u$, obtaining $partial_j A_(i j) = u_i$. Symmetrizing $A$ does not change its double divergence. Denote this symmetric matrix by $S$ and put
  $ h = S - 1 / 2 \( "tr" S \) delta . $
  Since $h - \( "tr" h \) delta = S$, its linearized scalar curvature is $f$. The two applications of the divergence inverse give the two-derivative gain.
  For the vector assertion, translation orthogonality gives $integral V_i = 0$. Solve $partial_j B_(i j) = V_i$ componentwise. Orthogonality to rotations and integration by parts imply
  $ 0 = integral \( x^j V_i - x^i V_j \) = - integral \( B_(i j) - B_(j i) \) . $
  Thus the skew part $S_(i j) = \( B_(i j) - B_(j i) \) \/ 2$ has zero integral. Apply the divergence inverse to its independent components to obtain a tensor $D$, skew in its first two indices, such that $partial_k D_(i j k) = - S_(i j)$. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.23)")
   $ C_(i j k) = D_(i j k) - D_(i k j) - D_(j k i) . $ <n3:reduction:stress-symmetrization>]
  Index interchange shows both $C_(i j k) = - C_(i k j)$ and $\( C_(i j k) - C_(j i k) \) \/ 2 = D_(i j k)$. Consequently
  $ T_(i j) = B_(i j) + partial_k C_(i j k) $
  is symmetric and satisfies $partial_j T_(i j) = V_i$. The last statement uses the skew symmetry in $j \, k$ to cancel the double divergence of $C$. Set $k = T - \( "tr" T \) delta \/ 2$ to invert trace reversal. The first inverse gains one derivative, the second gains another, and the last derivative of $C$ leaves a net gain of one. All constructions preserve smoothness and have the stated support and norm bounds. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.8.])[
  (Integrable sources give sublinear first moments).  #emph[For data satisfying Equation (1.8) and integrable $mu \, \| J \|$, put $h = g - delta$ and $pi = K - \( "tr"_delta K \) delta$. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.24)")
 $ cal(L) h & = 16 pi mu + O \( r^(- 2 - 2 q) \) \,\
partial_j pi_(i j) & = 8 pi J_i + O \( r^(- 2 - 2 q) \) . $ <n3:reduction:linear-source-integrability>]

 In particular these Euclidean sources are integrable. Their scalar boundary fluxes paired with affine functions having zero constant term, and their vector boundary fluxes paired with rotational Killing fields, are $o \( R \)$ on coordinate spheres of radius $R$.]
] <n3:reduction:moment-estimates>

#proof[
  The scalar-curvature expansion has remainder bounded by
  $ C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| h \| \| partial^2 h \| + \| partial h \|^2 + \| K \|^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  and the replacement of the momentum constraint by Euclidean divergence has remainder bounded by $C \( \| h \| \| partial K \| + \| partial h \| \| K \| \)$. These are $O \( r^(- 2 - 2 q) \)$, using exactly the derivatives in Equation (1.8). They are integrable because $2 + 2 q > 3$.
  For any integrable function $F$ on the end and fixed $c > 1$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.25)")
   $ 1 / R integral_(r_0 < \| x \| < c R) \| x \| thin \| F \( x \) \| thin thin upright(d) x arrow.r 0 . $ <n3:reduction:sublinear-tail>]
  Indeed the contribution inside any fixed radius $M$ tends to zero after division by $R$; the remaining contribution is at most $c integral_(\| x \| > M) \| F \|$. Let $R arrow.r oo$ and then $M arrow.r oo$.
  For an affine function $f$, the vector field
  $ cal(Q)_f \( h \)^i = f \( partial_j h_(i j) - partial_i "tr" h \) - \( partial_j f \) h_(i j) + \( partial_i f \) "tr" h $
  satisfies $partial_i cal(Q)_f \( h \)^i = f cal(L) h$. For a Euclidean Killing field $Y$,
  $ partial_j \( pi_(i j) Y^i \) = Y^i partial_j pi_(i j) \, $
  because $pi$ is symmetric. Integrating from a fixed inner sphere and using Equation (1.25) proves the asserted $o \( R \)$ estimates. This argument does not assert the existence of a center-of-mass or angular-momentum limit and requires no parity condition. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 1.9.])[
  (Annular replacement with a vanishing deficit).  #emph[Suppose $E > \| P \|$, and let $\( g_(*) \, K_(*) \)$ be the reference end from Lemma 6, identified with the given end in the common asymptotically Euclidean coordinates. For all sufficiently large $R$ there are smooth data $\( tilde(g)_R \, tilde(K)_R \)$ equal to $\( g \, K \)$ on $r lt.eq R$ and to $\( g_(*) \, K_(*) \)$ on $r gt.eq 4 R$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.26)")
 $ 16 pi \( tilde(mu)_R - \| tilde(J)_R \|_(tilde(g)_R) \) gt.eq - eta_R R^(- 3) \, #h(2em) eta_R arrow.r 0 . $ <n3:reduction:annular-deficit>]

 The possible negative part is supported in $R < r < 4 R$. The metrics there are uniformly Euclidean comparable and have uniformly bounded scaled first derivatives. The construction uses no derivatives of the original data beyond those in Equation (1.8) for its quantitative estimates.]
] <n3:reduction:annular-replacement>

#proof[
  Fix $1 / 2 < beta < min \( q \, 1 \)$ and work on the annulus $cal(A) = { 1 < \| y \| < 4 }$ with $x = R y$. The scaled fields are
  $ g_R \( y \) = g \( R y \) \, #h(2em) k_R \( y \) = R K \( R y \) \, $
  and likewise for the reference data. Their constraint densities are the original densities multiplied by $R^2$. If $h_R = g_R - delta$, then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.27)")
   $ parallel h_R parallel_(C^2 \( cal(A) \)) + parallel k_R parallel_(C^1 \( cal(A) \)) + parallel h_(* \, R) parallel_(C^2 \( cal(A) \)) + parallel k_(* \, R) parallel_(C^1 \( cal(A) \)) lt.eq C R^(- beta) . $ <n3:reduction:scaled-smallness>]
  Choose $chi in C^oo \( cal(A) \)$ with $0 lt.eq chi lt.eq 1$, equal to one near $\| y \| = 1$ and zero near $\| y \| = 4$, with all its derivatives supported in a fixed compact subannulus. Blend both fields by this cutoff.
  Write the constraints in the unnormalized form
  $ cal(C) \( g \, k \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Scal"_g + \( "tr"_g k \)^2 - \| k \|_g^2 \, "div"_g \( k - \( "tr"_g k \) g \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Their Euclidean linear part is $\( cal(L) h \, cal(D) k \)$. On fields satisfying Equation (1.27), the nonlinear remainder is bounded in supremum norm by $C R^(- 2 beta)$. Thus the blended constraints equal the blended original constraint sources, plus a quadratic error and the compact commutators
  $ f_R & = cal(L) \( chi \( h_R - h_(* \, R) \) \) - chi cal(L) \( h_R - h_(* \, R) \) \,\
V_R & = cal(D) \( chi \( k_R - k_(* \, R) \) \) - chi cal(D) \( k_R - k_(* \, R) \) . $
  They have $C^(0 \, 1)$ norms at most $C R^(- beta)$. In detail the scalar commutator applied to a symmetric tensor $h$ is
  $ & 2 partial_i chi \( partial_j h_(i j) - partial_i "tr" h \) + \( partial_i partial_j chi \) h_(i j) - \( Delta chi \) "tr" h \; $
  one derivative of this expression uses at most two derivatives of $h$. The vector commutator is $\( partial_j chi \) \( k_(i j) - \( "tr" k \) delta_(i j) \)$; one derivative uses at most one derivative of $k$. No derivative of the complete matter sources is needed.
  Every affine moment of $f_R$ and every Killing-field moment of $V_R$ is $o \( R^(- 1) \)$. For the constant moments, integration by parts on $cal(A)$ gives $R^(- 1)$ times the difference between the physical translation fluxes on its two boundary spheres, less the integrals of the blended linear sources. The flux difference tends to zero because the reference and original ADM vectors agree. Replacing the physical momentum integrand by its Euclidean trace reversal costs $O \( R^(1 - 2 q) \)$, which tends to zero. By Lemma 8, the physical linear-source integrals on this receding annulus tend to zero as well. For a linear scalar test or a rotational vector test the corresponding factor is $R^(- 2)$. Its physical boundary fluxes are $o \( R \)$ by the same lemma, while the weighted source integrals are $o \( R \)$ by Equation (1.25). This proves the claim.
  Remove these small moments by fixed smooth bumps. Explicitly, for a basis $p_1 \, dots.h \, p_4$ of affine functions and a fixed nonnegative bump $omega$ positive on a ball inside the annulus, the Gram matrix
  $ G_(a b) = integral omega p_a p_b $
  is positive definite. Subtract $omega sum_(a \, b) p_a \( G^(- 1) \)_(a b) integral p_b f_R$ from $f_R$. For the vector moments use in exactly the same way the Gram matrix $integral omega Y_a dot.op Y_b$ of a basis of the six Euclidean Killing fields. It too is positive definite, since a nonzero Killing field cannot vanish on an open ball. Both bump corrections have every fixed smooth norm $o \( R^(- 1) \)$.
  Apply Lemma 7 with the negative of the moment-free commutators. Since their $W^(1 \, p)$ norms are $O \( R^(- beta) \)$, choosing $p > 3$ gives metric and tensor corrections of size $O \( R^(- beta) \)$ in $W^(3 \, p)$ and $W^(2 \, p)$, respectively. Sobolev embedding gives the needed $C^(2 \, alpha)$ and $C^(1 \, alpha)$ bounds. Their supports stay in a fixed compact subset of $cal(A)$. Extend them by zero and undo the scaling. The metric remains positive definite for large $R$.
  To check DEC, denote the original scaled sources by $\( A_R \, B_R \)$, so $A_R gt.eq 2 \| B_R \|_(g_R)$. The reference sources vanish. The corrected sources therefore have the form
  $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] chi A_R \, chi B_R #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + o \( R^(- 1) \) + O \( R^(- 2 beta) \) $
  in supremum norm. The norm of $B_R$ changes by at most $C R^(- beta) \| B_R \| lt.eq C R^(- 2 beta)$ when the metric is changed, since the pointwise constraint formula gives $\| B_R \| lt.eq C R^(- beta)$. All other nonlinear correction terms have the same quadratic bound. Because $2 beta > 1$, the scaled DEC deficit is $o \( R^(- 1) \)$. Multiplication by $R^(- 2)$ gives Equation (1.26). Outside the correction annulus the data are either the original DEC data or the exact vacuum reference data. Smoothness follows from smoothness of the original fields and of the compact inverse constructions, irrespective of the sizes of their unneeded higher derivatives. ◻
]

===== 1.2.5 Bending to a rest slice and repairing the deficit <bending-to-a-rest-slice-and-repairing-the-deficit>

#thm([Lemma 1.10.])[
  (A vacuum bend with uniform geometry).  #emph[The data in Proposition 9 can be modified farther out, inside their exact Schwarzschild part, so that they are induced by a constant-static-time slice outside a compact set. This modification adds no constraint deficit. Write $\( g_R \, K_R \)$ for the resulting unscaled data. In the common end coordinates the metrics are uniformly Euclidean comparable, and throughout all transition annuli 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.28)")
 $ \| partial g_R \|_delta + \| K_R \|_delta lt.eq C \/ r . $ <n3:reduction:bend-geometry>]

 There is a smooth proper radius $rho_R$ on the end which equals the original coordinate radius on the gluing annulus and equals the rest isotropic radius beyond the transitions, with 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.29)")
 $ c r lt.eq rho_R lt.eq C r \, #h(2em) \| d rho_R \|_(g_R)^2 gt.eq c \, #h(2em) \| Delta_(g_R) rho_R \| lt.eq C \/ r . $ <n3:reduction:radius-geometry>]

 All transition annuli lie between fixed multiples of $R$; the constants can depend on the prescribed timelike ADM vector, but not on $R$.]
] <n3:reduction:vacuum-bend>

#proof[
  Use the description $T = v dot.op y$, $y = A x$, from Lemma 6. Choose $R_b = c_b R$ so large, with $c_b$ fixed, that the region $\| y \| gt.eq R_b$ is entirely inside the exact reference part. Let $psi$ be a smooth function equal to one on $\( - oo \, 0 \]$, zero on $\[ 1 \, oo \)$, and taking values in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$. Replace the plane by the graph
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.30)")
   $ T_R \( y \) = \( v dot.op y \) psi (frac(log \( \| y \| \/ R_b \), L)) . $ <n3:reduction:bending-graph>]
  Its Euclidean gradient satisfies
  $ \| d T_R \|_delta lt.eq \| v \| #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + parallel psi' parallel_oo \/ L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Choose a fixed $L$ making the right side strictly smaller than one. The lapse and spatial coefficients in Equation (1.20) converge to their Minkowski values, so for all sufficiently large $R$ this graph is uniformly spacelike. In fact for positive Schwarzschild mass the lapse is smaller than one and the spatial conformal factor exceeds one, which only improves this estimate on the static exterior.
  The graph agrees with the original plane before $R_b$ and with a constant-time slice after $e^L R_b$. Its first derivative is bounded, and its second and third derivatives are $O \( r^(- 1) \)$ and $O \( r^(- 2) \)$. The induced metric is uniformly comparable with $delta$; differentiating its pullback formula gives the first bound in Equation (1.28). The second fundamental form uses the graph’s second derivatives and ambient first derivatives, divided by a uniformly positive spacelikeness factor, and gives the other bound. These estimates remain true in the $x$ coordinates because $A$ is a fixed invertible linear map. The constraints vanish on the entire bent part by the Gauss–Codazzi equations in exact vacuum Schwarzschild spacetime.
  It remains to make the radius precise. On the bent region keep $rho_R = \| x \| = \| A^(- 1) y \|$. Beyond the bend, where the slice is already static, write $y = r omega$ and put $a \( omega \) = \| A^(- 1) omega \|$. Choose $R_c$ a sufficiently large fixed multiple of $R_b e^L$, and a cutoff $chi_0$ equal to one before zero and zero after one. In that static region set
  $ rho_R \( r \, omega \) = r exp [chi_0 (frac(log \( r \/ R_c \), L_0)) log a \( omega \)] . $
  For fixed sufficiently large $L_0$ its radial derivative is bounded below by a positive constant: differentiating in $r$ gives the positive factor $rho_R \/ r$ times $1 + L_0^(- 1) chi'_0 log a$, which is at least $1 \/ 2$. The function and its first two Euclidean derivatives have the bounds $rho_R asymp r$, $\| d rho_R \| lt.eq C$, and $\| partial^2 rho_R \| lt.eq C \/ r$. Before this interpolation, the same bounds hold for $\| A^(- 1) y \|$, and uniform metric comparability gives the gradient lower bound. Combining these facts with Equation (1.28) proves Equation (1.29). After the interpolation, $rho_R = r$ is precisely the static isotropic radius. All radius ratios used in the construction are fixed, independently of $R$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.11.])[
  (A radial DEC repair of vanishing mass cost).  #emph[Consider the data obtained from Proposition 9 and Lemma 10. There is a smooth positive conformal factor $u_R$, constant on the unchanged inner region and tending to one at infinity, such that $\( u_R^4 g_R \, u_R^2 K_R \)$ satisfy DEC everywhere. Moreover 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.31)")
 $ parallel u_R - 1 parallel_oo = o \( R^(- 1) \) \, #h(2em) \| d u_R \| = o \( R^(- 2) \) quad upright("on the transition region") . $ <n3:reduction:repair-size>]

 The final end is exactly Schwarzschild with $K = 0$ and mass $m + o \( 1 \)$. The boundary expansion retains its original weak sign.]
] <n3:reduction:radial-repair>

#proof[
  All geometry estimates below are uniform in $R$. Enlarge the fixed transition region slightly so that, in terms of $t = rho_R \/ R$, it lies inside an interval $\( a \, b \)$ with $0 < a < b < oo$. Arrange that $t gt.eq b$ is in the static spherical region, and that the support of the possible deficit lies in a fixed smaller interval $I subset.double \( a \, b \)$. Increasing $b$ if needed makes these assertions follow from Lemma 10. We take $a = 1$: the compact supports in the annular correction leave a fixed neighborhood of $t = 1$ free of deficit. The factor constructed below will consequently be constant throughout the original region $r lt.eq R$, and is extended by that constant over the compact core of $N$.
  Let $eta_R arrow.r 0$ be as in Equation (1.26), replacing it by a positive majorant if necessary. We will choose $epsilon_R = C_0 eta_R$ and construct
  $ u_R = 1 + epsilon_R / R F_R \( rho_R \/ R \) \, $
  where $F_R$ and the relevant derivatives are bounded independently of $R$. Write $z_R = - F'_R$. On $\[ a \, b + 1 \]$, initially define $z_R$ by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.32)")
   $ z'_R = C_1 z_R + omega_1 \, #h(2em) z_R \( a \) = 0 \, $ <n3:reduction:repair-ode>]
  where $omega_1$ is smooth, nonnegative, zero near $a$, and at least one on $I$. Take $C_1$ sufficiently large, depending only on the constants in Equations (1.28) and (1.29). The solution is nonnegative and has uniform bounds on this fixed interval. For a function with $F'_R = - z_R$ the chain rule gives
  $ - Delta_(g_R) u_R - \| K_R \|_(g_R) \| d u_R \|_(g_R) & = epsilon_R / R^3 [z'_R \| d rho_R \|_(g_R)^2 + R z_R Delta_(g_R) rho_R - R z_R \| K_R \|_(g_R) \| d rho_R \|_(g_R)]\
 & gt.eq epsilon_R / R^3 \( c_1 z'_R - C_2 z_R \) . $
  Choose $C_1$ so that this is nonnegative and is at least $c_1 epsilon_R R^(- 3)$ on $I$.
  The preceding differential inequality need not be imposed on the spherical tail. There $K_R = 0$ and the Schwarzschild radial Laplace flux coefficient is $\( r + m \/ 2 \)^2$. Put $c_R = m \/ \( 2 R \)$ and, on the still spherical interval near $b$, define
  $ A_R \( t \) = \( t + c_R \)^2 z_R \( t \) . $
  It has positive derivative wherever $z_R > 0$. Continue it to a positive constant on $\[ b + 1 \, oo \)$ while keeping $A'_R gt.eq 0$. For an explicit smooth continuation, continue the solution of Equation (1.32) to $b + 1$, multiply the derivative of $\( t + c_R \)^2 z_R \( t \)$ by a nonnegative cutoff equal to one near $b$ and zero near $b + 1$, and integrate with the initial value $A_R \( b \)$. Define $z_R = A_R \( t \) \/ \( t + c_R \)^2$ on this tail, and set it to zero for $t lt.eq a$. All joins are smooth, the functions are nonnegative, and their bounds on finite intervals are uniform in $R$. The constant tail value, denoted $A_R^oo$, is bounded independently of $R$.
  Now define
  $ F_R \( t \) = integral_t^oo z_R \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s . $
  It is nonnegative, uniformly bounded, and constant before $a$. The preceding construction gives a superharmonic $u_R$ on the spherical tail, because its signed radial flux is $- epsilon_R A_R \( t \)$ and is nonincreasing. It also gives Equation (1.31). For large $R$, $1 lt.eq u_R lt.eq 2$. Increasing $C_0$ ensures
  $ - Delta_(g_R) u_R - \| K_R \| \| d u_R \| gt.eq u_R / 8 thin #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] 16 pi \( \| J_R \|_(g_R) - mu_R \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]]_(+) . $
  On the original inner region the conformal factor is constant and the original data satisfy DEC. Elsewhere this inequality and Lemma 3 prove DEC for the repaired data. Using $u_R$, rather than estimating $log u_R$ separately, incorporates the gradient-square term exactly.
  On the final end,
  $ u_R = 1 + frac(epsilon_R A_R^oo, r + m \/ 2) . $
  Writing $a_R = epsilon_R A_R^oo = o \( 1 \)$, one obtains the exact identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.33)")
   $ u_R^4 (1 + frac(m, 2 r))^4 delta = (1 + frac(m \/ 2 + a_R, r))^4 delta . $ <n3:reduction:repaired-schwarzschild>]
  Thus the final mass is $m + 2 a_R = m + o \( 1 \)$ and the momentum is zero. Multiplication of $K_R$ by $u_R^2$ preserves its compact support. On the inner boundary, where $u_R$ is constant, the expansion is simply multiplied by $u_R^(- 2)$, so its weak sign is preserved. ◻
]

===== 1.2.6 The numerical reduction <the-numerical-reduction>

The preceding construction has replaced the end while preserving DEC and the boundary expansion sign. A final strict conformal change puts the data in the class used for the elliptic deformation.

#thm([Proposition 1.12.])[
  (Reduction to strict data with a controlled rest end).  #emph[Let $\( N \, g \, K \)$ satisfy the exterior hypotheses of Theorem 2, including $theta_(+) lt.eq 0$ on its smooth compact boundary, and let $m = \( E^2 - \| P \|^2 \)^(1 \/ 2) > 0$. There is a sequence of smooth exterior data $\( g_j \, K_j \)$ on $N$ with the following properties:]
  + #emph[$mu_j > \| J_j \|_(g_j)$ everywhere and $theta_(+ \, j) < 0$ on $B$. The constraint densities are integrable. On the end their DEC margin is bounded below by $c_j r^(- 3 - delta)$ for some $c_j > 0$ and one fixed $0 < delta < 1$.]
  + #emph[$K_j$ is compactly supported, and in a rest chart $g_j - delta = O_k \( r^(- 1) \)$ and $R_(g_j) = O_k \( r^(- 3 - delta) \)$ for every $k$. In particular $"Ric"_(g_j) = O \( r^(- 3) \)$ there. Their ADM momentum is zero.]
  + #emph[$\( g_j \, K_j \) arrow.r \( g \, K \)$ smoothly on every fixed compact subset up to the boundary, their ADM energies satisfy $E_j arrow.r m$, and $a_(g_j) \( B \) arrow.r a_g \( B \)$.]
  #emph[Consequently it suffices to prove the energy/enclosing-area inequality for the class in (i)–(ii) in order to prove Theorem 2 in its stated class.]
] <n3:reduction:rest-reduction>

#proof[
  Apply Proposition 9, Lemma 10, and Lemma 11 along any sequence $R_j arrow.r oo$. The resulting DEC exteriors have compactly supported second fundamental form and an exact rest Schwarzschild end with energy tending to $m$. They agree with the original data on expanding compact subsets except for a constant conformal factor tending to one. Hence they converge smoothly on each fixed compact subset. Their boundary expansion is weakly negative.
  Apply the last part of Lemma 4 separately to each of these fixed exteriors, using the same chosen $0 < delta < 1$ and a compactly supported drift coefficient. Choose its positive parameter $s$ in Equation (1.13) small enough that the energy change, $parallel log \( 1 + s phi.alt \) parallel_oo$, and the supremum of its first derivative multiplied by the fixed original coordinate radius are at most $1 \/ j$. Also require the change in the first $j$ smooth norms on the first $j$ members of a compact exhaustion to be at most $1 \/ j$. All these quantities are finite for the fixed $j$-th exterior, so these choices are possible. No estimate uniform in the receding bending radius is required for this final strictification.
  The resulting data satisfy (i). The strict conformal factor has symbol decay of all orders and does not enlarge the support of $K$. On the far Schwarzschild end the drift vanishes, so $- Delta phi.alt = w = r^(- 3 - delta)$. Since the rest Schwarzschild metric has zero scalar curvature and $K = 0$ there, the exact $u$-formula gives
  $ R_(g_j) = 8 s \( 1 + s phi.alt \)^(- 5) r^(- 3 - delta) . $
  This proves the scalar-curvature bound in (ii). The Ricci bound follows from the two-derivative metric decay. The energy change tends to zero and the momentum change is zero. The prescribed smallness gives smooth local convergence, proving (ii) and the local convergence and charge assertions in (iii).
  The metrics before strictification satisfy the uniform comparisons and scaled first-derivative bounds of Lemma 5, in the fixed original end chart: the reference plane, the bend, and the radius interpolation involve only fixed linear maps and fixed radius ratios. The radial repair preserves these bounds. The final strictification parameters were chosen to preserve them as well. That lemma therefore gives the convergence of enclosing infima.
  If the reduced energy inequality is available, applying it to these data gives
  $ E_j gt.eq sqrt(a_(g_j) \( B \) \/ \( 16 pi \)) . $
  Passing to the limit proves $m gt.eq sqrt(a_g \( B \) \/ \( 16 pi \))$. Only the energies and enclosing infima enter this passage to the original data. ◻
]

==== 1.3 Trapped domains, collars, and weak supports <n3:domains:section>

We prepare the domain and boundary values for the elliptic deformation. The construction has three steps: choose maximal trapped regions, foliate their outer collars, and fix small expansion offsets. Throughout, the data satisfy Proposition 12. Write $S_0$ for their inner boundary. Thus $S_0$ is strictly future outer trapped, $K$ has compact support, and the single end has the smooth $O \( r^(- 1) \)$ bounds obtained in that reduction. In particular, sufficiently large coordinate spheres have positive outward mean curvature. The function

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.34)")
 $ frak(m) \( x \) = 8 pi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] mu \( x \) - \| J \( x \) \|_g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $ <n3:domains:margin>]

is positive, has a positive lower bound on every compact set, and has the positive $r^(- 3 - delta)$ lower bound on the end supplied by the reduction, with $0 < delta < 1$. Let $A_(*)$ denote the reduced data’s filled enclosing perimeter infimum.

===== 1.3.1 The bounding-region convention <the-bounding-region-convention>

For a symmetric tensor $Q$ and a two-sided surface $Sigma$ with a specified unit normal $nu$, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.35)")
 $ Theta_Q \( Sigma \, nu \) = H_Sigma \( nu \) + "tr"_Sigma Q . $ <n3:domains:expansion>]

For the boundary of a domain the specified normal always points #emph[out of that domain]. Notice both elementary identities

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.36)")
 $ Theta_(Q - \( c \/ 2 \) g) = Theta_Q - c \, #h(2em) Theta_(- Q) \( Sigma \, - nu \) = - Theta_Q \( Sigma \, nu \) . $ <n3:domains:shift-reversal>]

The factor $1 \/ 2$ in the first formula is the reciprocal of the surface dimension.

We use the following geometric theorem. Its boundary parts may be disconnected.

#thm([Theorem 1.13.])[
  (MOTS barriers and the total trapped region).  #emph[Let $\( N \, g \, Q \)$ be a smooth compact three-dimensional initial data set with boundary $Gamma_(-) union.sq Gamma_(+)$. Assume $Gamma_(+)$ is nonempty and has positive expansion with its normal out of $N$. The inner part $Gamma_(-)$ may be empty; when it is present, assume its expansion is negative with its normal into $N$.]
  #emph[Consider all smooth domains whose inner boundary is $Gamma_(-)$ and whose remaining boundary $Sigma$ lies in the interior of $N$ and satisfies $Theta_Q \( Sigma \) lt.eq 0$. They avoid $Gamma_(+)$, and their normal on $Sigma$ points out of the domain. If their union is nonempty, its closure has a smooth compact embedded stable MOTS frontier in the interior, separating it from $Gamma_(+)$. This closure is itself the closure of such a domain and contains every domain in the defining class. If $Gamma_(-)$ is nonempty, the two strict barriers also give a nonempty smooth embedded enclosing MOTS.]
] <n3:domains:region-theorem>

The total-region assertion is #cite(label("AnderssonMetzger2009")) Definitions 7.1–7.2 and Theorem 7.3; the barrier assertion is Theorem 3.1 there, with stability furnished by Theorem 4.1. The preliminary conventions in Section 2 of that paper allow an empty inner boundary. These results require no energy condition on $Q$. We therefore apply them to the shifted tensors in Equation (1.36), even though these tensors need not satisfy the dominant energy condition. A component of the complement of a smooth trapped domain which meets no designated outer boundary may be filled: its entire boundary is then deleted and the remaining boundary has the same expansion. The maximal region is therefore filled in this relative sense.

We use compact inner fillings only to express enclosing competitors. One may attach a smooth collar behind $S_0$, extend the one-sided metric smoothly, and complete the inner side by a fixed compact filling. All competitors contain that filling and only their boundaries in the original exterior are counted. This introduces no geometric condition behind $S_0$. For perimeter minimization with this smooth obstacle, the relevant regularity input is #cite(label("HuiskenIlmanen2001")) Regularity Theorem 1.3(iii): the minimizing boundary is $C^(1 \, 1)$ and is smooth away from contact. We retain the filled enclosing class and its smooth approximation from Section 2. The trapped regions below are not assumed to minimize perimeter.

===== 1.3.2 Choosing the two maximal regions <choosing-the-two-maximal-regions>

Fix a large sphere $S_(R_0)$ outside $"supp" K$ such that all coordinate spheres with radius at least $R_0$ have positive outward mean curvature. A compact smooth domain with $Theta_(plus.minus K) lt.eq c lt.eq 0$ cannot reach this part of the end. Indeed, at a point where its boundary maximizes the coordinate radius, it lies inside the tangent coordinate sphere and has the same outward normal. The graph second-derivative comparison gives $H_(partial D) gt.eq H_(S_r) > 0$ there; also $K = 0$ there. This contradicts its assumed expansion. The same argument applies to an enclosing domain with prescribed inner obstacle, since the maximum radius is attained on its free boundary. Consequently all the negative-threshold regions used below lie in one fixed compact radial range. They may be constructed using any sufficiently distant sphere as the outer barrier, without dependence on that choice.

For

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.37)")
 $ max_(S_0) Theta_K \( S_0 \) < c < 0 \, $ <n3:domains:black-interval>]

let $cal(B)_c$ be the closed total trapped region for the condition $Theta_K lt.eq c$, with $S_0$ as the required inner boundary and with its inner filling understood. It is nonempty: a sufficiently short outward collar of $S_0$ supplies a strict seed. Theorem 13, applied to $K - \( c \/ 2 \) g$, gives its smooth stable frontier with expansion exactly $c$. The regions $cal(B)_c$ increase with $c$. Their complement toward the end has no bounded component, since such a component could be filled to enlarge the total trapped region.

We will choose $c_b < 0$ close to zero and set $cal(B) = cal(B)_(c_b)$. In the complement of $cal(B)$, use the full black frontier and a distant coordinate sphere as #emph[outer] barrier faces. With the normal out of this complement, the black frontier has expansion for $- K$ equal to $- c_b > 0$. For $c < 0$ close to zero, let $cal(W)_c$ be the closed total trapped region of compact smooth domains in that complement with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.38)")
 $ Theta_(- K) = H - "tr"_Sigma K lt.eq c . $ <n3:domains:white-condition>]

There is no required inner boundary in this application of Theorem 13. The shifted expansion of a reversed black face is $- c_b - c > 0$, and that of a distant sphere is also positive. Thus the theorem applies whenever the defining union is nonempty. When the union is empty we simply put $cal(W)_c = diameter$. When nonempty, it has smooth stable frontier of white expansion $c$ and lies a positive distance from all black faces. The family $cal(W)_c$ is increasing, with the black region held fixed.

To control limits of the deformation, we choose thresholds at which these regions are right-continuous. The following lemma includes empty regions.

#thm([Lemma 1.14.])[
  (Right-continuous thresholds).  #emph[Let $E_c$, $c$ in an interval, be an increasing family of closed subsets of a fixed compact metric space. Outside a countable set of parameters, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.39)")
 $ E_(c_j) arrow.r E_c quad upright("in Hausdorff distance whenever") quad c_j arrow.b c . $ <n3:domains:hausdorff-right>]

 Here continuity at $E_c = diameter$ means that $E_(c')$ is empty for every sufficiently close $c' > c$.]
] <n3:domains:right-continuity>

#proof[
  Choose a countable dense set ${ x_i }$ and a number $L$ larger than the diameter of the compact space. Set $d_c \( x \) = min { L \, "dist" \( x \, E_c \) }$, with the value $L$ when $E_c$ is empty. For each $i$, $c mapsto d_c \( x_i \)$ is a bounded nonincreasing real function, so its right discontinuities form a countable set. Avoid their countable union. Then $d_(c_j) \( x_i \) arrow.r d_c \( x_i \)$ for all $i$ whenever $c_j arrow.b c$. Every $d_c$ is $1$-Lipschitz. A finite net chosen from the dense set therefore upgrades this convergence to uniform convergence on the compact space.
  For nonempty $E_c$, if points $y_j in E_(c_j)$ stayed a fixed distance from $E_c$, uniform convergence evaluated at $y_j$ would contradict $d_(c_j) \( y_j \) = 0$. The opposite Hausdorff inclusion follows from $E_c subset E_(c_j)$. For empty $E_c$, uniform convergence to $L$ is incompatible with any nonempty $E_(c_j)$, since its distance function has a zero. This proves the stated convention as well. ◻
]

Choose $c_b$ at such a right-continuity point of the black family, then choose $c_w < 0$ at such a point of the white family for this fixed black region. Both choices can be made arbitrarily close to zero. Write $cal(W) = cal(W)_(c_w)$, and let $Omega$ be the closure of the component of the complement of $cal(B) union cal(W)$ which reaches the selected end. Its boundary $B$ is a finite disjoint union of complete components of the two smooth frontiers. Write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.40)")
 $ B = B_b union.sq B_w \, #h(2em) H = H_B \( nu \) \, #h(2em) P_B = "tr"_B K \, $ <n3:domains:faces>]

where $nu$ points into $Omega$. Thus

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.41)")
 $ H + P_B = c_b quad upright("on ") B_b \, #h(2em) H - P_B = c_w quad upright("on ") B_w . $ <n3:domains:face-expansions>]

The set $B_w$ may be empty, and $B_b$ need not contain every black frontier component: a white component may cut a black face off from the end. We retain exactly the boundary of the component that reaches infinity. The compact, disjoint frontiers create no intersection corners.

The excluded side of $B$ contains $S_0$ and a collar of it. Hence every compact smooth enclosing cut $T$ in $Omega$, with all its components retained, is an enclosing competitor for the original filled obstacle after adjoining the discarded regions to its inner side. It follows that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.42)")
 $ \| T \|_g gt.eq A_(*) . $ <n3:domains:inherited-area>]

The same conclusion holds for perimeter competitors. This is a statement about inclusion of competitor classes; no minimizing property of $B$ is used.

===== 1.3.3 Outward collars, including zero stability eigenvalue <outward-collars-including-zero-stability-eigenvalue>

#thm([Lemma 1.15.])[
  (Collars of a maximal frontier).  #emph[Let $Sigma$ be a connected component of the frontier of a closed total trapped region at threshold $c$, for a fixed tensor $Q$. There is a smooth foliation $Sigma_s$, $0 lt.eq s < s_0$, of an outward collar, with $Sigma_0 = Sigma$ and positive outward normal velocity, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.43)")
 $ Theta_Q \( Sigma_s \) > c quad \( 0 < s < s_0 \) \, #h(2em) Theta_Q \( Sigma_0 \) = c . $ <n3:domains:collar-expansion>]

 In particular its leaf parameter $s$ is a smooth defining function and $\| nabla s \|$ is bounded above and below by positive constants.]
] <n3:domains:stable-collar>

#proof[
  Use normal graphs $Sigma \( v \)$ over $Sigma$ and pull their expansion back to $Sigma$. The map
  $ Phi : C^(2 \, alpha) \( Sigma \) arrow.r C^(0 \, alpha) \( Sigma \) \, #h(2em) Phi \( v \) = Theta_Q \( Sigma \( v \) \) - c $
  is smooth near zero and has linearization $L = D Phi \( 0 \)$, the MOTS stability operator for $Q - \( c \/ 2 \) g$. Its principal part is $- Delta_Sigma$. Stability gives a principal eigenvalue $lambda gt.eq 0$ and a positive smooth eigenfunction $phi$. If $lambda > 0$, take $v = s phi$. Smooth dependence gives $Phi \( s phi \) = s lambda phi + O \( s^2 \)$ in $C^0$, which is positive for all sufficiently small $s > 0$. The graph velocity is positive as well.
  Suppose $lambda = 0$. The kernel of $L$ is spanned by $phi$, and the adjoint kernel is spanned by a positive smooth function $phi^(*)$. These are the principal-eigenfunction facts for a scalar elliptic operator on a connected closed surface. For example, simplicity of the kernel also follows by writing a kernel element as $v = phi w$ and applying the strong maximum principle to the resulting equation for $w$, whose zeroth-order term vanishes. Consider the augmented map
  $ cal(G) \( v \, a \) = (Phi \( v \) - a \, med integral_Sigma v thin thin upright(d) A) . $
  Its derivative at $\( 0 \, 0 \)$ is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.44)")
   $ \( v \, a \) mapsto (L v - a \, med integral_Sigma v thin thin upright(d) A) . $ <n3:domains:augmented-linearization>]
  This map is an isomorphism. Indeed, to solve $L v - a = f$ with $integral v = b$, the Fredholm compatibility condition uniquely fixes
  $ a = - frac(integral_Sigma phi^(*) f thin thin upright(d) A, integral_Sigma phi^(*) thin thin upright(d) A) . $
  The equation for $v$ then has a solution; adding a multiple of $phi$ imposes its prescribed integral uniquely. Elliptic estimates and this one-dimensional normalization give a bounded inverse between the indicated Hölder spaces.
  The implicit-function theorem consequently supplies smooth $v \( s \) \, a \( s \)$ with $cal(G) \( v \( s \) \, a \( s \) \) = \( 0 \, s \)$. Differentiating at zero and pairing with $phi^(*)$ gives $a' \( 0 \) = 0$ and
  $ v' \( 0 \) = frac(phi, integral_Sigma phi thin thin upright(d) A) > 0 . $
  After shortening the interval, all these graphs form an outward foliation and have constant expansion $c + a \( s \)$. If $a \( s \) lt.eq 0$ at any positive $s$ in this interval, replace only this frontier component by $Sigma \( v \( s \) \)$. The collar is disjoint from all other components and designated barriers, so the new smooth bounding domain strictly contains the maximal region and its every free boundary component has expansion at most $c$. This contradicts maximality. Therefore $a \( s \) > 0$ for every such $s > 0$.
  In either case positive graph velocity and compactness give the asserted bounds on $\| nabla s \|$. Smooth elliptic bootstrapping of the graph equation gives smooth leaves in the zero-eigenvalue case. ◻
]

Apply this lemma separately with $Q = K$ to black faces and $Q = - K$ to white faces. There are finitely many faces, so choose disjoint collars in $Omega$ and shorten them to have a common positive parameter range when convenient. Write $nu_s = nabla s \/ \| nabla s \|$ for their outward leaf normal; it agrees with $nu$ at $B$.

===== 1.3.4 From weak exterior supports to a smooth enclosure <from-weak-exterior-supports-to-a-smooth-enclosure>

The sublevel limits used below need not have smooth frontiers. We now show how an expansion bound on their exterior supports produces a smooth enclosure. The argument applies to arbitrary closed sets, without positive reach or a curvature bound on the supporting surfaces.

#prodef([Definition 1.16.])[
  Let $D$ be closed in a smooth three-manifold and let $x in partial D$. A smooth exterior support is a smooth function $phi.alt$ near $x$ with $phi.alt \( x \) = 0$, $thin upright(d) phi.alt \( x \) eq.not 0$, and $D subset { phi.alt lt.eq 0 }$ locally near $x$. Its normal is $n = nabla phi.alt \/ \| nabla phi.alt \|$. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.45)")
   $ cal(E)_Q \[ phi.alt \] \( x \) = frac(\( g^(i j) - n^i n^j \) nabla_i nabla_j phi.alt, \| nabla phi.alt \|) + "tr" Q - Q \( n \, n \) . $ <n3:domains:test-expansion>]
  We say $D$ has exterior-support expansion at most $c$ if every such support at every point of its frontier has $cal(E)_Q \[ phi.alt \] lt.eq c$.
] <n3:domains:support-definition>

For a smooth domain this is exactly the usual upper bound on its outward expansion: at contact an exterior supporting graph has mean curvature at most that of the enclosed smooth graph. The definition is unchanged by increasing smooth reparametrizations of $phi.alt$, because the additional Hessian term is a multiple of $thin upright(d) phi.alt ⊗ thin upright(d) phi.alt$ and has zero tangential trace.

#thm([Lemma 1.17.])[
  (Parallel neighborhoods and additive error).  #emph[Suppose the frontier of a closed set $D$ lies in a fixed compact interior region of smooth $\( N \, g \, Q \)$ and has exterior-support expansion at most $c$. Put $D_z = { y : "dist" \( y \, D \) lt.eq z }$. For sufficiently small $z > 0$, all exterior supports of $D_z$ have expansion at most 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.46)")
 $ c + C z . $ <n3:domains:parallel-estimate>]

 The allowable radius and $C$ depend only on the compact ambient geometry and $Q$, and not on the support, its second fundamental form, or the regularity of $D$.]
] <n3:domains:parallel-support>

#proof[
  Take a support $phi.alt$ to $D_z$ at $x'$. There is a nearest point $x in D$ and a length-$z$ minimizing unit-speed geodesic $gamma : \[ 0 \, z \] arrow.r N$ from $x$ to $x'$. For the radii under consideration the entire segment lies in a fixed smooth compact neighborhood and is shorter than its injectivity radius. The ball $overline(B)_z \( x \)$ is contained in $D_z$ and touches its exterior support at $x'$. Its tangent plane there therefore agrees with that of the support and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.47)")
   $ frac(nabla phi.alt \( x' \), \| nabla phi.alt \( x' \) \|) = dot(gamma) \( z \) . $ <n3:domains:radial-normal>]
  Let $P_t : T_x N arrow.r T_(gamma \( t \)) N$ denote parallel transport and put $P = P_z$.
  For every $X in T_x N$ solve the Jacobi boundary problem
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.48)")
   $ J''_X + "Rm" \( J_X \, dot(gamma) \) dot(gamma) = 0 \, #h(2em) J_X \( 0 \) = X \, #h(2em) J_X \( z \) = P X . $ <n3:domains:jacobi-endpoints>]
  Here primes denote covariant differentiation along $gamma$. The short interval and absence of conjugate points make this problem uniquely solvable. Its estimates can be seen without division by an uncontrolled quantity. In a parallel frame write $j_X \( t \) = P_t^(- 1) J_X \( t \)$ and $cal(R) \( t \)$ for the curvature coefficient. The integral equation is
  $ j_X \( t \) = X + t A_X - integral_0^t \( t - s \) cal(R) \( s \) j_X \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s \, #h(2em) A_X = 1 / z integral_0^z \( z - s \) cal(R) \( s \) j_X \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s . $
  It implies, by absorption for small $z$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.49)")
   $ sup_(0 lt.eq t lt.eq z) \| j_X \( t \) \| lt.eq 2 \| X \| \, #h(2em) \| A_X \| lt.eq C z \| X \| . $ <n3:domains:jacobi-estimates>]
  Also $chevron.l J_X \, dot(gamma) chevron.r$ is affine in $t$ and has equal endpoint values. Thus $chevron.l A_X \, dot(gamma) \( 0 \) chevron.r = 0$.
  We may therefore prescribe the first jet of a smooth unit vector field $V$ near $x$ by
  $ V \( x \) = dot(gamma) \( 0 \) \, #h(2em) nabla_X V \( x \) = A_X . $
  To realize it with uniform second-jet bounds, choose a smooth local orthonormal frame obtained by radial parallel transport from $x$. In that frame take the affine coefficient vector with the prescribed value and first derivatives, and normalize its length. The orthogonality just proved means normalization preserves its prescribed first derivatives. The frame has uniformly bounded derivatives on a fixed small coordinate ball, and Equation (1.49) therefore gives uniformly bounded second derivatives of $V$. This construction uses the radial direction and the ambient geometry, with no derivatives of the support $phi.alt$.
  Define the local endpoint map
  $ F_z \( y \) = exp_y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] z V \( y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Its differential at $x$ is the terminal value of the Jacobi field with initial values $X \, nabla_X V \( x \)$. By Equation (1.48), it has the exact property
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.50)")
   $ F_z \( x \) = x' \, #h(2em) \( thin upright(d) F_z \)_x = P \, #h(2em) \| \( nabla thin upright(d) F_z \)_x \| lt.eq C z . $ <n3:domains:exact-endpoint-isometry>]
  The last bound follows by differentiating the smooth map $\( y \, v \, z \) mapsto exp_y \( z v \)$ twice in $y$, including the first and second derivatives of $V$. At $z = 0$ the map is the identity and its covariant second derivative vanishes. Its $z$-derivative is uniformly bounded for the bounded jets just constructed, giving the displayed $C z$ bound. The exact middle identity, rather than a near-isometry estimate, is essential.
  The map $F_z$ takes $D$ locally into $D_z$, since its defining geodesic from any $y in D$ has length $z$. Consequently $psi = phi.alt compose F_z$ is an exterior support to $D$ at $x$. Its derivative is $P^(*) thin upright(d) phi.alt$, so its gradient length is exactly $\| nabla phi.alt \( x' \) \|$ and, by Equation (1.47), its normal is $dot(gamma) \( 0 \)$. The covariant chain rule gives
  $ "Hess" psi \( X \, Y \) = "Hess" phi.alt \( P X \, P Y \) + thin upright(d) phi.alt #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( nabla thin upright(d) F_z \) \( X \, Y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] quad upright("at ") x . $
  Trace over an orthonormal basis of $dot(gamma) \( 0 \)^perp$ and divide by the common gradient length. The first term is exactly the corresponding normalized tangential trace at $x'$, and the remaining term has absolute value at most $C z$. Finally, the tensor trace changes by at most $C z$, by the $C^1$ bound on $Q$ and parallel transport. It follows that
  $ \| cal(E)_Q \[ psi \] \( x \) - cal(E)_Q \[ phi.alt \] \( x' \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] lt.eq C z . $
  The assumed support inequality at $x$ proves Equation (1.46). There is no term containing $z \| "Hess" phi.alt \|$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.18.])[
  (A smooth enclosure from weak supports).  #emph[Let $N$ be a smooth compact connected three-manifold with boundary $Gamma_(-) union.sq Gamma_(+)$, where $Gamma_(+)$ is nonempty and $Gamma_(-)$ may be empty. Let $D$ be a nonempty closed subset which contains a relative collar of $Gamma_(-)$ when that boundary is prescribed, and stays a positive distance from $Gamma_(+)$. Suppose its interior frontier has exterior-support expansion at most $c$ for a smooth tensor $Q$.]
  #emph[For every $c' > c$ such that $Theta_Q \( Gamma_(+) \) > c'$ with its normal out of $N$, there is a smooth bounding domain $E$ containing $D$ in its relative interior, containing the required inner collar, and avoiding $Gamma_(+)$, whose free boundary satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.51)")
 $ Theta_Q \( partial E \\ Gamma_(-) \) = c' . $ <n3:domains:smooth-enclosure-expansion>]

 Its boundary and the domain may be disconnected. In particular $D$ is contained in the closed total trapped region at any such larger threshold. No regularity or positive-thickness assumption on $D$ is required.]
] <n3:domains:weak-support>

#proof[
  All distance neighborhoods in this proof are relative to $N$. Since $D$ contains a collar of $Gamma_(-)$ and avoids $Gamma_(+)$, their new frontiers, at sufficiently small radii, lie in a compact subset of the interior. A minimizing segment ending on a new frontier has its nearest point on the interior frontier of $D$; it cannot start on $Gamma_(-)$ because its covered collar has fixed positive width. Thus the proof of Lemma 17 applies unchanged. Choose $beta > 0$ so small that its conclusion holds for $0 < z lt.eq beta$, all these frontiers avoid the prescribed boundary, and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.52)")
   $ c + C beta < c' . $ <n3:domains:band-gap>]
  We first construct a smooth inner enclosure without imposing any expansion bound on it. Smoothly approximate the continuous distance function $d_D = "dist" \( dot.op \, D \)$ on $N$ uniformly, with error less than $beta \/ 32$, using a finite coordinate cover, mollification, and a partition of unity. Choose a regular value in $\( beta \/ 3 \, beta \/ 2 \)$ of the resulting smooth function. Its sublevel set $U$ has smooth interior boundary and, for example, satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.53)")
   $ D_(beta \/ 4) subset U subset D_(3 beta \/ 4) . $ <n3:domains:initial-smooth-neighborhood>]
  The boundary $Gamma_(-)$ is contained in its relative interior and $Gamma_(+)$ is excluded. If a component of $N \\ overline(U)$ meets no component of $Gamma_(+)$, fill it. This only deletes components of the smooth free boundary. After this filling the second inclusion in Equation (1.53) need not hold for its interior, but every remaining free boundary component still lies in the indicated thin neighborhood, and the first inclusion still holds.
  Choose $eta in C^oo \( N \)$, $0 lt.eq eta lt.eq 1$, equal to one on that free boundary and supported in ${ d_D < beta }$. Its existence uses the positive separation between this compact smooth boundary and the closed set ${ d_D gt.eq beta }$; it requires no smoothness of $d_D$. For a sufficiently large finite constant $A$, the tensor
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.54)")
   $ Q_A = Q - 1 / 2 \( c' + A eta \) g $ <n3:domains:modified-tensor>]
  makes the free boundary of $U$ a strictly negative inner barrier:
  $ Theta_(Q_A) \( partial U \) = Theta_Q \( partial U \) - c' - A < 0 . $
  All prescribed outer faces remain strictly positive for $Q_A$, because $eta = 0$ there and their original expansion exceeds $c'$. Apply the barrier part of Theorem 13 on each component of $N \\ overline(U)$. Each meets $Gamma_(+)$ by the filling step and has nonempty inner boundary by connectedness of $N$ and $U eq.not diameter$. We obtain smooth MOTSs $Sigma$ bounding a domain $E$ which contains $overline(U)$ and avoids $Gamma_(+)$. They satisfy, with their normal out of $E$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.55)")
   $ Theta_Q \( Sigma \) = c' + A eta gt.eq c' . $ <n3:domains:modified-mots-expansion>]
  We claim $Sigma$ misses ${ d_D < beta }$. Otherwise set $z = min_Sigma d_D < beta$. The first inclusion in Equation (1.53) gives $z gt.eq beta \/ 4 > 0$. Moreover $D_z$ lies on the inner side of $Sigma$. To see this directly, if $d_D \( y \) < z$, a shortest path from $y$ to $D$ of length less than $z$ cannot cross $Sigma$: such a crossing would have distance from $D$ less than $z$. Since $D subset E$, the whole path and $y$ lie in $E$. Closure gives $D_z subset overline(E)$.
  At a point attaining the minimum, a defining function for the smooth boundary $Sigma$ is therefore an exterior support to $D_z$, with the same outward normal as in Equation (1.55). The parallel support bound and Equation (1.52) imply
  $ Theta_Q \( Sigma \) lt.eq c + C z < c' \, $
  contradicting Equation (1.55). This excludes the entire modification range, so $eta = 0$ on $Sigma$ and proves Equation (1.51). The contained $D_(beta \/ 4)$ gives strict enclosure of $D$. If desired, fill any remaining complementary components which meet no designated outer face; this deletes smooth boundary components and preserves all stated properties. ◻
]

For an exterior, truncate by a distant sphere that is strict at the larger threshold. In the black construction, $cal(B)$ already contains the original inner collar. In the white construction there is no required inner boundary, and the black faces belong to $Gamma_(+)$. The later applications therefore need compact avoidance of the foreign faces; they need no boundary regularity of the weak set. Empty weak sets can be omitted.

===== 1.3.5 Small offsets and the prepared domain <small-offsets-and-the-prepared-domain>

#thm([Proposition 1.19.])[
  (Prepared domain).  #emph[The thresholds and the domain above can be chosen with the following properties. The numbers $c_b \, c_w < 0$ are right-continuity points of their respective full maximal-region families. The boundary of $Omega$ consists of the faces in Equation (1.41), has disjoint smooth outward leaf collars as in Lemma 15, and preserves the enclosing-area lower bound in Equation (1.42).]
  #emph[There are a smooth compactly supported function $C$ on $Omega$, constants $C_b gt.eq 0$, $C_w lt.eq 0$, and positive constants $k_B$ on its face collars such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.56)")
 $ C_w lt.eq C lt.eq C_b \, & #h(2em) C = C_b - k_B s &  & upright("in a black collar near ") B_b \,\
 & #h(2em) C = C_w + k_B s &  & upright("in a white collar near ") B_w . $ <n3:domains:offset-collars>]

 For an absent type, its corresponding constant is simply a bound. Set 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.57)")
 $ b_(-) = c_b - C_b < 0 \, #h(2em) b_(+) = - c_w - C_w > 0 . $ <n3:domains:assigned-heights>]

 There is a smooth positive function $rho$, equal to a positive constant times $r^(- 4)$ on the far end and satisfying symbol derivative bounds there, such that, for every $h in \[ b_(-) \, b_(+) \]$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.58)")
 $ \| \( h + C \) "tr" K \| + \| nabla C \| + rho lt.eq 1 / 2 frak(m) quad upright("everywhere on ") Omega . $ <n3:domains:offset-smallness>]

 The thresholds, collars, offset, and $rho$ are fixed before the deformation parameters are chosen. After fixing the thresholds and collars, the offset and its first derivatives can be made arbitrarily small.]
] <n3:domains:prepared-domain>

#proof[
  The large-sphere comparison above confines all negative-threshold regions to a fixed compact set, independently of how close the thresholds are to zero. Choose a slightly larger compact set $cal(K)$ containing this range and $"supp" K$ in its interior. Let
  $ m_0 = min_(cal(K)) frak(m) > 0 \, #h(2em) T_0 = sup_(cal(K)) \| "tr" K \| . $
  First restrict both negative thresholds to an interval so close to zero that
  $ max { \| c_b \| \, \| c_w \| } thin T_0 < m_0 \/ 8 \, $
  while retaining Equation (1.37). Lemma 14 allows the required successive choices in that interval. Construct the two regions, select $Omega$, and fix their disjoint collars inside $cal(K)$ as above. All ensuing constants may depend on these fixed choices.
  Here is an explicit offset construction. On each collar choose a smooth nonincreasing cutoff $chi \( s \)$, equal to one near $s = 0$ and zero near the collar’s far end. Choose $kappa_B > 0$ so that $1 - kappa_B s > 0$ throughout that collar. For a common amplitude $a > 0$, define the contribution of a black collar to be $a chi \( s \) \( 1 - kappa_B s \)$, and that of a white collar to be $- a chi \( s \) \( 1 - kappa_B s \)$. Extend by zero outside the disjoint collars and sum. This produces a smooth function on the manifold with boundary $Omega$, supported in $cal(K)$, with $C_b = a$, $C_w = - a$, and $k_B = a kappa_B$ on the respective collars. These remain valid bounds when a type is absent. Its $C^1$ norm tends to zero with $a$, even though the collar widths have already been fixed.
  For $h in \[ b_(-) \, b_(+) \]$ and $C in \[ C_w \, C_b \]$,
  $ \| h + C \| lt.eq max { \| c_b \| \, \| c_w \| } + C_b - C_w . $
  Choose $a$ so small that $\( C_b - C_w \) T_0 + parallel nabla C parallel_oo < m_0 \/ 8$. The first two terms of Equation (1.58) are then at most $m_0 \/ 4$ on $cal(K)$ and vanish outside $cal(K)$. Finally take a fixed smooth positive weight equal to $r^(- 4)$ far out and multiply it by a sufficiently small positive constant. The end lower bound for $frak(m)$ and $delta < 1$, together with compact positivity, allow the choice $rho lt.eq frak(m) \/ 4$ everywhere. This proves Equation (1.58). All remaining assertions were proved in constructing the regions and collars. ◻
]

At the assigned face heights the quantity $h + C$ consequently has the useful exact values

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.59)")
 $ h + C & = c_b = P_B + H &  & upright("on ") B_b upright(" when ") h = b_(-) \,\
h + C & = - c_w = P_B - H &  & upright("on ") B_w upright(" when ") h = b_(+) . $ <n3:domains:compatible-face-values>]

These identities determine the deformation’s boundary signs. Right-continuity and Lemma 18 will exclude limiting sublevel sets away from the corresponding full trapped regions.

==== 1.4 The elliptic deformation and exclusion of the floor <n3:deformation:section>

We define the coupled system, derive its scalar-curvature identity, and choose a continuation path along which the conformal factor cannot touch its lower bound. We use the fixed data of Proposition 19. Thus $Omega$ has one controlled asymptotically flat end, $K$ is compactly supported, and its compact boundary $B = B_b union B_w$ consists of black and white faces. Either type may be absent. The normal $nu$ points into $Omega$, and

$ H = "div"_B nu \, #h(2em) P_B = "tr"_B K . $

The numbers $b_(-) < 0 < b_(+)$, the smooth compactly supported offset $C$, and the positive function $rho = O \( r^(- 4) \)$ are fixed as in that proposition. In particular,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.60)")
 $ b_(-) + C = P_B + H med upright("on ") B_b \, #h(2em) b_(+) + C = P_B - H med upright("on ") B_w . $ <n3:deformation:face-thresholds>]

All compact sets and geometric constants in this section are fixed after this preparation. Extend the end radius to a smooth function on $overline(Omega)$ bounded below by one. Write $Omega_R$ for the large spherical truncations and $S_R$ for their outer boundary.

A #emph[polynomial constant] is bounded by $C \( 1 + n \)^N$, with $C \, N$ depending on the prepared data and fixed auxiliary profiles, but not on $R$, the homotopy parameter, or the solution. Constants permitted to depend arbitrarily on fixed $n$ will be identified explicitly. First fix $0 < epsilon.alt < 1$, then choose $n$ sufficiently large, and finally require $R gt.eq R_min \( n \, epsilon.alt \)$. We always take $n gt.eq 4$ and $n > 2 \/ epsilon.alt$.

===== 1.4.1 Variables and equations <n3:deformation:variables>

Choose a smooth nonincreasing $theta.alt : bb(R) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ equal to one on $\( - oo \, 0 \]$ and zero on $\[ 1 \, oo \)$. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.61)")
 $ p \( t \) = n theta.alt \( n t \) \, #h(2em) l \( t \) = exp (integral_0^t p \( s \) thin upright(d) s) \,\
L \( t \) = l \( t \) e^(2 t) \, #h(2em) p_L = p + 2 . $ <n3:deformation:l-profile>]

Thus $l \( 0 \) = 1$, $l \( t \) = e^(n t)$ for $t lt.eq 0$, and $l$ is constant for $t gt.eq 1 \/ n$. Derivatives of $p$ of every fixed order are polynomially bounded. Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.62)")
 $ ell = e^(- epsilon.alt n) \, #h(2em) tau = ell^(3 \/ 2) . $ <n3:deformation:small-parameters>]

Whenever $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$, one has $ell lt.eq l \( t \) lt.eq e$.

For a function $f$, set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.63)")
 $ sigma = \| nabla f \| \, quad D = 1 + l^2 sigma^2 \, quad d = D^(- 1) \,\
a = l sigma \/ sqrt(D) \, quad v = a^2 = 1 - d \, quad w = l nabla f \/ sqrt(D) \, quad chi = 4 d \/ \( 4 + p v \) \,\
Z = t + 1 / 8 log D \, quad u = L sqrt(D) \, quad U = l sqrt(D) \, quad h = tau f . $ <n3:deformation:basic-variables>]

Derivatives and contractions refer to $g$ unless indicated otherwise. At $sigma > 0$, write $e = nabla f \/ sigma$ and $A_j = I + \( j - 1 \) e ⊗ e$. The matrices used below have the direction-free expressions

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.64)")
 $ A_d = I - w ⊗ w \, #h(2em) A_chi = I - frac(p + 4, 4 + p v) w ⊗ w . $ <n3:deformation:axial-matrices>]

Vectors and covectors are identified with $g$.

The unknowns will be $\( f \, Z \)$. For fixed $nabla f$, $partial Z \/ partial t = 1 + p v \/ 4 > 0$, and its defining expression tends to $- oo$ and $+ oo$ at the two ends of the $t$-axis. It therefore defines a unique smooth $t = t \( Z \, nabla f \)$ for all inputs, without a floor assumption. The formulas above are smooth at $nabla f = 0$, and $0 < chi lt.eq d lt.eq 1$ for finite inputs. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.65)")
 $ macron(g) = g + l^2 thin upright(d) f^2 \, #h(2em) hat(g) = e^(4 t) macron(g) \, #h(2em) H^f = l / sqrt(D) "Hess" f \, #h(2em) S = K + H^f . $ <n3:deformation:metrics>]

The original trace equation is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.66)")
 $ "tr"_(A_chi) S = F \, #h(2em) F = h + C \, $ <n3:deformation:trace>]

where $"tr"_A S$ is contraction with the contravariant matrix $A$. Along the continuation path we will specify modified right sides $F$. The full flux is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.67)")
 $ V = u A_chi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 4 nabla Z + K \( w \, dot.op \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:deformation:flux>]

At $S_R$ prescribe $f = Z = 0$. Initially the inner Dirichlet values are $h = b_(-)$ on $B_b$ and $h = b_(+)$ on $B_w$.

===== 1.4.2 The exact scalar-curvature identity <the-exact-scalar-curvature-identity>

The next identity separates the original constraint densities, a nonnegative quadratic term, and a divergence. The second equation will prescribe that divergence so that the deformed metric has nonnegative scalar curvature.

#thm([Proposition 1.20.])[
  (Deformation identity).  #emph[Let $f \, t$ be smooth and $F = "tr"_(A_chi) \( K + H^f \)$. At $sigma > 0$ decompose $S$ relative to $bb(R) e xor e^perp$: 
$ T = S \|_(e^perp times e^perp) \, quad M = S \( e \, dot.op \) \|_(e^perp) \, quad q = S \( e \, e \) \, quad "tr" T = F - chi q . $

 Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.68)")
 $ 1 / 2 e^(4 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) =  & 8 pi \( mu + J \( w \) \) + cal(T) + w \( F \) - F "tr" K - u^(- 1) "div" V \, $ <n3:deformation:scalar-identity>]

 where 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.69)")
 $ cal(T) =  & 1 / 2 \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \( M + p a nabla_perp t \) dot.op \( M + \( p + 2 \) a nabla_perp t \)\
 & + 4 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 + \( chi - chi^2 \/ 4 \) q^2 + 2 \( p + 1 \) chi q a thin partial_e t\
 & - chi F q \/ 2 + 3 F^2 \/ 4 . $ <n3:deformation:quadratic-term>]

 After substituting $F = "tr"_(A_chi) S$, this expression extends smoothly across $sigma = 0$.]
] <n3:deformation:identity>

#proof[
  Introduce an auxiliary coordinate $s_0$ and the stationary Lorentz metric
  $ upright(bold(g)) = - l^2 \( thin upright(d) s_0 - thin upright(d) f \)^2 + macron(g) = - U^2 thin upright(d) s_0^2 + g \( thin upright(d) x + beta thin upright(d) s_0 \, thin upright(d) x + beta thin upright(d) s_0 \) \, #h(2em) beta = l^2 nabla f = U w . $
  Its $s_0$-slices have future normal $N_0 = \( partial_(s_0) - beta \) \/ U$ and second fundamental form
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.70)")
   $ k = - frac("sym" nabla beta, U) = - H^f - p \( thin upright(d) t ⊗ w^flat + w^flat ⊗ thin upright(d) t \) . $ <n3:deformation:auxiliary-k>]
  Write $theta = "tr" k$ and, for symmetric tensors, put
  $ cal(B) \( A \, B \) = chevron.l A \, B chevron.r - \( "tr" A \) \( "tr" B \) \, #h(2em) P_A = A - \( "tr" A \) g \, #h(2em) Q = K - k . $
  The normal expansion equation and contracted Gauss equation are
  $ "Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \( N_0 \, N_0 \) & = - N_0 \( theta \) - \| k \|^2 + U^(- 1) Delta U \,\
"Scal"_g & = "Scal"_(upright(bold(g))) + 2 "Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \( N_0 \, N_0 \) + \| k \|^2 - theta^2 . $
  Consequently $"Scal"_(upright(bold(g))) = "Scal"_g + \| k \|^2 + theta^2 + 2 N_0 \( theta \) - 2 U^(- 1) Delta U$. Stationarity gives $U N_0 \( theta \) = - beta \( theta \)$, and $"div" beta = - U theta$ follows from (1.70). Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.71)")
   $ U "Scal"_(upright(bold(g))) = U #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Scal"_g + cal(B) \( k \, k \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] - 2 "div" \( nabla U + theta beta \) . $ <n3:deformation:stationary-scalar>]
  In the coordinates $s_0 - f$ the same metric is a static warped product. Its mixed Christoffel symbols are $upright(bold(Gamma))_(i s)^s = partial_i log l$ and $upright(bold(Gamma))_(s s)^i = l macron(nabla)^i l$; contraction gives $"Scal"_(upright(bold(g))) = "Scal"_(macron(g)) - 2 l^(- 1) accent(Delta l, ‾)$. Since $macron(g)^(- 1) = A_d$ and $thin upright(d) V_(macron(g)) = \( U \/ l \) thin upright(d) V_g$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.72)")
   $ macron(Delta) phi.alt = l / U "div" (U / l A_d nabla phi.alt) . $ <n3:deformation:bar-laplacian>]
  Direct differentiation of $U$ and (1.70) gives
  $ nabla U - U / l A_d nabla l = - k \( beta \, dot.op \) . $
  In fact both sides equal
  $ l^4 / U "Hess" f \( nabla f \, dot.op \) + l^3 / U #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] \| nabla f \|^2 thin upright(d) l + \( nabla f \) \( l \) thin upright(d) f #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] \, $
  as follows by differentiating $U^2 = l^2 + l^4 \| nabla f \|^2$. Substitution in (1.71) yields
  $ U "Scal"_(macron(g)) = U #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Scal"_g + cal(B) \( k \, k \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + 2 "div" \( P_k beta \) . $
  The constraints read $16 pi mu = "Scal"_g - cal(B) \( K \, K \)$ and $8 pi J = "div" P_K$. Using $P_K : nabla beta = - U cal(B) \( K \, k \)$, expansion therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.73)")
   $ U "Scal"_(macron(g)) = U \[ 16 pi \( mu + J \( w \) \) + cal(B) \( Q \, Q \) \] - 2 "div" \( P_Q beta \) . $ <n3:deformation:unweighted-identity>]
  Indeed the derivative of $P_K$ cancels its term in $"div" \( P_K beta \)$, and $cal(B) \( Q \, Q \) + 2 cal(B) \( K \, k \) = cal(B) \( K \, K \) + cal(B) \( k \, k \)$.
  The three-dimensional conformal formula is
  $ 1 / 2 e^(4 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) = 1 / 2 "Scal"_(macron(g)) - 4 accent(Delta t, ‾) - 4 \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 . $
  For $Y = P_Q w$, replacing $U$ by $u = e^(2 t) U$ gives $- U^(- 1) "div" \( U Y \) = - u^(- 1) "div" \( u Y \) + 2 Y \( t \)$. Since $u \/ \( U \/ l \) = L$, Equation (1.72) similarly gives
  $ - 4 accent(Delta t, ‾) - 4 \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 = - u^(- 1) "div" \( 4 u A_d nabla t \) + 4 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 . $
  Before the flux modification, the scalar-curvature expression is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.74)")
   $ 8 pi \( mu + J \( w \) \) + 1 / 2 cal(B) \( Q \, Q \) + 2 P_Q \( w \, nabla t \) + 4 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2\
- u^(- 1) "div" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] u \( P_Q w + 4 A_d nabla t \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:deformation:pre-flux>]
  At $sigma > 0$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.75)")
   $ 4 thin upright(d) Z = \( 4 + p v \) thin upright(d) t + v thin upright(d) log sigma . $ <n3:deformation:Z-differential>]
  Write $x = partial_e t$ and $y = nabla_perp t$. The blocks of $Q$ are
  $ Q_(perp perp) = T \, #h(2em) Q_(e perp) = M + p a y \, #h(2em) Q_(e e) = q + 2 p a x \,\
"tr" Q = F + \( 1 - chi \) q + 2 p a x . $
  Hence $\( P_Q w \)_perp = a \( M + p a y \)$ and $\( P_Q w \)_e = a \( chi q - F \)$. Together with $a H^f \( e \, dot.op \) = v thin upright(d) log sigma$, Equation (1.75) proves component by component
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.76)")
   $ V \/ u = P_Q w + 4 A_d nabla t + w F . $ <n3:deformation:flux-rewrite>]
  Also $u w = e^(2 t) l^2 nabla f$, so
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.77)")
   $ "div" \( u w \) \/ u = F + \( 1 - chi \) q - "tr" K + 2 \( p + 1 \) a x . $ <n3:deformation:uw-divergence>]
  Replacing the flux in (1.74) adds $w \( F \) + F "div" \( u w \) \/ u$. The remaining quadratic expression is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.78)")
   $ cal(T) =  & 1 / 2 cal(B) \( Q \, Q \) + 2 P_Q \( w \, nabla t \) + 4 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2\
 & + F [F + frac(p + 4, 4 + p v) S \( w \, w \) + 2 \( p + 1 \) w \( t \)] . $ <n3:deformation:quadratic-invariant>]
  Here $\( 1 - chi \) q = \( p + 4 \) S \( w \, w \) \/ \( 4 + p v \)$, which gives a smooth formula at $w = 0$.
  For the explicit coefficients, substitute $\| T \|^2 = \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \( F - chi q \)^2 \/ 2$ in $cal(B) \( Q \, Q \) \/ 2$. The transverse terms become $\( M + p a y \) dot.op \( M + \( p + 2 \) a y \)$. The coefficients of $q^2 \, F q \, F^2$ are respectively $chi - chi^2 \/ 4 \, - chi \/ 2 \, 3 \/ 4$. The $q x$ coefficient is $2 \( p + 1 \) chi a$, the $F x$ terms cancel, and the remaining $x^2$ term is $4 \( p + 1 \) d x^2$. These are the terms in (1.69). ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.21.])[
  (Polynomial coercivity).  #emph[The expression $cal(T)$ is nonnegative. For $0 lt.eq p lt.eq n$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.79)")
 $ \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 + \| T \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + d q^2 + F^2 lt.eq C \( 1 + n \)^N cal(T) $ <n3:deformation:coercive-estimate>]

 with universal $C \, N$. At $thin upright(d) t = 0$ there is the stronger comparison, uniform in $p \, d$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.80)")
 $ c #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + chi q^2 + F^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq cal(T) lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + chi q^2 + F^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:deformation:floor-norm>]

]
] <n3:deformation:coercivity>

#proof[
  With $x = partial_e t$ and $y = nabla_perp t$, exact completion gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.81)")
   $ cal(T) =  & 1 / 2 \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \| M + \( p + 1 \) a y \|^2 + \[ 4 \( p + 1 \) - v \] \| y \|^2\
 & + 4 \( p + 1 \) d (x + frac(chi a q, 4 d))^2\
 & + 3 / 4 (F - frac(chi q, 3))^2 + frac(4 d \( 9 - d \), 3 \( 4 + p v \)^2) q^2 . $ <n3:deformation:square-completion>]
  Completing first in $x$ leaves $3 chi q^2 \/ \( 4 + p v \)$; completing next in $F$ leaves $3 chi \/ \( 4 + p v \) - chi^2 \/ 12 = 4 d \( 9 - d \) \/ \[ 3 \( 4 + p v \)^2 \]$. The latter coefficient is at least $32 d \/ \[ 3 \( 4 + n \)^2 \]$. It controls $d q^2$ polynomially. The $F$-square then controls $F^2$, since $chi^2 q^2 lt.eq d q^2$. The transverse gradient coefficient is at least three, and the shifted $M$-square controls $M$ with an additional factor $\( 1 + n \)^2$. The squared $d$-norm of the shift in the $x$-square is $chi^2 v q^2 \/ \( 16 d \) lt.eq d q^2 \/ 16$, so it also controls $d x^2$. Finally $\| T \|^2 = \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \( F - chi q \)^2 \/ 2$. This proves (1.79). If $thin upright(d) t = 0$, the sole cross term is $- chi F q \/ 2$. Using $\| chi F q \| \/ 2 lt.eq chi q^2 \/ 4 + chi F^2 \/ 4$ and $0 < chi lt.eq 1$ proves (1.80). ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.22.])[
  (Boundary identity).  #emph[On a face where $f$ is constant, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.82)")
 $ V_nu \/ u = d \( H + 4 partial_nu t \) - H + w_nu \( F - P_B \) . $ <n3:deformation:boundary>]

 Its mean curvature in $hat(g)$, with normal into $Omega$, is $e^(- 2 t) sqrt(d) thin \( H + 4 partial_nu t \)$.]
] <n3:deformation:boundary-identity>

#proof[
  The tangential Hessian is $"Hess" f \|_(T B) = \( partial_nu f \) "II"_g$. Thus $"tr"_perp H^f = w_nu H$ and $chi q = F - P_B - w_nu H$. The normal component of (1.67), using (1.75), is
  $ V_nu \/ u = 4 d partial_nu t + chi w_nu q = 4 d partial_nu t + w_nu \( F - P_B \) - v H . $
  The $macron(g)$-normal is $sqrt(d) thin nu$. Its mean curvature is $sqrt(d) thin H$, because the tangential metric is unchanged and its normal first variation is multiplied by $sqrt(d)$. The conformal mean-curvature formula gives the asserted mean curvature for $hat(g)$. ◻
]

===== 1.4.3 The source, boundary condition, and continuation path <the-source-boundary-condition-and-continuation-path>

We now prescribe the divergence and inner flux. For the original system, the source is chosen to give the deformed metric nonnegative scalar curvature. A penalty near the lower bound will exclude contact with that bound along the continuation path.

Choose $3 \/ 4 < gamma < 1$ and fixed positive smooth weights

$ rho_0 asymp r^(- 4) \, #h(2em) rho_1 asymp r^(- 2 gamma) \, $

with differentiated end bounds. Let $m : bb(R) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ be smooth, equal to one on $\( - oo \, 0 \]$ and zero on $\[ 1 \, oo \)$, and set

$ m_0 \( t \) = m \( n \( t + epsilon.alt \) \) \, #h(2em) cal(P) = C_n \( rho_0 + v rho_1 \) . $

The positive $delta_0$ is fixed independently of $n \, R$ in Proposition 28; thereafter $C_n$ is a sufficiently large polynomial constant. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.83)")
 $ Xi = delta_0 cal(T) + rho + delta_0 tau a sigma - m_0 \( t \) cal(P) . $ <n3:deformation:source>]

Choose a smooth face function $N_B > 1 + sup_B \| H \|$. The original second equation and its inner condition are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.84)")
 $ "div" V = u Xi \, #h(2em) V_nu \/ u = - H + w_nu \( F - P_B \) - N_B d . $ <n3:deformation:original-second-equation>]

Lemma 22 gives $H + 4 partial_nu t = - N_B$. Only $- epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq - epsilon.alt + 1 \/ n$ is penalized in the admissible range. Since $n > 2 \/ epsilon.alt$, this band has negative $t$, and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.85)")
 $ ell lt.eq l lt.eq e ell \, #h(2em) c ell lt.eq L lt.eq C ell quad upright("on the admissible penalty band") . $ <n3:deformation:penalty-band>]

The latter constants can be uniform for $0 < epsilon.alt < 1$.

#thm([Proposition 1.23.])[
  (Specified homotopy).  #emph[There is a continuous piecewise smooth family of systems indexed by $s in \[ 0 \, 4 \]$, beginning at (1.66), (1.84) and the original Dirichlet values, and ending at $f = 0$ and the scalar problem with zero source and zero inner conormal flux. Throughout its first three stages the source is (1.83), with $cal(T)$ evaluated using the actual trace $F$. Each trace equation has right-side derivative at least one in $h$, with $x \, Z \, nabla f$ fixed.]
] <n3:deformation:homotopy>

#proof[
  Fix a smooth $j : bb(R) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ which vanishes for $t lt.eq 0$ and equals one for $t gt.eq 1$. For $0 lt.eq lambda lt.eq 1$ put
  $ V_lambda = u A_chi \( 4 nabla Z + lambda K \( w \, dot.op \) \) \, #h(2em) cal(B) = - H + w_nu \( F - P_B \) - N_B d . $
  Before the last stage use the boundary condition
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.86)")
   $ \( V_lambda \)_nu \/ u =  & \[ 1 - \( 1 - lambda \) j \( t \) \]\
 & dot.op \[ cal(B) - \( 1 - lambda \) \( A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \) \)_nu \] . $ <n3:deformation:homotopy-boundary>]
  Outer values remain $f = Z = 0$ throughout.
  We specify two smooth collar modifications. Let $nu_s = nabla s \/ \| nabla s \|$ be the outward unit normal to a collar leaf; the leaf coordinate $s$ here is local and is separate from the homotopy parameter. Choose disjoint collar cutoffs equal to one at the faces. Take $eta > 0$ so small that $b_(-) + 3 eta < b_(+)$. A black contribution to $D_0 \( x \, w \, h \)$ is a negative constant times a collar cutoff, times a smooth directional cutoff supported where $w dot.op nu_s < - 1 \/ 2$ and equal to one at $- 1$, times a nonincreasing height cutoff equal to one below $b_(-) + eta$ and zero above $b_(-) + 2 eta$. A white contribution has positive sign, its directional cutoff supported where $w dot.op nu_s > 1 \/ 2$ and equal to one at $1$, and a nondecreasing height cutoff equal to zero below $b_(+) - 2 eta$ and one above $b_(+) - eta$. Decrease $eta$ to separate these ranges. Their sum $D_0$ is smooth, bounded, supported in the collars, and nondecreasing in $h$. It is nonpositive for $h lt.eq b_(-) + eta$, nonnegative for $h gt.eq b_(+) - eta$, and vanishes on black outward and white inward directions, for every length $0 lt.eq \| w \| lt.eq 1$.
  Choose the magnitudes larger than $2 sup_B \| H \| + 1$. Equation (1.60) then gives, for $F = h + C + D_0$ at the assigned boundary height $b = b_plus.minus$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.87)")
   $ F \( x \, + nu \, b \) gt.eq P_B + H \, #h(2em) F \( x \, - nu \, b \) lt.eq P_B - H . $ <n3:deformation:directional-barriers>]
  These are limiting directional evaluations at $a = 1 \, chi = 0$. The compatible direction has equality; $D_0$ corrects the other one. Its derivatives on bounded height ranges are independent of $n$. Choose a smooth compactly supported extension $b \( x \)$, constant equal to $b_plus.minus$ on each smaller collar. On these collars let $D_1 \( x \, w \) = A_1 w dot.op nu_s$, extended by a collar cutoff, with $A_1 > sup_B \| H \| + 1$. Then $D_1 \( x \, 0 \) = 0$ and $D_1 \( x \, plus.minus nu \) = plus.minus A_1$ on $B$.
  The four stages are the following.
  + For $0 lt.eq s lt.eq 1$, set $lambda = 1 - s$, keep $F = h + C$ and $h \|_B = b$, and use $"div" V_lambda = u Xi$ and (1.86).
  + For $1 lt.eq s lt.eq 2$, set $lambda = 0$, $alpha = s - 1$, retain $h \|_B = b$, and replace the trace right side by $F = h + C + alpha D_0 \( x \, w \, h \)$. Keep the same scalar equation and boundary prescription.
  + For $2 lt.eq s lt.eq 3$, set $lambda = 0$, $zeta = s - 2$, prescribe $h \|_B = \( 1 - zeta \) b$, and use
    #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.88)")
     $ F =  & h + \( 1 - zeta \) \[ C + D_0 \( x \, w \, h + zeta b \( x \) \) \]\
 & + zeta \[ "tr"_(A_chi) K + D_1 \( x \, w \) \] . $ <n3:deformation:interpolation-trace>]
    Keep the same scalar equation and boundary prescription. At a face, $h + zeta b = b$. The value at $w = plus.minus nu$ is the convex combination of the preceding directional value and $P_B + D_1 \( x \, plus.minus nu \)$, so (1.87) persists.
  + For $3 lt.eq s lt.eq 4$, retain the trace problem at $zeta = 1$ and multiply both $Xi$ and the right side of (1.86) by $eta_s = 4 - s$.
  The prescriptions agree at the common endpoints. Their $h$-derivatives are $1$, $1 + alpha partial_h D_0$, or $1 + \( 1 - zeta \) partial_h D_0$, all at least one.
  At $zeta = 1$ the trace equation is $"tr"_(A_chi) H^f = h + D_1 \( x \, w \)$ with zero Dirichlet values. At a positive interior maximum of $f$, one has $w = 0$, $A_chi = I$, $D_1 = 0$, and $l Delta f = tau f > 0$, a contradiction. A negative minimum is excluded in the same way. Thus $f = 0$ and $t = Z$ in the last stage. At $s = 4$ the remaining equation is $"div" \( 4 L \( Z \) nabla Z \) = 0$, with zero inner conormal flux and $Z = 0$ on $S_R$. Multiplication by $Z$ and integration give $Z = 0$. For the fixed-point construction in Section 6, the last map therefore has output identically zero. ◻
]

===== 1.4.4 Elementary height and end barriers <elementary-height-and-end-barriers>

#thm([Lemma 1.24.])[
  (Height control).  #emph[Every smooth trace solution in Proposition 23 satisfies $\| h \| lt.eq C_0$, independently of $n \, R$, the homotopy parameter, and the input $Z$. Its actual right side satisfies $\| F \| lt.eq C_F$ with the same independence.]
] <n3:deformation:height>

#proof[
  The collar modifications vanish at $w = 0$. At an interior extremum the trace equation reads either $"tr" K + l Delta f = h + C$ or $"tr" K + l Delta f = h + \( 1 - zeta \) C + zeta "tr" K$. The maximum and minimum inequalities bound $h$ by fixed bounds for $\| "tr" K \| + \| C \|$. Boundary values range between $b_(-) \, 0 \, b_(+)$. This proves the height bound without using the floor. Boundedness of $D_0 \, D_1$ and $\| "tr"_(A_chi) K \| lt.eq 3 \| K \|$ proves the assertion for $F$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.25.])[
  (End height and outer boundary).  #emph[There are $r_0 \, C > 0$, independent of $n \, R$ and the homotopy parameter, such that for $R gt.eq 2 r_0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.89)")
 $ \| h \| lt.eq C \( r^(- gamma) - R^(- gamma) \) quad \( r_0 lt.eq r lt.eq R \) . $ <n3:deformation:end-height-estimate>]

 In particular, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.90)")
 $ \| nabla f \| lt.eq C R^(- 1 - gamma) \/ tau quad upright("on ") S_R . $ <n3:deformation:outer-gradient>]

 For fixed $epsilon.alt \, n$, a sufficiently large lower bound for $R$ gives $t > - epsilon.alt$ on $S_R$.]
] <n3:deformation:end-height>

#proof[
  Take $r_0$ outside the supports of $K \, C \, D_0 \, D_1$. There $F = h$ in the first three stages, and $h = 0$ in the last one. For $psi = r^(- gamma)$, with axis $nabla psi \/ \| nabla psi \|$, the controlled end gives, uniformly for $0 < chi lt.eq 1$,
  $ "tr"_(A_chi) "Hess" psi = gamma \( \( gamma + 1 \) chi - 2 \) r^(- gamma - 2) + O \( r^(- gamma - 3) \) < 0 $
  after enlarging $r_0$. At a comparison contact, coefficients use the given $Z$ and the common gradient of test and solution. The positive factor $l \/ \( tau sqrt(D) \)$ preserves this sign. Height monotonicity therefore makes $C \( psi - R^(- gamma) \)$ an upper barrier and its negative a lower barrier. Choose $C$ to dominate $C_0$ on $r = r_0$ for every $R gt.eq 2 r_0$. Differentiating at the common outer value gives (1.90); tangential derivatives there vanish.
  At $S_R$, $Z = 0$. The monotonicity of its defining expression implies $t > - epsilon.alt$ if $ell^2 \| nabla f \|^2 < e^(8 epsilon.alt) - 1$. It suffices to require $C^2 ell^2 tau^(- 2) R^(- 2 - 2 gamma) < e^(8 epsilon.alt) - 1$, which is a permissible lower bound depending on fixed $n \, epsilon.alt$. ◻
]

===== 1.4.5 Exclusion of a contact with the floor <exclusion-of-a-contact-with-the-floor>

We exclude a proposed floor contact using the equations at that point. These estimates require neither an upper bound for $Z$ nor a bound for $\| nabla f \|$.

#thm([Lemma 1.26.])[
  (Curvature at a minimum of $t$).  #emph[At an interior point where $thin upright(d) t = 0$ and $"Hess" t gt.eq 0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.91)")
 $ 1 / 2 e^(4 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) lt.eq 1 / 2 "Scal"_g - v "Ric"_g \( e \, e \) + cal(S) \( H^f \) \, $ <n3:deformation:floor-gauss>]

 where, for a symmetric tensor $A$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.92)")
 $ cal(S) \( A \) =  & 1 / 4 \( "tr"_perp A \)^2 - 1 / 2 \| A_(perp perp)^("tf") \|^2\
 & + d A_(e e) "tr"_perp A - d \| A_(e perp) \|^2 . $ <n3:deformation:gauss-quadratic>]

 There are universal $c_(*) \, eta_(*) > 0$ and polynomial constants $C_n$ such that, at $thin upright(d) t = 0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.93)")
 $ cal(T) - cal(S) \( H^f \) gt.eq c_(*) cal(T) - C_n \( F^2 + \| K \|^2 \) . $ <n3:deformation:floor-algebra-general>]

 If $K = 0$, the more useful uniform estimate is 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.94)")
 $ cal(T) - cal(S) \( H^f \) gt.eq c_(*) cal(T) - C_n v F^2 . $ <n3:deformation:floor-algebra-end>]

]
] <n3:deformation:floor-curvature>

#proof[
  Consider the graph of $f$ in the Riemannian product with warped fiber $\( Omega times bb(R) \, g + l^2 thin upright(d) b_0^2 \)$. Its induced metric is $macron(g)$. At $thin upright(d) t = 0$ one also has $thin upright(d) l = 0$, and its second fundamental form, up to orientation, is $H^f$. Write $E = l^(- 1) partial_(b_0)$. The graph unit normal is $sqrt(d) thin E - a e$. The ambient curvature formulas at this point are
  $ "Scal"_(upright("amb")) = "Scal"_g - 2 Delta l \/ l \,\
"Ric"_(upright("amb")) \|_(T Omega) = "Ric"_g - "Hess" l \/ l \, #h(2em) "Ric"_(upright("amb")) \( E \, E \) = - Delta l \/ l \, $
  with zero mixed Ricci terms. Hence the ambient contribution to half the graph scalar curvature is
  $ 1 / 2 "Scal"_g - v "Ric"_g \( e \, e \) - v thin "tr"_perp "Hess" l \/ l . $
  The last term is nonpositive: at $thin upright(d) t = 0$, $"Hess" l = l p thin "Hess" t$ and $p gt.eq 0$. The second-form contribution is $\[ \( "tr"_(macron(g)) H^f \)^2 - \| H^f \|_(macron(g))^2 \] \/ 2$. Since the inverse graph metric is $A_d$, its expansion is exactly (1.92). Finally the conformal contribution at this point is $- 4 accent(Delta t, ‾) lt.eq 0$. This proves (1.91).
  For the algebraic estimates we must retain the degenerate normal coefficient. First evaluate $cal(S)$ on $S = K + H^f$, whose trace in the transverse plane is $F - chi q$. Subtracting (1.92) from (1.69) at $thin upright(d) t = 0$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.95)")
   $ cal(T) - cal(S) \( S \) =  & \| T^("tf") \|^2 + \( 1 + d \) \| M \|^2\
 & + chi \( 1 + d - chi \/ 2 \) q^2 - d F q + F^2 \/ 2 . $ <n3:deformation:floor-difference>]
  The normal coefficient is at least $chi$, while $\| d F q \| lt.eq chi q^2 \/ 2 + d^2 F^2 \/ \( 2 chi \)$. Because
  $ d^2 \/ chi = d \( 4 + p v \) \/ 4 lt.eq 1 + n \/ 4 \, $
  Equation (1.80) implies $cal(T) - cal(S) \( S \) gt.eq c cal(T) - C \( 1 + n \) F^2$ with universal $c > 0$.
  To replace $S$ by $H^f = S - K$, polarize (1.92). The difference is bounded by
  $ C \| K \| \( \| T^("tf") \| + \| M \| + \| F \| + d \| q \| \) + C \| K \|^2 . $
  Indeed the only extra normal product is $d q thin "tr"_perp K$; all remaining products involve transverse entries, $d M$, or $"tr"_perp S = F - chi q$. By (1.80) and $d \/ sqrt(chi) lt.eq C sqrt(1 + n)$, this bound is at most $C sqrt(1 + n) \| K \| sqrt(cal(T)) + C \| K \|^2$. Young’s inequality absorbs a fixed small fraction of $cal(T)$ and costs only $C \( 1 + n \) \| K \|^2$. This proves (1.93).
  If $\( 1 + p \) v lt.eq eta_(*)$, then $d$ and $chi$ are within $C eta_(*)$ of one. At $d = chi = 1$ the normal block in (1.95) is $3 q^2 \/ 2 - F q + F^2 \/ 2$; its symmetric matrix has smallest eigenvalue $\( 2 - sqrt(2) \) \/ 2 > 0$. Choose $eta_(*) > 0$ small enough that its coefficients remain within half this spectral gap. The transverse coefficients are already bounded below. For $K = 0$, Equation (1.80) therefore gives $cal(T) - cal(S) \( H^f \) gt.eq c_(*) cal(T)$ in this regime. In the remaining regime $v > eta_(*) \/ \( 1 + p \)$, use (1.93) with $K = 0$ and absorb $\( 1 + n \) F^2$ into $C \( 1 + n \)^2 v F^2$. This proves (1.94). All formulas extend to $sigma = 0$ by their invariant expressions; there $d = chi = 1$ and the full quadratic expressions are independent of the temporary choice of axis. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.27.])[
  (Homotopy errors at a floor contact).  #emph[There is a fixed compact set $cal(K) subset overline(Omega)$ containing the supports of $K \, C \, b \, D_0 \, D_1$ such that, at an interior minimum $t = - epsilon.alt$ of a smooth solution in the first three stages, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.96)")
 $ "div" V_lambda \/ u gt.eq 2 delta_0 cal(T) + tau a sigma - C \( 1 + n \)^N \[ upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) + v \( rho_0 + rho_1 \) \] . $ <n3:deformation:floor-divergence-lower>]

 Here $delta_0 > 0$ can be fixed sufficiently small independently of $n \, R$ and the homotopy parameter.]
] <n3:deformation:floor-errors>

#proof[
  At this contact $thin upright(d) t = 0$ and $p = n$ is locally constant as a function of $t$ near its value $- epsilon.alt$. Differentiating $w$ in an orthonormal frame gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.97)")
   $ nabla_i w_j = H_(i j)^f - w_j H_(i k)^f w^k . $ <n3:deformation:floor-w-derivative>]
  In the $e$-frame the normal vector slot is multiplied by $d$. Equations (1.80) and $d \/ sqrt(chi) lt.eq C sqrt(1 + n)$ therefore show
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.98)")
   $ \| nabla w \| lt.eq C \( 1 + n \)^N \( \| K \| + sqrt(cal(T)) \) . $ <n3:deformation:floor-w-control>]
  No bound for the uncontracted normal Hessian is asserted.
  Each first-three-stage right side can be written as a smooth function $F \( x \, w \, h \, p \)$. Its $h$-derivative is at least one. On the bounded height range supplied by Lemma 24, its explicit $x$- and $w$-derivatives are polynomially bounded and compactly supported, apart from the derivative of the term $h$. This follows directly from the fixed collar cutoffs and $A_chi = I - \( p + 4 \) w ⊗ w \/ \( 4 + p v \)$, whose denominator is at least four. For example its first $w$-derivatives on $\| w \| lt.eq 1$ are bounded by $C \( 1 + n \)^2$, and the other explicit modifications have bounds independent of $n$. Combining these bounds with (1.98) and Young’s inequality therefore produces a fixed finite power of $1 + n$. Since $nabla p = 0$ at the contact and $w \( h \) = tau a sigma$, the chain rule and (1.98) give, for every fixed $eta > 0$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.99)")
   $ w \( F \) gt.eq tau a sigma - eta cal(T) - C_eta \( 1 + n \)^N upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) . $ <n3:deformation:floor-F-control>]
  The dependence $D_0 \( x \, w \, h + zeta b \( x \) \)$ only adds the fixed compact derivative of $b$ and does not change this conclusion.
  The omitted flux is $\( 1 - lambda \) u A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \)$. Let $B_K = A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \)$. Its derivative is bounded by a polynomial constant times $1 + sqrt(cal(T))$ on $cal(K)$, by (1.98); derivatives of $p$ vanish. The weight term requires the contraction rather than a bound for $\| nabla log u \|$. At the contact,
  $ nabla log u = H^f \( w \, dot.op \) \, #h(2em) chevron.l nabla log u \, B_K chevron.r = a^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] chi H_(e e)^f K_(e e) + H_(e perp)^f dot.op K_(e perp) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  This involves only $chi H_(e e)^f$ and $H_(e perp)^f$ and is controlled by (1.80). It follows that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.100)")
   $ \| "div" \( u B_K \) \| \/ u lt.eq eta cal(T) + C_eta \( 1 + n \)^N upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) . $ <n3:deformation:floor-flux-control>]
  Combine the scalar identity with (1.91). The difference $8 pi mu - "Scal"_g \/ 2$ equals $\( \( "tr" K \)^2 - \| K \|^2 \) \/ 2$ and is compactly supported; so is $J$. The term $F "tr" K$ is compactly bounded by Lemma 24. On $cal(K)$, Equation (1.93) therefore contributes $c_(*) cal(T) - C \( 1 + n \)^N$. Outside $cal(K)$, $K = 0$, $F = h$, and (1.94) together with Lemma 25 gives $c_(*) cal(T) - C \( 1 + n \)^N v r^(- 2 gamma)$. The remaining Ricci term is bounded below by $- C upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) - C v r^(- 3)$; enlarge $cal(K)$ if necessary. Both end weights are bounded by a multiple of $rho_0 + rho_1$. Finally subtract the omitted flux and use (1.99)–(1.100), choosing their two $eta$’s with sum at most $c_(*) \/ 2$. Fix $0 < delta_0 < min \( c_(*) \/ 4 \, 1 \/ 4 \)$. Decreasing it slightly if necessary gives (1.96). ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 1.28.])[
  (Floor exclusion).  #emph[Choose $delta_0$ as in Lemma 27. There is a polynomial choice $C_n = C \( 1 + n \)^N$ in the penalty such that, for all sufficiently large $n$ and all $R gt.eq R_(upright("floor")) \( n \, epsilon.alt \)$, no smooth solution anywhere along Proposition 23 can satisfy $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$ and attain $t = - epsilon.alt$. In particular every such admissible solution has $t > - epsilon.alt$ on $overline(Omega_R)$.]
] <n3:deformation:floor>

#proof[
  Choose $R_(upright("floor"))$ to include the requirement of Lemma 25. A floor contact cannot occur on $S_R$. In the first three stages, an inner contact has $j \( t \) = 0$. Equation (1.86) then cancels the omitted flux on both sides and gives $V_nu \/ u = cal(B)$. Equation (1.82) forces $H + 4 partial_nu t = - N_B$, so $partial_nu t < 0$. This contradicts the nonnegative derivative into $Omega_R$ at a boundary minimum.
  Consider an interior contact. Let $E_n = C \( 1 + n \)^N \[ upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) + v \( rho_0 + rho_1 \) \]$ denote the error in (1.96). Because $rho_0$ has a positive minimum on $cal(K)$, $upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) lt.eq C_(cal(K)) rho_0$. Also $v rho_0 lt.eq rho_0$, and $rho \/ rho_0$ is globally bounded. We can therefore choose a polynomial $C_n$ so large that, pointwise for all $0 lt.eq v lt.eq 1$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.101)")
   $ cal(P) gt.eq E_n + 2 rho + rho_0 . $ <n3:deformation:penalty-choice>]
  At the floor $m_0 = 1$, so the scalar equation and the lower estimate give
  $ 0 & gt.eq delta_0 cal(T) + \( 1 - delta_0 \) tau a sigma + cal(P) - E_n - rho\
 & gt.eq rho + rho_0 > 0 \, $
  a contradiction. Enlarging $C_n$ below preserves this inequality and preserves its polynomial dependence.
  In the last stage $f = 0$, $t = Z$, $u = L$, and $v = 0$. At an interior minimum, $cal(T) = \( \| K \|^2 + \( "tr" K \)^2 \) \/ 2$. Increase $C_n$ polynomially if necessary so that $delta_0 cal(T) + rho - C_n rho_0 < 0$ at all such points. For $eta_s > 0$, the scalar equation at a floor minimum has left side $4 L Delta t gt.eq 0$ and strictly negative right side. At an inner floor minimum its flux is $4 partial_nu t = eta_s \( - H - N_B \) < 0$, also impossible. If $eta_s = 0$, multiplication of $"div" \( 4 L \( t \) nabla t \) = 0$ by $t$, with zero outer value and zero inner flux, gives $t = 0$. Thus the last stage has no floor contact either. ◻
]

We have excluded floor contact for every smooth admissible solution along the specified homotopy. Section 5 supplies the remaining a priori bounds, and Section 6 proves existence. The choice of $cal(P)$ uses only polynomial losses in $n$; it is independent of all fixed-$n$ Schauder constants.

==== 1.5 A priori estimates for the deformation <n3:apriori:section>

We bound the height, boundary slopes, and conformal variable before using classical regularity. Fix the prepared exterior of Proposition 19, its thresholds and collars, and $epsilon.alt > 0$. We use the variables and four stages of Proposition 23. A solution is smooth on a finite truncation and satisfies $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$, so the estimates include putative floor-contact solutions. No Hessian bound or upper bound for $Z$ is assumed.

A quantity denoted by $Pi_n$ is bounded by $C \( 1 + n \)^N$, with $C \, N$ independent of the truncation, the homotopy parameter, and $n$. It can increase at successive uses. The fixed data, collars, and $epsilon.alt$ can enter $C \, N$. A constant denoted by $C \( n \)$ can depend arbitrarily on these fixed data and on $n \, ell \, tau$; it is still independent of the truncation and the homotopy parameter. Constants explicitly described as fixed do not depend on $n$. Recall that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.102)")
 $ ell = e^(- epsilon.alt n) \, #h(2em) tau = ell^(3 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) ell lt.eq l lt.eq e \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq p lt.eq n \, #h(2em) frac(4 d, 4 + n) lt.eq chi lt.eq d . $ <n3:apriori:parameter-comparisons>]

In particular $Pi_n tau \/ ell arrow.r 0$. This small factor will absorb only terms whose constants are polynomial.

===== 1.5.1 Sublevels and exclusion of boundary layers <sublevels-and-exclusion-of-boundary-layers>

For notation in this section, let $cal(B)_c$ be the full closed maximal black region at threshold $c$, and let $cal(W)_c$ be the corresponding white region with $cal(B)_(c_b)$ held fixed. These are the families used in Proposition 19.

#thm([Lemma 1.29.])[
  (Uniform separation from the wrong height).  #emph[In the first two homotopy stages, the following holds. If $Q subset macron(Omega)$ is compact and disjoint from $cal(B)_(c_b)$, there are $eta_Q > 0$ and $n_Q$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.103)")
 $ h gt.eq b_(-) + eta_Q quad upright("on ") Q $ <n3:apriori:lower-height>]

 for every $n gt.eq n_Q$, every sufficiently large allowed truncation, and every admissible or floor-contact solution in those stages. If $Q$ is disjoint from $cal(W)_(c_w)$, the analogous conclusion is $h lt.eq b_(+) - eta_Q$. The compact sets are relative to the full closure: they may contain faces of the opposite color.]
] <n3:apriori:sublevels>

#proof[
  We prove the lower assertion. It is enough to consider an arbitrary sequence $n_i arrow.r oo$, $R_i arrow.r oo$ and solutions in either of the first two stages. The elementary height and end estimates in Lemmas 24 and 25 give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.104)")
   $ \| h_i \| lt.eq C_0 \, #h(2em) \| h_i \| lt.eq C \( r^(- gamma) - R_i^(- gamma) \) quad upright("on the end") \, #h(2em) 3 \/ 4 < gamma < 1 . $ <n3:apriori:elementary-height>]
  Near each white face extend $h_i$ to the other side by the constant $b_(+)$. Extend the fixed geometric coefficients smoothly there. The extended functions are continuous. Define the lower relaxed limit
  $ underline(h) \( x \) = lim_(j arrow.r oo) inf { h_i \( y \) : i gt.eq j \, med "dist" \( x \, y \) < 1 \/ j } . $
  It is lower semicontinuous, bounded, and satisfies the limiting end bound. No equicontinuity of $h_i$ is asserted.
  Choose $b_(-) < k' < min { 0 \, b_(-) + eta }$, where $eta$ is the low-height sign range of the added term $D_0$. Suppose that a smooth function $phi.alt$ touches $underline(h)$ from below at $x$, with $underline(h) \( x \) lt.eq k'$ and $d phi.alt \( x \) eq.not 0$. Exclude for the moment the black faces. Subtracting a small fourth-order localization term from $phi.alt$ makes the contact strict without changing its two-jet. Minimization on a small closed ball then produces points $x_i arrow.r x$ and constants $c_i arrow.r 0$ such that $phi.alt + c_i$ touches $h_i$ from below at $x_i$, and $h_i \( x_i \) arrow.r underline(h) \( x \)$. The boundary of this ball cannot contain $x_i$ by strictness. Nor can $x_i$ lie on a white face or on the extended side: their values there are $b_(+) > k'$. Thus every sufficiently large $i$ uses the interior trace equation.
  At these contacts,
  $ nabla f_i = tau_i^(- 1) nabla phi.alt \, #h(2em) d_i lt.eq frac(tau_i^2, ell_i^2 \| d phi.alt \( x_i \) \|^2) arrow.r 0 \, #h(2em) a_i arrow.r 1 \, #h(2em) chi_i arrow.r 0 . $
  Here $a_i = l_i \| nabla f_i \| \/ sqrt(D_i)$ is the deformation variable. Positivity of $A_(chi_i)$ and the Hessian inequality at a lower contact imply
  $ "tr"_(A_(chi_i)) K + frac(l_i, tau_i sqrt(D_i)) "tr"_(A_(chi_i)) "Hess" phi.alt lt.eq F_i \( x_i \) . $
  In the second stage $D_0 lt.eq 0$ at these values; in the first stage it is absent. Consequently $limsup F_i \( x_i \) lt.eq k' + C_b$. Passing to the limit gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.105)")
   $ frac("tr"_(\( d phi.alt \)^perp) "Hess" phi.alt, \| d phi.alt \|) + "tr"_(\( d phi.alt \)^perp) K lt.eq k' + C_b . $ <n3:apriori:relaxed-test>]
  This is the expansion of the level surface of $phi.alt$, oriented toward increasing $phi.alt$. The only Hessian used in this passage is the fixed test Hessian. Removing the localization proves the assertion for non-strict contacts as well.
  We now transfer the test inequality to a closed sublevel set. Fix $b_(-) < k < k'$ and put $E_k = { underline(h) lt.eq k }$. Set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.106)")
   $ psi_j \( s \) = frac(1, 1 + exp \[ - j^2 \( s - k - j^(- 1) \) \]) \, #h(2em) v_j = psi_j \( underline(h) \) . $ <n3:apriori:sigmoid>]
  The lower relaxed limit of $v_j$ is the function $v$ which equals zero on $E_k$ and one on its complement. Indeed, at a point of $E_k$ the fixed-point values $psi_j \( underline(h) \( x \) \)$ tend to zero. If $underline(h) \( x \) > k$, lower semicontinuity supplies a neighborhood where $underline(h) > k + delta$ for some $delta > 0$, and $v_j$ tends uniformly to one there.
  Let $phi$ be a nonzero-gradient lower test of $v$ at a zero value. The same strict-contact localization gives lower tests $phi + c_j$ of $v_j$ at $y_j arrow.r x$, with $v_j \( y_j \) arrow.r 0$. Since $psi_j \( k' \) arrow.r 1$, one has $underline(h) \( y_j \) < k'$ eventually. Locally about $y_j$, the test value is in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$, so $psi_j^(- 1) \( phi + c_j \)$ is a smooth lower test of $underline(h)$. An increasing change of defining function does not change its oriented level expansion: if $theta' > 0$, then
  $ "Hess" \( theta compose phi \) = theta' "Hess" phi + theta'' d phi ⊗ d phi \, #h(2em) frac("tr"_(\( d phi \)^perp) "Hess" \( theta compose phi \), \| d \( theta compose phi \) \|) = frac("tr"_(\( d phi \)^perp) "Hess" phi, \| d phi \|) . $
  Equation (1.105) therefore holds for the test $phi + c_j$ and passes to $phi$. A smooth exterior support of $E_k$, with defining function negative on the containing inner side, is a lower test of $v$: shrink its neighborhood so that its positive values are less than one. Thus every such support has expansion at most $k' + C_b$.
  This argument also rules out a hidden layer at a white face. The reversed white face is an exterior support of $E_k ∩ macron(Omega)$ at any contact there, with expansion
  $ - H_B + P_B = - c_w > 0 . $
  For $k'$ sufficiently close to $b_(-)$, this is strictly greater than $k' + C_b$. The high extension ensured that all preceding low-value tests came from the interior equation, so the support contradiction applies at the face itself. Hence $E_k$ avoids every white face. The end estimate makes $E_k$ compact.
  Adjoin the entire closed region $cal(B)_(c_b)$ and fill bounded complementary pockets. At a point on its smooth black frontier, every exterior support of the union also contains the black region locally. The tangency comparison therefore bounds its expansion by $c_b < k' + C_b$. At all other frontier points the preceding sublevel argument applies. Filling pockets only removes frontier pieces and preserves this property. The resulting compact closed filled set contains the required original inner barrier with a collar, because the black region already does. It avoids the outer barriers. Lemma 18 places it in a smooth black trapped region at any threshold $c'' > k' + C_b$ sufficiently close to $c_b$. It is consequently contained in $cal(B)_(c'')$.
  Finally let $Q$ be as in the lemma. Hausdorff right continuity at $c_b$ gives a $delta > 0$ such that $cal(B)_(c_b + delta) ∩ Q = diameter$; decrease $delta$ so that the enlarged threshold is still negative and all barriers remain strict. Choose $k \, k' \, c''$ with
  $ b_(-) < k < k' \, #h(2em) k' + C_b < c'' < c_b + delta . $
  The construction implies $E_k ∩ Q = diameter$ for every sequence under consideration. If the uniform estimate $h > k$ on $Q$ failed, choose violating solutions with $n_i arrow.r oo$, $R_i arrow.r oo$ and $x_i in Q$, $h_i \( x_i \) lt.eq k$. A subsequence has $x_i arrow.r x in Q$ and $underline(h) \( x \) lt.eq k$, a contradiction. This gives (1.103), after decreasing its positive margin.
  For the upper assertion apply the entire argument to $\( - h \, - K \, - C \)$, interchanging colors. The reversed black faces have strictly positive white expansion $- c_b$. Their exclusion and compactness give positive distance from the black region before applying the white version of Lemma 18. Adjoining the full white region is therefore legitimate in the fixed black complement. Empty white regions and missing face types cause no change: only an actually nonempty sublevel would supply a trapped seed and hence a contradiction. ◻
]

For the truncation quantifier, start with any $R_min \( n \) arrow.r oo$ that satisfies the outer estimates of Section 4, and enlarge it when necessary. Failure of any asserted estimate for arbitrarily large $n$ and $R gt.eq R_min \( n \)$ supplies exactly the sequence excluded above. Only finitely many compact sets will be needed below, so their thresholds can be chosen simultaneously.

===== 1.5.2 Signed collar barriers <signed-collar-barriers>

#thm([Lemma 1.30.])[
  (Boundary slopes).  #emph[There are fixed constants $c \, C_(*) > 0$ such that, for sufficiently large $n$ and the allowed truncations, the first two stages satisfy 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.107)")
 $ c \/ tau lt.eq partial_nu f lt.eq C_(*) \/ tau & upright("on black faces") \,\
c \/ tau lt.eq - partial_nu f lt.eq C_(*) \/ tau & upright("on white faces") . $ <n3:apriori:signed-slopes>]

 During the third stage one still has $\| partial_nu f \| lt.eq C_(*) \/ tau$. On every fixed compact region needed for the final scalar-curvature estimate, the first two stages satisfy $b_(-) lt.eq h lt.eq b_(+)$.]
] <n3:apriori:collars>

#proof[
  Write $s$ for the smooth leaf parameter in an outward collar and $N = nabla s \/ \| d s \|$. All geometric coefficients of this fixed collar are bounded, with $\| d s \|$ bounded above and below. At a comparison contact with $h_0 = b_(-) + psi \( s \)$, $psi' > 0$, the shared gradient gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.108)")
   $ d lt.eq C frac(tau^2, ell^2 \( psi' \)^2) \, #h(2em) 1 - a lt.eq d \, #h(2em) n chi gt.eq c_1 d quad \( n gt.eq 4 \) . $ <n3:apriori:contact-degeneracy>]
  If the slopes are in a fixed positive interval, the first bound is $d lt.eq C ell$. None of these comparisons uses an upper bound for $Z$.
  The trace operator on this test is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.109)")
   $ & "tr"_(A_chi) (K + frac(l, tau sqrt(D)) "Hess" h_0)\
 & quad = P_s + a H_s + chi K \( N \, N \) + a chi (frac("Hess" s \( N \, N \), \| d s \|) + \| d s \| psi'' / psi') \, $ <n3:apriori:collar-trace>]
  where $P_s$ is the tangential trace of $K$ on the leaf. Thus it is the leaf expansion $H_s + P_s$, with an error of absolute value at most $C d$, plus the displayed logarithmic-slope term. The same formula for a negative slope has $a$ replaced by $- a$ in the Hessian terms. This is an evaluation at the contact variables; it does not differentiate the unknown $t$.
  On a black collar $H_s + P_s gt.eq c_b$ and $C = C_b - k_B s$. Choose small positive slopes so that $psi \( 0 \) = 0$ and $psi \( s \) lt.eq k_B s \/ 2$. At the outer end of a sufficiently short fixed collar, Lemma 29 lets this test lie below the solution. In the compatible direction the add-on $D_0$ vanishes. The residual of the trace equation on the test is therefore at least
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.110)")
   $ k_B s \/ 2 - C d + a chi \| d s \| psi'' / psi' . $ <n3:apriori:lower-collar-residual>]
  Choose a smooth nonnegative $beta_n \( s \)$ equal to $A n$ for $0 lt.eq s lt.eq 1 \/ n$, supported in $0 lt.eq s lt.eq 2 \/ n$, and satisfying $integral beta_n lt.eq 2 A$. Define
  $ psi' \( s \) = m exp (integral_0^s beta_n \( q \) thin upright(d) q) . $
  Choose the fixed $A$ large enough that the final term of (1.110) dominates $C d$ on $s lt.eq 1 \/ n$, using (1.108) and $a gt.eq 1 \/ 2$ there. On $s gt.eq 1 \/ n$, the first term dominates $C d lt.eq C ell$ for large $n$; the taper contributes with the good sign. Since the slope multiplier is at most $e^(2 A)$, a fixed sufficiently small $m > 0$ enforces all the previous small-slope requirements. This constructs a strict lower barrier with slopes bounded above and below independently of $n$.
  An upper barrier from $b_(-)$ is constructed in the same collar with
  $ psi' \( s \) = M exp (- integral_0^s beta_n \( q \) thin upright(d) q) . $
  The smooth leaf expansion differs from its boundary value by $O \( s \)$. Choose the fixed $M$ so large that $psi \( s \) gt.eq C_2 s$ dominates this variation, the offset variation, and the height bound at the outer collar end. The logarithmic-slope term is now negative. On $s lt.eq 1 \/ n$ it dominates $C d$; on the rest of the collar the negative margin proportional to $s$ does so. The upper barrier is strict at every possible positive contact. Its slopes lie in $\[ M e^(- 2 A) \, M \]$, an interval independent of $n$.
  To check the comparison signs, at a negative interior minimum of $h - h_0$ the Hessian of $h$ dominates that of $h_0$, the gradients and $Z$ agree, and hence so do $t \, l \, A_chi$. The right side is strictly increasing in the height. Thus a strict positive residual for a lower barrier contradicts the equation. At a positive maximum the inequalities reverse, proving the upper-barrier assertion. Taking the one-sided derivative at $s = 0$ proves the black part of (1.107). Replacing $\( h \, K \, C \)$ by $\( - h \, - K \, - C \)$ proves the white part.
  In the third stage the boundary height is the assigned interpolated constant, say $b_zeta$. Construct tests $b_zeta + psi \( s \)$ and $b_zeta - psi \( s \)$ with large positive $psi'$ and $\( log psi' \)' = - beta_n$. At $s = 0$, the two directional inequalities of Proposition 23 say precisely that the positive-slope test has nonpositive limiting residual and the negative-slope test nonnegative limiting residual. At every comparison contact, the fixed positive barrier slope and Equation (1.108) give $d lt.eq C ell$, so $a gt.eq 1 \/ 2$ for large $n$. On $1 \/ 2 lt.eq \| w \| lt.eq 1$, the first $w$-derivatives of $A_chi$ in Equation (1.64) are bounded independently of $0 lt.eq p lt.eq n$. The other collar terms have fixed smooth coefficients on the bounded height range. Thus, at fixed boundary height the coefficient and geometric errors are $O \( s + d + chi \)$, since $\| w - \( plus.minus N \) \| = 1 - a lt.eq d$ and the radial comparison segment stays in this range. Changing the test height supplies, by height monotonicity, a residual of magnitude at least $psi \( s \)$ with the required sign. Large fixed slopes dominate the $O \( s \)$ error and the outer-end height bound. The logarithmic-slope term has the required sign for both tests, as follows from (1.109); it dominates the $O \( d \)$ error on the initial $1 \/ n$ interval. The remaining margin dominates it beyond that interval. This proves the two-sided upper derivative bound, without using sublevel exclusion in the third stage.
  Finally, on each own-color collar the lower barrier just constructed gives the required one-sided height bound. On the rest of a fixed compact region use Lemma 29; the opposite height bound on an own-color collar follows from that lemma as well, since the closed collar is disjoint from the other region. A finite cover proves $b_(-) lt.eq h lt.eq b_(+)$ on the compact regions in the statement. ◻
]

===== 1.5.3 A trace estimate with polynomial constants <a-trace-estimate-with-polynomial-constants>

The collar trace estimate must have polynomial constants: it will control the boundary flux both here and in the mass estimate. We therefore prove it before comparing with the ordinary product graph.

#thm([Lemma 1.31.])[
  (Weighted and unweighted collar traces).  #emph[In the first two stages let $cal(C)$ be a fixed union of disjoint collars of the inner faces, shortened if necessary. For every nonnegative smooth function $phi$ supported in those collars, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.111)")
 $ integral_B L phi thin upright(d) A_g & lt.eq Pi_n integral_(cal(C)) u #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) phi + \| d phi \|_(A_chi) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] thin upright(d) V_g \,\
integral_B phi thin upright(d) A_g & lt.eq Pi_n integral_(cal(C)) sqrt(D) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) phi + \| d phi \|_(A_chi) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] thin upright(d) V_g . $ <n3:apriori:weighted-trace>]

 The estimates also hold without a support condition on $phi$ if the right sides include a fixed collar cutoff equal to one on $B$, with its derivative included in the coefficient of $phi$.]
] <n3:apriori:trace>

#proof[
  For $sigma > 0$, write $e_f = nabla f \/ sigma$, the axis denoted by $e$ in Section 4. Set
  $ W = l accent(nabla f, ‾) = frac(l nabla f, D) = sqrt(d) thin a e_f . $
  Its norm in $macron(g)$ is $a lt.eq 1$. For any covector $xi$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.112)")
   $ \| xi \( W \) \| lt.eq \| xi \|_(A_d) lt.eq sqrt(1 + n \/ 4) thin \| xi \|_(A_chi) . $ <n3:apriori:W-gradient>]
  Direct differentiation, using $u W = L w$, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.113)")
   $ frac("div" \( u W \), u) & = sqrt(d) ["tr"_(A_d) H^f + \( d p + p_L \) a partial_(e_f) t] \,\
frac("div" \( sqrt(D) thin W \), sqrt(D)) & = sqrt(d) ["tr"_(A_d) H^f + d p thin a partial_(e_f) t] . $ <n3:apriori:W-divergence>]
  For example, $"div" w = "tr"_(A_d) H^f + d p thin a partial_(e_f) t$, while differentiating $L$ adds $p_L a partial_(e_f) t$ to the first formula. Coercivity in Lemma 21 bounds both right sides by $Pi_n \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \)$. More explicitly, writing $q = \( K + H^f \)_(e_f e_f)$, the normal Hessian term has coefficient $d^(3 \/ 2)$, so it is bounded by $sqrt(d) \| q \| + C$. The other Hessian terms are transverse traces, and the gradient term is at most $\( 2 n + 2 \) sqrt(d) \| partial_(e_f) t \|$. All are controlled by the stated coercivity estimate. This also establishes the formulas and bounds at zero gradient by their smooth direction-free extensions.
  Let $epsilon_B = 1$ on a black collar and $- 1$ on a white collar. The signed slopes give $w_nu = epsilon_B a$ on $B$ and $a gt.eq 1 \/ 2$ for large $n$. Thus
  $ u W_nu = epsilon_B L a \, #h(2em) sqrt(D) thin W_nu = epsilon_B a . $
  The outward integration normal is $- nu$. Apply the divergence theorem to $- epsilon_B u W phi$, with a fixed collar cutoff. Its boundary flux is $L a phi$. Equations (1.112) and (1.113), and the fixed cutoff derivative, give (1.111). Replace $u$ by $sqrt(D)$ and use (1.113) for (1.111). No factor $ell^(- 1)$ was used. ◻
]

===== 1.5.4 High-level energy and the first upper bound <high-level-energy-and-the-first-upper-bound>

The first stage contains the drift in the divergence equation. We first bound this stage uniformly for $lambda in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$. There is a number $M_0 = M_0 \( n \) > 0$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.114)")
 $ Xi gt.eq delta_0 cal(T) + rho + 1 / 2 delta_0 tau a sigma quad upright("on ") { Z > M_0 } . $ <n3:apriori:high-source>]

Indeed, outside the support of $m_0$ this is immediate. On its support, $- epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq - epsilon.alt + 1 \/ n$, so $l$ is comparable to $ell$. The identity $D = e^(8 \( Z - t \))$ shows that $a sigma$ tends uniformly to infinity as $Z arrow.r oo$ in that band. The penalty $cal(P)$ is bounded on the whole exterior by $Pi_n$. It is therefore absorbed by $delta_0 tau a sigma \/ 2$ above a fixed $M_0 \( n \)$. This choice is independent of $R$ and $lambda$.

Choose a smooth nondecreasing $k_0$ equal to zero on $Z lt.eq M_0$, equal to one on $Z gt.eq M_0 + 1$, and with bounded derivative. Test $"div" V_lambda = u Xi$ by $k_0 \( Z \)$. Its outer boundary term vanishes since $Z = 0$. The drift cross term is bounded by

$ u k'_0 lr(|chevron.l d Z \, K \( w \, dot.op \) chevron.r_(A_chi)|) lt.eq 2 u k'_0 \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2 + C u k'_0 \| K \|^2 . $

The last term has bounded integral: it is supported on a fixed compact set and on $M_0 lt.eq Z lt.eq M_0 + 1$, where the floor and $D = e^(8 \( Z - t \))$ bound $u$ and $sigma$ by $C \( n \)$.

At a black face $F - P_B = H$, $w_nu = a$; at a white face $F - P_B = - H$, $w_nu = - a$. In both cases

$ - H + w_nu \( F - P_B \) = \( a - 1 \) H . $

The additional omitted-drift term in the homotopy boundary condition has absolute value at most $C chi lt.eq C d$, since $A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \)$ has normal component $chi w_nu K \( nu \, nu \)$. The remaining term is $- N_B d$, and the boundary multiplier lies between zero and one. Hence

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.115)")
 $ \| \( V_lambda \)_nu \| lt.eq C u d = C L sqrt(d) lt.eq C tau / ell L quad upright("on ") B \, $ <n3:apriori:small-boundary-flux>]

where the last inequality uses the lower slope in (1.107). Integration now gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.116)")
 $ & integral_(Omega_R) u k_0 (delta_0 cal(T) + rho + 1 / 2 delta_0 tau a sigma) thin upright(d) V_g + 2 integral_(Omega_R) u k'_0 \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2 thin upright(d) V_g\
 & #h(18mm)  lt.eq C \( n \) + C tau / ell integral_B L k_0 thin upright(d) A_g . $ <n3:apriori:high-energy-before-trace>]

Apply (1.111) with the fixed collar cutoff. The boundary cost is at most

$ Pi_n tau / ell integral_(cal(C)) u [k_0 \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) + k'_0 \| d Z \|_(A_chi)] thin upright(d) V_g . $

Let $rho_(cal(C)) > 0$ be the minimum of $rho$ on the closed collars. Pointwise, $1 + sqrt(cal(T)) lt.eq C \( rho + delta_0 cal(T) \)$ there, with $C$ fixed. Since $Pi_n tau \/ ell arrow.r 0$, the first summand is absorbed in the first integral of (1.116). Young’s inequality bounds the derivative summand by

$ u k'_0 \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2 + C \( Pi_n tau \/ ell \)^2 u k'_0 . $

The second term again has bounded compact integral because it lies in the transition strip of $k_0$. We obtain

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.117)")
 $ integral u k_0 \( cal(T) + rho + tau a sigma \) thin upright(d) V_g + integral u k'_0 \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2 thin upright(d) V_g lt.eq C \( n \) . $ <n3:apriori:high-energy>]

The smallness requirement on $n$ in this absorption is independent of $M_0$; only its resulting right-hand constant depends on $M_0$.

The polynomial boundary absorption is complete. We may now use arbitrary fixed-$n$ comparison constants. Write $Gamma$ for the ordinary product graph of $f$ in $g + \( thin upright(d) b_0 \)^2$, and $thin upright(d) Gamma = sqrt(1 + sigma^2) thin upright(d) V_g$. The measures $thin upright(d) Gamma$ and $sqrt(D) thin upright(d) V_g$ are comparable by constants depending only on $ell$. On every fixed compact set $Q$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.118)")
 $ integral_(Gamma ∩ pi^(- 1) \( Q \)) e^(2 Z) thin upright(d) Gamma lt.eq C_Q \( n \) \, $ <n3:apriori:L2>]

where $pi$ is projection to the base. To check this without an upper bound for $t$, observe that

$ e^(2 Z) = e^(2 t) D^(1 \/ 4) \, #h(2em) u = l e^(2 t) sqrt(D) \, #h(2em) D^(1 \/ 4) lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + tau a sigma \) . $

The last inequality follows by splitting $l sigma lt.eq 1$ and $l sigma > 1$, using $ell lt.eq l lt.eq e$; on the second set $a gt.eq 1 \/ sqrt(2)$ and the right side grows linearly in $sigma$, whereas $D^(1 \/ 4)$ grows at most as $C sqrt(sigma)$. Thus the high-level part of (1.118) follows from (1.117) and the positive minimum of $rho$ on $Q$. On $Z lt.eq M_0 + 1$, both $sigma$ and $e^(2 Z)$ are bounded by the floor identity, which controls the remaining compact integral.

===== 1.5.5 Sobolev inequality and iteration on the ordinary graph <sobolev-inequality-and-iteration-on-the-ordinary-graph>

The comparisons in this subsection are uniform in the solution, $R$, and the homotopy parameter, but may depend arbitrarily on $n$. If $d_0 = \( 1 + sigma^2 \)^(- 1)$, then

$ \| d phi \|_(A_(d_0)) = \| nabla_Gamma phi \| \, #h(2em) d / d_0 = frac(1 + sigma^2, 1 + l^2 sigma^2) . $

The last ratio is bounded above and below by constants depending only on $ell$; Equation (1.102) therefore compares $A_(d_0) \, A_d \, A_chi$ without any bound for $Z$.

#thm([Lemma 1.32.])[
  (Local graph Sobolev inequality).  #emph[For a smooth function $omega$ supported over a fixed compact base patch, which may meet an inner face, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.119)")
 $ parallel omega parallel_(L^6 \( thin upright(d) Gamma \))^2 lt.eq C \( n \) integral_Gamma [\| nabla_Gamma omega \|^2 + \( 1 + cal(T) \) omega^2] thin upright(d) Gamma . $ <n3:apriori:sobolev-equation>]

]
] <n3:apriori:sobolev>

#proof[
  Extend the fixed base patch smoothly and embed a compact enlargement isometrically in Euclidean space by Nash’s embedding theorem #cite(label("Nash1956")) Theorem 2. Its product with the vertical line gives an isometric Euclidean immersion of the graph. The second fundamental form of the base embedding is bounded, so its contribution to the graph mean curvature vector is bounded. The scalar mean curvature in the product is
  $ H_Gamma = frac(sqrt(D), l sqrt(1 + sigma^2)) ("tr"_(e_f^perp) H^f + d_0 H^f \( e_f \, e_f \)) . $
  The prefactor is bounded by $C \( n \)$, and $d_0 \/ sqrt(d) = sqrt(1 + l^2 sigma^2) \/ \( 1 + sigma^2 \) lt.eq e$. Coercivity therefore gives $\| bold(H)_Gamma \| lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \)$. This estimate involves controlled quadratic energy, not a pointwise bound for the Hessian.
  The ordinary Michael–Simon inequality #cite(label("MichaelSimon1973")), in the compact-support form stated in #cite(label("Simon2014")) Chapter 4, Section 5, Theorem 5.7, gives after the boundary extension described below, for nonnegative $phi$,
  $ (integral_Gamma phi^(3 \/ 2) thin upright(d) Gamma)^(2 \/ 3) lt.eq C [integral_Gamma \( \| nabla_Gamma phi \| + \| bold(H)_Gamma \| phi \) thin upright(d) Gamma + integral_(partial Gamma) phi thin upright(d) A] . $
  One can obtain the boundary form directly from the compact-support version by smoothly extending each smooth graph across its boundary and cutting off on a collar tending to zero: the cutoff derivative integral converges to the boundary integral, and the added curvature integral tends to zero. This argument is applied to each smooth graph before the uniform estimate is taken. The only boundary in the support is an inner face, where $f$ is constant and $thin upright(d) A = thin upright(d) A_g$. Equation (1.111) and the fixed-$n$ graph comparisons bound its contribution by
  $ C \( n \) integral_Gamma \[ \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) phi + \| nabla_Gamma phi \| \] thin upright(d) Gamma . $
  Apply the resulting inequality to $phi = \| omega \|^4$. Cauchy–Schwarz bounds its right side by
  $ C \( n \) parallel omega parallel_6^3 (integral_Gamma \[ \| nabla_Gamma omega \|^2 + \( 1 + cal(T) \) omega^2 \] thin upright(d) Gamma)^(1 \/ 2) . $
  The left side is $parallel omega parallel_6^4$; division, with the zero case separate, and squaring prove the assertion. ◻
]

To turn the integral bound into an upper bound, let $eta$ be a nonnegative cutoff over a fixed compact patch. For $k$ sufficiently large, independently of $R$ and the solution, test the first-stage divergence equation by

$ q = eta^2 e^(2 k Z) \/ L . $

The positive measure after cancellation of $L$ is $sqrt(D) thin upright(d) V_g$. The differentiated test is

$ d q = e^(2 k Z) / L \[ 2 eta thin d eta + eta^2 \( 2 k thin d Z - p_L thin d t \) \] . $

Its principal exponential contribution is $8 k eta^2 e^(2 k Z) \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2$. The source is bounded below by $delta_0 cal(T) - Pi_n$ everywhere. Coercivity and $A_chi lt.eq A_d$ give

$ 4 p_L \| chevron.l d t \, d Z chevron.r_(A_chi) \| lt.eq 1 / 4 delta_0 cal(T) + C \( n \) \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2 . $

Choose $k gt.eq k_(*) \( n \)$ so that the second term is absorbed by the principal contribution. The drift terms, with $\| K \( w \, dot.op \) \|_(A_chi) lt.eq \| K \|$, obey

$ 2 k \| chevron.l K \( w \, dot.op \) \, d Z chevron.r_(A_chi) \| & lt.eq k \| d Z \|_(A_chi)^2 + C k \| K \|^2 \,\
p_L \| chevron.l K \( w \, dot.op \) \, d t chevron.r_(A_chi) \| & lt.eq 1 / 4 delta_0 cal(T) + C \( n \) \| K \|^2 . $

The cutoff cross terms are handled by the same Cauchy inequalities, leaving $C \( n \) k e^(2 k Z) \( \| d eta \|_g^2 + eta^2 \)$. The penalty is bounded and contributes to this latter expression. Finally, Equation (1.115) before its last inequality gives $\| q \( V_lambda \)_nu \| lt.eq C eta^2 e^(2 k Z) sqrt(d) lt.eq C eta^2 e^(2 k Z)$. Converting to the ordinary graph yields

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.120)")
 $ & integral_Gamma e^(2 k Z) eta^2 \( cal(T) + k \| nabla_Gamma Z \|^2 \) thin upright(d) Gamma\
 & quad lt.eq C \( n \) k integral_Gamma e^(2 k Z) \( eta^2 + \| d eta \|_g^2 \) thin upright(d) Gamma + C \( n \) integral_B e^(2 k Z) eta^2 thin upright(d) A_g . $ <n3:apriori:iteration-energy-boundary>]

Use the unweighted trace with $phi = e^(2 k Z) eta^2$. The boundary term is at most

$ C \( n \) integral_Gamma e^(2 k Z) [eta^2 \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) + k \| nabla_Gamma Z \| \) + eta \| d eta \|_g] thin upright(d) Gamma . $

Young’s inequality absorbs its $sqrt(cal(T))$ term into the $cal(T)$ integral and its $k \| nabla_Gamma Z \|$ term into the $k \| nabla_Gamma Z \|^2$ integral. The latter absorption costs $C \( n \) k eta^2 e^(2 k Z)$, which has the allowed size. Hence

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.121)")
 $ integral_Gamma e^(2 k Z) eta^2 \( cal(T) + k \| nabla_Gamma Z \|^2 \) thin upright(d) Gamma lt.eq C \( n \) k integral_Gamma e^(2 k Z) \( eta^2 + \| d eta \|_g^2 \) thin upright(d) Gamma \, $ <n3:apriori:iteration-energy>]

with constants independent of $k gt.eq k_(*)$.

Apply Lemma 32 to $omega = eta e^(k Z)$ and use (1.121). For nested base patches $U_r subset U_(r')$ and a cutoff satisfying $\| d eta \|_g lt.eq C \/ \( r' - r \)$, this gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.122)")
 $ parallel e^Z parallel_(L^(6 k) \( Gamma \|_(U_r) \)) lt.eq [C \( n \) k^2 \( 1 + \( r' - r \)^(- 2) \)]^(1 \/ \( 2 k \)) parallel e^Z parallel_(L^(2 k) \( Gamma \|_(U_(r')) \)) . $ <n3:apriori:moser-step>]

Here $Gamma \|_U$ denotes the part of the graph projecting to $U$. Iterate with $k_j = 3^j k_(*)$ and radii decreasing geometrically from $r'$ to $r$. The sums $sum k_j^(- 1)$ and $sum j \/ k_j$ are finite. Taking the product in (1.122) therefore gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.123)")
 $ S \( r \) := sup_(U_r) e^Z lt.eq C \( n \) \( r' - r \)^(- A \( n \)) parallel e^Z parallel_(L^(2 k_(*)) \( Gamma \|_(U_(r')) \)) . $ <n3:apriori:moser-high-norm>]

Take all these patches inside a slightly larger fixed patch on which (1.118) holds. Increasing $k_(*)$ to at least two, interpolation gives

$ parallel e^Z parallel_(2 k_(*)) lt.eq S \( r' \)^(1 - 1 \/ k_(*)) (integral e^(2 Z) thin upright(d) Gamma)^(1 \/ \( 2 k_(*) \)) . $

Thus $S \( r \) lt.eq C \( n \) \( r' - r \)^(- A \( n \)) S \( r' \)^(1 - 1 \/ k_(*))$. For every $alpha > 0$, Young’s inequality makes this

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.124)")
 $ S \( r \) lt.eq alpha S \( r' \) + C \( n \, alpha \) \( r' - r \)^(- A \( n \) k_(*)) . $ <n3:apriori:moser-interpolation>]

Choose radii increasing geometrically to a fixed outer radius $r_1$, and then choose $alpha < 2^(- A \( n \) k_(*) - 1)$. Iterating (1.124) has a convergent geometric sum. Its final remainder $alpha^j S \( r_j \)$ tends to zero, because each individual smooth solution is bounded on the closed outer patch. The resulting smaller-patch bound is independent of that individual supremum. A finite cover proves a uniform compact upper bound for $Z$, including at every inner face.

Beyond a fixed sphere containing the drift support, the equation is $"div" \( 4 u A_chi nabla Z \) = u Xi$, and $Xi > 0$ above $M_0$. At an interior maximum above $M_0$ its left side is nonpositive, because $d Z = 0$ and $"Hess" Z lt.eq 0$. The outer boundary value is zero. The compact bound on that fixed sphere consequently extends the first-stage upper bound to the whole truncation.

===== 1.5.6 The remaining stages and the bounded-variable conclusion <the-remaining-stages-and-the-bounded-variable-conclusion>

In the second and third stages the drift is zero. Their source has the same high-$Z$ positivity as (1.114), with a uniform threshold for the bounded homotopy range. By Lemma 30, on an inner face

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.125)")
 $ Z = t + 1 / 8 log \( 1 + l^2 sigma^2 \) lt.eq t + 1 / 8 log \( 1 + e^2 C_(*)^2 \/ tau^2 \) . $ <n3:apriori:high-boundary-t>]

Thus sufficiently high boundary $Z$ forces $j \( t \) = 1$. The prescribed boundary flux is then zero, since $lambda = 0$. At the face $A_chi nu = chi nu$, so this is exactly $partial_nu Z = 0$. The maximum principle excludes a high interior maximum, and the boundary point principle excludes a high maximum on a smooth inner face with this zero conormal derivative. Both principles are applied to the smooth individual solution on its finite domain; its coefficients are positive definite there. Locating the maximum requires no uniform ellipticity constant. The outer value is zero, so these two stages also have a uniform upper bound.

In the fourth stage $f = 0$ and $t = Z$. When the common source/flux scale is positive, the same maximum and boundary argument works with its positive high-level source. At scale zero the homogeneous mixed problem and the outer zero Dirichlet value give $Z = 0$. The upper bound is consequently uniform also as the scale tends to zero.

#thm([Proposition 1.33.])[
  (Bounds before regularity).  #emph[Fix the prepared data and $epsilon.alt > 0$. There are $n_0$ and $R_min \( n \) arrow.r oo$ such that, for each fixed $n gt.eq n_0$ and every $R gt.eq R_min \( n \)$, all smooth solutions of the four-stage homotopy with $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$ satisfy 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.126)")
 $ \| h \| lt.eq C_0 \, #h(2em) \| f \| lt.eq C_0 \/ tau \, #h(2em) - epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq Z lt.eq C \( n \) \, #h(2em) \| nabla f \| lt.eq C \( n \) . $ <n3:apriori:bounded-variables>]

 The constants are uniform in $R$ and in the homotopy parameter. They include putative floor-contact solutions, which are then excluded by Proposition 28. The fixed signed slopes, the third-stage upper slopes, and the polynomial trace estimates are those of Lemmas 30 and 31.]
] <n3:apriori:bounds>

#proof[
  It remains to extract the bounds for the conformal variable and the gradient. Since $Z = t + log D \/ 8$ and $D gt.eq 1$, the floor gives $- epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq Z$. The upper bound for $Z$ gives
  $ sigma^2 = frac(e^(8 \( Z - t \)) - 1, l^2) lt.eq ell^(- 2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e^(8 \( C \( n \) + epsilon.alt \)) - 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  This proves the asserted fixed-$n$ gradient bound. The height bounds are Lemma 24. The finite collection of sublevel and collar estimates and the polynomial absorption choose $n_0$ once. Enlarge $R_min \( n \)$ to include the outer estimates and all fixed compact patches used above. The next section derives Hessian and continuity estimates from these bounds and uses them to prove existence. ◻
]

==== 1.6 Regularity and existence of the elliptic deformation <n3:existence:section>

We now pass from the bounded variables in Proposition 33 to smooth solutions. The second equation contains Hessian squares, so classical estimates cannot yet be applied directly. We first control the gradient of the first unknown with a measurable axial coefficient. Its Hessian Morrey bound then gives continuity of the second unknown and permits classical regularity. Finally we define the scalar Dirichlet solution operator on all bounded input sets and apply degree to Proposition 23.

Throughout this section, ordinary regularity constants may depend on all fixed deformation parameters, including $n$ and $tau$. No such constant will be used as a polynomial-in-$n$ estimate. Bounds uniform in the outer truncation radius will be stated explicitly.

===== 1.6.1 An estimate for a measurable axial coefficient <an-estimate-for-a-measurable-axial-coefficient>

We use $D$ for coordinate derivatives in the analytic lemmas. Balls are Euclidean coordinate balls, and a boundary ball is their intersection with one side of a smooth boundary. A family of boundary patches has bounded geometry here if normal-coordinate charts, their inverses, the metric and its inverse, and the derivatives through the orders used below have fixed bounds. We only apply the lemmas to smooth metrics and smooth boundaries. Their doubled metrics will be Lipschitz and smooth on each side of a single plane.

#thm([Lemma 1.34.])[
  (Axial gradient estimate).  #emph[Let $U'$ be compactly contained in an interior or boundary coordinate patch $U$ in dimension three. In a boundary patch assume that $z$ is constant on the boundary face. Suppose $z$ is smooth up to that face, $\| nabla z \| lt.eq L$, and, almost everywhere, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.127)")
 $ A_chi : "Hess" z = G \, #h(2em) A_chi = I - \( 1 - chi \) e ⊗ e \, #h(2em) e = frac(nabla z, \| nabla z \|) \, #h(2em) 0 < chi_0 lt.eq chi lt.eq 1 \, $ <n3:existence:axial-equation>]

 where $parallel G parallel_oo lt.eq Q$. At zero gradients any measurable unit axis for which the equation holds is allowed. Then there are $alpha in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ and $C < oo$, depending only on $chi_0$, the patch geometry and the separation of $U'$ from the other patch boundaries, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.128)")
 $ parallel nabla z parallel_(C^(0 \, alpha) \( U' \)) lt.eq C \( L + Q \) . $ <n3:existence:gradient-estimate>]

 For all sufficiently small coordinate balls centered in $U'$, with intersection with the domain understood at a boundary, one also has 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.129)")
 $ integral_(B_r ∩ U) \| "Hess" z \|^2 thin upright(d) V_g lt.eq C \( L + Q \)^2 r^(1 + 2 alpha) . $ <n3:existence:hessian-morrey>]

 In particular, the constants are independent of continuity moduli of $chi$ and $G$.]
] <n3:existence:gradient>

#proof[
  The proof combines a Cordes estimate, a divergence identity for the gradient, and improvement of affine approximations. We first establish these estimates, then handle boundary reflection and the iteration.
  ======= 1.6.1.1 A Cordes estimate above exponent two. <a-cordes-estimate-above-exponent-two.>
  For a Euclidean unit vector $e$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.130)")
   $ parallel I - A_chi parallel_F = 1 - chi lt.eq 1 - chi_0 . $ <n3:existence:cordes-defect>]
  For a compactly supported smooth function $v$, Plancherel’s theorem gives $parallel D^2 v parallel_2 = parallel Delta v parallel_2$. The Calderón–Zygmund operator taking $Delta v$ to the full array $D^2 v$ is bounded on $L^p$, and interpolation makes its norm arbitrarily close to one as $p arrow.r 2$. Choose once and for all
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.131)")
   $ 2 < p_0 < 3 \, #h(2em) C_(p_0) \( 1 - chi_0 \/ 2 \) < 1 . $ <n3:existence:p-zero>]
  Rescale a metric-normalized patch until the coordinate principal matrix differs from its Euclidean axial matrix by less than $chi_0 \/ 2$ in Frobenius norm. Absorbing this difference in the Laplacian estimate, applying a cutoff, and using $parallel D v parallel_p lt.eq eta parallel D^2 v parallel_p + C_eta parallel v parallel_p$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.132)")
   $ parallel D^2 v parallel_(L^(p_0) \( B_(1 \/ 2) \)) + parallel D v parallel_(L^(p_0) \( B_(1 \/ 2) \)) lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] parallel v parallel_(L^(p_0) \( B_1 \)) + parallel A_chi : "Hess" v parallel_(L^(p_0) \( B_1 \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:existence:local-cordes>]
  The Christoffel terms are bounded first-order terms in this estimate. The same argument applies when $v = z - L_0$ and $L_0$ is coordinate affine: its extra right side is bounded by $C parallel D g parallel_oo \| D L_0 \|$. The estimate uses bounded measurable principal coefficients only; the smoothness of the solutions allows all of its applications without an approximation issue. The linear estimates just used are the ordinary Laplacian estimates; see #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")) Chapters 7 and 9.
  ======= 1.6.1.2 The identity controlling a near-maximal gradient. <the-identity-controlling-a-near-maximal-gradient.>
  Write $H = "Hess" z$ and $P = nabla z$. Equation (1.127) gives the pointwise vector identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.133)")
   $ A_chi nabla frac(\| P \|^2, 2) - G P = \( H - Delta z thin g \) P . $ <n3:existence:gradient-flux>]
  Indeed $H P = \| P \| H \( e \, dot.op \)$ and $G = Delta z - \( 1 - chi \) H \( e \, e \)$, so the two axial terms cancel. The identity is also valid at $P = 0$. Taking the divergence of its right side, rather than differentiating $chi$, yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.134)")
   $ "div" (A_chi nabla frac(\| P \|^2, 2) - G P) = \| H \|^2 - \( Delta z \)^2 + "Ric" \( P \, P \) . $ <n3:existence:bochner-flux>]
  Choose $a_0 > 0$ so small that $\( 1 + a_0 \) \( 1 - chi_0 \)^2 < 1$. Young’s inequality and the equation show
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.135)")
   $ \| H \|^2 - \( Delta z \)^2 gt.eq c_0 \| H \|^2 - C_0 G^2 \, $ <n3:existence:positive-hessian>]
  where $c_0 > 0$ depends only on $chi_0$.
  Consider solutions normalized by $\| nabla z \| lt.eq 1$ on $B_1$. After making the scale small, let the forcing and the rescaled geometric errors have size at most $delta$. The nonnegative function $s = 1 - \| nabla z \|^2$ then satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.136)")
   $ "div" \( A_chi nabla s + 2 G nabla z \) lt.eq - 2 c_0 \| "Hess" z \|^2 + C delta $ <n3:existence:s-supersolution>]
  in a weak sense. In the Euclidean case the last term can be replaced by $C delta^2$. We allow $C delta$ to include the geometric errors.
  For the compactness step, suppose the normalized errors tend to zero in a sequence and $inf_(B_(1 \/ 2)) s arrow.r 0$. The inhomogeneous weak Harnack inequality, applied to Equation (1.136) after dropping its favorable Hessian term, gives $s arrow.r 0$ in measure on $B_(1 \/ 2)$. The boundedness $0 lt.eq s lt.eq 1$ also gives convergence in every finite $L^p$ norm there. To recover the Hessian information, take a cutoff $eta$ supported inside $B_(1 \/ 2)$ and use $eta^2 \( b - s \)_(+)$ in the weak supersolution inequality. Ellipticity and Young’s inequality give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.137)")
   $ integral_({ s < b }) eta^2 \| D s \|^2 lt.eq C b^2 integral \| D eta \|^2 + o \( 1 \) $ <n3:existence:low-truncation>]
  for each fixed $b > 0$ as the errors vanish. The error fields in Equation (1.136) are bounded and tend to zero, so their products with $D s$ are absorbed in deriving this inequality. Equation (1.132) supplies a uniform $L^2$ bound for $D s$. Its integral over ${ s gt.eq b }$ tends to zero in $L^1$ by Cauchy’s inequality and convergence in measure. On ${ s < b }$ use Equation (1.137), then let $b arrow.b 0$. Thus $D s arrow.r 0$ locally in $L^1$. Testing Equation (1.134) with a nonnegative compact cutoff and using Equations (1.135) and (1.133) now gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.138)")
   $ integral_(B_(1 \/ 4)) \| "Hess" z \|^2 arrow.r 0 . $ <n3:existence:hessian-flat-limit>]
  Uniform Lipschitz compactness implies that, after subtracting a constant and taking a subsequence, $z$ tends uniformly on a smaller ball to an affine function. Its slope has length one because $\| nabla z \| arrow.r 1$ in measure and the Hessians tend to zero.
  Consequently, for any prescribed sufficiently small $b_(*) > 0$ there are fixed $r_(*) in \( 0 \, 1 \/ 4 \)$, $k_(*) in \( r_(*) \, 1 \)$ and $delta_(*) > 0$ such that every normalized solution with errors at most $delta_(*)$ has one of the following properties:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.139)")
   $ & sup_(B_(r_(*))) \| nabla z \| lt.eq k_(*) \; #h(2em) upright("or")\
 & sup_(B_(r_(*))) \| z - L_0 \| lt.eq b_(*) r_(*) \, #h(2em) 1 / 2 lt.eq \| D L_0 \| lt.eq 2 . $ <n3:existence:drop-or-flat>]
  If this conclusion failed, solutions whose gradient suprema approach one and whose errors approach zero would contradict Equation (1.138). A gradient-drop constant can always be weakened toward one to ensure $k_(*) > r_(*)$.
  ======= 1.6.1.3 A fixed-axis equation. <a-fixed-axis-equation.>
  Once a nonzero affine slope appears, we compare with an equation whose axis is fixed. Fix a Euclidean unit vector $e_0$. Suppose
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.140)")
   $ Delta_(e_0^perp) Y_0 + chi \( x \) partial_(e_0 e_0) Y_0 = 0 quad upright("in ") B_(3 \/ 4) \, #h(2em) parallel Y_0 parallel_(W^(2 \, 2) \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C_1 . $ <n3:existence:fixed-axis>]
  The derivative $v = partial_(e_0) Y_0$ belongs to $W^(1 \, 2)$ and, in distributions, satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.141)")
   $ Delta_(e_0^perp) v + partial_(e_0) \( chi thin partial_(e_0) v \) = 0 . $ <n3:existence:axial-derivative-divergence>]
  This follows by differentiating the distribution $chi partial_(e_0) v$ as a whole. It does not assume a derivative of $chi$ exists as a function. De Giorgi’s estimate for this uniformly elliptic divergence equation, followed by Caccioppoli applied to $v - v \( x_0 \)$, gives an exponent $alpha_1 in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.142)")
   $ \[ v \]_(C^(0 \, alpha_1) \( B_(1 \/ 2) \)) lt.eq C \, #h(2em) integral_(B_r \( x_0 \)) \| D v \|^2 lt.eq C r^(1 + 2 alpha_1) $ <n3:existence:axial-derivative-morrey>]
  for balls in a smaller fixed interior region. These are the scalar divergence estimates with ellipticity $chi_0$; see #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")) Chapter 8.
  All components of $D Y_0$, not only $v$, are controlled by this estimate. In fact,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.143)")
   $ Delta Y_0 = f_0 \, #h(2em) f_0 = \( 1 - chi \) partial_(e_0) v \, #h(2em) integral_(B_r \( x_0 \)) \| f_0 \| lt.eq C r^(2 + alpha_1) . $ <n3:existence:poisson-source>]
  The last inequality follows from Cauchy’s inequality and Equation (1.142). Take the Newton potential of $f_0$ restricted to a slightly larger interior ball; its difference from $Y_0$ is harmonic in that ball. For two points at distance $h$, the contribution to the difference of gradient potentials from annuli of radius $r lt.eq 2 h$ is bounded by $C sum r^(alpha_1) lt.eq C h^(alpha_1)$. On the remaining annuli the difference of gradient kernels is at most $C h r^(- 3)$, so their contribution is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.144)")
   $ C h sum_(r gt.eq 2 h) r^(alpha_1 - 1) lt.eq C h^(alpha_1) . $ <n3:existence:potential-annuli>]
  The harmonic remainder has its usual interior derivative estimates. Therefore, decreasing the interior ball if needed,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.145)")
   $ parallel Y_0 parallel_(C^(1 \, alpha_1) \( B_(1 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C . $ <n3:existence:model-gradient>]
  ======= 1.6.1.4 Improvement of a nonzero affine approximation. <improvement-of-a-nonzero-affine-approximation.>
  Suppose now
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.146)")
   $ parallel z - L_0 parallel_(L^oo \( B_1 \)) lt.eq b \, #h(2em) 1 / 4 lt.eq \| D L_0 \| lt.eq 4 \, #h(2em) \| nabla z \| lt.eq 8 . $ <n3:existence:flat-input>]
  Let the forcing and the first-derivative metric errors be at most $b delta$. For $Y = \( z - L_0 \) \/ b$, Equation (1.132) on fixed nested balls gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.147)")
   $ parallel Y parallel_(W^(2 \, p_0) \( B_(7 \/ 8) \)) lt.eq C . $ <n3:existence:normalized-residual>]
  In particular, this estimate does not require a bound for $D Y$ in the supremum norm. Since $D z = D L_0 + b D Y$, the variable axis converges in measure to $e_0 = D L_0 \/ \| D L_0 \|$ as $b arrow.r 0$. More quantitatively, the coefficient error $E$ caused by replacing this axis by $e_0$ is bounded and satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.148)")
   $ parallel E parallel_(L^q \( B_(7 \/ 8) \)) lt.eq C b^theta.alt + C b delta \, #h(2em) q = frac(2 p_0, p_0 - 2) \, #h(2em) theta.alt = frac(p_0 - 2, 2) > 0 . $ <n3:existence:axis-error>]
  Indeed the unit-direction map is Lipschitz where $\| b D Y \| < \| D L_0 \| \/ 2$, and elsewhere its difference is bounded; the $L^(p_0)$ bound in Equation (1.147) and interpolation give Equation (1.148). The same estimate includes the metric principal-part error. Hölder’s inequality, with $1 \/ 2 = 1 \/ q + 1 \/ p_0$, shows that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.149)")
   $ parallel \[ I - \( 1 - chi \) e_0 ⊗ e_0 \] : D^2 Y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[parallel]_(L^2 \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C \( b^theta.alt + delta \) . $ <n3:existence:small-fixed-axis-source>]
  Solve for a zero-Dirichlet correction $w$ on $B_(3 \/ 4)$ with the left side of Equation (1.149) as its source. This does not require a nondivergence Green function. The convex Dirichlet Hessian inequality $parallel D^2 w parallel_2 lt.eq parallel Delta w parallel_2$ and Equation (1.130) make the Laplace solution operator a contraction on $W^(2 \, 2) ∩ W_0^(1 \, 2)$. Hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.150)")
   $ parallel w parallel_(W^(2 \, 2) \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C chi_0^(- 1) \( b^theta.alt + delta \) \, #h(2em) parallel w parallel_oo lt.eq C \( b^theta.alt + delta \) . $ <n3:existence:fixed-axis-correction>]
  The last implication uses $W^(2 \, 2) arrow.r.hook C^(0 \, 1 \/ 2)$ in dimension three. The convex Hessian inequality is the Miranda–Talenti estimate; see #cite(label("Talenti1965")) Section 2, Theorem 3. Now $Y_0 = Y - w$ satisfies Equation (1.140) with a uniform $W^(2 \, 2)$ bound.
  Choose $0 < alpha_2 < alpha_1$ and then a fixed $lambda_0 in \( 0 \, 1 \/ 4 \)$ such that $C lambda_0^(1 + alpha_1) lt.eq lambda_0^(1 + alpha_2) \/ 4$ in Equation (1.145). By first taking $delta$ small and then $b lt.eq b_(*)$ small, Equation (1.150) has supremum norm at most $lambda_0^(1 + alpha_2) \/ 4$. Taylor expansion of $Y_0$ at the center therefore gives an affine function $L_1$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.151)")
   $ sup_(B_(lambda_0)) \| z - L_1 \| lt.eq b lambda_0^(1 + alpha_2) \, #h(2em) \| D L_1 - D L_0 \| lt.eq C b . $ <n3:existence:improved-flatness>]
  All constants are uniform in the slope range in Equation (1.146).
  ======= 1.6.1.5 Reflection at a constant Dirichlet face. <reflection-at-a-constant-dirichlet-face.>
  Subtract the boundary value and use normal coordinates with the face given by $x_3 = 0$. Reflect the metric across this plane and reflect $z$ oddly. Tangential derivatives of $z$ vanish on the face, so the extension is $C^1$. There is no distributional Hessian atom. The doubled metric is Lipschitz; the coefficient and lower-order estimates in Equation (1.132) hold with its essential first-derivative bound.
  The flux in Equation (1.133) may have a normal jump. On either side of the face its normal trace is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.152)")
   $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( "Hess" z - Delta z thin g \) nabla z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]_nu = - \( "tr"_T "Hess" z \) thin partial_nu z . $ <n3:existence:reflected-flux>]
  Because the boundary value is constant, $"tr"_T "Hess" z$ is its mean-curvature coefficient times $partial_nu z$. The jump is therefore bounded by $C \| upright("II") \| thin \| nabla z \|^2$. At scale $r$ it is a bounded plane density of size $O \( r \)$. Write the plane density and flux after multiplication by the reflected coordinate volume density, so that the following divergence is Euclidean. Such a signed density $a \( x' \) thin delta_({ x_3 = 0 })$ is the divergence of the bounded field $a \( x' \) upright(bold(1))_({ x_3 > 0 }) partial_3$. Thus its negative part is another small divergence error in Equation (1.136). Smooth-side curvature errors are $O \( r^2 \)$. Weak Harnack and the low-truncation argument remain valid, and the flatness comparison uses the same Lipschitz metric estimates. This proves all the preceding alternatives for balls meeting the reflection plane as well.
  ======= 1.6.1.6 Iteration and the Morrey consequence. <iteration-and-the-morrey-consequence.>
  Keep $alpha_2 \, lambda_0$ from the affine improvement fixed. Choose $b_(*)$ sufficiently small that $C b_(*) \/ \( 1 - lambda_0^(alpha_2) \) < 1 \/ 4$, with $C$ the slope-increment constant in Equation (1.151). Then choose $r_(*) \, k_(*) \, delta_(*)$ in Equation (1.139) for this fixed $b_(*)$. Normalize initially by a fixed multiple of $L + Q$ and choose a uniformly small initial radius so that the metric and forcing errors satisfy all the preceding smallness conditions. If the first case of Equation (1.139) holds, rescale the ball by $r_(*)$ and the gradient bound by $k_(*)$. The normalized forcing decreases by $r_(*) \/ k_(*) < 1$. Repeating this step either continues forever or reaches the second case. In the former case the gradient decays with exponent $log k_(*) \/ log r_(*)$. In the latter case apply Equation (1.151) successively, retaining exponent $alpha_2$. At each affine step, rescale $z$ by dividing by the spatial scale, subtracting only an additive constant; this preserves its gradient bound. Its slope changes have total size at most $C b_(*) \/ \( 1 - lambda_0^(alpha_2) \)$, so the slopes remain in $\[ 1 \/ 4 \, 4 \]$. At the $j$th step the error size is $b_(*) lambda_0^(j alpha_2)$, while the normalized forcing and metric first-derivative errors have size $O \( lambda_0^j \)$. Since $alpha_2 < 1$, their ratio to the error size only decreases. This justifies every subsequent application of the improvement lemma. To combine the two branches, now choose
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.153)")
   $ 0 < alpha lt.eq min {alpha_2 \, frac(log k_(*), log r_(*))} \, #h(2em) alpha < 1 . $ <n3:existence:iteration-exponent>]
  At every center we have consequently obtained affine functions $L_(x \, r)$ satisfying
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.154)")
   $ sup_(B_r \( x \)) \| z - L_(x \, r) \| lt.eq C \( L + Q \) r^(1 + alpha) . $ <n3:existence:campanato-affine>]
  The slope differences at consecutive radii are bounded by $C \( L + Q \) r^alpha$; comparing the affine approximations on overlapping balls gives Equation (1.128). This also identifies their limiting slope with $D z$, since $z$ is smooth. The reflected version gives the estimate up to a boundary.
  Finally apply the $L^2$ version of Equation (1.132) to $z - L_(x \, 2 r)$ after rescaling. The affine error contributes $C \( L + Q \) r^(1 \/ 2 + alpha)$ to the Hessian $L^2$ norm; the bounded forcing and affine Christoffel term contribute $C \( L + Q \) r^(3 \/ 2)$. Squaring and using $alpha < 1$ proves Equation (1.129). ◻
]

===== 1.6.2 Bounded solutions with quadratic gradient growth <bounded-solutions-with-quadratic-gradient-growth>

The Hessian Morrey bound controls the forcing in the second equation through the following oscillation estimate. A positive measure in the source bound also accommodates reflected boundary terms.

#thm([Lemma 1.35.])[
  (Natural-growth oscillation estimate).  #emph[On a three-dimensional ball suppose $Z$ is bounded, $\| Z \| lt.eq M$, and is a smooth solution, or a reflected weak solution, of 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.155)")
 $ "div" \( a D Z + b \) = e . $ <n3:existence:natural-equation>]

 Assume $a$ is measurable and symmetric, $lambda I lt.eq a lt.eq Lambda I$, $\| b \| lt.eq B$, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.156)")
 $ \| e \| lt.eq C_0 \( 1 + \| D Z \|^2 \) thin upright(d) x + mu \, #h(2em) mu \( B_r \( x \) \) lt.eq C_mu r^(1 + beta) $ <n3:existence:natural-source>]

 for some $beta > 0$ and every sufficiently small ball in the patch. Then $Z$ has a local Hölder estimate whose constants depend only on the displayed bounds and the patch separation. The conclusion also holds up to a smooth boundary with bounded conormal flux or constant Dirichlet data, provided the corresponding reflected measure satisfies Equation (1.156).]
] <n3:existence:natural-growth>

#proof[
  Write $cal(L) = - "div" \( a D \)$ and, for $s in { - 1 \, 1 }$, set $V_s = e^(s k Z)$. The weak chain rule gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.157)")
   $ cal(L) V_s = "div" \( s k V_s b \) - s k V_s e - k^2 V_s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a D Z dot.op D Z + b dot.op D Z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:existence:exponential-identity>]
  Choose $k gt.eq max { 1 \, 2 C_0 \/ lambda }$ and use $B \| D Z \| lt.eq \( lambda \/ 2 \) \| D Z \|^2 + B^2 \/ \( 2 lambda \)$. Since $e^(- k M) lt.eq V_s lt.eq e^(k M)$, we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.158)")
   $ cal(L) V_s lt.eq "div" F_s + nu \, #h(2em) \| F_s \| lt.eq k e^(k M) B \, #h(2em) nu lt.eq C thin upright(d) x + C mu . $ <n3:existence:exponential-subsolution>]
  On a ball $B_R$ compactly contained in the patch, solve $cal(L) w_s = "div" F_s + nu$ with zero Dirichlet data. The bounded vector source has a solution of supremum norm at most $C R$: apply the $L^p$ source estimate, $p > 3$, whose scaled factor is $R^(1 - 3 \/ p)$, to $parallel F_s parallel_p lt.eq C R^(3 \/ p)$. For the positive measure source use the Green bound $0 lt.eq cal(G) \( x \, y \) lt.eq C \| x - y \|^(- 1)$ for uniformly elliptic measurable divergence coefficients #cite(label("LittmanStampacchiaWeinberger1963")) Section 7, Theorem (7.1) and Remark 2. To use that interior comparison uniformly for all $x \, y in B_R$, extend the coefficients elliptically to $B_(2 R)$. Domain monotonicity bounds the zero-Dirichlet Green function on $B_R$ by the one on $B_(2 R)$, where $B_R$ is a fixed compact interior region after rescaling. This gives the stated uniform bound. Integration over concentric annuli gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.159)")
   $ sup_(x in B_R) integral_(B_R) cal(G) \( x \, y \) thin upright(d) nu \( y \) lt.eq C integral_0^(2 R) frac(nu \( B_t \( x \) \), t^2) thin upright(d) t + C R^(- 1) nu \( B_(2 R) \( x \) \) lt.eq C \( R^2 + R^beta \) . $ <n3:existence:green-morrey-correction>]
  One may first approximate the measure by smooth positive densities. The uniform potential bound and the energy identity $integral a D w dot.op D w = integral w thin upright(d) nu$ give a bounded sequence in $W_0^(1 \, 2)$ and hence the required weak solution. Thus, with $gamma = min { 1 \, beta } > 0$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.160)")
   $ parallel w_s parallel_oo lt.eq epsilon_R \, #h(2em) epsilon_R = C R^gamma . $ <n3:existence:correction-size>]
  Let $M_R = sup_(B_R) Z$ and $m_R = inf_(B_R) Z$. The functions
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.161)")
   $ H_(+) = e^(k M_R) - e^(k Z) + w_(+) + epsilon_R \, #h(2em) H_(-) = e^(- k m_R) - e^(- k Z) + w_(-) + epsilon_R $ <n3:existence:exponential-deficits>]
  are nonnegative and satisfy $cal(L) H_plus.minus gt.eq 0$. Weak Harnack controls their $L^q$ means on $B_(R \/ 2)$ by their infima on $B_(R \/ 4)$. Each exponential deficit is comparable, with constants depending on $k M$, to $M_R - Z$ or $Z - m_R$, respectively. At least one of the sets
  $ { Z lt.eq \( M_R + m_R \) \/ 2 } ∩ B_(R \/ 2) \, #h(2em) { Z gt.eq \( M_R + m_R \) \/ 2 } ∩ B_(R \/ 2) $
  has at least half the measure of $B_(R \/ 2)$. Apply weak Harnack to the corresponding deficit. It follows that either the upper supremum decreases, or the lower infimum increases, by at least $c \( M_R - m_R \) - C epsilon_R$ on $B_(R \/ 4)$. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.162)")
   $ "osc"_(B_(R \/ 4)) Z lt.eq \( 1 - c \) "osc"_(B_R) Z + C R^gamma . $ <n3:existence:oscillation-contraction>]
  Iteration proves a Hölder estimate with any sufficiently small positive exponent below both $gamma$ and $- log \( 1 - c \) \/ log 4$.
  For the boundary estimate, flatten a face and put $S = "diag" \( 1 \, 1 \, - 1 \)$. An even extension of $Z$ uses $a_(-) = S a_(+) S$ and $b_(-) = S b_(+)$. Its flux has a plane jump whose density is twice the prescribed normal flux, hence bounded. An odd extension after subtracting a constant Dirichlet value uses $a_(-) = S a_(+) S$, $b_(-) = - S b_(+)$, and the sign-reflected bulk source. Its normal flux is continuous. The transformed metrics and volume densities are absorbed into the displayed coefficients. A bounded plane density has ball mass $O \( r^2 \)$, so it is admissible with exponent one. This proves the asserted boundary version. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 1.36.])[
  (Classical bounds for bounded-variable solutions).  #emph[Fix the deformation parameters and assume the bounds in Proposition 33. Every smooth solution in the homotopy of Proposition 23, with $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$, has uniform local classical estimates of every finite order, up to the inner and outer faces. At fixed $n$ the constants are independent of the homotopy parameter and of all sufficiently large outer radii $R$. The local patches can be chosen with a uniform positive radius throughout the end.]
] <n3:existence:classical-bounds>

#proof[
  Start with the bounds for $f \, Z \, t \, sigma$ from Proposition 33. At fixed parameters they bound $u \, l \, D$ above and away from zero, and give a uniform $chi_0 > 0$. After dividing the scalar trace equation by $l \/ sqrt(D)$, its right side is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.163)")
   $ G_f = sqrt(D) / l #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] F_s - "tr"_(A_chi) K #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:existence:normalized-f-source>]
  It is bounded throughout the homotopy, including its added collar terms. Lemma 34 therefore gives a uniform $C^(1 \, alpha)$ estimate for $f$ and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.164)")
   $ integral_(B_r) \| "Hess" f \|^2 lt.eq C r^(1 + 2 alpha) $ <n3:existence:f-morrey-application>]
  on the uniform patches, including boundary patches where $f$ is constant.
  After multiplying by the Riemannian volume density, the second equation has coordinate principal coefficients $a^(i j) = 4 sqrt(det g) thin u A_chi^(i j)$, a bounded flux vector $b$, and a source bounded in absolute value by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.165)")
   $ C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + \| D Z \|^2 + \| D^2 f \|^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <n3:existence:Z-quadratic-source>]
  Indeed $t$ is a smooth function of $x \, Z \, D f$ on the bounded variable range. Thus $D t$ is a bounded linear combination of $D Z \, D^2 f$ and fixed smooth terms; the expression $cal(T)$ is quadratic in these quantities. All other source terms have lower order. The same volume density is included in $b \, e$; it is smooth and bounded above and away from zero on the chosen patches, so it preserves these bounds and uniform ellipticity. The prescribed conormal flux at the inner boundary is bounded. At the outer face $Z = 0$. Apply Lemma 35, with $mu = C \| D^2 f \|^2 thin upright(d) x$ and the bounded reflected plane density. Equation (1.164) supplies its measure growth hypothesis. We obtain a uniform positive Hölder exponent for $Z$.
  Now $Z$ and $D f$ are Hölder, so all coefficients and the right side of the scalar $f$-equation are Hölder on their bounded variable range. Interior and constant-Dirichlet boundary Schauder estimates give $f in C^(2 \, theta)$ for a uniform $theta > 0$. Expand the $Z$-equation in nondivergence form. Its principal coefficients are uniformly elliptic and $C^(0 \, theta)$, while its right side is bounded by $C \( 1 + \| D Z \|^2 \)$. On an inner face $D f$ is normal. Therefore $A_chi$ has zero normal–tangential components there and normal eigenvalue $chi$. Its boundary equation solves for
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.166)")
   $ partial_nu Z = cal(H)_s \( x \, Z \, f \, D f \) \, $ <n3:existence:ordinary-normal-condition>]
  where $cal(H)_s$ is smooth on a fixed bounded variable range. Its derivatives with respect to $x$ and its displayed scalar arguments are bounded at fixed parameters. The outer condition remains constant Dirichlet. These are different, disjoint smooth boundary components; there is no change of boundary condition along an edge or a corner.
  It remains to absorb the quadratic gradient growth. Fix $p > 3$. The linear local $W^(2 \, p)$ estimate for the leading nondivergence operator with Equation (1.166) or outer Dirichlet data has the form, on fixed nested patches $U_x subset.double V_x$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.167)")
   $ parallel Z parallel_(W^(2 \, p) \( U_x \)) lt.eq C (1 + parallel D Z parallel_(L^(2 p) \( V_x \))^2 + parallel Z parallel_(W^(1 \, p) \( V_x \))) . $ <n3:existence:Wp-pre-absorption>]
  Here the boundary norm is controlled by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.168)")
   $ parallel cal(H)_s \( x \, Z \, f \, D f \) parallel_(W^(1 - 1 \/ p \, p) \( partial V_x \)) lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + parallel Z parallel_(W^(1 \, p) \( V_x^(+) \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $ <n3:existence:boundary-trace-composition>]
  using trace and composition after extending its smooth expression through the collar. The derivatives of $f \, D f$ entering this extension are already bounded. The leading matrix has a fixed Hölder modulus, so its small oscillation on sufficiently small patches permits the usual freezing and absorption in the linear estimate. The normal boundary operator is uniformly oblique and satisfies the complementing condition. These are the classical local elliptic boundary estimates #cite(label("AgmonDouglisNirenberg1959")) #cite(label("AgmonDouglisNirenberg1964")).
  Let $\[ Z \]_(C^(0 \, theta)) lt.eq H_0$. On balls or half-balls of radius $h$, the scaled Gagliardo–Nirenberg inequality, applied after subtracting a constant, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.169)")
   $ parallel D Z parallel_(L^(2 p) \( B_h \))^2 & lt.eq C "osc"_(B_h) Z thin parallel D^2 Z parallel_(L^p \( B_h \))\
 & quad + C h^(3 \/ p - 2) \( "osc"_(B_h) Z \)^2 . $ <n3:existence:small-oscillation-GN>]
