==== 4.5 The scalar system and exclusion of the first floor <four:sec:system>

Fix the prepared exterior and the geometric choices of Proposition 118. All contractions and derivatives in this section use its background metric $g$, unless another metric is indicated. In particular, $tau = "tr"_g K$ is unrestricted. The boundary normal $nu$ points into $Omega$. Let $Omega_R$ denote the truncation at a large coordinate sphere $S_R$, so its ordinary outward normal at its inner boundary $B$ is $- nu$.

The purpose of the system is to produce a metric $hat(g) = e^(2 t) \( g + l \( t \)^2 thin upright(d) f^2 \)$ with nonnegative scalar curvature and negative boundary mean curvature. The parameter $eta_n$ below makes the prescribed trace depend strictly increasingly on $f$. A separate penalty prevents $t$ from reaching $- epsilon.alt$. This section proves that prevention using a coarse height bound. The narrower height interval needed to use (4.66) will be proved subsequently.

===== 4.5.1 Variables and a scalar-curvature identity <variables-and-a-scalar-curvature-identity>

Fix $0 < epsilon.alt < 1$ and a smooth nonincreasing function $theta.alt : bb(R) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ equal to one on $\( - oo \, 0 \]$ and to zero on $\[ 1 \, oo \)$. For every integer $n gt.eq 4$ define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.71)")
 $ p \( t \) = n theta.alt \( n t \) \, #h(2em) l \( t \) = exp (integral_0^t p \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s) \,\
L_0 \( t \) = e^(2 t) l \( t \) \, #h(2em) ell = e^(- epsilon.alt n) \, #h(2em) eta_n = ell^(3 \/ 2) . $ <four:sys:parameters>]

Thus $0 lt.eq p lt.eq n$, $l \( t \) = e^(n t)$ for $t lt.eq 0$, and $l$ is constant with value between one and $e$ for $t gt.eq 1 \/ n$. In particular $l \( - epsilon.alt \) = ell$. The unknowns are real functions $f \, Z$. Define $t$ implicitly, and then all the remaining variables, by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.72)")
 $ sigma = \| "grad" f \| \, #h(2em) D = 1 + l \( t \)^2 sigma^2 \, #h(2em) d = D^(- 1) \, #h(2em) Z = t + 1 / 6 log D \, #h(2em) h = eta_n f \,\
w = frac(l "grad" f, sqrt(D)) \, #h(2em) a = \| w \| \, #h(2em) v = a^2 = 1 - d \, #h(2em) chi = frac(3 d, 3 + p v) \,\
A_d = "Id" - w ⊗ w \, #h(2em) A_chi = "Id" - frac(3 + p, 3 + p v) w ⊗ w \, #h(2em) u = L_0 sqrt(D) . $ <four:sys:variables>]

We identify vectors and covectors using $g$. A symmetric endomorphism also denotes the corresponding contravariant tensor, and $\| xi \|_A^2 = A \( xi \, xi \)$ for a covector $xi$. For fixed $thin upright(d) f$, the derivative of the defining expression for $Z$ with respect to $t$ is $1 + p v \/ 3 gt.eq 1$. That expression tends to $- oo$ and $+ oo$ at the two ends of the real line. It therefore defines a unique smooth $t = t \( x' \, Z \, thin upright(d) f \)$, with $x'$ the base point, for all inputs, including inputs below the proposed floor. No gradient bound is needed for this definition. The eigenvalues of $A_d$ and $A_chi$ on the line spanned by $"grad" f$ are $d$ and $chi$; their other three eigenvalues are one. Consequently

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.73)")
 $ 0 < frac(3 d, 3 + n) lt.eq chi lt.eq d lt.eq 1 . $ <four:sys:eigenvalues>]

Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.74)")
 $ macron(g) = g + l^2 thin upright(d) f^2 \, #h(2em) hat(g) = e^(2 t) macron(g) \, #h(2em) H^f = l / sqrt(D) "Hess"_g f \, #h(2em) S_f = K + H^f \,\
F = "tr"_(A_chi) S_f \, #h(2em) V = u A_chi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 3 "grad" Z + K \( w \, dot.op \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:sys:metric-and-trace>]

Where $sigma > 0$, let $e = "grad" f \/ sigma$. Decompose $S_f$ into its transverse block $T = S_f \|_(e^perp)$, mixed block $M = S_f \( e \, dot.op \) \|_(e^perp)$, and axial entry $j = S_f \( e \, e \)$; write $T^0 = T - 1 / 3 \( "tr" T \) g \|_(e^perp)$. Thus $"tr" T = F - chi j$. Also write $x = e \( t \)$ and $y = \( thin upright(d) t \) \|_(e^perp)$. The symbol $M$ in this block notation is a covector, not the original manifold.

#thm([Proposition 4.23.])[
  (Scalar identity).  #emph[For arbitrary smooth $f \, Z$ and arbitrary symmetric $K$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.75)")
 $ 1 / 2 e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) = mu + J \( w \) + cal(T) + w \( F \) - F tau - u^(- 1) "div"_g V \, $ <four:eq:scalar-identity>]

 where the smooth scalar $cal(T)$ is given, on ${ thin upright(d) f eq.not 0 }$, by 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.76)")
 $ cal(T) =  & 1 / 2 \| T^0 \|^2 + \| M + \( p + 1 \) a y \|^2 - v \| y \|^2 + 3 \( p + 1 \) \( \| y \|^2 + d x^2 \)\
 & - 1 / 3 \( F - chi j \)^2 + chi j^2 - chi F j + F^2 + 2 \( p + 1 \) chi j a x . $ <four:eq:quadratic-form>]

 The expression extends smoothly across ${ thin upright(d) f = 0 }$, where any unit axis may be used in the block expression.]
] <four:sys:scalar-proposition>

#proof[
  We derive the identity in a stationary Lorentzian metric, keeping its normal second form distinct from the prescribed tensor $K$. On the product with coordinate $s'$ use
  $ upright(bold(g)) = - l^2 \( thin upright(d) s' - thin upright(d) f \)^2 + macron(g) . $
  Its constant-$s'$ slices have metric $g$. Their lapse, shift, and normal second form, with the convention that the second form is half the normal metric rate, are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.77)")
   $ U = l sqrt(D) \, #h(2em) beta = l^2 "grad" f = U w \,\
k = - frac("sym" nabla beta, U) = - H^f - p \( thin upright(d) t ⊗ w + w ⊗ thin upright(d) t \) . $ <four:sys:stationary-data>]
  Here $"sym"$ includes the factor $1 \/ 2$. Put $theta = "tr"_g k$, and let $N_0$ be the future unit normal. The contracted Gauss and normal expansion identities are
  $ "Scal"_g = upright(bold(R)) + 2 upright(bold(R i c)) \( N_0 \, N_0 \) + \| k \|^2 - theta^2 \, #h(2em) upright(bold(R i c)) \( N_0 \, N_0 \) = - N_0 theta - \| k \|^2 + U^(- 1) Delta_g U . $
  Stationarity gives $U N_0 theta = - beta \( theta \)$ and $"div" beta = - U theta$. If
  $ sans(B) \( A \, B \) = A : B - \( "tr"_g A \) \( "tr"_g B \) \, #h(2em) P_A = A - \( "tr"_g A \) g \, $
  the preceding two formulas consequently yield
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.78)")
   $ U upright(bold(R)) = U #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Scal"_g + sans(B) \( k \, k \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] - 2 "div" \( "grad" U + theta beta \) . $ <four:sys:stationary-scalar>]
  In the static coordinate $s' - f$ one instead has
  $ upright(bold(R)) = "Scal"_(macron(g)) - 2 l^(- 1) Delta_(macron(g)) l \, #h(2em) Delta_(macron(g)) phi.alt = l / U "div" (U / l A_d "grad" phi.alt) . $
  Direct differentiation of $U = l sqrt(D)$ gives
  $ "grad" U - U / l A_d "grad" l = - k \( beta \, dot.op \) . $
  Moreover, using $J = "div" P_K$, symmetry of $P_K$, and $"sym" nabla beta = - U k$ gives
  $ "div" \( P_K beta \) = U { J \( w \) - sans(B) \( K \, k \) } . $
  Substitute these identities into (4.78), use $mu = \( "Scal"_g - sans(B) \( K \, K \) \) \/ 2$, and put $Q = K - k$. The result is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.79)")
   $ 1 / 2 "Scal"_(macron(g)) = mu + J \( w \) + 1 / 2 sans(B) \( Q \, Q \) - U^(- 1) "div" \( U P_Q w \) . $ <four:sys:bar-scalar>]
  For example, the divergence arising directly from (4.78) is $U^(- 1) "div" \( U P_k w \)$; adding $J \( w \)$ combines it with $- U^(- 1) "div" \( U P_K w \)$ to give the last term in (4.79). This also fixes its sign.
  The four-dimensional conformal formula is
  $ 1 / 2 e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) = 1 / 2 "Scal"_(macron(g)) - 3 Delta_(macron(g)) t - 3 \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 . $
  Since $Delta_(macron(g)) t = U^(- 1) "div" \( U A_d "grad" t \) - p \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2$ and $u = e^(2 t) U$, changing the divergence weight from $U$ to $u$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.80)")
   $ 1 / 2 e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) =  & mu + J \( w \) + 1 / 2 sans(B) \( Q \, Q \) + 2 P_Q \( w \, "grad" t \) + 3 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2\
 & - u^(- 1) "div" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] u \( P_Q w + 3 A_d "grad" t \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:sys:pretrace-identity>]
  The following differential identities follow from (4.72):
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.81)")
   $ 3 thin upright(d) Z & = \( 3 + p v \) thin upright(d) t + H^f \( w \, dot.op \) \,\
"div" w & = "tr"_(A_d) H^f + p d thin w \( t \) \,\
thin upright(d) log u & = \( p + 2 + p v \) thin upright(d) t + H^f \( w \, dot.op \) . $ <four:sys:differential-identities>]
  They imply
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.82)")
   $ V / u & = P_Q w + 3 A_d "grad" t + F w \,\
u^(- 1) "div" \( u w \) & = "tr"_g H^f + 2 \( p + 1 \) w \( t \)\
 & = F + \( 1 - chi \) j - tau + 2 \( p + 1 \) w \( t \) . $ <four:sys:trace-divergence>]
  Adding $u F w$ inside the divergence in (4.80) therefore leaves $w \( F \) + F u^(- 1) "div" \( u w \)$. Its trace contribution is exactly $- F tau$, while the remaining quadratic expression is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.83)")
   $ cal(T) =  & 1 / 2 sans(B) \( Q \, Q \) + 2 P_Q \( w \, "grad" t \) + 3 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2\
 & + F (F + frac(3 + p, 3 + p v) S_f \( w \, w \) + 2 \( p + 1 \) w \( t \)) . $ <four:sys:invariant-quadratic>]
  This is smooth even at $w = 0$, since $\( 1 - chi \) j = \( 3 + p \) S_f \( w \, w \) \/ \( 3 + p v \)$. For the block expansion, $Q$ has blocks $T$, $M + p a y$, $j + 2 p a x$, and $"tr" T = F - chi j$. Expanding (4.83) gives (4.76). At $w = 0$ it reduces to
  $ cal(T) = 1 / 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| S_f \|^2 + \( "tr"_g S_f \)^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + 3 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|^2 \, $
  which is independent of the auxiliary axis. This proves all assertions. ◻
]

===== 4.5.2 Coercivity and the boundary identity <coercivity-and-the-boundary-identity>

Throughout the proof, $Pi_n$ denotes a bound of the form $C \( 1 + n \)^N$ for a finite nonnegative integer $N$. Its coefficient $C$ and exponent $N$ may depend on the fixed prepared data, $epsilon.alt$, the chosen collars and smooth switches, and any explicitly fixed small absorption constant. They never depend on $R$, a homotopy parameter, or a solution. Different occurrences may denote larger such bounds. Constants denoted by $C \( n \)$ in later fixed-$n$ analytic estimates have no polynomial restriction and will not enter a limit requiring polynomial control.

#thm([Lemma 4.24.])[
  (Polynomial coercivity).  #emph[For all inputs in (4.72), 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.84)")
 $ \| T \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + d j^2 + F^2 + \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)^2 lt.eq Pi_n cal(T) . $ <four:eq:coercivity>]

 At a point where $thin upright(d) t = 0$, there are absolute constants $c \, C > 0$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.85)")
 $ c #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| T^0 \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + chi j^2 + F^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] & lt.eq cal(T)\
 & lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| T^0 \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + chi j^2 + F^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:sys:stationary-coercivity>]

]
] <four:sys:coercivity-lemma>

#proof[
  Completing squares in (4.76) gives the exact identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.86)")
   $ cal(T) =  & 1 / 2 \| T^0 \|^2 + \| M + \( p + 1 \) a y \|^2 + #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 3 \( p + 1 \) - v #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \| y \|^2\
 & + 3 \( p + 1 \) d (x + frac(chi a j, 3 d))^2 + 2 / 3 (F - frac(chi j, 4))^2 + frac(d \( 48 - 3 d \), 8 \( 3 + p v \)^2) j^2 . $ <four:sys:exact-squares>]
  For the final coefficient, the identity used in this completion is
  $ chi - 3 / 8 chi^2 - frac(\( p + 1 \) chi^2 v, 3 d) = frac(d \( 48 - 3 d \), 8 \( 3 + p v \)^2) . $
  The coefficient of $\| y \|^2$ is at least two, and the last coefficient is at least $45 d \/ \[ 8 \( 3 + n \)^2 \]$. Thus $d j^2 lt.eq C \( 3 + n \)^2 cal(T)$. The inequalities $chi^2 lt.eq d^2 lt.eq d$ then recover $F^2$ from its shifted square and $d x^2$ from its shifted square, at polynomial cost. Recovering $M$ costs at most $C \( 1 + n \)^2 \| y \|^2$, and recovering $T$ uses $"tr" T = F - chi j$. This proves (4.84); these observations, for instance, allow one common bound $C \( 1 + n \)^2$ in that inequality.
  When $thin upright(d) t = 0$, complete only the $\( F \, j \)$ terms to obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.87)")
   $ cal(T) = 1 / 2 \| T^0 \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + 2 / 3 (F - frac(chi j, 4))^2 + chi (1 - frac(3 chi, 8)) j^2 . $ <four:sys:stationary-squares>]
  Since $5 \/ 8 lt.eq 1 - 3 chi \/ 8 lt.eq 1$ and $chi^2 lt.eq chi$, this gives (4.85) with absolute constants. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.25.])[
  (Boundary conversion).  #emph[On any smooth face on which $f$ is constant, let $P_B = "tr"_B K$, $w_nu = chevron.l w \, nu chevron.r$, and let $H$ be its background mean curvature for the normal into $Omega$. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.88)")
 $ V_nu / u = d \( H + 3 partial_nu t \) - H + w_nu \( F - P_B \) \, #h(2em) hat(H) = e^(- t) sqrt(d) thin \( H + 3 partial_nu t \) . $ <four:eq:boundary-identity>]

]
] <four:sys:boundary-lemma>

#proof[
  With $upright("II")_B \( X \, Y \) = g \( nabla_X nu \, Y \)$ for tangent vectors, one has tangentially $"Hess" f = \( partial_nu f \) upright("II")_B$, so $"tr" T = P_B + w_nu H$ and $chi j = F - P_B - w_nu H$. Use this relation in the first identity of (4.82); as $w$ is normal on the face, it gives
  $ V_nu \/ u = 3 d partial_nu t + w_nu \( F - P_B - w_nu H \) \, $
  which is the first assertion because $w_nu^2 = 1 - d$. The induced metric of $macron(g)$ on the face equals $g \|_B$, and its normal in base coordinates is $sqrt(d) thin nu$. In the tangential first variation, $l^2 thin upright(d) f^2$ contributes zero since the tangential derivatives of $f$ vanish there. Hence $H_(macron(g)) = sqrt(d) thin H$. Applying the boundary conformal formula with $nu_(macron(g)) = sqrt(d) thin nu$ proves the second assertion. Both assertions also hold when $thin upright(d) f = 0$. ◻
]

===== 4.5.3 The equations and their four-stage homotopy <the-equations-and-their-four-stage-homotopy>

Extend the end radius to a positive smooth function $r gt.eq 1$ on $Omega$ and set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.89)")
 $ rho_0 = r^(- 4 - delta_1) \, #h(2em) rho_1 = r^(- 3) \, #h(2em) m_0 \( t \) = theta.alt #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] n \( t + epsilon.alt \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) cal(P) = C_n \( rho_0 + v rho_1 \) \,\
Xi = delta_0 cal(T) + rho + delta_0 eta_n a sigma - m_0 \( t \) cal(P) . $ <four:sys:source>]

The constant $delta_0 > 0$ and the polynomial $C_n$ will be fixed in Proposition 125. The smooth positive weight $rho$ comes from (4.66); in particular $rho lt.eq C rho_0$. The combination $a sigma = l \| thin upright(d) f \|^2 \/ sqrt(D)$ is smooth also at $thin upright(d) f = 0$, so all terms of $Xi$ are smooth functions of the indicated jets. Choose a constant $N_B > 1 + sup_B \| H \|$, and put

$ cal(B) = - H + w_nu \( F - P_B \) - N_B d . $

The desired system is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.90)")
 $ cases(delim: "{", F = h + C \, & upright("in ") Omega_R \,, "div" V = u Xi \, & upright("in ") Omega_R \,, h = b \, quad V_nu \/ u = cal(B) \, & upright("on ") B \,, f = Z = 0 \, & upright("on ") S_R .) $ <four:eq:system>]

In every boundary flux expression the symbol $F$ is evaluated using the prescribed trace right side. Thus the boundary condition contains no second derivative of $f$. For a solution of (4.90), (4.88) immediately gives $hat(H) = - e^(- t) sqrt(d) thin N_B < 0$.

We specify the entire homotopy because its boundary modifications and strictly increasing height term will be used in the a priori estimates. The stages run from the desired system to the terminal problem with solution $f = Z = 0$; Section 8 transfers existence back along this homotopy. Take a smooth $j_0 : bb(R) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ which is zero for $t lt.eq 0$ and one for $t gt.eq 1$. For $0 lt.eq lambda lt.eq 1$ put

$ V_lambda = u A_chi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 3 "grad" Z + lambda K \( w \, dot.op \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

In stages 1–3 use

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.91)")
 $ cases(delim: "{", "div" V_lambda = u Xi & upright("in ") Omega_R \,, \( V_lambda \)_nu \/ u = \[ 1 - \( 1 - lambda \) j_0 \( t \) \] \[ cal(B) - \( 1 - lambda \) \( A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \) \)_nu \] & upright("on ") B .) $ <four:eq:homotopy-flux>]

Keep the outer data $f = Z = 0$ throughout.

Here are explicit smooth choices of the auxiliary trace terms. Fix $0 < 4 xi_1 < b_(+) - b_(-)$ and smooth height switches $q_(-) \, q_(+)$ such that

$ q_(-) \( z \) = 1 & \( z lt.eq b_(-) + xi_1 \) \, & q_(-) \( z \) = 0 quad \( z gt.eq b_(-) + 2 xi_1 \) \,\
q_(+) \( z \) = 0 & \( z lt.eq b_(+) - 2 xi_1 \) \, & q_(+) \( z \) = 1 quad \( z gt.eq b_(+) - xi_1 \) \, $

where $q_(-)$ is nonincreasing and $q_(+)$ is nondecreasing. Choose a smooth direction switch $q_d$ with values in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$, equal to one on $\( - oo \, - 3 \/ 4 \]$ and zero on $\[ - 1 \/ 2 \, oo \)$. On each collar write $N = "grad" s \/ \| thin upright(d) s \|$, so $N = nu$ at the face, and choose a smooth cutoff $omega_B$ supported there and equal to one near the face. The collars are disjoint. With fixed constants

$ A_0 > 2 sup_B \| H \| + 1 \, #h(2em) A_1 > sup_B \| H \| + 1 \, $

define, extending by zero outside the collars,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.92)")
 $ D_0 \( x' \, w \, z \) & = - A_0 omega_B \( x' \) q_d \( w dot.op N \) q_(-) \( z \) &  & upright("on black collars") \,\
D_0 \( x' \, w \, z \) & = A_0 omega_B \( x' \) q_d \( - w dot.op N \) q_(+) \( z \) &  & upright("on white collars") \,\
D_1 \( x' \, w \) & = A_1 omega_B \( x' \) thin w dot.op N &  & upright("on every collar") . $ <four:sys:switches>]

Both terms vanish at $w = 0$, are bounded for $\| w \| lt.eq 1$, and have bounded derivatives of every order on bounded height ranges. These bounds are independent of $n$. In addition $partial_z D_0 gt.eq 0$.

The successive trace prescriptions and inner Dirichlet data are as follows; the indicated parameter moves in the displayed direction.

+ Decrease $lambda$ from one to zero, keeping $F = h + C$ and $h \|_B = b$.
+ Keep $lambda = 0$, increase $alpha_0$ from zero to one, and use $F = h + C + alpha_0 D_0 \( x' \, w \, h \)$ and $h \|_B = b$.
+ Keep $lambda = 0$, increase $zeta$ from zero to one, and use
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.93)")
   $ F =  & h + \( 1 - zeta \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] C + D_0 \( x' \, w \, h + zeta b \( x' \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]]\
 & + zeta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] "tr"_(A_chi) K + D_1 \( x' \, w \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] \, #h(2em) h \|_B = \( 1 - zeta \) b . $ <four:sys:trace-prescriptions>]
+ Retain the terminal prescription $F = h + "tr"_(A_chi) K + D_1$ and $h \|_B = 0$. Keep $lambda = 0$ and multiply both right sides of (4.91) by a common parameter $gamma$, decreasing from one to zero.

The endpoints agree, so this is a continuous homotopy. All trace right sides have derivative at least one with respect to $h$, with $\( x' \, w \, t \)$ fixed. This is also the relevant derivative when comparing scalar solutions at a shared gradient and shared $Z$, since $t$ is independent of the value of $f$.

Two consequences of these choices will be useful. First, at a stage 3 face, evaluate the prescribed right side at $h = \( 1 - zeta \) b$ and at the limiting values $w = plus.minus nu$, $d = chi = 0$. It obeys

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.94)")
 $ F \( nu \) gt.eq P_B + H \, #h(2em) F \( - nu \) lt.eq P_B - H . $ <four:sys:direction-inequalities>]

Indeed, on a black face $b + C = P_B + H$, $D_0 \( nu \, b \) = 0$, and $D_0 \( - nu \, b \) = - A_0$; on a white face $b + C = P_B - H$, $D_0 \( - nu \, b \) = 0$, and $D_0 \( nu \, b \) = A_0$. The terminal expressions are $P_B plus.minus A_1$. Each inequality follows for both endpoints from the choices of $A_0 \, A_1$, and hence for their convex combination.

Second, throughout stage 4 the scalar solution is $f = 0$, for every trial $Z$. Its equation is $"tr"_(A_chi) H^f = eta_n f + D_1$, with zero boundary values. At a positive interior maximum $w = 0$, $D_1 = 0$, $A_chi = "Id"$, and $l Delta f = eta_n f > 0$, a contradiction; a negative minimum is excluded in the same way. Consequently in this stage

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.95)")
 $ f = 0 \, quad t = Z \, quad cal(T) = cal(T)_K + 3 \( p + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) t \|^2 \, #h(2em) cal(T)_K = 1 / 2 \( \| K \|^2 + tau^2 \) \, $ <four:sys:terminal-system>]

and its two remaining equations are exactly

$ "div" \( 3 u "grad" t \) = gamma u Xi \, #h(2em) 3 partial_nu t = gamma \[ 1 - j_0 \( t \) \] \( - H - N_B \) . $

===== 4.5.4 Coarse heights and an outer boundary estimate <coarse-heights-and-an-outer-boundary-estimate>

#thm([Lemma 4.26.])[
  (Height bound before the floor).  #emph[There exist fixed $r_0$ and $C_(*) > 0$, independent of $n$, $R$, and all homotopy parameters, with the following property. Every smooth solution of the trace equation in any stage, with its prescribed Dirichlet data, on $Omega_R$ for $R gt.eq 2 r_0$ satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.96)")
 $ \| h \| + \| F \| lt.eq C_(*) \, #h(2em) \| h \| lt.eq C_(*) \( r^(- 3 \/ 2) - R^(- 3 \/ 2) \) quad \( r_0 lt.eq r lt.eq R \) . $ <four:sys:coarse-height>]

 Here $Z$ may be arbitrary; the divergence equation and the condition $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$ are not assumed. In particular, with the prescribed outer value $Z = 0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.97)")
 $ \| "grad" f \| lt.eq C_(*) eta_n^(- 1) R^(- 5 \/ 2) quad upright("on ") S_R . $ <four:sys:outer-gradient>]

 For every fixed $n$ there is $R_min \( n \) gt.eq 2 r_0$, chosen to tend to infinity with $n$, such that every such solution for $R gt.eq R_min \( n \)$ has 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.98)")
 $ t > - epsilon.alt quad upright("on ") S_R . $ <four:sys:outer-floor>]

]
] <four:lem:coarse-height>

#proof[
  At an interior extremum of $h$, $w = 0$ and both auxiliary terms vanish. The trace equation becomes
  $ l Delta f = h + C - tau quad upright("in stages 1 and 2") \, #h(2em) l Delta f = h + \( 1 - zeta \) \( C - tau \) quad upright("in stage 3") . $
  Since $l > 0$, comparison at a maximum and a minimum bounds $\| h \|$ by $max \( sup_B \| b \| \, parallel C - tau parallel_oo \)$. Stage 4 has $h = 0$. All prescribed trace right sides then bound $\| F \|$ by a fixed constant, because $D_0 \, D_1$ are bounded and $parallel A_chi parallel lt.eq 1$.
  Choose $r_0$ beyond the supports of $K \, C \, b \, D_0 \, D_1$. At a comparison with a radial function, the axis of $A_chi$ is $"grad"_g r \/ \| thin upright(d) r \|_g$. Uniformly for $0 < chi lt.eq 1$, the prepared end expansion gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.99)")
   $ "tr"_(A_chi) "Hess"_g r^(- 3 \/ 2) = 3 / 2 (- 3 + 5 / 2 chi) r^(- 7 \/ 2) + O \( r^(- 11 \/ 2) \) < 0 $ <four:sys:radial-trace>]
  after increasing $r_0$. The leading coefficient is at most $- 3 \/ 4$, so the choice is independent of $n$. Take $C_(*)$ large enough that $C_(*) \( r_0^(- 3 \/ 2) - R^(- 3 \/ 2) \)$ dominates the global height bound for every $R gt.eq 2 r_0$. On this annulus the scalar equation is
  $ frac(l, eta_n sqrt(D)) "tr"_(A_chi) "Hess"_g h = h . $
  The functions $plus.minus C_(*) \( r^(- 3 \/ 2) - R^(- 3 \/ 2) \)$ are respectively upper and lower barriers by (4.99). At a putative first comparison point the gradients coincide, so the same positive trace matrix and the same positive prefactor occur in both expressions. The Hessian order and the proper height sign give a contradiction. This proves the end estimate. Differentiating the comparison at the outer boundary, where $h = 0$ tangentially, gives (4.97), absorbing the uniform end normal comparison into $C_(*)$.
  Finally let $G \( t \) = t + 1 / 6 log \( 1 + l \( t \)^2 sigma^2 \)$ at an outer point. It is strictly increasing and $G \( t \) = Z = 0$. Thus $t > - epsilon.alt$ if
  $ G \( - epsilon.alt \) = - epsilon.alt + 1 / 6 log \( 1 + ell^2 sigma^2 \) < 0 . $
  By (4.97), it suffices to choose $R_min \( n \)$ so large that $C_(*)^2 ell^2 eta_n^(- 2) R_min \( n \)^(- 5) < e^(6 epsilon.alt) - 1$. This uses only fixed-$n$ enlargement of the outer radius. ◻
]

===== 4.5.5 The stationary comparison at a minimum of $t$ <the-stationary-comparison-at-a-minimum-of-t>

The scalar identity is useful at a floor contact because a second, Riemannian Gauss calculation gives an upper bound for the same scalar curvature. Define, for a symmetric tensor $A$ and a chosen unit axis $e$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.100)")
 $ cal(S) \( A \) = 1 / 3 \( "tr"_perp A \)^2 - 1 / 2 \| A_perp^0 \|^2 + d A_(e e) "tr"_perp A - d \| A_(e perp) \|^2 . $ <four:sys:gauss-quadratic>]

#thm([Lemma 4.27.])[
  (Stationary Gauss comparison).  #emph[At an interior local minimum of $t$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.101)")
 $ 1 / 2 e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) lt.eq 1 / 2 "Scal"_g - v "Ric"_g \( e \, e \) + cal(S) \( H^f \) . $ <four:sys:gauss-bound>]

 At every point where $thin upright(d) t = 0$ there are an absolute $c_2 > 0$ and a polynomial bound $Pi_n$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.102)")
 $ cal(T) - cal(S) \( H^f \) gt.eq c_2 cal(T) - Pi_n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] v F^2 + \| K \|^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:sys:floor-coercivity>]

 These conclusions extend to $thin upright(d) f = 0$ with any unit axis.]
] <four:sys:gauss-lemma>

#proof[
  Use the Riemannian warped product $g + l^2 \( thin upright(d) b' \)^2$ and its graph $b' = f$. At $thin upright(d) t = 0$ one has $thin upright(d) l = 0$, so the graph second form is $H^f$, up to its immaterial overall sign. Its inverse metric is $A_d$. The contracted second-form contribution is
  $ 1 / 2 { \( "tr"_(A_d) H^f \)^2 - \| H^f \|_(A_d ⊗ A_d)^2 } = cal(S) \( H^f \) \, $
  as is seen by writing $A_d = "diag" \( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \, d \)$. The ambient scalar curvature and Ricci blocks are
  $ "Scal"_g - 2 Delta l \/ l \, #h(2em) "Ric"_g - "Hess" l \/ l \, #h(2em) - Delta l \/ l \, $
  for the scalar, horizontal block, and unit vertical direction; the mixed block is zero. The graph unit normal has horizontal part $- w$ and vertical component $sqrt(d)$. Therefore the ambient scalar minus twice its normal Ricci, divided by two, is
  $ 1 / 2 "Scal"_g - v "Ric"_g \( e \, e \) - v "tr"_perp \( "Hess" l \/ l \) . $
  At a stationary point $"Hess" l \/ l = p "Hess" t$, and the conformal term is $- 3 "tr"_(A_d) "Hess" t$. Thus we have, more precisely, the equality
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.103)")
   $ 1 / 2 e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) =  & 1 / 2 "Scal"_g - v "Ric"_g \( e \, e \) + cal(S) \( H^f \)\
 & - \( 3 + p v \) "tr"_perp "Hess"_g t - 3 d "Hess"_g t \( e \, e \) . $ <four:sys:stationary-gauss-exact>]
  At a minimum the last two terms are nonpositive, proving (4.101).
  For (4.102), first subtract $cal(S) \( S_f \)$. An expansion using $"tr" T = F - chi j$ gives exactly
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.104)")
   $ cal(T) - cal(S) \( S_f \) =  & \| T^0 \|^2 + \( 1 + d \) \| M \|^2 + chi \( 1 + d - 2 chi \/ 3 \) j^2\
 & + \( chi \/ 3 - d \) F j + F^2 \/ 3 . $ <four:sys:stationary-difference>]
  At $d = chi = 1$ the $\( F \, j \)$ quadratic is $F^2 \/ 3 - 2 F j \/ 3 + 4 j^2 \/ 3$, whose matrix has determinant $1 \/ 3$ and positive trace. It is uniformly positive in a fixed neighborhood of this point. Since
  $ 1 - d = v \, #h(2em) 1 - chi = frac(\( 3 + p \) v, 3 + p v) \, $
  there is an absolute $kappa_0 > 0$ such that this neighborhood contains all inputs with $\( 1 + p \) v lt.eq kappa_0$. In that region (4.104) controls an absolute fraction of $\| T^0 \|^2 + \| M \|^2 + chi j^2 + F^2$.
  In the remaining region, use $chi \( 1 + d - 2 chi \/ 3 \) gt.eq chi$ and $\| chi \/ 3 - d \| lt.eq d$ to absorb the mixed term into, for example, $chi j^2 \/ 4$ and a multiple of $\( d^2 \/ chi \) F^2$. The only ratio required is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.105)")
   $ d^2 / chi = frac(d \( 3 + p v \), 3) lt.eq frac(3 + n, 3) \, #h(2em) v > frac(kappa_0, 1 + p) gt.eq frac(kappa_0, 1 + n) . $ <four:sys:polynomial-ratios>]
  Keeping also a fixed fraction of $F^2$ at the cost of another multiple of $F^2$, this proves a lower bound by an absolute fraction of the stationary norm minus $C \( 1 + n \)^2 v F^2$. Equation (4.85) converts the norm to $cal(T)$.
  It remains to replace $S_f$ by $S_f - K$. Polarizing (4.100) bounds the difference by
  $ C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| T \| + d \| j \| + d \| M \| #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \| K \| + C \| K \|^2 . $
  The stationary norm controls $\| T \|$ and $\| M \|$, while $d \| j \| lt.eq \( d^2 \/ chi \)^(1 \/ 2) \( chi j^2 \)^(1 \/ 2)$. By (4.105) and Young’s inequality, for every fixed $alpha > 0$ this is at most $alpha cal(T) + C_alpha \( 1 + n \) \| K \|^2$. Choose $alpha$ to retain half the already obtained positive coefficient of $cal(T)$. This proves (4.102) with absolute $c_2 > 0$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.28.])[
  (Errors from the trace switches and drift).  #emph[Let $cal(K)$ be a fixed compact enlargement of the supports of $K \, C \, b \, D_0 \, D_1$. For every fixed $delta_2 > 0$, every smooth trace solution in stages 1–3 satisfies, at a point where $thin upright(d) t = 0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.106)")
 $ w \( F \) & gt.eq eta_n a sigma - delta_2 cal(T) - Pi_n upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) \,\
u^(- 1) \| "div" \( u A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] & lt.eq delta_2 cal(T) + Pi_n upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) . $ <four:sys:derivative-errors>]

 The polynomial may depend on $delta_2$ and the fixed geometric choices, but no negative power of $ell$ or $eta_n$ is required.]
] <four:sys:stationary-errors>

#proof[
  At $thin upright(d) t = 0$, differentiation gives
  $ nabla_i w = A_d H_(i dot.op)^f \, #h(2em) thin upright(d) log u = H^f \( w \, dot.op \) \, #h(2em) thin upright(d) p = p' \( t \) thin upright(d) t = 0 . $
  These formulas and (4.85) show
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.107)")
   $ \| nabla w \| + \| thin upright(d) log u \|_(A_chi) + \| nabla A_chi \| lt.eq Pi_n \( sqrt(cal(T)) + \| K \| \) . $ <four:sys:stationary-derivatives>]
  For clarity, the axial entry in $nabla w$ is $d H_(e e)^f$, and its control uses $d^2 \/ chi lt.eq \( 3 + n \) \/ 3$. In $\| thin upright(d) log u \|_(A_chi)$ the axial entry has the factor $sqrt(chi)$. Differentiating $A_chi = "Id" - \( 3 + p \) w ⊗ w \/ \( 3 + p v \)$ uses only the preceding $nabla w$ and coefficients bounded by powers of $1 + n$, since $3 + p v gt.eq 3$. This proves (4.107) without dividing by $l$ or $eta_n$.
  Write the relevant prescribed trace as $Phi \( x' \, w \, t \, h \)$. Its height derivative is at least one, so the chain rule contains the favorable term
  $ Phi_h w \( h \) gt.eq w \( h \) = eta_n a sigma . $
  All other terms are supported in $cal(K)$. Lemma 122 bounds their height arguments in a fixed range; derivatives of $D_0 \, D_1$ and $b$ are consequently bounded. Derivatives of $"tr"_(A_chi) K$ use $nabla K$ and (4.107). The $t$ derivative has zero contribution at the point in question. Thus the remaining absolute error is bounded by $Pi_n \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) upright(bold(1))_(cal(K))$. Young’s inequality proves the first assertion.
  Expanding the divergence in the second assertion differentiates $A_chi$, $K$, and $w$, and adds $chevron.l K \( w \, dot.op \) \, thin upright(d) log u chevron.r_(A_chi)$. The latter is bounded by $C \| K \| thin \| thin upright(d) log u \|_(A_chi)$. Equation (4.107), compact support, and Young’s inequality give the second assertion with the same allowed dependencies. ◻
]

===== 4.5.6 Exclusion of first contact with the floor <exclusion-of-first-contact-with-the-floor>

#thm([Proposition 4.29.])[
  (First-floor exclusion).  #emph[There are a fixed $delta_0 > 0$ and a positive polynomial $C_n$ in (4.89) such that, for every $n gt.eq 4$ and every $R gt.eq R_min \( n \)$, no smooth solution at any stage of the homotopy satisfies 
$ min_(overline(Omega)_R) t = - epsilon.alt . $

 The choices depend only on the prepared data, $epsilon.alt$, and the fixed switches and collars. They do not use a height bound sharper than Lemma 122.]
] <four:prop:floor-exclusion>

#proof[
  Suppose first that a floor minimum is interior and the parameter is in one of stages 1–3. At that point $thin upright(d) t = 0$ and $"Hess" t gt.eq 0$. Subtract (4.101) from (4.75) and use $V_lambda = V - \( 1 - lambda \) u A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \)$. This gives
  $ u^(- 1) "div" V_lambda gt.eq  & mu - 1 / 2 "Scal"_g + J \( w \) + v "Ric"_g \( e \, e \) + cal(T) - cal(S) \( H^f \) + w \( F \) - F tau\
 & - \( 1 - lambda \) u^(- 1) "div" \( u A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \) \) . $
  Choose $delta_2 > 0$ in Lemma 124 so small that the two losses $delta_2 cal(T)$ leave at least $c_2 cal(T) \/ 2$ in (4.102). The remaining tensor expressions $mu - "Scal"_g \/ 2 = \( tau^2 - \| K \|^2 \) \/ 2$, $J$, and $F tau$ are compactly supported and bounded by fixed constants, using Lemma 122. On the complement of $cal(K)$, $F = h$ and $F^2 lt.eq C r^(- 3)$; also $\| "Ric"_g \| lt.eq C r^(- 4)$. On $cal(K)$ the positive weight $rho_0$ has a fixed positive minimum. Hence
  $ upright(bold(1))_(cal(K)) + v F^2 + v \| "Ric"_g \| lt.eq C \( rho_0 + v rho_1 \) . $
  Combining these bounds yields an absolute $c_3 > 0$ and a polynomial $Q_n gt.eq 0$ such that, at the proposed minimum,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.108)")
   $ u^(- 1) "div" V_lambda gt.eq c_3 cal(T) + eta_n a sigma - Q_n \( rho_0 + v rho_1 \) . $ <four:sys:floor-divergence-lower>]
  Fix $0 < delta_0 < min \( c_3 \/ 2 \, 1 \/ 4 \)$. Since $t = - epsilon.alt$ gives $m_0 \( t \) = 1$, choose $C_n$ to exceed $Q_n + 1 + sup_Omega \( rho \/ rho_0 \)$. The right side of (4.108) then strictly exceeds $delta_0 cal(T) + rho + delta_0 eta_n a sigma - C_n \( rho_0 + v rho_1 \) = Xi$, contradicting (4.91). This choice is polynomial in $n$.
  At an interior minimum in stage 4, (4.95) gives $f = 0$, $t = Z$, and $v = 0$. If $gamma > 0$, its equation at the stationary point is
  $ 3 Delta t = gamma #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] delta_0 cal(T)_K + rho - C_n rho_0 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Enlarge $C_n$, still polynomially, so that $C_n > 1 + sup_Omega \( delta_0 cal(T)_K + rho \) \/ rho_0$. The right side is strictly negative, contradicting $Delta t gt.eq 0$. This additional bound is finite because $K$ is compactly supported.
  Consider next an inner boundary minimum in stages 1–3. There $j_0 \( - epsilon.alt \) = 0$, so the same drift correction appears on both sides of (4.91). Cancelling it gives $V_nu \/ u = cal(B)$. Equation (4.88) and $d > 0$ imply
  $ 3 partial_nu t = - H - N_B < 0 . $
  This contradicts the nonnegative derivative into $Omega_R$ at a boundary minimum. In stage 4 with $gamma > 0$, the corresponding exact formula from (4.95) is $3 partial_nu t = gamma \( - H - N_B \) < 0$, giving the same contradiction. The outer boundary is excluded by (4.98).
  Finally, at $gamma = 0$, $f = 0$ and the remaining problem is $"div" \( 3 u "grad" t \) = 0$ with homogeneous inner conormal data and $t = 0$ on $S_R$. Testing by $t$ gives $integral_(Omega_R) 3 u \| thin upright(d) t \|^2 = 0$. Connectedness and the nonempty Dirichlet boundary imply $t = 0$. Thus no stage admits floor equality. ◻
]

The order of choices is now fixed: choose the background geometric data and $epsilon.alt$, then $delta_0$ and the polynomial penalty $C_n$. At each $n$, enlarge the outer radius as in Lemma 122. Subsequent estimates may increase the lower bound on $n$ or $R_min \( n \)$, but none will replace $C_n$ by an arbitrary fixed-$n$ constant. In particular, the first-floor argument has paid for $F tau$ using a bounded compact error and has not used the eventual small-height absorption in (4.66).

==== 4.6 Height separation and global bounds <four:sec:bounds>

Throughout this section the prepared geometry, its thresholds and collars, and $epsilon.alt in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ are fixed. We consider arbitrary smooth solutions of the four stages of the homotopy in Section 5, on $Omega_R$, satisfying $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$. The admissible radii satisfy $R gt.eq R_min \( n \)$, where $R_min \( n \) arrow.r oo$ and is large enough for the outer-boundary conclusion of Proposition 125. Every assertion of uniformity includes the homotopy parameter and $R$. As in Remark 101, $Pi_n$ denotes a polynomial bound in $n$, whereas $C \( n \)$ can depend arbitrarily on the fixed integer $n$. We shall keep this distinction through the boundary-flux estimate.

===== 4.6.1 The limiting heights and the two total regions <the-limiting-heights-and-the-two-total-regions>

The large factor $l \/ eta_n$ forces a nonconstant test level of $h$ to approach a hypersurface whose expansion is controlled by the trace equation. A sublevel of the relaxed limiting height need not have a regular boundary. We first establish the expansion inequality for every smooth exterior support, including at a plateau and at a face of the opposite color.

#thm([Proposition 4.30.])[
  (Separation from the two total regions).  #emph[Let $A subset overline(Omega)$ be compact. In the first two homotopy stages the following statements hold.]
  + #emph[If $A ∩ cal(D)_(c_b) = diameter$, there exist $xi_A > 0$ and $n_A$ such that $h gt.eq b_(-) + xi_A$ on $A$ for every $n gt.eq n_A$.]
  + #emph[If $A ∩ cal(W)_(c_w) = diameter$, there exist $xi_A > 0$ and $n_A$ such that $h lt.eq b_(+) - xi_A$ on $A$ for every $n gt.eq n_A$.]
  #emph[Here the total regions are the full closed regions of Proposition 118, including components whose frontiers do not border $Omega$.]
] <four:prop:height-exclusion>

#proof[
  We give the lower-height argument first. Consider any sequence $n_i arrow.r oo$ of the indicated solutions; its radii tend to infinity. Extend $h_i$ by the constant $b_(+)$ into a small collar across each white face. The functions on these enlarged neighborhoods are continuous. Define their lower relaxed limit by
  $ underline(h) \( x \) = liminf_(i arrow.r oo\
y arrow.r x) h_i \( y \) . $
  It is lower semicontinuous and locally bounded by Lemma 122. Fix levels
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.109)")
   $ b_(-) < k < k' < min { 0 \, b_(-) + xi_1 } \, #h(2em) k' + a_0 < 0 \, $ <four:bnd:levels>]
  where $xi_1$ is the height-cutoff constant in $D_0$. We initially work away from black faces.
  Suppose a smooth $phi.alt$ touches $underline(h)$ from below at $x$, with $underline(h) \( x \) < k'$ and $thin upright(d) phi.alt \( x \) eq.not 0$. Subtracting a small fourth-order function of distance makes the contact strict on a small closed neighborhood without changing its second jet. The definition of the relaxed limit and minimization on this neighborhood give, after passing to a subsequence, points $x_i arrow.r x$ and constants $c_i$ such that $phi.alt + c_i$ touches $h_i$ from below at $x_i$, and $h_i \( x_i \) arrow.r underline(h) \( x \)$. These contacts are interior contacts of the original exterior: their heights are eventually strictly below $k' < b_(+)$, so they cannot occur on a white face or in its constant extension. This remains true when $x$ itself is on a white face.
  At contact, $thin upright(d) h_i = thin upright(d) phi.alt$ and $"Hess" h_i gt.eq "Hess" phi.alt$. The positive trace matrix therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.110)")
   $ "tr"_(A_(chi_i)) K + 1 / sqrt(\( eta_(n_i) \/ l_i \)^2 + \| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|^2) "tr"_(A_(chi_i)) "Hess" phi.alt lt.eq h_i + C + alpha_i D_0 \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq alpha_i lt.eq 1 . $ <four:bnd:test-trace>]
  Here $alpha_i = 0$ in the first stage. The cutoff construction gives $D_0 lt.eq 0$ at these low heights, everywhere: its white contribution vanishes there, and its black contribution is nonpositive. Moreover
  $ l_i / eta_(n_i) gt.eq ell_i / eta_(n_i) = e^(epsilon.alt n_i \/ 2) arrow.r oo . $
  Consequently $\| w_i \| arrow.r 1$, $d_i arrow.r 0$, $chi_i arrow.r 0$, and $A_(chi_i)$ tends to the orthogonal projection onto $\( "grad" phi.alt \)^perp$. Passing to the limit in (4.110) proves
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.111)")
   $ Theta_K \[ phi.alt \] := "tr"_(\( "grad" phi.alt \)^perp) K + frac("tr"_(\( "grad" phi.alt \)^perp) "Hess" phi.alt, \| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|) lt.eq k' + a_0 . $ <four:bnd:limiting-expansion>]
  The normal here points toward increasing $phi.alt$.
  We next transfer this test inequality to the closed set $D_k = { underline(h) lt.eq k }$. This step does not assume that $k$ is a regular value. For integers $j gt.eq 1$, put
  $ S_j \( z \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + e^(- j^2 \( z - k - j^(- 1) \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(- 1) . $
  The lower relaxed limit of $S_j \( underline(h) \)$ is the function $I_k$ equal to zero on $D_k$ and one off $D_k$. To verify this, at a point of $D_k$ use the constant sequence of points and $S_j \( underline(h) \( x \) \) lt.eq S_j \( k \) arrow.r 0$. Off $D_k$, lower semicontinuity gives a neighborhood on which $underline(h) gt.eq k + delta$ for some $delta > 0$, and hence uniform convergence to one there.
  A smooth exterior support of $D_k$, with defining function $phi.alt = 0$ and $D_k$ on the side $phi.alt lt.eq 0$, gives a lower test for $I_k$: shrink the neighborhood and multiply $phi.alt$ by a positive constant so that $\| phi.alt \| < 1 \/ 2$. Strict localization and the same minimization argument give lower tests $phi.alt + a_j$ for $S_j \( underline(h) \)$ at points $x_j arrow.r x$, with contact values $v_j arrow.r 0$. Each $v_j$ lies in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$, so locally $S_j^(- 1) \( phi.alt + a_j \)$ is a smooth lower test for $underline(h)$. Its contact height satisfies
  $ S_j^(- 1) \( v_j \) = k + j^(- 1) + j^(- 2) log frac(v_j, 1 - v_j) lt.eq k + j^(- 1) < k' $
  for all large $j$. Its gradient is nonzero. Composition with a smooth increasing function preserves the oriented level expansion: the additional Hessian term is a multiple of $thin upright(d) phi.alt ⊗ thin upright(d) phi.alt$, whose tangential trace is zero. Thus (4.111) applies to these inverse tests. Passing to the limit shows that every smooth exterior support of $D_k$, away from black faces, has expansion at most $k' + a_0$.
  This argument also rules out a boundary layer at a white face. The constant extension need not make $underline(h)$ continuous there. Nevertheless all approximating low contacts used above are interior, so the support inequality holds at a limiting white-face contact. The reversed white face would itself be an exterior support of $D_k$ with expansion
  $ - H + P_B = - c_w > 0 \, $
  contradicting $k' + a_0 < 0$. Hence $D_k$ misses every white face.
  Adjoin the full black total region and consider $D_k union cal(D)_(c_b)$ in the filled black barrier manifold. At a frontier point belonging to $partial cal(D)_(c_b)$, an exterior support of this union also supports the smooth region $cal(D)_(c_b)$, so smooth tangency bounds its expansion by $c_b$. At other frontier points the bound is $k' + a_0 > c_b$. The coarse end-height decay makes the additional sublevel compact. It lies strictly inside the distant barrier sphere: otherwise a sphere at its greatest radius is an exterior support with positive expansion, a contradiction. There are no remaining contacts with the boundary of the filled barrier manifold. Lemma 117 therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.112)")
   $ D_k union cal(D)_(c_b) subset cal(D)_(c') quad upright("whenever ") k' + a_0 < c' < 0 $ <four:bnd:sublevel-enclosure>]
  in the chosen threshold range.
  If $A$ is disjoint from $cal(D)_(c_b)$, its distance from that closed region is positive. Right continuity first permits $c' > c_b$ close enough that $A ∩ cal(D)_(c') = diameter$. We then choose $k \, k'$ as in (4.109), close enough to $b_(-)$ that $k' + a_0 < c'$. It follows that $A ∩ D_k = diameter$. If a uniform eventual lower bound $h gt.eq k$ on $A$ failed, a sequence of violating points in the compact set $A$ would give a point of $A ∩ D_k$ in its lower relaxed limit. This contradiction proves the first claim, with, for example, $xi_A = k - b_(-)$.
  For the second claim apply the same proof to $\( - h \, - K \, - C \)$. The low-height modification is now $- D_0$ and again has the required nonpositive sign. Extend across black faces by the high constant $- b_(-)$. At a limiting contact, the reversed black face has expansion $- c_b > 0$ for $- K$, and is excluded before using the white barrier manifold. Adjoining $cal(W)_(c_w)$ and applying Lemma 117 now places the sublevel in $cal(W)_(c')$. Right continuity at $c_w$ finishes the proof. This also covers an empty chosen white region: the distance-cap formulation of right continuity makes the nearby regions empty if necessary, and a nonempty enclosed sublevel is then impossible. ◻
]

===== 4.6.2 Collar comparisons and boundary slopes <collar-comparisons-and-boundary-slopes>

We shorten the disjoint collars to $0 lt.eq s lt.eq s_0$ so that the offset is exactly linear there. Their outer leaves are a positive distance from the corresponding closed total region. Proposition 126 therefore provides a strict height gap on those leaves in the first two stages. All these leaves and the compact sets needed below are fixed before increasing $n$.

#thm([Lemma 4.31.])[
  (Collar slopes).  #emph[For all sufficiently large $n$, there are constants $0 < c_4 < C_4$, independent of $n \, R$ and the homotopy parameter, such that in the first two stages 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.113)")
 $ c_4 / eta_n lt.eq epsilon_B partial_nu f lt.eq C_4 / eta_n \, #h(2em) epsilon_B = cases(delim: "{", 1 & upright("on black faces") \,, - 1 & upright("on white faces") .) $ <four:bnd:signed-slopes>]

 On these collars $h gt.eq b_(-)$ on black faces and their exterior collars, and $h lt.eq b_(+)$ on white faces and their exterior collars. In stage three, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.114)")
 $ \| partial_nu f \| lt.eq C_4 \/ eta_n . $ <four:bnd:third-slopes>]

 In stage four $f = 0$.]
] <four:bnd:collar-slopes>

#proof[
  Write $N = "grad" s \/ \| thin upright(d) s \|$ and let $P_s \, H_s$ be the tangential trace of $K$ and the mean curvature of the collar leaf, with normal $N$. For a test height $h_(*) = b_(*) + psi \( s \)$ of positive slope, comparison with a solution uses the test gradient and the solution’s value of $Z$. With the resulting implicit $t$, direct contraction gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.115)")
   $ "tr"_(A_chi) \( K + H^(f_(*)) \) = P_s + a H_s + chi K \( N \, N \) + a chi (frac("Hess" s \( N \, N \), \| thin upright(d) s \|) + \| thin upright(d) s \| psi'' / psi') . $ <four:bnd:collar-trace>]
  Indeed $f_(*) = h_(*) \/ eta_n$ and $"Hess" f_(*) = \( psi' "Hess" s + psi'' thin upright(d) s^2 \) \/ eta_n$. The factor $l psi' \/ \( eta_n sqrt(D) \)$ equals $a \/ \| thin upright(d) s \|$, and $"tr"_(N^perp) "Hess" s = \| thin upright(d) s \| H_s$. For a test $b_(*) - psi \( s \)$ with $psi' > 0$, all terms with prefactor $a$ in (4.115) change sign.
  If the slope is between two fixed positive constants, the floor and the fixed collar geometry imply
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.116)")
   $ d lt.eq C \( eta_n \/ ell \)^2 = C e^(- epsilon.alt n) \, #h(2em) 1 - a = frac(d, 1 + a) lt.eq d \, #h(2em) n chi = frac(3 n, 3 + p v) d gt.eq d \, $ <four:bnd:collar-smallness>]
  and $a gt.eq 1 \/ 2$ for large $n$. Choose a smooth nonnegative cutoff $theta.alt_2$, equal to one on $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ and zero on $\[ 2 \, oo \)$, and set
  $ b_n \( s \) = A_2 n theta.alt_2 \( n s \) \, #h(2em) integral_0^(s_0) b_n \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s lt.eq 2 A_2 . $
  Thus slopes whose logarithmic derivative is $plus.minus b_n$ remain within a fixed multiplicative factor $e^(2 A_2)$ of their initial slopes.
  On a black collar a lower barrier is $b_(-) + psi_(-) \( s \)$, with $psi_(-) \( 0 \) = 0$ and $psi''_(-) \/ psi'_(-) = b_n$. First choose $A_2$ large, depending only on bounded collar geometry. Then choose the slope multiplier sufficiently small that $psi'_(-) lt.eq kappa_B \/ 2$ throughout the collar and that its value at $s_0$ is below the solution by the strict outer-leaf gap. The direction of its gradient is compatible with the face, so $D_0 = 0$ at a contact. Since $P_s + H_s gt.eq c_b$ and $C = a_0 - kappa_B s$, the left side of the trace equation minus its prescribed right side at this test height is at least
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.117)")
   $ frac(kappa_B s, 2) - C d + a chi \| thin upright(d) s \| b_n . $ <four:bnd:lower-residual>]
  The constant multiplying $d$ depends on collar geometry, not on the size of the chosen slope multiplier. On $s lt.eq 1 \/ n$, the last term dominates $C d$ by (4.116) and the choice of $A_2$. On $s gt.eq 1 \/ n$, the first term dominates the exponentially small $C d$ for sufficiently large $n$. The residual is therefore strictly positive.
  For an upper barrier use $b_(-) + psi_(+) \( s \)$ with $psi''_(+) \/ psi'_(+) = - b_n$. Choose its minimum slope sufficiently large to dominate the coarse height at $s_0$ and all fixed linear spatial variations. Smoothness and $P_0 + H_0 = c_b$ give $\| P_s + H_s - c_b \| lt.eq C s$. The residual is consequently bounded above by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.118)")
   $ - c s + C d - a chi \| thin upright(d) s \| b_n $ <four:bnd:upper-residual>]
  for a fixed $c > 0$. It is strictly negative by the same two ranges of $s$. In particular, the favorable linear term in these comparisons comes from the height and the offset; we have not assumed a positive derivative of the leaf expansion at $s = 0$.
  At any hypothetical crossing extremum, the test and the solution have the same gradient and the same $Z$, hence the same $t$ and positive trace matrix. The prescribed right side is increasing in $h$ with derivative at least one. The strict residual signs, Hessian comparison, and the endpoint inequalities exclude such a crossing. Differentiating the resulting barriers at $s = 0$, and using upper and lower positive bounds for $\| thin upright(d) s \|$, proves (4.113) on black faces. Sign reversal of $h \, K \, C$ proves the white assertion. The compatible-direction modification again vanishes there.
  In stage three let $b_(*) = \( 1 - zeta \) b$ be the assigned face height. Use $b_(*) + psi \( s \)$ and $b_(*) - psi \( s \)$, with $psi' > 0$, $psi'' \/ psi' = - b_n$, and with a large fixed minimum slope. For the limiting coefficients $a = 1$, $chi = 0$ at $s = 0$ and height $b_(*)$, the directional inequalities in the homotopy construction are
  $ F_(upright("presc")) \( nu \) gt.eq P_B + H \, #h(2em) F_(upright("presc")) \( - nu \) lt.eq P_B - H . $
  Their residuals have precisely the signs needed for the upper and lower tests, respectively. At positive $s$ and finite $d$, smooth spatial variation and $1 - a lt.eq d$, $chi lt.eq d$ cost at most $C \( s + d \)$. Increasing or decreasing the height by $psi \( s \)$ improves the respective residual by at least $psi \( s \)$. Choose the minimum slope to dominate the $C s$ error and the coarse height at the outer collar leaf. The logarithmic curvature contribution in (4.115) has the favorable sign for both tests and dominates $C d$ on $s lt.eq 1 \/ n$; on $s gt.eq 1 \/ n$ the linear height term dominates it. The same comparison proves (4.114). All constants are uniform in $zeta in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ by smoothness of the prescribed convex combination. The assertion for stage four is the scalar maximum-principle conclusion already proved in the homotopy construction. ◻
]

Combining the own-color collar comparison with Proposition 126 on the compact complement of smaller own-color collars yields

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.119)")
 $ b_(-) lt.eq h lt.eq b_(+) quad upright("on ") overline(Omega) ∩ "supp" K quad upright("in the first two stages, for all sufficiently large ") n . $ <four:eq:height-interval>]

The finitely many compact sets used here are fixed first. Thus (4.119) has exactly the range required in (4.66); it was not used to establish the floor.

===== 4.6.3 Polynomial weighted traces and flux <polynomial-weighted-traces-and-flux>

The slope sign supplies a trace inequality with a small coefficient in the boundary flux. Its proof uses weighted divergences, so it does not require an upper bound for $Z$ or $\| thin upright(d) f \|$. All measures in the next lemma are background $g$ measures.

#thm([Lemma 4.32.])[
  (Weighted trace inequalities).  #emph[Let $cal(C)$ be the union of fixed collars, slightly enlarged if necessary. In stages one and two, for every nonnegative smooth $phi$ and every sufficiently large $n$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.120)")
 $ integral_B L_0 phi thin thin upright(d) A_g & lt.eq Pi_n integral_(cal(C)) u #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) phi + \| thin upright(d) phi \|_(A_chi) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] thin thin upright(d) V_g \,\
integral_B phi thin thin upright(d) A_g & lt.eq Pi_n integral_(cal(C)) sqrt(D) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) phi + \| thin upright(d) phi \|_(A_chi) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] thin thin upright(d) V_g . $ <four:bnd:weighted-trace>]

 On $B$, the corresponding homotopy flux satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.121)")
 $ \| \( V_lambda \)_nu \| lt.eq C u d lt.eq C eta_n / ell L_0 . $ <four:eq:boundary-flux-bound>]

 The constants $C$ and the coefficients of $Pi_n$ are independent of $n \, R$ and the solution.]
] <four:lem:weighted-trace>

#proof[
  Set $W = sqrt(d) thin w$. For every covector $xi$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.122)")
   $ \| xi \( W \) \| lt.eq \| xi \|_(A_d) lt.eq sqrt(\( n + 3 \) \/ 3) thin \| xi \|_(A_chi) \, #h(2em) u W = L_0 w \, #h(2em) sqrt(D) thin W = w . $ <four:bnd:W-dual>]
  The first assertion follows along the axis from $\| xi \( W \) \|^2 = d v xi \( e \)^2 lt.eq d xi \( e \)^2$ and is immediate transversely.
  For completeness, write $Q_f = "tr"_(A_d) H^f$. Differentiating $w$ and $D$ gives
  $ nabla_i w_j & = H_(i j)^f - H^f \( w \, partial_i \) w_j + p d thin t_i w_j \,\
"div" w & = Q_f + p d thin w \( t \) \, & Q_f & = F + \( d - chi \) j - "tr"_(A_d) K . $
  The two weighted divergences are exactly
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.123)")
   $ D^(- 1 \/ 2) "div" \( sqrt(D) thin W \) & = sqrt(d) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Q_f + p d thin w \( t \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
u^(- 1) "div" \( u W \) & = sqrt(d) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Q_f + \( p + 2 + p d \) w \( t \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:bnd:first-divergence>]
  Indeed the second formula follows from $u W = L_0 w$ and $thin upright(d) log L_0 = \( p + 2 \) thin upright(d) t$. Since $sqrt(d) thin \| w \( t \) \| lt.eq \| thin upright(d) t \|_(A_d)$ and $0 lt.eq d - chi lt.eq d$, coercivity (4.84) bounds both right sides by $Pi_n \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \)$ on the fixed collars. No factor $l^(- 1)$ or $eta_n^(- 1)$ occurs.
  On a face $epsilon_B w_nu = a gt.eq 1 \/ 2$ by (4.113). Integrate the divergence of $epsilon_B zeta u W phi$ separately on each collar, where $zeta = 1$ at $B$ and $zeta = 0$ near its outer leaf. The outward normal of $Omega_R$ at $B$ is $- nu$, so the boundary term is $- integral_B L_0 a phi$. Taking absolute values of the remaining terms and using (4.122)–(4.123) proves (4.120). Replacing $u$ by $sqrt(D)$ proves (4.120). The derivatives of $zeta$ cost only a fixed constant.
  On the boundary the compatible slope makes $D_0 = 0$, and the prescribed heights and offsets give exactly $F - P_B = epsilon_B H$. Moreover $w_nu = epsilon_B a$ and $\( A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \) \)_nu = epsilon_B a chi K \( nu \, nu \)$. Writing $q_lambda = 1 - \( 1 - lambda \) j_0 \( t \) in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$, the flux formula (4.91) is therefore
  $ frac(\( V_lambda \)_nu, u) = q_lambda [- frac(d, 1 + a) H - N_B d - \( 1 - lambda \) epsilon_B a chi K \( nu \, nu \)] . $
  This is bounded in absolute value by $C d$ since $chi lt.eq d$. Finally $u d = L_0 sqrt(d)$ and $sqrt(d) lt.eq C eta_n \/ ell$ at $B$ by (4.113). This proves (4.121). ◻
]

===== 4.6.4 A high-level integral and graph Sobolev inequality <a-high-level-integral-and-graph-sobolev-inequality>

From this point, constants used to obtain a bounded range may depend arbitrarily on fixed $n$. The only large-$n$ absorption needed first uses the polynomial constants just proved:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.124)")
 $ Pi_n eta_n / ell = Pi_n e^(- epsilon.alt n \/ 2) arrow.r 0 . $ <four:bnd:small-boundary-factor>]

There exists $Z_0 \( n \) > 0$, independent of the solution and $R$, such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.125)")
 $ Xi gt.eq delta_0 cal(T) + rho + 1 / 2 delta_0 eta_n a sigma quad upright("on ") { Z > Z_0 \( n \) } \, #h(2em) Xi gt.eq delta_0 cal(T) - C \( n \) quad upright("everywhere") . $ <four:bnd:high-source>]

To see the first assertion, only the support of $m_0$ matters. There $- epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq - epsilon.alt + 1 \/ n$, whereas

$ D = e^(6 \( Z - t \)) \, #h(2em) eta_n a sigma = eta_n / l (sqrt(D) - 1 / sqrt(D)) . $

At fixed $n$ this last expression tends uniformly to infinity with $Z$, and dominates the bounded penalty $cal(P)$. The global lower bound follows from the bounded weights defining $cal(P)$.

#thm([Lemma 4.33.])[
  (High-level integral).  #emph[In stage one, choose a smooth nondecreasing $k_0$ which is zero on $\( - oo \, Z_0 \]$ and one on $\[ Z_0 + 1 \, oo \)$. For all sufficiently large fixed $n$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.126)")
 $ integral_(Omega_R) u k_0 \( Z \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(T) + rho + eta_n a sigma #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] thin thin upright(d) V_g lt.eq C \( n \) . $ <four:bnd:high-integral>]

]
] <four:bnd:high-level-integral>

#proof[
  The test $k_0 \( Z \)$ vanishes on $S_R$. Integration by parts gives
  $ integral u k_0 Xi + 3 integral u k'_0 \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)^2 = - integral_B k_0 \( V_lambda \)_nu - lambda integral u k'_0 chevron.l K \( w \, dot.op \) \, thin upright(d) Z chevron.r_(A_chi) . $
  The last integral is absorbed into part of the gradient term plus $C \( n \)$. Indeed it is supported in a fixed compact set and in the strip $Z_0 lt.eq Z lt.eq Z_0 + 1$, where
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.127)")
   $ u = l e^(3 Z - t) lt.eq e^(1 + 3 \( Z_0 + 1 \) + epsilon.alt) . $ <four:bnd:strip-weight>]
  Thus the residual strip integral has bounded background volume and a bounded weight, without any preliminary gradient estimate.
  By Lemma 128, the absolute boundary contribution is at most
  $ Pi_n eta_n / ell integral_(cal(C)) u #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) k_0 + k'_0 \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The fixed collars have $min_(cal(C)) rho > 0$. Consequently $1 + sqrt(cal(T)) lt.eq C \( rho + delta_0 cal(T) \)$ there. Choose $n$ large using (4.124) to absorb the nondifferentiated term into the positive source in (4.125). Young’s inequality absorbs the differentiated term into another part of $integral u k'_0 \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)^2$, with a bounded remainder by (4.127). This proves (4.126). ◻
]

Let $Gamma_f$ be the ordinary product graph of $f$ in $\( Omega_R times bb(R) \, g + thin upright(d) z^2 \)$. Put $E_f = 1 + sigma^2$ and $G_f = "Id" - \( sigma^2 \/ E_f \) e ⊗ e$, the inverse graph metric on base covectors. The following comparisons hold without a bound for $sigma$:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.128)")
 $ e^(- 1) sqrt(D) thin thin upright(d) V_g lt.eq thin upright(d) V_(Gamma_f) = sqrt(E_f) thin thin upright(d) V_g lt.eq ell^(- 1) sqrt(D) thin thin upright(d) V_g \,\
ell^2 A_chi lt.eq G_f lt.eq frac(e^2 \( n + 3 \), 3) A_chi . $ <four:bnd:graph-comparison>]

They follow from $ell lt.eq l lt.eq e$, hence $ell^2 lt.eq D \/ E_f lt.eq e^2$, and $3 d \/ \( n + 3 \) lt.eq chi lt.eq d$. Thus graph measure and gradient norms are comparable to $sqrt(D) thin thin upright(d) V_g$ and the $A_chi$ norm, with constants depending only on fixed $n$.

On every fixed compact base set $Q$, (4.126) implies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.129)")
 $ integral_(Gamma_f \|_Q) e^(2 Z) thin thin upright(d) V_(Gamma_f) lt.eq C \( n \, Q \) . $ <four:bnd:starting-L2>]

In fact, for $x = sqrt(D) gt.eq 1$, the elementary estimate $x^(2 \/ 3) lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + \( eta_n \/ l \) \( x - x^(- 1) \) \)$ gives

$ D^(1 \/ 3) lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + eta_n a sigma \) \, #h(2em) e^(2 Z) sqrt(D) = u / l D^(1 \/ 3) . $

On ${ Z gt.eq Z_0 + 1 }$ use (4.126) and the positive minimum of $rho$ on $Q$. On its complement the floor bounds $D$ and $e^(2 Z)$, and the fixed base volume suffices.

#thm([Lemma 4.34.])[
  (Sobolev inequality on the graph).  #emph[Fix a compact base patch and a slightly larger compact neighborhood. For every smooth $phi$ supported over the patch, allowed to meet $B$, a stage-one solution satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.130)")
 $ (integral_(Gamma_f) \| phi \|^4 thin thin upright(d) V_(Gamma_f))^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq C \( n \) integral_(Gamma_f) (\| nabla_(Gamma_f) phi \|^2 + \( 1 + cal(T) \) phi^2) thin thin upright(d) V_(Gamma_f) . $ <four:bnd:sobolev>]

 The constant depends on the fixed neighborhoods, and is independent of the graph and the outer radius.]
] <four:bnd:graph-sobolev>

#proof[
  Extend the compact base neighborhood smoothly to a compact Riemannian manifold and fix a smooth Euclidean isometric embedding by Nash’s theorem #cite(label("Nash1956")). Its product with a line embeds the product graph isometrically. The scalar mean curvature of the product graph is, up to its normal sign,
  $ H_(Gamma_f) = frac(sqrt(D), l sqrt(E_f)) ["tr" T - "tr"_(e^perp) K + E_f^(- 1) \( j - K \( e \, e \) \)] . $
  The prefactor is at most $ell^(- 1)$ and $E_f^(- 1) lt.eq e^2 d$. Coercivity therefore bounds its magnitude by $C \( n \) \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \)$. The additional mean-curvature vector of the fixed embedding has magnitude at most four times the bound for its second fundamental form. Thus the Euclidean mean-curvature vector has the same bound, independently of the graph slope.
  The four-dimensional Michael–Simon $L^1$ Sobolev inequality #cite(label("MichaelSimon1973")) #cite(label("Simon2014")), applied to $\| phi \|^3$, gives
  $ (integral_(Gamma_f) \| phi \|^4)^(3 \/ 4) lt.eq C \( n \) integral_(Gamma_f) (\| phi \|^2 \| nabla_(Gamma_f) phi \| + \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) \| phi \|^3) + C integral_(partial Gamma_f) \| phi \|^3 . $
  We have retained the boundary term. Its form follows, for each individual smooth graph, by applying the boundaryless inequality to cutoffs tending to one up to the face: the integral of their normal derivatives tends to the face integral. Artificial patch edges do not contribute because $phi$ vanishes there. Along $B$, $f$ is constant on each face, so the graph face measure is exactly $thin upright(d) A_g$. Apply (4.120) to $\| phi \|^3$ and use (4.128). This bounds the face contribution by the same two interior expressions. If $I = integral_(Gamma_f) \| phi \|^4$, Cauchy–Schwarz now gives
  $ I^(3 \/ 4) lt.eq C \( n \) I^(1 \/ 2) (integral_(Gamma_f) \( \| nabla_(Gamma_f) phi \|^2 + \( 1 + cal(T) \) phi^2 \))^(1 \/ 2) . $
  For $I > 0$ divide by $I^(1 \/ 2)$ and square. The case $I = 0$ is immediate, proving (4.130). ◻
]

===== 4.6.5 Iteration and a bounded range for all stages <iteration-and-a-bounded-range-for-all-stages>

#thm([Proposition 4.35.])[
  (Global bounded range).  #emph[For every sufficiently large fixed $n$ there is a finite $C \( n \)$ such that every above-floor smooth solution in any of the four homotopy stages satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.131)")
 $ - epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq Z lt.eq C \( n \) \, #h(2em) \| f \| + \| "grad" f \| lt.eq C \( n \) quad upright("on ") overline(Omega)_R . $ <four:eq:bounded-range>]

 The constant is uniform in the allowed radii and homotopy parameters.]
] <four:prop:bounded-range>

#proof[
  We first treat stage one, where the compactly supported drift can prevent a direct maximum argument. Let $psi$ be a smooth base cutoff supported in a fixed compact patch, permitted to meet $B$. For $k gt.eq k_(*) \( n \) gt.eq 1$, test the divergence equation with $psi^2 e^(2 k Z) \/ L_0$. We claim
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.132)")
   $ integral_(Gamma_f) e^(2 k Z) psi^2 \( cal(T) + k \| nabla_(Gamma_f) Z \|^2 \) lt.eq C \( n \) k integral_(Gamma_f) e^(2 k Z) \( psi^2 + \| thin upright(d) psi \|_g^2 \) \, $ <four:bnd:exponential-energy>]
  where $C \( n \)$ is independent of $k$.
  Here are the error estimates establishing the claim. Let $thin upright(d) mu_D = sqrt(D) thin thin upright(d) V_g$, and write $kappa = K \( w \, dot.op \)$ in this computation. Since $thin upright(d) log L_0 = \( p + 2 \) thin upright(d) t$, the exact tested identity is
  $ & integral e^(2 k Z) psi^2 \( Xi + 6 k \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)^2 \) thin thin upright(d) mu_D\
 & = - integral_B frac(psi^2 e^(2 k Z), L_0) \( V_lambda \)_nu thin thin upright(d) A_g - 2 k lambda integral e^(2 k Z) psi^2 chevron.l kappa \, thin upright(d) Z chevron.r_(A_chi) thin thin upright(d) mu_D\
 & quad - 2 integral e^(2 k Z) psi chevron.l 3 thin upright(d) Z + lambda kappa \, thin upright(d) psi chevron.r_(A_chi) thin thin upright(d) mu_D + integral e^(2 k Z) psi^2 \( p + 2 \) chevron.l 3 thin upright(d) Z + lambda kappa \, thin upright(d) t chevron.r_(A_chi) thin thin upright(d) mu_D . $
  The global lower bound in (4.125) supplies $delta_0 cal(T)$ and costs only $C \( n \) psi^2$ on the right. Coercivity bounds the final integrand, by Young’s inequality, by
  $ 1 / 8 delta_0 psi^2 cal(T) + C \( n \) psi^2 \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)^2 + C \( n \) psi^2 . $
  Choose $k_(*) \( n \)$ sufficiently large to absorb this fixed multiple of the gradient square. The drift term carrying $2 k$ is bounded by $k psi^2 \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)^2 + C \( n \) k psi^2$. The cutoff terms cost another fixed fraction of the $k$-weighted gradient square plus $C \( n \) k \( psi^2 + \| thin upright(d) psi \|_g^2 \)$.
  For the boundary term, (4.121) gives $\| \( V_lambda \)_nu \| \/ L_0 lt.eq C sqrt(d) lt.eq C$. Apply (4.120) to $psi^2 e^(2 k Z)$. The resulting integrand, apart from its common factor $e^(2 k Z)$, is bounded by
  $ C \( n \) [\( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) psi^2 + 2 \| psi \| \| thin upright(d) psi \|_(A_chi) + 2 k psi^2 \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)] . $
  For any fixed coefficient $b = C \( n \)$ the estimates
  $ b sqrt(cal(T)) lt.eq 1 / 8 delta_0 cal(T) + 2 b^2 \/ delta_0 \, #h(2em) 2 b k \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi) lt.eq k \| thin upright(d) Z \|_(A_chi)^2 + b^2 k $
  show that the boundary cost is absorbed with the claimed remainder linear in $k$. Leaving fixed positive fractions of the coercive terms and using (4.128) proves (4.132).
  Set $U = e^Z$. Applying (4.130) to $psi U^k$ and using (4.132) yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.133)")
   $ (integral_(Gamma_f) psi^4 U^(4 k))^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq C \( n \) k^2 integral_(Gamma_f) U^(2 k) \( psi^2 + \| thin upright(d) psi \|_g^2 \) . $ <four:bnd:iteration-step>]
  Choose nested base patches $Q_r subset.double Q_s$ in a fixed coordinate neighborhood, or corresponding half patches at $B$, with $0 < s - r lt.eq 1$. Cutoffs have $\| thin upright(d) psi \| lt.eq C \/ \( s - r \)$. Iterate (4.133) with $k_j = 2^j k_(*)$ and geometric gaps. The factor at the $j$th step is at most
  $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] C \( n \) k_j^2 \( 1 + upright("gap")_j^(- 2) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]]^(1 \/ \( 2 k_j \)) . $
  Its product is finite, since $sum_j \( j + 1 \) \/ k_j < oo$. Tracking the total gap exponent gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.134)")
   $ sup_(Q_r) U lt.eq C \( n \) \( s - r \)^(- 2 \/ k_(*)) (integral_(Gamma_f \|_(Q_s)) U^(2 k_(*)) thin thin upright(d) V_(Gamma_f))^(1 \/ \( 2 k_(*) \)) . $ <four:bnd:first-iteration>]
  All comparisons here use the same graph measure of the individual solution; its constants are uniform by the preceding lemmas.
  The starting exponent $2 k_(*)$ can exceed the exponent in (4.129). A second, elementary nested-patch argument closes this point. Write $M \( s \) = sup_(Q_s) U$. Interpolation gives
  $ (integral_(Gamma_f \|_(Q_s)) U^(2 k_(*)))^(1 \/ \( 2 k_(*) \)) lt.eq M \( s \)^(1 - 1 \/ k_(*)) (integral_(Gamma_f \|_(Q_s)) U^2)^(1 \/ \( 2 k_(*) \)) . $
  The last integral is uniformly bounded by (4.129). If $k_(*) = 1$ this already suffices. Otherwise Young’s inequality applied to (4.134) gives, for any $q in \( 0 \, 1 \)$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.135)")
   $ M \( r \) lt.eq q M \( s \) + C \( n \, q \) \( s - r \)^(- 2) . $ <four:bnd:second-iteration>]
  Choose increasing radii $r_j$ tending to a fixed larger radius, with $r_(j + 1) - r_j$ proportional to $2^(- j)$, and take $q < 1 \/ 4$. Iteration sums a geometric series with ratio $4 q < 1$. The remaining $q^j M \( r_j \)$ tends to zero: for each individual smooth solution all these patches lie in one compact neighborhood with finite supremum. Thus $M \( r_0 \) lt.eq C \( n \)$ uniformly. A finite cover gives a uniform $Z$ bound on a compact set containing $B$ and the support of $K$.
  Outside this set $K = 0$. At an interior maximum with $Z > Z_0 \( n \)$, $thin upright(d) Z = 0$ and
  $ "div" \( 3 u A_chi "grad" Z \) = 3 u "tr"_(A_chi) "Hess" Z lt.eq 0 \, $
  whereas $u Xi > 0$ by (4.125). The outer value is $Z = 0$, so this maximum argument extends the compact bound through the end. This proves the stage-one $Z$ estimate.
  In stages two and three $lambda = 0$, so the same maximum argument works everywhere in the interior. On $B$ the already established face-gradient bounds show that $Z$ can be high only if $t gt.eq 1$: if $t < 1$, then
  $ Z = t + 1 / 6 log \( 1 + l^2 \| thin upright(d) f \|^2 \) lt.eq 1 + 1 / 6 log \( 1 + e^2 C_4^2 eta_n^(- 2) \) . $
  For $t gt.eq 1$ the boundary switch satisfies $j_0 = 1$ and the prescribed conormal flux in (4.91) is zero. A high boundary maximum would contradict the boundary point principle applied locally on the high-level set to $"div" \( 3 u A_chi "grad" Z \) = u Xi > 0$. For this application the individual smooth solution gives a smooth positive elliptic matrix; no uniform ellipticity constant is being assumed before the bound. Equivalently, the outward derivative at a nonconstant boundary maximum is strictly positive, whereas the zero conormal flux forces it to vanish. This bounds $Z$ in these two stages.
  In stage four $f = 0$, hence $t = Z$. At a positive scale multiplying the source and boundary data, the same high-level interior and boundary arguments apply. At zero scale the homogeneous mixed problem gives $Z = 0$. The resulting bound is uniform in the scale.
  Finally $Z - t = 1 / 6 log D gt.eq 0$, so the floor and the upper bound give $D lt.eq e^(6 \( C \( n \) + epsilon.alt \))$. Since $l gt.eq ell > 0$, $\| thin upright(d) f \| lt.eq ell^(- 1) sqrt(D - 1) lt.eq C \( n \)$. The coarse bound for $h = eta_n f$ gives $\| f \| lt.eq C \( n \)$. This proves all of (4.131). ◻
]

The bounded range now permits fixed-$n$ regularity and exhaustion. The final mass estimate will use only the earlier polynomial bounds in Lemma 128 and (4.121); the graph Sobolev and Moser constants are not needed in that limit.

==== 4.7 Axial regularity and the coupled estimates <four:sec:regularity>

The second equation has quadratic growth in both $"grad" Z$ and $"Hess" f$. A bound for its ellipticity constants therefore does not close the regularity argument. We first prove an independent scalar estimate which gives a positive Morrey exponent for $\| "Hess" f \|^2$. That exponent permits a measure correction in the second equation. All constants in this section may depend arbitrarily on a fixed $n$; none of them is subsequently used as a polynomial bound $Pi_n$. In coordinate calculations $D_x$, or $D$ when there is no ambiguity, denotes ordinary differentiation, whereas $D = 1 + l^2 \| "grad" f \|^2$ in the system retains its earlier meaning.

===== 4.7.1 An independent scalar estimate <an-independent-scalar-estimate>

#thm([Theorem 4.36.])[
  (Measurable axial coefficient).  #emph[Let $U$ be a four-dimensional coordinate ball $B_2$, or a half-ball $B_2^(+) = B_2 ∩ { x_4 > 0 }$. Assume that the metric is smooth, $C_g^(- 1) "Id" lt.eq \( g_(i j) \) lt.eq C_g "Id"$, and that its coordinate $C^2$ norm is at most $C_g$. Assume also uniformly bounded $C^3$ norms for the charts and inverse charts. On a half-ball use boundary normal coordinates, so $g_44 = 1$ and $g_(a 4) = 0$ for $a < 4$. Let $z$ be $C^3$, up to the flat face when present, and constant on that face. Suppose, almost everywhere, that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.136)")
 $ A_z : "Hess"_g z = G \, #h(2em) A_z = "Id" - \( 1 - tilde(chi) \) e_z ⊗ e_z \,\
e_z = frac("grad" z, \| "grad" z \|) \, #h(2em) 0 < chi_0 lt.eq tilde(chi) lt.eq 1 . $ <four:reg:axial-equation>]

 Here $tilde(chi)$ and $G$ are measurable. At $"grad" z = 0$, any measurable unit axis making the equation true is allowed. If $\| "grad" z \| lt.eq M_1$ and $\| G \| lt.eq N_1$, then there are $alpha in \( 0 \, 1 \)$, $r_0 > 0$ and $C < oo$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.137)")
 $ parallel "grad" z parallel_(C^alpha \( U_1 \)) lt.eq C \( M_1 + N_1 \) \, #h(2em) integral_(B_r \( x \) ∩ U) \| "Hess"_g z \|^2 thin thin upright(d) V_g lt.eq C \( M_1 + N_1 \)^2 r^(2 + 2 alpha) $ <four:eq:axial-estimate>]

 for $x in overline(U_1)$ and $0 < r lt.eq r_0$, where $U_1 = B_1$ or $B_1^(+)$. The constants depend only on $chi_0$, the stated geometry bounds and the separation from the curved patch edges. The $C^alpha$ norm may equivalently be taken for the coordinate gradient components. In particular, no modulus of continuity of $G$ or $tilde(chi)$, and no bound for a derivative of either, is assumed.]
] <four:thm:axial-regularity>

#proof[
  We prove the assertion on uniformly small full balls, using a reflection at the flat face. Rescaling back and a finite local cover give the stated form. The argument first establishes a local Hessian estimate and a dichotomy: on a smaller ball, either the gradient bound decreases or the solution is close to a nonconstant affine function. A fixed-axis estimate then improves the affine approximation. Iterating these alternatives gives Hölder gradient control, from which the local Hessian estimate yields the Morrey bound.
  ======= 4.7.1.1 Doubling and a quantitative Hessian estimate. <doubling-and-a-quantitative-hessian-estimate.>
  Subtract the constant boundary value. With $R_4 = "diag" \( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \, - 1 \)$, extend the tangential metric block evenly and set $tilde(z) \( x \) = - z \( R_4 x \)$ below the plane. Tangential derivatives of $z$ vanish on the face and the two normal derivatives agree. Thus $tilde(z)$ is $C^1$; its distributional coordinate Hessian has no surface measure. The doubled metric is Lipschitz and has the prescribed smooth bounds on each closed side. On the reflected side the gradient and covariant Hessian are $- R_4 "grad" z$ and $- R_4 \( "Hess"_g z \) R_4$, respectively. Extend $tilde(chi)$ evenly and $G$ oddly. Equation (4.136) then holds almost everywhere on the double. Each individual doubled function is locally $W^(2 \, p)$ for every finite $p$, although these norms are not yet uniformly bounded.
  Make a constant linear coordinate change so that the metric at the center of a ball is the identity. A plane in the double remains a plane under this change. If the ball is sufficiently small, the coordinate principal matrix $a = \( a^(i j) \)$ in (4.136) satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.138)")
   $ \| "Id" - a \( x \) \|_F lt.eq 1 - chi_0 \/ 2 = : kappa < 1 . $ <four:reg:defect>]
  The reason for the strict gap is the exact identity $\| "Id" - A_z \|_F = 1 - tilde(chi)$ in an orthonormal frame; the small metric oscillation consumes at most half the gap.
  Let $T = D^2 Delta^(- 1)$ on $bb(R)^4$, with its values measured in the Frobenius norm. Its $L^2$ operator norm is exactly one, since its Fourier multiplier satisfies
  $ sum_(i \, j) lr(|frac(xi_i xi_j, \| xi \|^2)|)^2 = 1 . $
  The Calderón–Zygmund bound gives a finite $L^4$ operator norm $C_4$. Interpolation between $2$ and $4$ therefore gives $parallel T parallel_p lt.eq C_4^(theta.alt \( p \))$, where $1 \/ p = \( 1 - theta.alt \( p \) \) \/ 2 + theta.alt \( p \) \/ 4$ and $theta.alt \( p \) arrow.b 0$ as $p arrow.b 2$. Choose once and for all $2 < p_0 < 4$ such that $C_4^(theta.alt \( p_0 \)) kappa < 1$. For a compactly supported $y$, write $Delta y = a : D^2 y + \( "Id" - a \) : D^2 y$ and absorb to obtain
  $ parallel D^2 y parallel_p lt.eq frac(parallel T parallel_p, 1 - kappa parallel T parallel_p) parallel a : D^2 y parallel_p \, #h(2em) p = 2 \, p_0 . $
  This proves the needed Cordes estimate without differentiating $a$.
  For later use its local, scaled form is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.139)")
   $ parallel D^2 y parallel_(L^p \( B_(r \/ 2) \)) lt.eq C (parallel a : D^2 y parallel_(L^p \( B_r \)) + r^(- 2) parallel y parallel_(L^p \( B_r \))) \, #h(2em) p = 2 \, p_0 . $ <four:reg:cordes-local>]
  Here is the localization. A cutoff across a gap $h$ gives the additional terms $C h^(- 1) parallel D y parallel_p + C h^(- 2) parallel y parallel_p$. On a slightly larger ball, derivative interpolation bounds the first by $epsilon parallel D^2 y parallel_p + C_epsilon h^(- 2) parallel y parallel_p$. Use concentric gaps decreasing geometrically and choose $epsilon$ so that the resulting geometric series absorbs the larger-ball Hessian norms. For each individual function these norms are finite, so the terminal term tends to zero. This proves (4.139); the same argument absorbs bounded first order coefficients. In particular the Christoffel terms in the covariant equation cause no difficulty. The analytic inputs here are the scalar Calderón–Zygmund and interpolation estimates #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")).
  ======= 4.7.1.2 A divergence inequality for the gradient deficit. <a-divergence-inequality-for-the-gradient-deficit.>
  Divide by $M_1 + N_1$ unless this number is zero, in which case the claim is immediate. Subtract a constant and rescale space by $r$ and height by $r$. The new gradient has norm at most one and the new forcing has norm at most $r$. Metric first derivatives and the second fundamental form of a reflecting plane are $O \( r \)$; curvature on the smooth sides is $O \( r^2 \)$. After normalizing the metric at the center, all of these errors can be made uniformly small. In this paragraph write $P = "grad" z$, $H_z = "Hess"_g z$, and $s = 1 - \| P \|_g^2 gt.eq 0$.
  There is an exact cancellation of the measurable axial coefficient:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.140)")
   $ A_z "grad" \( \| P \|_g^2 \/ 2 \) - G P & = \( H_z - Delta_g z thin g \) P = : Q \,\
"div" Q & = \| H_z \|_g^2 - \( Delta_g z \)^2 + "Ric"_g \( P \, P \) . $ <four:reg:flux-identity>]
  Indeed $"grad" \( \| P \|_g^2 \/ 2 \) = H_z P$ and $Delta_g z = G + \( 1 - tilde(chi) \) H_z \( e_z \, e_z \)$. The axial parts in the first formula cancel. In the second formula, $"div" H_z = "grad" Delta_g z + "Ric"_g \( P \, dot.op \)$ cancels the third derivatives. Both identities hold also at $P = 0$, because the first has both sides zero there. At no point is a derivative of $G$ or $tilde(chi)$ taken. Choose $epsilon_0 > 0$ with $\( 1 + epsilon_0 \) \( 1 - chi_0 \)^2 < 1$ when $chi_0 < 1$. Young’s inequality, or directly $Delta_g z = G$ if $chi_0 = 1$, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.141)")
   $ \| H_z \|_g^2 - \( Delta_g z \)^2 gt.eq c \| H_z \|_g^2 - C \| G \|^2 \, #h(2em) c = c \( chi_0 \) > 0 . $ <four:reg:flux-coercivity>]
  We compute the only surface error in this identity. In boundary normal coordinates on the original side, $P = z_4 partial_4$ at the plane and, for $a \, b < 4$,
  $ \( H_z \)_(a b) = - Gamma_(a b)^4 z_4 = 1 / 2 \( partial_4 g_(a b) \) z_4 . $
  Writing $H_B = 1 / 2 g^(a b) partial_4 g_(a b)$ for the mean curvature toward increasing $x_4$, this gives
  $ Q_4 = \( z_44 - Delta_g z \) z_4 = - H_B z_4^2 . $
  Thus the jump of the volume-weighted normal flux on the double is bounded by $C \| "II"_B \| \| P \|^2$. In particular it contains no uncontrolled second derivative of $z$. A bounded plane density $j \( x' \)$ is the distributional divergence of $j \( x' \) upright(bold(1))_({ x_4 > 0 }) partial_4$; only a normal derivative is taken in this assertion. After a linear coordinate change use the corresponding constant normal direction. Subtracting this bounded step field handles the signed jump, without imposing any tangential regularity on $j$.
  More explicitly, put $J_g = sqrt(det g)$ and let
  $ a_1^(i j) = J_g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] g^(i j) - \( 1 - tilde(chi) \) e_z^i e_z^j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  be the coordinate divergence matrix. Let $j = \[ J_g Q^4 \]$ be the upper trace minus the lower trace. Reflection gives $Q^(-) = R_4 Q^(+)$, so $j = - 2 J_g H_B z_4^2$. Then
  $ "div" \( J_g Q \) = J_g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| H_z \|^2 - \( Delta_g z \)^2 + "Ric"_g \( P \, P \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + j thin thin upright(d) cal(H)^3 \|_({ x_4 = 0 }) . $
  Since $a_1 D s + 2 J_g G P = - 2 J_g Q$, the error field $2 J_g G P + 2 j \( x' \) upright(bold(1))_({ x_4 > 0 }) partial_4$ cancels the plane contribution exactly.
  Consequently (4.140)–(4.141), with volume factors included, imply on a full normalized ball
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.142)")
   $ "div" \( a_1 D s + E_1 \) lt.eq - c_1 \| H_z \|^2 + e_1 \, #h(2em) parallel E_1 parallel_oo + parallel e_1 parallel_oo arrow.r 0 $ <four:reg:deficit-inequality>]
  as the normalized source and geometry errors tend to zero. The matrix $a_1$ is bounded, symmetric and uniformly elliptic, with constants depending only on $chi_0$ and the fixed comparison bounds. For example, away from the plane one may take the volume-weighted matrix of $A_z$, $E_1 = 2 sqrt(det g) thin G P$, and $e_1 lt.eq C \( \| G \|^2 + \| "Ric"_g \| \)$; the step field just described supplies the remaining term in $E_1$.
  ======= 4.7.1.3 The gradient drop or affine alternative. <the-gradient-drop-or-affine-alternative.>
  Fix $r_(*) = 1 \/ 32$. We claim that, for every $b_0 > 0$, there are $r_(*) < k_(*) < 1$ and $delta_(upright("alt")) > 0$, depending only on the fixed parameters and $b_0$, such that a normalized solution on $B_1$, with gradient at most one and errors at most $delta_(upright("alt"))$, satisfies one of
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.143)")
   $ sup_(B_(r_(*))) \| "grad" z \|_g lt.eq k_(*) \; #h(2em) parallel z - L parallel_(L^oo \( B_(r_(*)) \)) lt.eq r_(*) b_0 \, quad 1 / 2 lt.eq \| D L \| lt.eq 2 \, $ <four:reg:alternative>]
  where $L$ is a coordinate affine function.
  To prove the claim, consider a sequence with errors tending to zero and $inf_(B_(r_(*))) s arrow.r 0$. Weak Harnack for nonnegative scalar divergence supersolutions, applied to (4.142) with its bounded vector and scalar errors, yields for some $q > 0$
  $ (\| B_(1 \/ 2) \|^(- 1) integral_(B_(1 \/ 2)) s^q)^(1 \/ q) lt.eq C (inf_(B_(r_(*))) s + parallel E_1 parallel_oo + parallel e_1 parallel_oo) arrow.r 0 . $
  One obtains these fixed radii from the usual concentric weak Harnack inequality by a finite chain of overlapping balls. Thus $s arrow.r 0$ in measure. The scalar weak Harnack and energy estimates used here are valid for bounded measurable divergence matrices #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")).
  There is also $D s arrow.r 0$ in $L^1$ on smaller balls. Indeed, for a fixed cutoff $zeta$ and $b > 0$, test the weak inequality by $zeta^2 \( b - s \)_(+)$. Discarding its favorable Hessian term and using Young’s inequality gives
  $ integral_({ s < b }) zeta^2 \| D s \|^2 lt.eq C b^2 integral \| D zeta \|^2 + C parallel E_1 parallel_oo^2 + C b #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] parallel E_1 parallel_oo + parallel e_1 parallel_oo #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The full local $L^2$ norm of $D s$ is bounded by (4.139), the gradient bound and the geometry bounds. On ${ s gt.eq b }$, Cauchy–Schwarz and convergence in measure give a vanishing $L^1$ norm. On ${ s < b }$ the displayed estimate bounds the limiting $L^1$ norm by $C b$. Letting $b arrow.b 0$ proves the claim. Testing (4.142) with a fixed nonnegative cutoff equal to one on $B_(1 \/ 4)$ now gives
  $ integral_(B_(1 \/ 4)) \| H_z \|^2 arrow.r 0 . $
  The coordinate Hessian also tends to zero in $L^2$, because the connection coefficients tend to zero and $D z$ is bounded. After subtracting $z \( 0 \)$, the functions are equi-Lipschitz. Poincaré gives convergence of their gradients in $L^2$ to constants, and compactness gives uniform convergence to an affine function on smaller balls. Since $s arrow.r 0$ in measure and the metrics converge to the identity, its slope has length one. If (4.143) failed for every $k_(*) arrow.t 1$ and every tolerance tending to zero, this conclusion would contradict failure of its affine alternative. This proves the claim.
  ======= 4.7.1.4 All derivatives for a fixed exceptional axis. <all-derivatives-for-a-fixed-exceptional-axis.>
  For a constant Euclidean unit vector $e_0$, let
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.144)")
   $ cal(A)_0 Y := Delta_(e_0^perp) Y + tilde(chi) \( x \) partial_(e_0 e_0) Y . $ <four:reg:fixed-axis-operator>]
  If $Y_0 in W^(2 \, 2) \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)$ and $cal(A)_0 Y_0 = 0$ almost everywhere, we claim that, for some $alpha_1 in \( 0 \, 1 \)$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.145)")
   $ parallel D Y_0 parallel_(C^(alpha_1) \( B_(1 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C parallel Y_0 parallel_(W^(2 \, 2) \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)) . $ <four:reg:fixed-axis-estimate>]
  Set $v_1 = partial_(e_0) Y_0 in W^(1 \, 2)$. Differentiating the equation in distributions, with no differentiation of its coefficient as a separate function, gives
  $ Delta_(e_0^perp) v_1 + partial_(e_0) \( tilde(chi) partial_(e_0) v_1 \) = 0 . $
  This is a scalar uniformly elliptic divergence equation. Its local boundedness and Hölder estimate bound $v_1$ in $C^(alpha_1)$. Caccioppoli, subtracting the value at the center of a ball of radius $h$, then gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.146)")
   $ integral_(B_h \( x \)) \| D v_1 \|^2 lt.eq C h^(2 + 2 alpha_1) parallel Y_0 parallel_(W^(2 \, 2) \( B_(3 \/ 4) \))^2 $ <four:reg:fixed-axis-morrey>]
  at all centers in a fixed smaller ball. The power follows from $h^(- 2)$ for the cutoff, $h^(2 alpha_1)$ for the squared oscillation, and volume $h^4$.
  Normalize the norm on the right to one. Since $Delta Y_0 = \( 1 - tilde(chi) \) partial_(e_0) v_1$, it follows that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.147)")
   $ integral_(B_h \( x \)) \| Delta Y_0 \| lt.eq C h^(3 + alpha_1) . $ <four:reg:laplacian-morrey>]
  Let $N$ be the Newtonian potential of $zeta Delta Y_0$, where $zeta$ is supported inside $B_(1 \/ 2)$ and equals one on a slightly smaller ball. The remainder $Y_0 - N$ is harmonic there, with bounded local $L^2$ norm; the potential has such a bound by local integrability of $\| x \|^(- 2)$ and the $L^2$ bound for $Delta Y_0$. For the gradient of $N$, the kernel and its derivative are bounded by $C \| x \|^(- 3)$ and $C \| x \|^(- 4)$. If two points are separated by $h$, the annuli of radii $s lt.eq 4 h$ give, by (4.147), $C sum s^(alpha_1) lt.eq C h^(alpha_1)$. On annuli $s > 4 h$ the kernel difference instead gives $C h sum s^(alpha_1 - 1) lt.eq C h^(alpha_1)$. The sums run over dyadic scales; both converge because $0 < alpha_1 < 1$. The harmonic remainder has bounded smooth derivatives on the final smaller ball. Thus every component of $D Y_0$, and not only $partial_(e_0) Y_0$, satisfies (4.145).
  ======= 4.7.1.5 Improvement of a nonzero affine approximation. <improvement-of-a-nonzero-affine-approximation.-1>
  Suppose on $B_1$ that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.148)")
   $ parallel z - L parallel_oo lt.eq b \, quad 0 < b lt.eq b_0 \, quad 1 / 4 lt.eq \| D L \| lt.eq 4 \, quad \| "grad" z \|_g lt.eq 8 \, $ <four:reg:affine-input>]
  and the source, metric oscillation from the identity and metric first derivatives are at most $b delta_(upright("aff"))$. Put $Y = \( z - L \) \/ b$. Its coordinate equation has uniformly bounded right hand side on $B_1$: the covariant Hessian of $L$ is a Christoffel symbol times its bounded slope, and division by $b$ leaves a bound $C delta_(upright("aff"))$. Consequently (4.139) gives a uniform $W^(2 \, p_0) \( B_(7 \/ 8) \)$ bound for $Y$.
  Let $e_0 = D L \/ \| D L \|$ and let $a_0 = "Id" - \( 1 - tilde(chi) \) e_0 ⊗ e_0$. Since $D z = D L + b D Y$, the set $E_b = { \| b D Y \| > sqrt(b) } ∩ B_(3 \/ 4)$ has measure at most $C b^(p_0 \/ 2)$. On its complement the actual gradient is bounded away from zero and the coordinate principal matrix differs from $a_0$ by at most $C \( sqrt(b) + b delta_(upright("aff")) \)$. On $E_b$ this difference is bounded. Hölder’s inequality, with $1 \/ 2 = 1 \/ p_0 + 1 \/ q$, shows explicitly that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.149)")
   $ parallel a_0 : D^2 Y parallel_(L^2 \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C (delta_(upright("aff")) + sqrt(b_0) + b_0^(\( p_0 - 2 \) \/ 4)) = : epsilon_1 . $ <four:reg:small-fixed-source>]
  Zeros of $D z$ can occur only in the exceptional set when $b_0$ is small. The arbitrary permitted choice of their axis is therefore covered by the same estimate.
  Remove this residual by solving $a_0 : D^2 y_1 = a_0 : D^2 Y$ on $B_(3 \/ 4)$ with zero Dirichlet data. To justify this solve for measurable $tilde(chi)$, recall the integration-by-parts identity on a Euclidean ball for zero-trace $y$:
  $ integral #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( Delta y \)^2 - \| D^2 y \|^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = integral_(partial B) H_(partial B) \( partial_nu y \)^2 gt.eq 0 . $
  It extends by density to $W^(2 \, 2) ∩ W_0^(1 \, 2)$. Hence the Dirichlet inverse of $Delta$ has Hessian norm at most one. On this space the map
  $ y mapsto Delta_D^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a_0 : D^2 Y + \( "Id" - a_0 \) : D^2 y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  is a contraction in the Hessian $L^2$ norm with factor at most $1 - chi_0$. Poincaré and the Dirichlet Laplace estimate identify this as a complete norm and give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.150)")
   $ parallel y_1 parallel_(W^(2 \, 2) \( B_(3 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq C epsilon_1 . $ <four:reg:small-correction>]
  The function $Y_0 = Y - y_1$ has uniformly bounded $W^(2 \, 2)$ norm and satisfies the homogeneous fixed-axis equation. Therefore (4.145) applies to it.
  The passage to a small supremum norm must use a separate estimate in dimension four. Choose any $0 < eta < min { 1 \, 2 - 4 \/ p_0 }$. The $W^(2 \, p_0)$ bound gives a uniform $C^(0 \, eta)$ modulus for $Y$ on smaller balls. The fixed-axis estimate and the local $L^2$ norm give a uniform Lipschitz bound for $Y_0$. Thus $y_1$ has a common $C^(0 \, eta)$ modulus on $B_(1 \/ 4)$. If $m = parallel y_1 parallel_(L^oo \( B_(1 \/ 8) \))$, a ball of radius $c m^(1 \/ eta)$ around a point with absolute value at least $m \/ 2$ has absolute value at least $m \/ 4$, provided $m$ is small. Hence
  $ parallel y_1 parallel_2^2 gt.eq c m^(2 + 4 \/ eta) \, #h(2em) m lt.eq C epsilon_1^(eta \/ \( eta + 2 \)) . $
  If $m$ is not small, the same reasoning on a fixed smaller ball first excludes that case when $epsilon_1$ tends to zero. This argument uses no embedding of $W^(2 \, 2) \( bb(R)^4 \)$ into $C^0$.
  Choose $0 < alpha_2 < alpha_1$, then $0 < lambda_0 < 1 \/ 8$ so that the Taylor remainder constant in (4.145) satisfies $C lambda_0^(1 + alpha_1) lt.eq 1 / 2 lambda_0^(1 + alpha_2)$. Next choose $delta_(upright("aff")) \, b_0 > 0$ so that the correction supremum above is at most $1 / 2 lambda_0^(1 + alpha_2)$. Taylor approximation of $Y_0$ at the center gives an affine $L_1$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.151)")
   $ parallel z - L_1 parallel_(L^oo \( B_(lambda_0) \)) lt.eq b lambda_0^(1 + alpha_2) \, #h(2em) \| D L_1 - D L \| lt.eq C b . $ <four:reg:affine-improvement>]
  Reduce $b_0$ further so that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.152)")
   $ C b_0 \/ \( 1 - lambda_0^(alpha_2) \) < 1 \/ 4 . $ <four:reg:slope-sum>]
  These estimates concern the almost everywhere coordinate equation on full balls and therefore apply equally to doubled patches.
  ======= 4.7.1.6 The complete scaling iteration. <the-complete-scaling-iteration.>
  The choices are ordered as follows: first $p_0 \, alpha_1$ and their constants; then $alpha_2 \, lambda_0 \, delta_(upright("aff")) \, b_0$; then $k_(*) \, delta_(upright("alt"))$ from (4.143) for that $b_0$. Finally shrink the initial spatial scale so that its normalized source and geometry errors are bounded by $delta$, where
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.153)")
   $ delta lt.eq c min { delta_(upright("alt")) \, b_0 delta_(upright("aff")) \/ r_(*) } . $ <four:reg:initial-tolerance>]
  Here the fixed $c > 0$ incorporates all comparison constants in the geometry rescalings. This scale depends only on the theorem’s parameters, since division by $M_1 + N_1$ makes the initial source at most one. Write the resulting function as $u$ and center all following balls at zero.
  After $m$ successive gradient drops the actual rescaled solution is
  $ u_m \( x \) = frac(u \( r_(*)^m x \) - u \( 0 \), r_(*)^m k_(*)^m) \, #h(2em) \| "grad" u_m \|_(g_m) lt.eq 1 \, #h(2em) parallel G_m parallel_oo lt.eq delta \( r_(*) \/ k_(*) \)^m . $
  The metric remains the identity at the center; its oscillation and first derivative errors are at most $C delta r_(*)^m$, with its curvature error shrinking at least as fast. A reflecting plane has the correspondingly scaled second fundamental form. Since $r_(*) < k_(*)$, the next application of the alternative is legitimate. If the affine alternative first occurs after these $m$ drops, rescale its ball by setting $v_0 \( x \) = u_m \( r_(*) x \) \/ r_(*)$. Then $v_0$ has affine error at most $b_0$, slope in $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 2 \]$, bounded gradient, and source at most
  $ r_(*) delta \( r_(*) \/ k_(*) \)^m lt.eq b_0 delta_(upright("aff")) . $
  Its geometry satisfies the same tolerance by (4.153).
  In all subsequent affine steps use
  $ v_i \( x \) = lambda_0^(- i) v_0 \( lambda_0^i x \) \, #h(2em) b_i = b_0 lambda_0^(i alpha_2) . $
  The derivative $D v_i \( x \) = D v_0 \( lambda_0^i x \)$ retains the actual gradient bound. The source and first order geometry errors are at most $b_0 delta_(upright("aff")) lambda_0^i lt.eq b_i delta_(upright("aff"))$, because $alpha_2 < 1$. The affine function used at stage $i$ is rescaled in the same way, including its constant term. Thus (4.151) applies inductively. Its changes of slope sum to less than $1 \/ 4$ by (4.152), so all slopes remain in $\[ 1 \/ 4 \, 4 \]$. Only the auxiliary residual in the proof of an improvement is divided by $b_i$; that residual is never asserted to solve the original nonlinear axial equation.
  Choose
  $ 0 < alpha lt.eq min { alpha_2 \, log k_(*) \/ log r_(*) } . $
  If the drops never end, the gradient on $B_(r_(*)^m)$ is at most $k_(*)^m lt.eq r_(*)^(m alpha)$, and subtraction of the central value gives a constant affine approximation with error $C r_(*)^(m \( 1 + alpha \))$. If the drops end after $m$ steps, the affine approximation at $R_i = r_(*)^(m + 1) lambda_0^i$ has error at most
  $ b_0 k_(*)^m r_(*)^(m + 1) lambda_0^(i \( 1 + alpha_2 \)) lt.eq b_0 r_(*)^(- alpha) R_i^(1 + alpha) . $
  The previous drop scales have the same type of estimate. Adjacent radii have ratio $r_(*)$ or $lambda_0$, both fixed, so every intermediate radius has an affine approximation with error $C r^(1 + alpha)$. This conclusion holds at every center of the smaller full patches, including centers whose balls cross the plane.
  For clarity, the usual comparison of affine approximations gives the gradient conclusion directly. If $L_r \, L_(r \/ 2)$ are two such approximations, then $parallel L_r - L_(r \/ 2) parallel_(L^oo \( B_(r \/ 2) \)) lt.eq C r^(1 + alpha)$, whence $\| D L_r - D L_(r \/ 2) \| lt.eq C r^alpha$. Their slopes converge, and the limit is $D z$ since $z$ is $C^1$. At points separated by $h$, compare the two approximations on overlapping balls of radius $4 h$; the same affine norm estimate gives a slope difference $C h^alpha$. Their limits differ from those slopes by $C h^alpha$ as well. This proves the first estimate in (4.137).
  Finally take an affine approximation $L$ on $B_r$ with error $C r^(1 + alpha)$. Its $L^2$ error is at most $C r^(3 + alpha)$. The coordinate equation for $z - L$ has right hand side equal to $G$ plus a bounded Christoffel coefficient times $D z$, whose $L^2$ norm is at most $C r^2$ before these iterative rescalings. Equation (4.139) with $p = 2$ therefore gives
  $ parallel D^2 z parallel_(L^2 \( B_(r \/ 2) \)) lt.eq C \( r^(1 + alpha) + r^2 \) . $
  The difference $"Hess"_g z - D^2 z$ has $L^2$ norm at most $C r^2$. Since $alpha < 1$, squaring and restoring $M_1 + N_1$ proves the second estimate in (4.137). ◻
]

===== 4.7.2 A scalar equation with a measure source <a-scalar-equation-with-a-measure-source>

The next lemma describes the second, scalar regularity mechanism. Its hypotheses permit the surface measure produced by a prescribed conormal flux, as well as the squared Hessian supplied by Theorem 132.

#thm([Lemma 4.37.])[
  #emph[On a ball in $bb(R)^4$ let $Z$ be continuous, bounded by $M_Z$, and locally $W^(1 \, 2)$. Suppose 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.154)")
 $ "div" \( cal(A) D Z + B_1 \) = E \, $ <four:reg:natural-equation>]

 where $cal(A)$ is a bounded measurable symmetric matrix with ellipticity constants $0 < lambda lt.eq Lambda$, $B_1$ is bounded, and $E$ is a signed measure satisfying 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.155)")
 $ \| E \| lt.eq C_0 \( 1 + \| D Z \|^2 \) thin thin upright(d) x + mu_1 \, #h(2em) mu_1 \( B_h \( x \) \) lt.eq C_mu h^(2 + beta) \, #h(2em) 0 < beta lt.eq 1 . $ <four:reg:natural-growth-bound>]

 The growth bound is required at every center and sufficiently small radius in a larger patch. Assume the weak product rule used below is valid; in particular it is valid if $Z$ is smooth on the two closed sides of a flat interface, continuous across it, and the equation includes the normal-flux jump. Then $Z$ has a uniform Hölder modulus on every fixed smaller ball. Its constants depend only on $M_Z \, lambda \, Lambda \, C_0 \, C_mu \, beta \, parallel B_1 parallel_oo$ and the separation of the patches.]
] <four:reg:natural-growth>

#proof[
  Put $cal(L) = - "div" \( cal(A) D \)$, and let $q_s = e^(s k Z)$ for $s in { 1 \, - 1 }$. The product rule gives the exact distributional identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.156)")
   $ cal(L) q_s = "div" \( s k q_s B_1 \) - s k q_s E - k^2 q_s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(A) D Z dot.op D Z + B_1 dot.op D Z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:reg:exponential-identity>]
  For verification, $"div" \( cal(A) D Z \) = E - "div" B_1$ and $s k q_s "div" B_1 = "div" \( s k q_s B_1 \) - k^2 q_s B_1 dot.op D Z$. This also checks the sign of the last drift term for both choices of $s$. In the piecewise smooth case, integration on the two sides gives this rule including the plane measure, since $q_s$ is continuous and multiplies the flux jump by its common trace.
  By ellipticity and Young’s inequality,
  $ - k^2 q_s cal(A) D Z dot.op D Z - k^2 q_s B_1 dot.op D Z lt.eq - 1 / 2 k^2 lambda q_s \| D Z \|^2 + frac(k^2 q_s, 2 lambda) \| B_1 \|^2 . $
  Choose $k gt.eq max { 1 \, 4 C_0 \/ lambda }$. The term $k C_0 q_s \| D Z \|^2$ coming from $- s k q_s E$ is then absorbed. The bounded range of $Z$ therefore gives, for both signs,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.157)")
   $ cal(L) q_s lt.eq "div" F_s + mu_2 \, #h(2em) parallel F_s parallel_oo lt.eq C \, #h(2em) mu_2 = C \( thin upright(d) x + mu_1 \) gt.eq 0 . $ <four:reg:exponential-inequality>]
  In particular the gradient-square term has been eliminated before any estimate for its small-ball integral is used.
  On $B_r$ solve with zero Dirichlet boundary value
  $ cal(L) h_s = "div" F_s + mu_2 . $
  The correction exists in $W_0^(1 \, 2) \( B_r \)$ and satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.158)")
   $ parallel h_s parallel_oo lt.eq C \( r + r^beta \) = : delta_r . $ <four:reg:potential-correction>]
  Here are the details of the measure part of this assertion. Extend $cal(A)$ measurably with the same ellipticity to $B_(2 r)$. The Green upper bound for symmetric uniformly elliptic divergence operators with bounded measurable coefficients, together with domain monotonicity, gives
  $ 0 lt.eq G_(B_r) \( x \, y \) lt.eq G_(B_(2 r)) \( x \, y \) lt.eq C \| x - y \|^(- 2) \, #h(2em) x \, y in B_r . $
  This is the scalar Green comparison theorem of Littman–Stampacchia–Weinberger #cite(label("LittmanStampacchiaWeinberger1963")) Theorem 7.1 and Remark 2, p. 66; using the larger ball keeps the two points uniformly away from its boundary. Dyadic annuli and (4.155) give
  $ sup_(x in B_r) integral_(B_r) G_(B_r) \( x \, y \) thin thin upright(d) mu_2 \( y \) & lt.eq C sum_(j gt.eq 0) \( 2^(- j) r \)^(- 2) mu_2 \( B_(2^(1 - j) r) \( x \) \)\
 & lt.eq C sum_(j gt.eq 0) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 2^(- j) r \)^2 + \( 2^(- j) r \)^beta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq C \( r^2 + r^beta \) . $
  The finitely many largest annuli are absorbed in the same bound. All ball-growth assertions needed here hold on the fixed larger patch.
  For completeness this potential also defines an energy solution. Mollify the restriction of $mu_2$ to $B_r$, and restrict the resulting smooth nonnegative density again to $B_r$. The growth bound is uniform under mollification: for radii larger than the mollifying radius use an enlarged ball, and for smaller radii use the bound for the mollified density. The variational Dirichlet solutions have the preceding uniform supremum bound and satisfy
  $ lambda integral_(B_r) \| D h_j \|^2 lt.eq integral_(B_r) h_j thin thin upright(d) mu_(2 \, j) lt.eq parallel h_j parallel_oo mu_(2 \, j) \( B_r \) . $
  Weak compactness in $W_0^(1 \, 2)$ and distributional convergence give the desired solution with measure source and the same bound. The bounded divergence source has a variational solution and, for any fixed $p > 4$, the scalar bounded-solution estimate gives
  $ parallel h_(F_s) parallel_oo lt.eq C r^(1 - 4 \/ p) parallel F_s parallel_(L^p \( B_r \)) lt.eq C r . $
  This follows as well by scaling the zero-boundary divergence-source estimate in #cite(label("LittmanStampacchiaWeinberger1963")) Theorem 2.6, p. 50; see also #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")). Adding these two solutions proves (4.158).
  Set $M_s = sup_(B_r) q_s$. By (4.157),
  $ W_s = M_s - q_s + h_s + delta_r gt.eq 0 \, #h(2em) cal(L) W_s gt.eq 0 . $
  Write $M = sup_(B_r) Z$, $m = inf_(B_r) Z$ and $omega = M - m$. At least half of $B_(r \/ 2)$ satisfies either $Z lt.eq \( M + m \) \/ 2$ or $Z gt.eq \( M + m \) \/ 2$. In the first case select $s = 1$; in the second select $s = - 1$. On that set $M_s - q_s gt.eq c omega$, because the magnitudes of both exponential derivatives are bounded above and away from zero on $\[ - M_Z \, M_Z \]$. Since $h_s + delta_r gt.eq 0$, weak Harnack for $W_s$ gives
  $ inf_(B_(r \/ 4)) \( M_s - q_s \) gt.eq c' omega - 2 delta_r . $
  In the first case this decreases the supremum of $Z$; in the second it increases the infimum. Converting back with the same exponential derivative bounds yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.159)")
   $ "osc"_(B_(r \/ 4)) Z lt.eq \( 1 - c'' \) "osc"_(B_r) Z + C \( r + r^beta \) \, #h(2em) c'' > 0 . $ <four:reg:oscillation-decay>]
  Choose $gamma > 0$ with $gamma < beta$ and $1 - c'' < 4^(- gamma)$. Iterating (4.159) on $r \, r \/ 4 \, r \/ 16 \, dots.h$ gives a convergent geometric series after division by the corresponding $gamma$ power of the radius. Consequently $"osc"_(B_h \( x \)) Z lt.eq C h^gamma$ at all centers in smaller balls. This proves the lemma. ◻
]

===== 4.7.3 Application to the homotopy and the boundary estimates <application-to-the-homotopy-and-the-boundary-estimates>

#thm([Proposition 4.38.])[
  #emph[Fix the prepared data, $epsilon.alt$, a sufficiently large $n$ and any stage of the homotopy. Every smooth solution on an allowed finite truncation $Omega_R$ with $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$ has uniform local estimates for every finite number of derivatives of $f$ and $Z$, up to both the inner and outer boundary faces. The constants on the fixed compact part and on uniform unit patches of the end are independent of $R$ and of the homotopy parameters. They may depend arbitrarily on $n$. On each fixed finite truncation they therefore bound $parallel Z parallel_(C^(1 \, alpha_0))$ for every prescribed $0 < alpha_0 < 1$.]
] <four:prop:coupled-regularity>

#proof[
  We use the uniform family of interior and boundary patches described in the statement, shrinking them by fixed factors when necessary.
  ======= 4.7.3.1 The first Hölder estimates and the reflected equation. <the-first-hölder-estimates-and-the-reflected-equation.>
  The bounded range (4.131) keeps $f \, Z \, t \, "grad" f$ bounded and $l \, d \, chi \, u$ above positive constants depending on $n$. Divide the trace equation by $l \/ sqrt(D)$ to obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.160)")
   $ A_chi : "Hess"_g f = G_f \, #h(2em) G_f = sqrt(D) / l #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] F_(upright("presc")) - "tr"_(A_chi) K #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
\| G_f \| lt.eq C \( n \) \, #h(2em) 0 < chi_0 \( n \) lt.eq chi lt.eq 1 . $ <four:reg:trace-normalized>]
  All prescribed trace expressions have bounded values on this range. Every boundary value of $f$ is constant on its face. Thus Theorem 132 gives a Hölder bound for $D f$ and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.161)")
   $ integral_(B_r \( x \) ∩ Omega_R) \| D_x^2 f \|^2 thin thin upright(d) x lt.eq C \( n \) r^(2 + 2 alpha) . $ <four:reg:f-morrey>]
  The coordinate and covariant Hessians differ by a bounded Christoffel term times $D f$, so they have the same bound at these radii. The bound also holds for the odd double at a face.
  In coordinates set
  $ cal(A)^(i j) = 3 sqrt(det g) thin u thin A_chi^(i j) \, #h(2em) B_1^i = lambda sqrt(det g) thin u thin #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^i \, $
  using $lambda = 0$ in the later stages. The coordinate $Z$ equation is (4.154), with these bounded coefficients and the appropriate scalar homotopy factor in $E$. Its matrix is symmetric and uniformly elliptic at fixed $n$. Differentiating the implicit relation for $t$ gives the exact covector identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.162)")
   $ 3 thin thin upright(d) Z = \( 3 + p v \) thin thin upright(d) t + H^f \( w \, dot.op \) . $ <four:reg:t-differential>]
  Since $3 + p v gt.eq 3$ and all its coefficients are bounded on the range, $\| D_x t \| lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + \| D_x Z \| + \| D_x^2 f \| \)$ in coordinates. The quadratic formula (4.76) and the bounded lower order terms in the source therefore give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.163)")
   $ \| E \| lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + \| D_x Z \|^2 + \| D_x^2 f \|^2 \) $ <four:reg:coupled-source>]
  in the interior. No derivative of the principal matrix has been used to obtain this bound.
  At a face take the domain to be $x_4 > 0$ and write $Q = cal(A) D Z + B_1$. For $x_4 < 0$ define, with $s = 1$ or $- 1$,
  $ tilde(Z) \( x \) = s Z \( R_4 x \) \, quad tilde(cal(A)) \( x \) = R_4 cal(A) \( R_4 x \) R_4 \, quad tilde(B)_1 \( x \) = s R_4 B_1 \( R_4 x \) . $
  The reflected flux is $tilde(Q) \( x \) = s R_4 Q \( R_4 x \)$. Integration by parts on the two sides shows that its divergence has the reflected bulk source $s E \( R_4 x \)$ and the plane term
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.164)")
   $ \( 1 + s \) Q_4^(+) thin thin upright(d) cal(H)^3 \|_({ x_4 = 0 }) . $ <four:reg:reflection-measure>]
  Indeed the upper normal component is $Q_4^(+)$ and the lower one is $- s Q_4^(+)$, so their difference is $\( 1 + s \) Q_4^(+)$. For an inner face use even reflection, $s = 1$. The total conormal flux is prescribed by (4.91); its coordinate density $Q_4^(+)$ is bounded on the established range. For an outer face use odd reflection, $s = - 1$. The zero Dirichlet condition makes $Z$ continuous across the face, and the plane term vanishes. Even reflection at the inner face is also continuous. Each individual reflected function belongs to $W^(1 \, 2)$ and is smooth on the two closed sides, which verifies the weak chain rule required in Lemma 133.
  In the full patch, (4.163) and (4.164) give a signed measure dominated by
  $ C \( n \) \( 1 + \| D_x Z \|^2 \) thin thin upright(d) x + mu_1 \, #h(2em) mu_1 = C \( n \) \| D_x^2 f \|^2 thin thin upright(d) x + 2 \| Q_4^(+) \| thin thin upright(d) cal(H)^3 \|_({ x_4 = 0 }) \, $
  where the Hessian density is reflected and the plane term is omitted for an odd face or an interior patch. By (4.161), this positive measure satisfies, at every center,
  $ mu_1 \( B_h \( x \) \) lt.eq C \( n \) h^(2 + beta) \, #h(2em) beta = min { 2 alpha \, 1 } > 0 . $
  For balls crossing the plane this follows either from the doubled estimate or by enlarging a ball centered on the plane by a fixed factor. Lemma 133 now supplies a uniform Hölder modulus for $Z$, including at both boundary types.
  ======= 4.7.3.2 Schauder for $f$ and the ordinary normal condition for $Z$. <schauder-for-f-and-the-ordinary-normal-condition-for-z.>
  The actual coefficients in (4.160) are smooth functions of $\( x \, Z \, D f \)$ on the bounded range. This remains true at $D f = 0$, as is manifest from
  $ A_chi = "Id" - frac(3 + p, 3 + p v) w ⊗ w . $
  The source is smooth in these variables and $f$, uniformly over the four parameter ranges. The Hölder bounds just proved therefore make the coefficients and source uniformly $C^(0 \, theta)$ for some $theta > 0$. The scalar Dirichlet Schauder estimate, with the constant face data, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.165)")
   $ parallel f parallel_(C^(2 \, theta) \( U' \)) lt.eq C \( n \) $ <four:reg:f-schauder>]
  on every smaller interior or boundary patch #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")). This application has Hölder principal coefficients, a Hölder right hand side, smooth boundary, and uniform ellipticity; it does not apply a system estimate.
  Now expand the divergence equation for $Z$. Derivatives of its coefficients, which depend smoothly on $\( x \, Z \, D f \)$, are sums of bounded terms and bounded multiples of $D Z \, D^2 f$. The latter Hessian is bounded by (4.165). Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.166)")
   $ a_2^(i j) \( x \) D_(i j) Z = R_2 \, #h(2em) \| R_2 \| lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + \| D Z \|^2 \) \, $ <four:reg:Z-nondivergence>]
  with bounded $C^(0 \, theta)$ uniformly elliptic principal coefficients. The source contains no second derivative of $Z$.
  On an inner face $D f$ is normal and hence $A_chi nu = chi nu$. Dividing the prescribed flux by $3 u chi > 0$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.167)")
   $ partial_nu Z = b_2 \( x \, Z \, f \, D f \) . $ <four:reg:normal-data>]
  The function $b_2$ is smooth on the bounded range, separately on each homotopy stage, with uniform bounds through their matching endpoints. Extend its displayed expression smoothly in the spatial variable into the collar. Equation (4.165) then gives
  $ \| D_x b_2 \( x \, Z \, f \, D f \) \| lt.eq C \( n \) \( 1 + \| D_x Z \| \) . $
  Consequently the Sobolev trace theorem bounds the $W^(1 - 1 \/ p \, p)$ norm of this normal datum by $C \( n \) \( 1 + parallel Z parallel_(W^(1 \, p)) \)$ on a larger patch, for each finite $p > 1$. This derivative count costs $D Z$ and $D^2 f$, never $D^2 Z$.
  ======= 4.7.3.3 Linear estimates and absorption of the quadratic term. <linear-estimates-and-absorption-of-the-quadratic-term.>
  Fix $p > 4$. On a ball or smooth half-patch of size $h$, the local linear $W^(2 \, p)$ estimate for (4.166), with zero outer Dirichlet data or (4.167), has the form
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.168)")
   $ parallel D^2 Z parallel_(L^p \( Q_h \)) lt.eq C_(*) parallel D Z parallel_(L^(2 p) \( Q_(2 h) \))^2 + C_h #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + parallel Z parallel_(W^(1 \, p) \( Q_(2 h) \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:reg:Z-linear>]
  Here $C_(*)$ is independent of sufficiently small $h$; $C_h$ may contain inverse powers of $h$. The constants are uniform over the locations and the truncations in question.
  We specify the linear estimate underlying this assertion. Freeze the continuous principal matrix at the patch center. The interior constant-coefficient Hessian estimate follows from the Euclidean one by a fixed linear change of coordinates. At the boundary, normal coordinates give no mixed normal–tangential entries in the frozen matrix, because its exceptional axis is normal there. A tangential linear transformation and a normal dilation reduce the frozen principal part to the Laplacian and preserve the plane. Subtract a Sobolev extension of the normal datum, or of the Dirichlet datum, to obtain homogeneous boundary data. Even reflection for homogeneous normal data and odd reflection for homogeneous Dirichlet data give the constant-coefficient half-space estimate. The extension costs precisely the $W^(1 - 1 \/ p \, p)$ norm for the normal datum. Coefficient oscillation contributes $sup \| a_2 - a_2 \( x_0 \) \| thin parallel D^2 Z parallel_p$ and is absorbed on small patches, uniformly by the established Hölder bound. Cutoffs and interpolation handle the first order terms. Scaling shows that the coefficient of the interior $L^p$ source norm is the constant $C_(*)$, independent of $h$; the lower order and trace terms account for $C_h$. This proves (4.168) by the scalar local estimates #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")). The construction requires no meeting of different boundary types, and the inner and outer faces here are disjoint.
  For use in its nonlinear right hand side, the scaled scalar interpolation inequality on $Q_h$ is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.169)")
   $ parallel D Z parallel_(L^(2 p) \( Q_h \))^2 lt.eq C thin "osc"_(Q_h) Z thin parallel D^2 Z parallel_(L^p \( Q_h \)) + C h^(4 \/ p - 2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "osc"_(Q_h) Z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^2 . $ <four:reg:quadratic-interpolation>]
  One can obtain it on the unit patch by subtracting a constant, using a bounded Sobolev extension on a smooth ball or half-patch, and applying the Gagliardo–Nirenberg inequality with derivative orders $1 \, 2$, exponents $2 p \, p \, oo$ and parameter $1 \/ 2$ #cite(label("LiZhang2022")) Theorem 1.3. To see the interpolation mechanism directly for a compactly supported function $v$, integration by parts gives
  $ integral \| D v \|^(2 p) lt.eq C_p parallel v parallel_oo integral \| D v \|^(2 p - 2) \| D^2 v \| lt.eq C_p parallel v parallel_oo (integral \| D v \|^(2 p))^(1 - 1 \/ p) parallel D^2 v parallel_p . $
  Division gives the required product estimate. Extension and cutoff produce the additional $parallel v parallel_oo^2$ term on a bounded unit patch. Finally rescaling $x = h y$ multiplies a squared $L^(2 p)$ gradient norm and the Hessian product by $h^(4 \/ p - 2)$, which proves precisely the last power in (4.169).
  Here is a cover argument that absorbs larger-patch norms without assuming their uniform boundedness in advance. Use uniformly comparable patches of radius $h$ covering the finite truncation, and their fixed-factor enlargements. Each enlargement is covered by at most $N$ smaller patches, where $N$ is independent of $h$, $R$ and the total number of patches. Let
  $ S = sup_j parallel D^2 Z parallel_(L^p \( Q_h^j \)) . $
  For each individual smooth solution on the finite truncation, $S$ is finite. The Hölder estimate for $Z$ gives $"osc"_(Q_(2 h)^j) Z lt.eq C \( n \) h^gamma$. Apply (4.169) in the enlarged patches and then cover them by smaller ones. Its contribution to (4.168) is at most $C_(*) C \( n \) h^gamma N^(1 \/ p) S + C \( n \, h \)$. Choose $h$ small enough, also satisfying the linear freezing condition, that this coefficient is at most $1 \/ 4$. For the remaining first order term, local interpolation gives, for any $epsilon > 0$,
  $ parallel D Z parallel_(L^p \( Q_(2 h)^j \)) lt.eq epsilon parallel D^2 Z parallel_(L^p \( Q_(2 h)^j \)) + C \( h \, epsilon \) parallel Z parallel_(L^p \( Q_(2 h)^j \)) . $
  Choose $epsilon$ so that $C_h epsilon N^(1 \/ p) lt.eq 1 \/ 4$. The bounded range controls the last term. Taking the supremum in (4.168) now gives $S lt.eq C \( n \, h \) + S \/ 2$, and hence a uniform bound for $S$. First order interpolation supplies the corresponding local $W^(2 \, p)$ bounds for $Z$. All constants may depend on the now fixed $h$ and on $n$, but not on the total number of patches or $R$.
  ======= 4.7.3.4 Alternating Schauder estimates and uniform geometry. <alternating-schauder-estimates-and-uniform-geometry.>
  Since $p > 4$, Sobolev embedding gives $Z in C^(1 \, theta')$ for some $theta' > 0$. In conjunction with (4.165), the trace equation now has $C^(1 \, theta'')$ coefficients and source for $0 < theta'' lt.eq min { theta \, theta' }$. One differentiation and the scalar Dirichlet Schauder estimate give $f in C^(3 \, theta'')$. The equation for $Z$ then has $C^(0 \, theta'')$ source, and its normal datum (4.167) is $C^(1 \, theta'')$. The scalar normal-derivative or Dirichlet Schauder estimate gives $Z in C^(2 \, theta'')$. The boundary estimate is justified by the same frozen half-space problem used above: its normal derivative does not annihilate a nonzero decaying solution of the homogeneous principal equation. Smooth normal coordinates and smooth normal field therefore satisfy its obliqueness condition.
  Repeating in the order $f$ then $Z$ increases the number of derivatives by one each time. The trace equation uses $Z$ without derivatives; the expanded second equation uses only $D^2 f$ and $D Z$ in its lower terms; and its boundary expression uses only $D f$. These are exactly the derivative counts needed for the iteration. The smooth fixed coefficients give all finite orders.
  The compact inner faces have uniform smooth collars. On the prepared end, the metric and all fixed coefficient fields have uniform bounds on unit patches. Large coordinate spheres have boundary normal charts with uniform bounds of each finite order, and are separated from the inner faces. Thus every radius, ellipticity bound, chart constant and local covering number used above is independent of the sufficiently large truncation radius. Finally a uniform local $C^2$ bound controls any prescribed $C^(1 \, alpha_0)$ norm, $alpha_0 < 1$, on a fixed finite truncation. This proves the proposition. ◻
]

#prodef([Remark 4.39.])[
  Theorem 132 also applies in the scalar trial problem below. Its separate height and gradient bounds give bounded source and a positive axial eigenvalue without the floor. Interpolation of the principal matrix with $"Id"$ preserves its three unit transverse eigenvalues. A trial $Z in C^(1 \, alpha_0)$ then supplies the coefficient regularity for scalar Schauder estimates through $f in C^(3 \, alpha_0)$; this requires $D Z$, but no second derivative of the trial input.
] <four:reg:scalar-trial-interface>

==== 4.8 Existence and the energy comparison <four:sec:existence>

We now construct solutions of the system, exhaust the asymptotically flat end, and compare the resulting Riemannian energy with the energy of the prepared data. The distinction between two kinds of constants is essential. Constants in the scalar solution map and in local regularity may depend arbitrarily on a fixed $n$; constants used in the final flux estimate are the polynomial bounds $Pi_n$ established earlier.

===== 4.8.1 A scalar solution map on the whole trial space <a-scalar-solution-map-on-the-whole-trial-space>

Fix $0 < alpha < 1$, a sufficiently large integer $n$, and an allowed truncation $Omega_R$. In this subsection constants may depend on $R$, $n$, and a bound for the trial input in

$ X_R = { Z in C^(1 \, alpha) \( overline(Omega)_R \) : Z \|_(S_R) = 0 } . $

The four stages of the homotopy are parametrized consecutively by $s in \[ 0 \, 4 \]$, with $s = 0$ the desired system and $s = 4$ its zero terminal scale. Denote their trace prescriptions by $F_s^(upright("presc")) \( x \, w \, h \, t \)$. All dependence suppressed in this notation is the smooth dependence specified in the homotopy.

#thm([Lemma 4.40.])[
  (Scalar trial problem).  #emph[For every $Z in X_R$ and $s in \[ 0 \, 4 \]$, the prescribed trace equation with the assigned constant Dirichlet value on every boundary component has a unique solution $f_s \[ Z \] in C^(3 \, alpha) \( overline(Omega)_R \)$. The map $\( s \, Z \) mapsto f_s \[ Z \]$ is continuous into $C^(3 \, alpha)$ and maps bounded trial sets to bounded sets, uniformly in $s$. No lower bound for the implicitly defined $t$ is imposed on the trial inputs.]
] <four:ex:scalar-trial>

#proof[
  The normalized equation is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.170)")
   $ A_chi : "Hess"_g f = G_s \( x \, Z \, f \, thin upright(d) f \) \, #h(2em) G_s = sqrt(D) / l #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] F_s^(upright("presc")) - "tr"_(A_chi) K #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <four:ex:scalar-normalized>]
  The implicit relation defining $t$ is smoothly solvable for every $\( Z \, thin upright(d) f \)$. Neither $t$, the matrix, nor the positive prefactor $sqrt(D) \/ l$ depends on the value of $f$. The prescriptions satisfy
  $ \| F_s^(upright("presc")) - h \| lt.eq C \, #h(2em) partial_h F_s^(upright("presc")) gt.eq 1 \, #h(2em) h = eta_n f \, $
  with the other arguments held fixed. For a second parameter $beta in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$, use the scalar interpolation
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.171)")
   $ A_beta : "Hess"_g f = G_beta \, quad A_beta = \( 1 - beta \) "Id" + beta A_chi \, quad G_beta = \( 1 - beta \) eta_n f + beta G_s \, $ <four:ex:scalar-interpolation>]
  with the same Dirichlet values. At a positive interior maximum above a fixed height threshold, both source summands are positive, whereas the left side is nonpositive. The negative minimum argument is identical with signs reversed. Including the assigned boundary values gives $\| h \| lt.eq C$, uniformly in both parameters and the bounded trial set.
  We next check the degeneracy at large gradient, before invoking any regularity theorem. Put $sigma = \| thin upright(d) f \|_g$. Uniformly for bounded $Z$, $sigma arrow.r oo$ forces $t arrow.r - oo$: a lower bound on $t$ would bound $e^(6 t) l \( t \)^2$ away from zero, contradicting $e^(6 Z) = e^(6 t) \( 1 + l \( t \)^2 sigma^2 \)$. Thus eventually $t < 0$ and $l \( t \) = e^(n t)$, giving the exact equation
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.172)")
   $ e^(6 Z) = e^(6 t) + e^(\( 2 n + 6 \) t) sigma^2 . $ <four:ex:large-gradient-equation>]
  Writing $gamma_n = 6 \/ \( n + 3 \)$, its consequences, uniformly for $Z$ in a fixed bounded interval, are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.173)")
   $ t & = - frac(log sigma, n + 3) + frac(3 Z, n + 3) + o \( 1 \) \,\
d & = e^(- 6 n Z \/ \( n + 3 \)) sigma^(- gamma_n) \( 1 + o \( 1 \) \) \, & chi & = frac(3, n + 3) d \( 1 + o \( 1 \) \) \,\
sqrt(D) / l & = sigma \( 1 + o \( 1 \) \) . $ <four:ex:large-gradient>]
  For example, the first line follows by taking logarithms of $e^(\( 2 n + 6 \) t) sigma^2 = e^(6 Z) \( 1 - d \)$; the last follows directly from $sqrt(D) \/ l = \( sigma^2 + l^(- 2) \)^(1 \/ 2)$. Since $n gt.eq 4$, one has $gamma_n < 1$. Compactness of the remaining bounded-gradient range therefore gives constants $c \, C > 0$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.174)")
   $ chi_beta := 1 - beta + beta chi gt.eq frac(c, 1 + sigma) \, #h(2em) \| G_beta \| lt.eq C \( 1 + sigma \) . $ <four:ex:linear-growth>]
  The estimate for the source uses the height bound just proved. Notice also the exact structural identity
  $ A_beta = "Id" - \( 1 - chi_beta \) e ⊗ e \, #h(2em) e = "grad" f \/ \| "grad" f \| \, #h(2em) 0 < chi_beta lt.eq 1 . $
  In particular, all three transverse eigenvalues remain exactly one throughout the scalar interpolation.
  Here is a gradient estimate using only (4.174). Extend $g$ smoothly across the boundary to a compact enlargement. All distances below are distances of this extension, at scales below its injectivity radius. If $H_0$ is the uniform bound for $\| f \|$, set
  $ psi \( r \) = 1 / k log \( 1 + B' r \) \, #h(2em) q_1 \( r \) = psi' \( r \) \, #h(2em) psi'' \( r \) = - k q_1 \( r \)^2 . $
  First choose $k$ large relative to the constants in (4.174) and the bounded geometry. Next choose $h_0 < 1 \/ \( 4 k \)$ below all collar, injectivity, and distinct-boundary-component separation scales. Finally choose $B'$ so large that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.175)")
   $ q_1 gt.eq 1 quad \( 0 lt.eq r lt.eq h_0 \) \, #h(2em) psi \( h_0 \) > 2 H_0 . $ <four:ex:log-choices>]
  These requirements are compatible: $q_1 \( h_0 \)$ tends to $1 \/ \( k h_0 \) > 4$ and $psi \( h_0 \)$ tends to infinity as $B' arrow.r oo$.
  On the inward collar of a boundary face with constant value $f_0$, let $r$ be its distance function. The functions $f_0 plus.minus psi \( r \)$ are upper and lower barriers. To check the upper barrier at a hypothetical positive maximum of $f - f_0 - psi \( r \)$, the common gradient is $q_1 "grad" r$. The tangential Hessian of the barrier contributes at most $C q_1$, and its axial contribution is at most $- c k q_1^2 \/ \( 1 + q_1 \)$. By enlarging $k$ this is strictly less than $- C \( 1 + q_1 \)$, the lower bound for the equation’s source at the solution height. Ellipticity and the second derivative test contradict the equation. For the lower barrier both inequalities reverse. At the outer edge of the collar the comparisons follow from (4.175). Consequently
