===== 4.8.2 The frozen return map and degree <the-frozen-return-map-and-degree>

For a trial pair $\( f_s \[ Z \] \, Z \)$, evaluate all expressions in the homotopy at that pair. Write $A = u A_chi$, let $B_s = lambda u A_chi K \( w \, dot.op \)$ be its drift, and write $Q_s$ and $q_s$ for the prescribed interior source and inner flux, including the common terminal scale. Define $T_s Z = Y$ by the linear problem

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.177)")
 $ "div"_g \( 3 A "grad" Y + B_s \) & = Q_s &  & upright("in ") Omega_R \,\
\( 3 A "grad" Y + B_s \) dot.op nu & = q_s &  & upright("on ") B \,\
Y & = 0 &  & upright("on ") S_R . $ <four:ex:frozen-problem>]

Thus only the occurrence of $3 "grad" Z$ in the flux is replaced by $3 "grad" Y$; the coefficients, source, and boundary expression are frozen. In $q_s$ the value of $F$ is always its trace prescription.

The matrix $A$ is symmetric positive definite and $C^(1 \, alpha)$. On bounded trial sets its ellipticity constants, $C^(1 \, alpha)$ norm, and those of $B_s \, q_s$ are bounded; $Q_s$ is bounded in $C^(0 \, alpha)$. In particular the source uses $thin upright(d) Z$ and $"Hess" f$, whose regularity is already available. With

$ cal(H)_R = { Y in H^1 \( Omega_R \) : Y \|_(S_R) = 0 } \, $

the weak problem has coercive bilinear form $3 integral_(Omega_R) A "grad" Y dot.op "grad" phi$. Indeed $Omega_R$ is connected and $S_R$ is nonempty, so Poincaré’s inequality holds on $cal(H)_R$. The prescribed flux is a bounded linear functional by the trace inequality. Lax–Milgram gives a unique weak solution. At an inner face $f$ is constant, so $A_chi nu = chi nu$, and its flux condition is equivalently

$ partial_nu Y = frac(q_s - B_s dot.op nu, 3 u chi) . $

This is a $C^(1 \, alpha)$ ordinary normal datum. The disjoint Dirichlet and normal faces have no corners. Linear boundary and interior estimates give $Y in C^(2 \, alpha)$ with bounds uniform on bounded trial sets; the weak energy estimate supplies the lower norm in these estimates. Coefficient dependence and uniqueness, or the same linear estimates applied to differences, give continuous dependence. Consequently

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.178)")
 $ T : \[ 0 \, 4 \] times X_R arrow.r X_R quad upright("is continuous and compact on bounded sets") . $ <four:ex:compact-map>]

Here compactness uses the compact embedding $C^(2 \, alpha) arrow.r.hook C^(1 \, alpha)$ on the fixed truncation.

Every fixed point is smooth. Initially it has $Z in C^(2 \, alpha)$ and $f in C^(3 \, alpha)$. The trace equation raises the regularity of $f$, and then expansion of the divergence equation, with its normal boundary datum, raises the regularity of $Z$. Repetition gives all orders. The smooth a priori estimates from the preceding sections therefore apply to every fixed point.

#thm([Proposition 4.41.])[
  (Existence and exhaustion).  #emph[Fix the prepared geometry and $0 < epsilon.alt < 1$. For every sufficiently large $n$ and every allowed $R$, system (4.90) has a smooth solution on $overline(Omega)_R$ with $t > - epsilon.alt$. For each such fixed $n$ there is a sequence $R arrow.r oo$ whose solutions converge smoothly on compact subsets up to $B$ to a solution on $overline(Omega)$ with $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$. This solution satisfies the prescribed inner boundary conditions, the height interval (4.119), and the weighted trace and flux estimates of Lemma 128.]
] <four:prop:existence>

#proof[
  Choose $n$ large enough for Proposition 126, the collar estimates, and the polynomial absorption conditions used in Proposition 131. Fix an allowed $R$. By (4.131) and Proposition 134, choose $M_0$ strictly larger than every $C^(1 \, alpha)$ norm of an above-floor fixed point on this truncation. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.179)")
   $ cal(O)_s = {Z in X_R : parallel Z parallel_(C^(1 \, alpha)) < M_0 \, quad min_(overline(Omega)_R) t #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Z \, thin upright(d) f_s \[ Z \] #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] > - epsilon.alt} . $ <four:ex:moving-sections>]
  Lemma 136 and smooth implicit dependence of $t$ make their union relatively open in $\[ 0 \, 4 \] times X_R$.
  For clarity, the varying domain causes no extra degree assumption. Let $cal(K)$ consist of fixed points in the closure of this union with norm at most $M_0$. A sequence in $cal(K)$ has a convergent subsequence because of (4.178); continuity makes its limit a fixed point. The limit has $min t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$. Proposition 125 excludes equality, and the choice of $M_0$ excludes contact with the norm boundary. Thus $cal(K)$ is compact and lies in the open union of the sections.
  Fix a parameter $s_0$. Cover its compact fixed point slice by finitely many small open balls in $X_R$ whose closed balls remain in all $cal(O)_s$ for $s$ close to $s_0$. Let $U$ be their union. All fixed points in the closed sections for sufficiently close parameters lie in $U$: otherwise a sequence of omitted fixed points converging to the slice would contradict compactness and its containment in the open set $U$. Excision identifies the degree on $cal(O)_s$ with that on $U$, and fixed-domain homotopy invariance makes the latter constant. If the slice is empty, compactness gives no fixed points nearby and the same conclusion with degree zero. Hence $deg \( "Id" - T_s \, cal(O)_s \, 0 \)$ is locally constant, and therefore constant, on $\[ 0 \, 4 \]$ #cite(label("Deimling1985")).
  At $s = 4$, the scalar prescription is $"tr"_(A_chi) H^f = h + D_1 \( x \, w \)$ with zero boundary heights. At an interior extremum $w = 0$ and $D_1 = 0$, so the maximum principle gives $f = 0$ for every trial $Z$, and then $t = Z$. The terminal scale sets both frozen right sides to zero and the drift is absent. Testing the frozen equation with $Y$ gives $Y = 0$; thus $T_4$ is the zero map, even though its positive coefficients may depend on the input. Its sole fixed point, $Z = 0$, belongs to $cal(O)_4$ and has degree one. The degree at $s = 0$ is consequently one, proving existence there.
  Now keep $n$ fixed. The preceding choices of sufficiently large $n$ used only the already proved uniform height and collar results and polynomial losses such as $Pi_n eta_n \/ ell arrow.r 0$. The arbitrary fixed-$n$ constants in the scalar construction impose no subsequent condition on $n$. The bounds in Proposition 134 are uniform in allowed $R$ on each compact set, including its portions on $B$. A diagonal Arzelà–Ascoli argument along $R arrow.r oo$ gives smooth convergence on all these sets. The equation and inner data pass to the limit, as do $t gt.eq - epsilon.alt$, the height interval, and the collar estimates. Smooth convergence on the fixed collars passes the weighted trace and flux inequalities as well. Strictness of the floor in this exhaustion limit is unnecessary below. ◻
]

===== 4.8.3 Decay on the end <decay-on-the-end>

#thm([Lemma 4.42.])[
  (End estimates).  #emph[For every solution obtained in Proposition 137, with $n$ fixed, there is $a_n > 0$ such that, for every integer $j gt.eq 0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.180)")
 $ \| nabla^j f \| + \| nabla^j \( t - Z \) \| lt.eq C_j \( n \) e^(- a_n r) quad upright("on the distant end") . $ <four:ex:end-f>]

 Moreover 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.181)")
 $ t \, Z = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \) \, #h(2em) Delta_g t = O \( r^(- 4 - delta_1) \) . $ <four:ex:end-tz>]

 The constants in these assertions may depend arbitrarily on $n$. The zeroth-order estimates and (4.180) hold uniformly for the finite-truncation solutions up to their outer faces.]
] <four:lem:end-decay>

#proof[
  Choose a fixed sphere beyond the supports of $K \, C$, and all collar terms. Along each finite-truncation solution the trace equation is the homogeneous linear equation
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.182)")
   $ A_chi : "Hess"_g f - c \( x \) f = 0 \, #h(2em) c \( x \) = eta_n sqrt(D) / l gt.eq c_n > 0 . $ <four:ex:f-end-linear>]
  By (4.131) the coefficients are uniformly elliptic; by Proposition 134 all their derivatives are bounded on uniform unit patches, independently of $R$. On the end,
  $ \( A_chi : "Hess"_g - c \) e^(- a r) = e^(- a r) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a^2 A_chi \( thin upright(d) r \, thin upright(d) r \) - a A_chi : "Hess"_g r - c #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Choose $a = a_n > 0$ so small that the first term is less than $c_n \/ 4$, and then enlarge the fixed sphere so the second term has magnitude less than $c_n \/ 4$. Thus $e^(- a_n r)$ is a strict supersolution. A fixed multiple dominates both signs of $f$ on the initial sphere and dominates the zero Dirichlet data on $S_R$. Linear comparison gives $\| f \| lt.eq C \( n \) e^(- a_n r)$ uniformly in $R$. Local estimates for (4.182), including the zero-Dirichlet estimates on the uniform outer-sphere charts, give the same exponential decay for every derivative, after harmless reduction of $a_n$ if necessary. The identity
  $ Z - t = 1 / 6 log \( 1 + l \( t \)^2 \| thin upright(d) f \|^2 \) $
  and the uniform smooth local bounds then give (4.180). Every graph contribution to the metric and to the equations is exponentially small with unit-patch derivatives.
  At zero graph and $K = 0$, the exact formulas give
  $ u = e^(2 Z) l \( Z \) \, #h(2em) V = 3 u "grad" Z \, #h(2em) cal(T) = 3 \( p \( Z \) + 1 \) \| thin upright(d) Z \|^2 . $
  Hence the actual divergence equation, divided by $u$, becomes
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.183)")
   $ 3 Delta_g Z + 3 frak(b)_n \( Z \) \| thin upright(d) Z \|_g^2 = rho - m_0 \( Z \) C_n rho_0 + cal(E)_n \, quad frak(b)_n \( z \) = p \( z \) + 2 - delta_0 \( p \( z \) + 1 \) \, $ <four:ex:Z-end-equation>]
  where $cal(E)_n$ and its derivatives on unit patches are $O_j \( e^(- a_n r) \)$. Define the strictly increasing function
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.184)")
   $ Phi \( 0 \) = 0 \, #h(2em) Phi' \( z \) = exp (integral_0^z frak(b)_n \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s) . $ <four:ex:Phi>]
  Its derivative is bounded above and away from zero on the fixed bounded range of $Z$. The chain rule cancels the quadratic term:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.185)")
   $ Delta_g Phi \( Z \) = frac(Phi' \( Z \), 3) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] rho - m_0 \( Z \) C_n rho_0 + cal(E)_n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) \| Delta_g Phi \( Z \) \| lt.eq C \( n \) r^(- 4 - delta_1) . $ <four:ex:Phi-Poisson>]
  Put $beta_1 = delta_1 \/ 2$ and $W \( r \) = r^(- 2) \( 1 - r^(- beta_1) \)$. In dimension four,
  $ Delta_delta W = - beta_1 \( 2 + beta_1 \) r^(- 4 - beta_1) \, #h(2em) Delta_g W = - beta_1 \( 2 + beta_1 \) r^(- 4 - beta_1) + O \( r^(- 6) \) . $
  Since $0 < beta_1 < delta_1 < 1$, a sufficiently large multiple of $W$ is positive and has negative Laplacian dominating the absolute source in (4.185) on a sufficiently distant end. Enlarge the multiple to dominate $\| Phi \( Z \) \|$ on its initial sphere. Its value is positive on $S_R$, whereas $Phi \( Z \) = 0$ there. Applying the maximum principle to both $Phi \( Z \) - C W$ and $- Phi \( Z \) - C W$ gives
  $ \| Phi \( Z \) \| lt.eq C \( n \) r^(- 2) \, #h(2em) \| Z \| lt.eq C \( n \) r^(- 2) \, $
  uniformly on truncations and then in the exhaustion limit.
  Here are the differentiated estimates, which do not follow from the pointwise barrier alone. On an annulus of radius $r_1$, rescale by $x = r_1 y$ and set $U \( y \) = r_1^2 Phi \( Z \( r_1 y \) \)$. Its supremum and its Poisson source in the rescaled equation are bounded: the source is $r_1^4$ times the right side of (4.185), of size $O_n \( r_1^(- delta_1) \)$. The rescaled background coefficients have uniform smooth bounds. Interior $W^(2 \, p)$ estimates, for a fixed $p > 4$, give bounded $C^(1 \, theta)$ norms of $U$ on a smaller annulus. The weights have their differentiated power decay, the compositions with $Z$ now have bounded scaled Hölder norms, and the exponential errors remain bounded with all scaled derivatives. The right side therefore has a bounded $C^(0 \, theta)$ norm. Schauder estimates give a bounded $C^(2 \, theta)$ norm of $U$. The inverse of $Phi$ has bounded smooth derivatives on the relevant range. Rescaling back gives $Z = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$, and (4.180) gives the same statement for $t$. Finally $\| thin upright(d) Z \|^2 = O \( r^(- 6) \)$ in (4.183); since $delta_1 < 1$, this is lower order than $r^(- 4 - delta_1)$. Equation (4.180) now gives the asserted bound for $Delta_g t$. ◻
]

===== 4.8.4 Scalar curvature, boundary sign, and finite flux <scalar-curvature-boundary-sign-and-finite-flux>

For the desired system $F = h + C$ and $w \( h \) = eta_n a sigma$. Substitute these into (4.75) and use $"div" V = u Xi$ to obtain

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.186)")
 $ 1 / 2 e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) =  & mu + J \( w \) + w \( C \) - rho - \( h + C \) tau\
 & + \( 1 - delta_0 \) \( cal(T) + eta_n a sigma \) + m_0 cal(P) med gt.eq 0 . $ <four:ex:positive-scalar>]

Indeed $\| w \| lt.eq 1$, and on $"supp" K$ the height interval (4.119) permits use of (4.66):

$ mu + J \( w \) + w \( C \) - rho - \( h + C \) tau gt.eq mu - \| J \| - \| thin upright(d) C \| - rho - \| \( h + C \) tau \| gt.eq 0 . $

Outside $"supp" K$ the same slack inequality needs no height restriction. The other terms are nonnegative by (4.84) and the definitions of the penalty and $delta_0$. Thus the nonmaximal trace term has been retained and controlled at this final step.

The inner flux condition and (4.88) give exactly

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.187)")
 $ H + 3 partial_nu t = - N_B \, #h(2em) hat(H) = - e^(- t) sqrt(d) thin N_B < 0 quad upright("on every component of ") B . $ <four:ex:negative-boundary>]

All quantities here are finite and $d > 0$ for each fixed $n$. Furthermore (4.180) and (4.181) imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.188)")
 $ hat(g) - delta = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \) \, #h(2em) "Scal"_(hat(g)) = O \( r^(- 4 - delta_1) \) . $ <four:ex:final-AF>]

For the scalar estimate one may use the conformal scalar formula with $macron(g) = g + O_j \( e^(- a_n r) \)$: $e^(2 t) "Scal"_(hat(g)) = "Scal"_(macron(g)) - 6 Delta_(macron(g)) t - 6 \| thin upright(d) t \|_(macron(g))^2$. The prepared scalar decay and (4.181) give precisely (4.188). The nonnegative scalar curvature is therefore integrable. The inequality

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.189)")
 $ hat(g) = e^(2 t) \( g + l^2 thin upright(d) f ⊗ thin upright(d) f \) gt.eq e^(- 2 epsilon.alt) g $ <four:ex:metric-comparison>]

also proves completeness with $B$ included: a $hat(g)$-Cauchy sequence is $g$-Cauchy, its limit belongs to the closed complete exterior, and smooth local metric equivalence gives convergence in $hat(g)$.

#thm([Proposition 4.43.])[
  (Energy comparison).  #emph[For each fixed $epsilon.alt > 0$ and all sufficiently large $n$, the limiting metric has finite ADM energy and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.190)")
 $ E_(hat(g)) lt.eq E_g + frac(Pi_n, 3 omega_3) (ell + ell^2 / eta_n) . $ <four:ex:energy-error>]

 The polynomial $Pi_n$ depends only on the prepared data, $epsilon.alt$, and the fixed geometric choices. In particular the error tends to zero as $n arrow.r oo$ with $epsilon.alt$ fixed.]
] <four:prop:mass-comparison>

#proof[
  We first establish the exact sign and normalization at fixed $n$. Lemma 138 gives $u = 1 + O_n \( r^(- 2) \)$ and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.191)")
   $ V = 3 "grad"_g t + O_n \( r^(- 5) \) + O_n \( e^(- a_n r) \) . $ <four:ex:V-asymptotics>]
  Also $u Xi in L^1 \( Omega \, thin upright(d) V_g \)$: on the end the quadratic term is $O_n \( r^(- 6) \)$, the graph and height-gradient terms are exponentially small, and the remaining weights are $O_n \( r^(- 4 - delta_1) \)$. The divergence theorem on a large truncation consequently gives a finite limit
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.192)")
   $ frak(F) := lim_(r arrow.r oo) integral_(S_r) V dot.op nu_g thin thin upright(d) A_g = integral_Omega u Xi thin thin upright(d) V_g + integral_B V_nu thin thin upright(d) A_g . $ <four:ex:flux>]
  The plus sign before the inner flux arises because the domain-outward normal on $B$ is $- nu$.
  To compute the metric flux, write $hat(g) = e^(2 t) g$ up to exponential errors. The leading perturbation relative to $g$ is $2 t delta_(i j)$, so in four dimensions the change of its energy numerator is
  $ partial_j \( 2 t delta_(i j) \) - partial_i \( 2 t delta_(j j) \) = - 6 partial_i t . $
  All omitted derivatives are $O_n \( r^(- 5) \)$ or exponentially small. Their sphere integrals tend to zero. Since $thin upright(d) t = O_n \( r^(- 3) \)$ and $g - delta = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$, changing the normal and measure in its flux from Euclidean to background ones also has vanishing error. Equation (4.191) thus proves both existence of the new energy and the exact relation
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.193)")
   $ E_(hat(g)) - E_g = - 1 / omega_3 lim_(r arrow.r oo) integral_(S_r) partial_(n_delta) t thin thin upright(d) A_delta = - frac(frak(F), 3 omega_3) . $ <four:ex:mass-flux>]
  We now use polynomial constants only. Apply Lemma 128 with $phi = 1$ and use (4.121). On the fixed compact collars $cal(C)$ one has $min_(cal(C)) rho > 0$, and therefore
  $ integral_B \| V_nu \| thin thin upright(d) A_g & lt.eq Pi_n eta_n / ell integral_(cal(C)) u \( 1 + sqrt(cal(T)) \) thin thin upright(d) V_g\
 & lt.eq Pi_n eta_n / ell integral_(cal(C)) u \( rho + delta_0 cal(T) \) thin thin upright(d) V_g . $
  The polynomial absorbs the fixed comparison constant in the second line. Because $eta_n \/ ell = e^(- epsilon.alt n \/ 2)$, for sufficiently large $n$ this is at most one half of $integral_Omega u \( rho + delta_0 cal(T) \)$.
  It remains to estimate the penalty. Its support in an above-floor solution lies in $- epsilon.alt lt.eq t lt.eq - epsilon.alt + 1 \/ n$. For $n > 1 \/ epsilon.alt$ this interval is negative, and uniformly there
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.194)")
   $ l asymp ell \, #h(2em) L_0 asymp ell \, #h(2em) u lt.eq C \( ell + ell^2 sigma \) \, #h(2em) u v lt.eq C ell^2 sigma \, #h(2em) u a sigma = L_0 l sigma^2 gt.eq c ell^2 sigma^2 . $ <four:ex:penalty-band>]
  For example $u v = L_0 l^2 sigma^2 \/ sqrt(1 + l^2 sigma^2) lt.eq L_0 l sigma$. Thus on that band
  $ u m_0 cal(P) lt.eq Pi_n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] ell rho_0 + ell^2 sigma \( rho_0 + rho_1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] . $
  Young’s inequality applied to $sqrt(eta_n) ell sigma$ and $Pi_n ell \( rho_0 + rho_1 \) \/ sqrt(eta_n)$, together with (4.194), yields everywhere
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.195)")
   $ u m_0 cal(P) lt.eq 1 / 2 delta_0 u eta_n a sigma + Pi_n [ell rho_0 + ell^2 / eta_n \( rho_0^2 + rho_1^2 \)] . $ <four:ex:penalty-absorption>]
  The enlarged $Pi_n$ is still polynomial. The three residual weights are integrable against $thin upright(d) V_g$ in dimension four: $rho_0 = r^(- 4 - delta_1)$, $rho_0^2 = r^(- 8 - 2 delta_1)$, and $rho_1^2 = r^(- 6)$ on the end, and they are bounded on the compact part. Combining (4.192), the boundary absorption, and (4.195) gives
  $ frak(F) gt.eq - Pi_n (ell + ell^2 / eta_n) . $
  This and (4.193) prove (4.190). Finally $ell = e^(- epsilon.alt n)$ and $ell^2 \/ eta_n = e^(- epsilon.alt n \/ 2)$; both dominate every polynomial loss. No constant from the fixed-$n$ decay or Schauder estimates appears in this bound. ◻
]

===== 4.8.5 A minimal enclosing hypersurface <a-minimal-enclosing-hypersurface>

The boundary $B$ has strictly negative mean curvature in $hat(g)$. The full-perimeter enclosure of Proposition 102 therefore gives the smooth minimal boundary needed for the Riemannian comparison. We relate its total three-volume to the original enclosing quantity $A_(*)$.

#thm([Lemma 4.44.])[
  (Minimal enclosure).  #emph[Let $Omega$ and $B$ be the exterior and its boundary from Proposition 118, and let $epsilon.alt > 0$. Suppose $hat(g)$ is a smooth metric on $Omega$, complete with $B$ included, such that $hat(g) - delta = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$ on its end, $hat(H)_B < 0$ for the normal into $Omega$, and $hat(g) gt.eq e^(- 2 epsilon.alt) g$. Then there is a nonempty compact smooth embedded minimal hypersurface $Sigma$ enclosing $B$. Its exterior is connected, complete with boundary, and has the same single asymptotically flat end. The hypersurface is outer area-minimizing, with disconnected competitors permitted, and its area $hat(A) = \| Sigma \|_(hat(g))$ satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.196)")
 $ hat(A) gt.eq e^(- 3 epsilon.alt) A_(*) . $ <four:end:area-comparison>]

]
] <four:lem:minimal-enclosure>

#proof[
  Proposition 118 gives a smooth connected orientable four-dimensional exterior $Omega$ with nonempty compact smooth boundary $B$ and exactly one asymptotically flat end. By hypothesis, $hat(g)$ is smooth, complete with $B$ included, and satisfies $hat(g) - delta = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$. Thus $\( X \, g \) = \( Omega \, hat(g) \)$ satisfies the geometric hypotheses of Proposition 102, with asymptotic exponent $q = 2 > 1$.
  Apply that Proposition to obtain a full-perimeter minimizing enclosure of $B$, and denote its frontier by $Sigma$. Since $hat(H)_B < 0$ for the normal into $Omega$, the strict inner-barrier conclusion places $Sigma$ in $"int" Omega$ and makes it a nonempty compact smooth embedded minimal hypersurface. Its closed exterior is smooth, connected, complete with $Sigma$ included, and has the same single end. The Proposition also gives outer area-minimization there, with all boundary components counted and disconnected enclosing competitors permitted.
  For every smooth enclosing cut $Gamma$ of $B$, its closed connected outer domain is also an admissible outer domain in the original prepared manifold, by Proposition 118. This includes frontier coincident with $B$. Restricting $hat(g) gt.eq e^(- 2 epsilon.alt) g$ to its three-dimensional tangent planes gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.197)")
   $ \| Gamma \|_(hat(g)) gt.eq e^(- 3 epsilon.alt) \| Gamma \|_g gt.eq e^(- 3 epsilon.alt) A_(*) . $ <four:ex:cut-volume>]
  Apply this to $Sigma$ to obtain (4.196). ◻
]

===== 4.8.6 The Riemannian comparison <the-riemannian-comparison>

We use the following dimension-four instance of the boundary Riemannian Penrose inequality #cite(label("BrayLee2009")) Theorem 1.4, p. 84. Let $\( N \, g_N \)$ be a smooth connected four-dimensional Riemannian manifold, complete with its nonempty compact boundary included, having exactly one end, which is asymptotically flat. Suppose in that end

$ \( g_N \)_(i j) - delta_(i j) = O_2 \( r^(- p_0) \) \, quad p_0 > 1 \, #h(2em) R_(g_N) = O \( r^(- s_0) \) \, quad s_0 > 4 \, $

and $R_(g_N) gt.eq 0$ everywhere. If its boundary is minimal and outer area-minimizing, then

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.198)")
 $ E_(g_N) gt.eq 1 / 2 (frac(\| partial N \|_(g_N), omega_3))^(2 \/ 3) . $ <four:end:bray-lee>]

The boundary may have one or more components, and the ADM normalization is $1 \/ \( 6 omega_3 \)$. The numerical inequality does not require a spin assumption; the additional spin hypothesis in the published theorem concerns its equality conclusion. We use only (4.198).

===== 4.8.7 The prepared-data energy theorem <the-prepared-data-energy-theorem>

#thm([Theorem 4.45.])[
  (Energy inequality for prepared data).  #emph[Let $\( M^4 \, g \, K \)$ be smooth, connected, and orientable, complete with a nonempty compact smooth boundary $S$ included, and with exactly one end, which is asymptotically flat. Let $K$ be a smooth compactly supported symmetric covariant tensor. Suppose, with a smooth positive function $r$ equal to the coordinate radius on the end, constants $c_0 > 0$ and $0 < delta_1 < 1$, that 
$ mu - \| J \|_g gt.eq c_0 r^(- 4 - delta_1) quad upright("on ") M \, #h(2em) H + "tr"_S K < 0 quad upright("on every component of ") S \, $

 where the boundary normal points into $M$. Suppose on the end that 
$ g - delta = O_k \( r^(- 2) \) quad upright("for every integer ") k gt.eq 0 \, #h(2em) "Scal"_g = O \( r^(- 4 - delta_1) \) \, $

 and that the ADM energy $E_g$ is finite in the stated normalization. Let $A_(*)$ be the infimum of the total $g$-three-volume of the entire intrinsic boundaries of connected closed smooth outer domains containing the distant end, with interiors disjoint from $S$, including disconnected cuts and frontier coincident with $S$. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.199)")
 $ E_g gt.eq 1 / 2 (A_(*) / omega_3)^(2 \/ 3) . $ <four:ex:prepared-inequality>]

]
] <four:thm:prepared-energy>

#proof[
  Proposition 118 supplies the closed exterior $Omega$ with its nonempty compact smooth boundary $B$ and the data used in this section. It is connected, orientable, and one-ended, and its background energy is still $E_g$. Fix $0 < epsilon.alt < 1$. For every sufficiently large $n$, Proposition 137 constructs a smooth metric $hat(g)$ on this exterior. By (4.186) and (4.188), it has nonnegative scalar curvature and the pointwise end bounds
  $ hat(g) - delta = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \) \, #h(2em) R_(hat(g)) = O \( r^(- 4 - delta_1) \) \, #h(2em) 0 < delta_1 < 1 . $
  It is complete by (4.189), and every component of $B$ has strictly negative mean curvature by (4.187).
  Lemma 140 supplies a nonempty compact smooth minimal boundary $Sigma$, with connected one-ended exterior and outer area-minimization against disconnected competitors. Restricting the metric to this exterior preserves scalar curvature, asymptotic decay and ADM energy. Thus the metric and scalar exponents in (4.198) are $p_0 = 2 > 1$ and $s_0 = 4 + delta_1 > 4$, and all hypotheses of that theorem hold. Proposition 139 gives its finite energy. The enclosure is an admissible smooth cut, so (4.196) compares its three-volume with $A_(*)$.
  Combining the Riemannian inequality with (4.190) gives
  $ E_g + frac(Pi_n, 3 omega_3) (ell + ell^2 / eta_n) gt.eq E_(hat(g)) gt.eq 1 / 2 (frac(\| Sigma \|_(hat(g)), omega_3))^(2 \/ 3) gt.eq 1 / 2 e^(- 2 epsilon.alt) (A_(*) / omega_3)^(2 \/ 3) . $
  For each fixed $epsilon.alt$ let $n arrow.r oo$, after the fixed-$n$ exhaustion already carried out. Then let $epsilon.alt arrow.b 0$. This proves (4.199). ◻
]

Theorem 141 supplies the energy estimate used in Proposition 112. Removing strictification and then the rest-end replacement, applying the positive-shell argument, and truncating the unwanted ends complete Theorem 98.

===== 4.8.8 The general maximal DEC energy inequality <the-general-maximal-dec-energy-inequality>

The maximal special case requires no assumption that a momentum limit exists. The following end calculation supplies that limit from the decay and integrability already imposed on the data.

#thm([Lemma 4.46.])[
  (Existence of the momentum flux).  #emph[On a smooth four-dimensional asymptotically flat end, suppose $g - delta = O_2 \( r^(- q) \)$ and $K = O_1 \( r^(- 1 - q) \)$ for some $q > 1$. If $\| J \|_g$ is integrable, where $J_i = nabla^j \( K_(i j) - \( "tr"_g K \) g_(i j) \)$, then each ADM momentum limit in (4.1) exists and is finite.]
] <four:lem:momentum-exists>

#proof[
  In the given coordinates set
  $ pi_(i j)^g = K_(i j) - \( "tr"_g K \) g_(i j) \, #h(2em) pi_(i j)^delta = K_(i j) - \( "tr"_delta K \) delta_(i j) . $
  The differentiated decay assumptions give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.200)")
   $ pi^g - pi^delta = O_1 \( r^(- 1 - 2 q) \) \, #h(2em) partial_j pi_(i j)^delta - J_i = O \( r^(- 2 - 2 q) \) . $ <four:end:momentum-integrability>]
  To verify the second bound, expand the covariant divergence as
  $ J_i = g^(j k) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] partial_k pi_(i j)^g - Gamma_(k i)^a pi_(a j)^g - Gamma_(k j)^a pi_(i a)^g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Replacing $g^(j k)$ by $delta^(j k)$ costs $O \( r^(- q) \) O \( r^(- 2 - q) \)$; replacing $pi^g$ by $pi^delta$ costs the derivative of the first bound in (4.200). Each connection term has size $O \( r^(- 1 - q) \) O \( r^(- 1 - q) \)$. These are exactly the asserted errors.
  Their Euclidean radial volume bound is $C r^(1 - 2 q)$, integrable because $q > 1$. The asymptotic metric and Euclidean volume and covector norms are uniformly comparable, so $partial_j pi_(i j)^delta in L^1$. The Euclidean divergence theorem between two large spheres makes the flux $integral_(S_R) pi_(i j)^delta n_delta^j thin thin upright(d) A_delta$ a Cauchy function of $R$, with finite limit. By the first bound in (4.200), its difference from the flux of $pi^g$ is $O \( R^(2 - 2 q) \) arrow.r 0$. This is the tensor appearing in (4.1), which proves the lemma. ◻
]

#thm([Corollary 4.47.])[
  (Maximal DEC energy inequality).  #emph[ Let $\( X^4 \, g_X \, K_X \)$ be smooth and connected, with $X$ orientable, $g_X$ complete as a metric space with its nonempty compact smooth boundary $S_X$ included, and with exactly one end, which is asymptotically flat and diffeomorphic to the complement of a closed ball in $bb(R)^4$. Let $K_X$ be a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor. Suppose 
$ "tr"_(g_X) K_X = 0 \, #h(2em) mu_X = 1 / 2 \( R_(g_X) - \| K_X \|_(g_X)^2 \) gt.eq \| "div"_(g_X) K_X \|_(g_X) \,\
mu_X \, \| "div"_(g_X) K_X \|_(g_X) in L^1 \( X \, thin upright(d) V_(g_X) \) . $

 Assume for some $q > 1$ that 
$ g_X - delta = O_2 \( r^(- q) \) \, #h(2em) K_X = O_1 \( r^(- 1 - q) \) \, $

 and that the ADM energy $E_X$ in (4.1) is finite. On every component of $S_X$, require $H + "tr"_(S_X) K_X lt.eq 0$ with the normal into $X$. For the one-ended enclosing-cut infimum of Definition 97, denoted here by $a_(g_X) \( S_X \)$, one has 
$ E_X gt.eq 1 / 2 (frac(a_(g_X) \( S_X \), 2 pi^2))^(2 \/ 3) . $

 The boundary may have any topology and finitely many components. No symmetry, vacuum, spin, outermostness, outer area-minimization or momentum-flux assumption is imposed.]
] <four:cor:maximal-dec>

#proof[
  Maximality identifies the momentum density with $J_X = "div"_(g_X) K_X$. Lemma 142 supplies the finite ADM momentum. All hypotheses of Theorem 98 therefore hold for this one-ended exterior. Its invariant-mass inequality in particular gives the displayed energy bound. The theorem has already been proved using the Riemannian inequality (4.198), so this corollary is a consequence of the proof, not an input to it. ◻
]

==== 4.9 Variation at equality and localization of the area term <rig4:var:section>

Throughout this section, $\( Omega \, g \, K \)$ satisfies the hypotheses of Definition 99, and equality holds in Theorem 98. Thus $Omega$ has one end and its only boundary $S$ is a connected, outermost, outer area-minimizing future MOTS. We use the full enclosing-cut convention of Definition 97. Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.201)")
 $ A = "Area"_g \( S \) \, #h(2em) m = sqrt(E^2 - \| P \|^2) \, #h(2em) r_0 = \( A \/ omega \)^(1 \/ 3) = sqrt(2 m) \,\
b^0 = E \/ m \, #h(2em) b^i = - P_i \/ m \, #h(2em) frak(m) \( a \) = 1 / 2 \( a \/ omega \)^(2 \/ 3) \, #h(2em) c = 6 omega frak(m)' \( A \) = 2 \/ r_0 . $ <rig4:var:constants>]

The strict future timelikeness needed here is already part of Theorem 98. For nearby data with charges $\( tilde(E) \, tilde(P) \)$, define the fixed linear charge functional

$ cal(E) \( tilde(g) \, tilde(K) \) = b^0 tilde(E) + sum_(i = 1)^4 b^i tilde(P)_i . $

The reverse Cauchy–Schwarz inequality on the future timelike cone gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.202)")
 $ cal(E) \( tilde(g) \, tilde(K) \) gt.eq sqrt(tilde(E)^2 - \| tilde(P) \|^2) . $ <rig4:var:supporting-charge>]

Equality holds at the original data. In particular, whenever the varied data satisfy the numerical theorem, their enclosing infimum $a \( tilde(g) \)$ satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.203)")
 $ cal(E) \( tilde(g) \, tilde(K) \) - frak(m) \( a \( tilde(g) \) \) gt.eq 0 . $ <rig4:var:nonnegative-defect>]

The varied data need not preserve outermostness or outer area minimization: those assumptions are absent from the numerical theorem.

The right-derivative formula in Proposition 103 accounts for all minimizing frontiers without choosing a differentiable family. It gives the positive-measure identity of Proposition 149. Compact normal tests and outermostness then localize its area term to $S$ in Proposition 151, supplying the identity used to construct the causal adjoint field in Theorem 152.

===== 4.9.1 Constraint derivatives and a strict direction <constraint-derivatives-and-a-strict-direction>

On the closed unit ball bundle of $g$, introduce

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.204)")
 $ C \( x \, v \) = 2 \( mu \( x \) + J_x \( v \) \) = R_g + tau^2 - \| K \|_g^2 + 2 J_x \( v \) \, #h(2em) \| v \|_g lt.eq 1 \,\
cal(A) = { \( x \, v \) : C \( x \, v \) = 0 } . $ <rig4:var:active-set>]

DEC is equivalent to $C gt.eq 0$ on this bundle. When the metric varies, we identify its unit ball with the original one by the positive symmetric inverse square root. More precisely, if $g_s \( V \, W \) = g \( G_s V \, W \)$, then $I_s = G_s^(- 1 \/ 2)$ sends the $g$ unit ball isometrically to the $g_s$ unit ball. For a variation $H = \( h \, p \) = \( dot(g)_0 \, dot(K)_0 \)$, all derivatives $C'_H$ below include this identification; in particular,

$ dot(I)_0 v = - 1 / 2 h^sharp v . $

#thm([Lemma 4.48.])[
  (Conformal constraint identities).  #emph[For a positive smooth function $f$, let $g_f = f^2 g$ and $K_f = f K$. Using $f^(- 1) v$ in the varied unit ball, one has 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.205)")
 $ f^2 C_f \( x \, f^(- 1) v \) & = C \( x \, v \) + 6 f^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] - Delta_g f + K \( nabla f \, v \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] \,\
f theta_(+ \, f) & = theta_(+) + 3 partial_nu log f . $ <rig4:var:conformal-C>]

 Here $nu$ points into the exterior and $Delta_g = "div"_g nabla$.]
] <rig4:var:conformal>

#proof[
  Apply Lemma 106. Its density and momentum identities give
  $ f^2 mu_f = mu - 3 f^(- 1) Delta_g f \, #h(2em) f^2 J_f \( f^(- 1) v \) = J \( v \) + 3 f^(- 1) K \( nabla f \, v \) . $
  Adding twice these expressions proves (4.205). Equation (4.205) is the expansion formula in that same lemma, with the stated orientation. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.49.])[
  (Strict conformal direction).  #emph[Choose 
$ 1 < q_0 < min \( q \, 2 \) \, #h(2em) 0 < delta < min \( q_0 \, 1 \) \, #h(2em) w = r^(- 4 - delta) \, $

 where the end radius is extended to a positive smooth function on $Omega$. There is a smooth nonnegative function $phi.alt$, smooth up to $S$, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.206)")
 $ partial_nu phi.alt = - 1 quad upright("on ") S \, #h(2em) - Delta_g phi.alt - \| K \|_g \| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|_g gt.eq w \, #h(2em) phi.alt = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \) \, #h(2em) - Delta_g phi.alt \/ w arrow.r 1 . $ <rig4:var:phi-properties>]

 Its limiting Euclidean sphere gradient flux exists and is finite. For 
$ Q = \( 2 phi.alt g \, phi.alt K \) $

 one consequently has 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.207)")
 $ C'_Q gt.eq 6 w quad upright("on ") cal(A) \, #h(2em) - theta'_Q = 3 quad upright("on ") S . $ <rig4:var:strict-derivatives>]

 Moreover, uniformly for all $\| v \|_g lt.eq 1$ on the end, $C'_Q \/ w arrow.r 6$.]
] <rig4:var:strict-direction>

#proof[
  Choose a smooth majorant $d_0 gt.eq \| K \|_g$ which on the end is a sufficiently large radial multiple of $r^(- 1 - q_0)$. It can be chosen with all symbol bounds there. We solve
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.208)")
   $ - Delta_g phi.alt = d_0 sqrt(\| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|_g^2 + r^(- 6)) + w \, #h(2em) partial_nu phi.alt = - 1 upright(" on ") S \, #h(2em) phi.alt arrow.r 0 upright(" at infinity") . $ <rig4:var:phi-equation>]
  First truncate at a large coordinate sphere $S_R$, imposing $phi.alt = 0$ on $S_R$, and replace $d_0$ in the equation by $t d_0$, $0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1$. The inner and outer boundary components are disjoint smooth hypersurfaces; there is no corner where the two boundary conditions meet. The $t = 0$ problem is the invertible mixed Laplace problem. Every linearization in $phi.alt$ adds to $- Delta_g$ a smooth drift of norm at most $d_0$, with homogeneous Neumann data at $S$ and homogeneous Dirichlet data at $S_R$. The maximum and boundary point principles give uniqueness, and the mixed elliptic Fredholm theory then gives invertibility. These are the usual local elliptic and boundary estimates on a fixed smooth truncated domain; see #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")).
  Solutions are nonnegative. Indeed a negative minimum cannot occur in the interior because the right side of the equation is positive. At $S$, the outward normal of the truncated domain is $- nu$, so the prescribed outward derivative is $+ 1$, also excluding such a minimum by the boundary point principle. The outer Dirichlet value is zero.
  For completeness, the continuation bounds do not require a monotonicity assumption in the unknown. Fix $p > 4$. The mixed $W^(2 \, p)$ estimate, the linear growth of the right side in $thin upright(d) phi.alt$, and first-derivative interpolation give, on this fixed truncation,
  $ parallel phi.alt parallel_(W^(2 \, p)) lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + parallel phi.alt parallel_(L^oo) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  uniformly in $t$. If the supremum were unbounded, division by that supremum and compactness in $C^1$ would produce a nonnegative function $z$ with supremum one satisfying
  $ - Delta_g z = t_(*) d_0 \| thin upright(d) z \|_g \, #h(2em) partial_nu z = 0 upright(" on ") S \, #h(2em) z = 0 upright(" on ") S_R $
  for some $t_(*) in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$. At points where $thin upright(d) z eq.not 0$ the right side is a bounded drift applied to $thin upright(d) z$, and it is zero otherwise. The strong maximum and boundary point principles for this bounded-drift equation contradict the positive maximum. The resulting $W^(2 \, p)$ and Schauder bounds close the continuation argument and give a smooth solution at $t = 1$ on every sufficiently large truncation.
  We next make the bounds independent of $R$. For $W = r^(- 2) - r^(- 2 - delta)$, the Euclidean identity
  $ - Delta_delta W = \( 2 + delta \) delta r^(- 4 - delta) $
  and the AF estimates imply, beyond a fixed radius $R_1$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.209)")
   $ - Delta_g W - d_0 \| thin upright(d) W \|_g gt.eq c_1 w > 0 . $ <rig4:var:tail-barrier>]
  The metric errors and the drift term are smaller than $w$ because $delta < q_0 < q$. Also $d_0 r^(- 3) = o \( w \)$. If $M_R$ is the supremum of the truncated solution on the fixed core bounded by $S_(R_1)$, a sufficiently large fixed multiple of $\( 1 + M_R \) W$ is a supersolution on $R_1 lt.eq r lt.eq R$. To see this, use $sqrt(a^2 + b^2) lt.eq a + b$ and (4.209); choose the multiple also to dominate $M_R$ on $S_(R_1)$. It dominates the zero outer value. Comparison is valid by writing the difference of the two gradient nonlinearities as a bounded drift. Therefore
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.210)")
   $ 0 lt.eq phi.alt_R lt.eq C \( 1 + M_R \) W #h(2em) \( R_1 lt.eq r lt.eq R \) . $ <rig4:var:tail-bound>]
  If $M_R$ diverged along a sequence, normalize by $M_R$. Local mixed estimates near $S$ and interior estimates elsewhere give a subsequential limit $z$ on every fixed compact set. More precisely, (4.210) bounds $phi.alt_R \/ M_R$ on a neighborhood of the fixed closed core. Local Neumann $W^(2 \, p)$ estimates at $S$, and interior $W^(2 \, p)$ estimates away from it, give uniform bounds there for $p > 4$. Compactness in $C^1$ on the closed core retains both $sup_(upright("core")) z = 1$ and $partial_nu z = 0$. The limit satisfies $- Delta_g z = d_0 \| thin upright(d) z \|_g$, homogeneous Neumann data on $S$, and, by (4.210), $z arrow.r 0$ at infinity. Its positive global maximum is attained in a compact set. The same strong maximum and boundary point principles again give a contradiction. Thus $M_R$ is bounded, and exhaustion produces a smooth solution of (4.208) with $phi.alt = O \( r^(- 2) \)$.
  Here is the derivative control needed later, using only the stated derivatives of the original data. On a fixed annulus in the $y$ variables put $g_rho \( y \) = \( g_(i j) \( rho y \) \)$, $U_rho \( y \) = rho^2 phi.alt \( rho y \)$, and $a_rho \( y \) = rho d_0 \( rho y \)$. The exact rescaled equation is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.211)")
   $ - Delta_(g_rho) U_rho = a_rho sqrt(\| thin upright(d) U_rho \|_(g_rho)^2 + \| y \|^(- 6)) + rho^(- delta) \| y \|^(- 4 - delta) . $ <rig4:var:rescaled-phi>]
  The metric components have uniform $C^(1 \, 1)$ bounds from $g - delta = O_2$. Consequently both the principal coefficients of this Laplacian and its first-order coefficients have uniform $C^(0 \, alpha)$ bounds for every fixed $alpha < 1$. No third derivative of $g$ is needed. The chosen radial $d_0$ gives $a_rho = O \( rho^(- q_0) \)$ with uniform symbol bounds, independently of derivatives of $K$. First-derivative interpolation in the local $W^(2 \, p)$ estimates gives a uniform $W^(2 \, p)$ bound on a smaller annulus. Taking $p > 4$ controls $thin upright(d) U_rho$ in $C^(0 \, alpha)$ for some $alpha > 0$. The positive $\| y \|^(- 6)$ term is bounded away from zero there, so the entire right side of (4.211) is uniformly $C^(0 \, alpha)$. The usual Schauder estimate, including the first-order Laplacian coefficients with these $C^(0 \, alpha)$ bounds, now gives a uniform $C^(2 \, alpha)$ bound on a further smaller annulus. Scaling back proves $phi.alt = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$ without an additional metric or tensor falloff derivative. The equation now yields
  $ d_0 sqrt(\| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|_g^2 + r^(- 6)) = O \( r^(- 4 - q_0) \) = o \( w \) \, $
  which proves both the differential inequality and the last limit in (4.206). The right side of (4.208) is integrable. The divergence theorem consequently gives a finite limiting physical sphere flux. Its difference from the Euclidean sphere flux tends to zero, since $g - delta = O \( r^(- q) \)$ and $thin upright(d) phi.alt = O \( r^(- 3) \)$.
  Finally, differentiating Lemma 144 at $f = 1 + s phi.alt$ gives
  $ C'_Q = - 2 phi.alt C + 6 \[ - Delta_g phi.alt + K \( nabla phi.alt \, v \) \] \, #h(2em) theta'_Q = - phi.alt theta_(+) + 3 partial_nu phi.alt . $
  On $cal(A)$ the first term is zero, and on $S$ we have $theta_(+) = 0$. This proves (4.207). On the full end ball bundle, $C = O \( r^(- 2 - q) \)$, $phi.alt C = O \( r^(- 4 - q) \) = o \( w \)$, and $K \( nabla phi.alt \, v \) = O \( r^(- 4 - q) \) = o \( w \)$. Thus the normalized derivative tends uniformly to six. ◻
]

===== 4.9.2 Feasible variations and a measure identity <feasible-variations-and-a-measure-identity>

#prodef([Definition 4.50.])[
  (Variation space).  Let $cal(V)_0$ be the real vector space generated by all smooth compactly supported pairs $\( h \, p \)$ up to $S$ and the following five pairs, cut off smoothly to the end. The scalar pair is
  $ h_(i j) = r^(- 2) delta_(i j) \, #h(2em) p = 0 . $
  For each vector $e$ in a fixed basis of $bb(R)^4$, the momentum pair has $h = 0$ and is defined by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.212)")
   $ p - \( "tr"_delta p \) delta = r^(- 3) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e ⊗ hat(x) + hat(x) ⊗ e - \( e dot.op hat(x) \) delta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) hat(x) = x \/ r . $ <rig4:var:momentum-prototype>]
  Set $cal(V) = bb(R) Q + cal(V)_0$. We write its elements as $H = a Q + H_0$, where $H_0 = \( h_0 \, p_0 \) in cal(V)_0$.
] <rig4:var:variation-space>

#thm([Lemma 4.51.])[
  (End behavior of the variation space).  #emph[The five prototypes have independent charge derivatives. For every fixed $H_0 in cal(V)_0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.213)")
 $ C'_(H_0) = O \( r^(- 4 - q) \) = o \( w \) $ <rig4:var:prototype-constraint-decay>]

 uniformly on the full end ball bundle. The coefficient $a$ in Definition 146 is uniquely determined, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.214)")
 $ C'_H \/ w arrow.r 6 a $ <rig4:var:end-value>]

 uniformly there. All elements of $cal(V)$ have well-defined charge derivatives.]
] <rig4:var:end-variations>

#proof[
  Trace reversal on symmetric tensors in dimension four is invertible. The right side of (4.212), written as
  $ D_(i j) = r^(- 4) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e_i x_j + x_i e_j - \( e dot.op x \) delta_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  satisfies $partial_j D_(i j) = 0$ and $D_(i j) hat(x)^j = r^(- 3) e_i$. The scalar prototype satisfies the Euclidean linearized scalar constraint because $Delta_delta r^(- 2) = 0$ off the origin. With the ADM normalizations (4.1)–(4.1), the scalar prototype has $E' = 1$, $P' = 0$, and the momentum prototype for $e$ has $E' = 0$, $P' = e \/ 3$. Background corrections to these fluxes vanish by the AF estimates, so the derivatives are independent.
  On the end, $h_0 = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$ and $p_0 = O_1 \( r^(- 3) \)$. Their Euclidean linearized scalar and momentum constraints vanish. Each remaining linear constraint term has a factor from the decaying background: for example, $\( g - delta \) partial^2 h_0$, $partial g thin partial h_0$, $partial^2 g thin h_0$, $K p_0$, or $\( g - delta \) partial p_0$ has order at most $r^(- 4 - q)$. The variation of the vector identification contributes $- J \( h_0^sharp v \)$, of the same order. This proves (4.213). Lemma 145 then proves (4.214) and the uniqueness of $a$: an element of $cal(V)_0$ cannot have the normalized end derivative of $Q$.
  Compact variations have zero charge derivatives. The prototypes have the fluxes just computed. For $Q$, the momentum derivative is zero and
  $ E'_Q = - 1 / omega lim_(R arrow.r oo) integral_(\| x \| = R) partial_r phi.alt thin thin upright(d) A_delta \, $
  which is finite by Lemma 145. Products with background errors have zero limiting flux. Linearity completes the proof. ◻
]

Let $cal(T)$ be the compact space of minimizing frontiers for $g$, with their filled sets, from Proposition 103. For $T in cal(T)$, write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.215)")
 $ a'_T \( h \) = 1 / 2 integral_T "tr"_T h thin thin upright(d) A_g . $ <rig4:var:fixed-area-derivative>]

This is a continuous function of $T$ for each fixed variation $h$.

#thm([Lemma 4.52.])[
  (Strict linear inequalities give feasible nonlinear data).  #emph[Suppose $H = a Q + H_0 in cal(V)$ has $a > 0$ and, for some $epsilon > 0$, 
$ C'_H gt.eq epsilon w upright(" on ") cal(A) \, #h(2em) - theta'_H > 0 upright(" on ") S . $

 Then, for every sufficiently small $s > 0$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.216)")
 $ g_s = e^(2 a s phi.alt) \( g + s h_0 \) \, #h(2em) K_s = e^(a s phi.alt) \( K + s p_0 \) $ <rig4:var:nonlinear-path>]

 satisfies every hypothesis of Theorem 98. Its boundary has strictly negative future expansion. The path has derivative $H$, is uniformly comparable to $g$, has uniform large-sphere barriers, and its charges are differentiable at $s = 0$.]
] <rig4:var:feasible-path>

#proof[
  Positivity of the metric and uniform comparability hold for small $s$, since $h_0$ and $phi.alt$ are bounded relative to $g$. They also give completeness with the boundary included. The topology, smoothness, end count and orientability remain those of $Omega$.
  We check DEC on the whole ball of test vectors. First use the intermediate metric and tensor $overline(g)_s = g + s h_0$, $overline(K)_s = K + s p_0$, identifying its vector ball with that of $g$ by $overline(I)_s$. For sufficiently large $r$, Taylor expansion and the Euclidean cancellations in Lemma 147 give, uniformly in $\| v \|_g lt.eq 1$,
  $ overline(C)_s \( x \, overline(I)_s v \) = C \( x \, v \) + O \( s r^(- 4 - q) \) + O \( s^2 r^(- 6) \) . $
  Here the remainder is uniform in $r$, $s$ in a fixed small interval, and the whole vector ball. To check all its components, the coordinate constraint expressions have the schematic forms
  $ R_g & = g^(- 1) partial^2 g + "smooth" \( g^(- 1) \) \( partial g \)^2 \,\
J & = "smooth" \( g^(- 1) \) partial K + "smooth" \( g^(- 1) \) \( partial g \) K \, $
  and $2 mu - R_g$ is quadratic in $K$, with smooth coefficients in $g^(- 1)$. Each second $s$ derivative on the intermediate path is $O \( r^(- 6) \)$: the largest terms are $h_0 partial^2 h_0$, $\( partial h_0 \)^2$, $p_0^2$, $h_0 partial p_0$, and $\( partial h_0 \) p_0$. Terms containing an original $K$, $partial K$, or background metric derivative decay faster. This calculation includes all occurrences of $tau = g^(i j) K_(i j)$, without a separate trace estimate. The vector identification also has the uniform matrix expansion
  $ overline(I)_s = \( "Id" + s h_0^sharp \)^(- 1 \/ 2) = "Id" - s / 2 h_0^sharp + O \( s^2 r^(- 4) \) \; $
  the eigenvalues lie in a fixed positive interval, so the matrix-function Taylor bound is uniform. Multiplication by the original or varied momentum covector keeps the same remainder order. In fact the preceding calculation gives the componentwise estimates
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.217)")
   $ overline(mu)_s & = mu + O \( s r^(- 4 - q) \) + O \( s^2 r^(- 6) \) \,\
overline(J)_s & = J + O \( s r^(- 4 - q) \) + O \( s^2 r^(- 6) \) . $ <rig4:var:component-remainders>]
  Apply Lemma 144 to these intermediate data with $f_s = e^(a s phi.alt)$. After multiplication by the positive factor $f_s^2$, the constraint for the final data is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.218)")
   $ C \( x \, v \) + s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 6 a w + o \( w \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + O \( s^2 r^(- 6) \) \, $ <rig4:var:end-feasibility-expansion>]
  again uniformly for the full vector ball. Indeed, $- Delta_g phi.alt \/ w arrow.r 1$ and $K \( nabla phi.alt \, v \) = o \( w \)$. Changing the metric, tensor, and vector identification in these conformal differential terms adds only the above decay orders, while the extra exponential term uses $\| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|^2 = O \( r^(- 6) \)$. Since $a > 0$ and $r^(- 6) = O \( w \)$, one can first choose a fixed large radius so the coefficient of $s$ in (4.218) is positive, and then choose $s$ small so its remainder is absorbed. DEC follows on this fixed far end, using $C gt.eq 0$.
  The remaining base region and its closed unit ball bundle are compact. The derivative of the constraint is strictly positive on its original zero set. By continuity it is positive on a neighborhood of that zero set; on the complement the original constraint has a positive minimum. A uniform Taylor expansion therefore gives $C_s gt.eq 0$ everywhere in the compact region for small positive $s$. The same compactness argument on $S$, where $theta_(+) = 0$, gives $theta_(+ \, s) < 0$.
  The new densities are integrable componentwise, not merely after contraction with active vectors. The exact conformal formulas give
  $ f_s J_s & = overline(J)_s + 3 a s overline(K)_s \( nabla_(overline(g)_s) phi.alt \, dot.op \) \,\
f_s^2 mu_s & = overline(mu)_s - 3 a s Delta_(overline(g)_s) phi.alt - 3 a^2 s^2 \| thin upright(d) phi.alt \|_(overline(g)_s)^2 . $
  The extra covector in the first line is $O \( s r^(- 4 - q) \) + O \( s^2 r^(- 6) \)$. Also $Delta_(overline(g)_s) phi.alt = Delta_g phi.alt + O \( s r^(- 6) \)$, with $Delta_g phi.alt = O \( w \)$. Together with (4.217), these identities express the new densities as bounded smooth scaling factors times the original integrable densities, plus integrable errors of orders $r^(- 4 - delta)$, $r^(- 4 - q)$, and $r^(- 6)$. Uniform metric comparability controls both norms and volume elements. The differentiated falloff has exponent $min \( q \, 2 \) > 1$, uniformly for $s$ in a small fixed interval, so large coordinate spheres remain mean convex uniformly.
  The charge limits can also be checked before differentiation. Write $pi = K - tau g$. Since conformal trace reversal gives $pi_s = f_s \( overline(K)_s - overline(tau)_s overline(g)_s \)$, expansion on the end yields
  $ pi_s = pi + s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p_0 - \( "tr"_delta p_0 \) delta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + O \( s r^(- 3 - q) \) + O \( s^2 r^(- 5) \) . $
  The terms with the original trace are of size $h_0 K = O \( r^(- 3 - q) \)$; multiplication by $f_s - 1 = O \( s r^(- 2) \)$ adds only the displayed error orders. All errors have zero limiting sphere flux. Therefore $P_s = P + s P'_(H_0)$ in the prescribed coordinates, without an extra decay assumption on $tau$. Similarly,
  $ g_s - g - s \( h_0 + 2 a phi.alt g \) = O_1 \( s^2 r^(- 4) \) \, $
  whose energy flux vanishes. The finite flux of $phi.alt$ and the scalar prototype thus give $E_s = E + s E'_H$. In particular both charge functions exist and are differentiable with the derivatives computed in Lemma 147. The path and its first two parameter derivatives are uniformly bounded relative to $g$, as required by Proposition 103. This verifies all claims and all hypotheses of the numerical theorem. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 4.53.])[
  (Multiplier identity at equality).  #emph[There exist a probability measure $pi.alt$ on $cal(T)$, a nonnegative finite measure $eta$ on $S$, a nonnegative locally finite measure $Lambda$ on $cal(A)$, and $z gt.eq 0$, with $integral_(cal(A)) w thin thin upright(d) Lambda < oo$, such that for every $H = a Q + H_0 = \( h \, p \) in cal(V)$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.219)")
 $ 6 omega cal(E)' \( H \) - c integral_(cal(T)) a'_T \( h \) thin thin upright(d) pi.alt \( T \) = chevron.l C'_H \, Lambda chevron.r - integral_S theta'_H thin thin upright(d) eta + z a . $ <rig4:var:measure-identity>]

 The constraint pairing is absolutely defined, since $C'_H \/ w$ is bounded on the compactified active set.]
] <rig4:var:multiplier>

#proof[
  Compactify $cal(A)$ by one end point. If $cal(A)$ is already compact, adjoin an isolated point; if it is empty, use just that point. The ball fibers are compact and the base has one end, so this is a compact Hausdorff space, denoted $overline(cal(A))$. By Lemma 147, $C'_H \/ w$ extends continuously to it with value $6 a$ at the added point. On the disjoint compact union
  $ cal(K) = overline(cal(A)) union.sq S union.sq cal(T) $
  consider the linear map to $C \( cal(K) \)$ whose three components are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.220)")
   $ H mapsto (C'_H / w \, med - theta'_H \, med c a'_T \( h \) - 6 omega cal(E)' \( H \)) . $ <rig4:var:separation-map>]
  Its image does not meet the open cone of functions strictly positive everywhere. Otherwise its value at the added point gives $a > 0$, and Lemma 148 supplies actual feasible data (4.216). Let $a \( s \)$ be their enclosing infimum. The area derivative formula in Proposition 103 gives
  $ a' \( 0 + \) = min_(T in cal(T)) a'_T \( h \) . $
  Strict positivity of the third component in (4.220), and compactness of $cal(T)$, imply
  $ frac(thin upright(d), thin upright(d) s)\|_(0 +) 6 omega #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(E) \( g_s \, K_s \) - frak(m) \( a \( s \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 6 omega cal(E)' \( H \) - c min_(T in cal(T)) a'_T \( h \) < 0 . $
  The expression starts at zero and is nonnegative by (4.203), a contradiction.
  Hahn–Banach separation of a linear subspace from this open convex cone gives a nonzero positive continuous functional on $C \( cal(K) \)$ annihilating the image. The subspace need not be closed: its closure is still disjoint from the open cone. The Riesz representation theorem identifies the functional with a nonzero finite positive measure on $cal(K)$.
  Its mass on $cal(T)$ is positive. If that mass were zero, testing $Q$ would give a strictly positive value on every remaining component: $C'_Q \/ w gt.eq 6$ on $cal(A)$, the added-point value is six, and $- theta'_Q = 3$ on $S$. This would contradict annihilation. Normalize the mass on $cal(T)$ to one and call the resulting measure there $pi.alt$. Call its restriction to $S$ $eta$. On $cal(A)$, divide the remaining measure by the positive function $w$ to obtain $Lambda$; this is locally finite and has finite weighted mass. If the normalized added-point mass is $lambda_oo$, put $z = 6 lambda_oo$. Rearranging annihilation of (4.220) is exactly (4.219). ◻
]

===== 4.9.3 Normal graph tests <normal-graph-tests>

The measure $pi.alt$ may initially be supported on several enclosures of the same area. The next argument uses variations of the original data to show that it is supported on the original horizon. It is local in the open exterior and assumes no complete ambient development.

#thm([Lemma 4.54.])[
  (Compact normal tests annihilating active constraints).  #emph[For every $ell in C_c^oo \( "int" Omega \)$, there are smooth variations $H_j = \( h_j \, p_j \) in cal(V)_0$, all supported in a fixed compact subset of the open exterior, such that 
$ h_j = 2 ell K \, #h(2em) C'_(H_j) arrow.r 0 $

 uniformly on the active rays over that compact set.]
] <rig4:var:normal-tests>

#proof[
  Use local Lorentzian metrics in Gaussian time, $upright(bold(g))_j = - thin upright(d) t^2 + g_j \( t \)$, near a compact neighborhood of $"supp" ell$, and prescribe $g_j \( 0 \) = g$, $partial_t g_j \( 0 \) = 2 K$. At $t = 0$, prescribe their spatial Einstein tensors to be
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.221)")
   $ \( upright(bold(G))_j \)_(i k) = \( D_j \)_(i k) \, #h(2em) D_j = frac(J ⊗ J, mu + 1 \/ j) . $ <rig4:var:spatial-Einstein>]
  This is possible by choosing the second time derivative of $g_j$. Indeed its coefficient in the spatial Einstein equation is a nonzero multiple of trace reversal on symmetric spatial tensors, which is invertible in dimension four. The normal components are fixed by the Gauss–Codazzi constraints:
  $ upright(bold(G))_j \( n \, n \) = mu \, #h(2em) upright(bold(G))_j \( n \, e_i \) = J_i . $
  A smooth choice of the second time jet realizes the prescribed tensor in a sufficiently small time neighborhood. No equation or energy condition away from the initial slice is required.
  We first check the convergence of these tensors along the slice, within the chosen open interior neighborhood. Put $Z = { mu = 0 }$ there. At every point of $Z$, smoothness and $mu gt.eq 0$ give $thin upright(d) mu = 0$. DEC implies $\| J \| lt.eq mu$, so $J = 0$ and $nabla J = 0$ there as well; for example, along a smooth curve through the point, $mu = O \( t^2 \)$ and thus $J = O \( t^2 \)$. The bounds
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.222)")
   $ \| D_j \| lt.eq mu \, #h(2em) \| nabla D_j \| lt.eq 2 \| nabla J \| + \| thin upright(d) mu \| $ <rig4:var:D-bounds>]
  follow by differentiating the quotient and using $\| J \| lt.eq mu$. On every compact subset of this interior neighborhood, these imply convergence in $C^1$ to
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.223)")
   $ D = cases(delim: "{", J ⊗ J \/ mu \, & mu > 0 \,, 0 \, & mu = 0 .) $ <rig4:var:D-limit>]
  To justify the assertion at the zero set, choose a small neighborhood of its intersection with the compact set. The right sides of (4.222) are uniformly small there by continuity and their vanishing on $Z$. On the remaining compact part, $mu$ is bounded away from zero and the usual quotient derivatives converge uniformly. The zero extension in (4.223) is differentiable on $Z$, with derivative zero, because $\| D \| lt.eq mu$ and $thin upright(d) mu = 0$ there. The same bounds give continuity of that derivative.
  The full tensors $upright(bold(G))_j$ restricted to the slice, and their covariant derivatives in spatial directions, consequently converge in $C^0$. The spacetime connection in a spatial direction along $t = 0$ depends only on the common $g \, K$, not on the chosen second time jets.
  Consider a positive-density active ray. Its activity and DEC imply $\| v \| = 1$, $\| J \| = mu$, and $v = - J^sharp \/ mu$. Thus $zeta = n + v$ is future null. On a neighborhood in the slice where $mu > 0$, define a covector $alpha$ by
  $ alpha \( n \) = mu \, #h(2em) alpha \|_(T Omega) = J . $
  It is causal and nonnegative on the future cone, and the limiting Einstein tensor along that neighborhood is $upright(bold(G))_0 = alpha ⊗ alpha \/ mu$. At the active point,
  $ alpha \( zeta \) = 0 \, #h(2em) alpha = - mu zeta^flat . $
  Parallel transport $zeta$ along any spatial curve. It remains future null, so its pairing with $alpha$ is nonnegative and has value zero at the active point. Its derivative there is zero. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.224)")
   $ \( nabla_(e_i) alpha \) \( zeta \) = 0 \, #h(2em) \( nabla_(e_i) upright(bold(G))_0 \) \( e_i \, zeta \) = 0 $ <rig4:var:tangential-cancellation>]
  at that point, for every spatial orthonormal vector $e_i$.
  Contracted Bianchi, with signature $\( - \, + \, + \, + \, + \)$, gives the exact contraction needed for the graph variation:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.225)")
   $ \( nabla_n upright(bold(G))_j \) \( n \, zeta \) = sum_(i = 1)^4 \( nabla_(e_i) upright(bold(G))_j \) \( e_i \, zeta \) arrow.r 0 . $ <rig4:var:Bianchi-cancellation>]
  There is no assertion of convergence for every normal derivative of $upright(bold(G))_j$; only (4.225) is used.
  Now vary the hypersurface to the graph $t = s ell \( x \)$ in $upright(bold(g))_j$. Its induced data have metric derivative $h_j = 2 ell K$ and some smooth tensor derivative $p_j$. They give compactly supported variations on $Omega$: outside a fixed neighborhood of $"supp" ell$ the graph is the original slice, so extend the variations by zero. The same first jets of $g_j \( t \)$ along the initial slice imply uniform compact bounds, independent of $j$, for the derivatives of the unit normal $n_s$ and of the isometrically identified vector $v_s$.
  These first derivatives can be written explicitly. The graph velocity is $ell n$. Orthogonality to its tangent vectors and the unit-normal condition give $nabla_s n_s \|_0 = nabla ell$. The material derivative of a graph-pushed vector $v$ is $thin upright(d) ell \( v \) n + ell K^sharp v$. Since $h_j = 2 ell K$, its isometric identification has derivative $I'_0 v = - ell K^sharp v$, canceling the last spatial term. Hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.226)")
   $ nabla_s n_s \|_0 = nabla ell \, #h(2em) nabla_s v_s \|_0 = thin upright(d) ell \( v \) n . $ <rig4:var:argument-derivatives>]
  Differentiating the constraint $2 upright(bold(G))_j \( n_s \, n_s + v_s \)$ and using Bianchi gives the exact formula
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.227)")
   $ 1 / 2 C'_(H_j) \( x \, v \) =  & ell sum_i \( nabla_(e_i) upright(bold(G))_j \) \( e_i \, zeta \) + upright(bold(G))_j \( nabla ell \, zeta \)\
 & + upright(bold(G))_j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] n \, nabla ell + thin upright(d) ell \( v \) n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rig4:var:graph-material-derivative>]
  Thus only spatial first derivatives of the Einstein tensor occur in the final expression. At a positive-density active ray the first limiting term is zero by (4.224), the second is zero because $upright(bold(G))_0 \( dot.op \, zeta \) = 0$, and the last is zero because
  $ alpha #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] nabla ell + thin upright(d) ell \( v \) n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = J \( nabla ell \) + mu thin thin upright(d) ell \( v \) = 0 . $
  Equivalently, $zeta_s = n_s + v_s$ remains null at a unit active vector, and its derivative pairs to zero with the proportional null covector $alpha$.
  If $mu = 0$, the full tensor $upright(bold(G))_j$ and all its spatial covariant derivatives are zero at the point, by (4.221), (4.222), and the vanishing first jets of $mu \, J$. Bianchi therefore proves the same cancellation for every $\| v \| lt.eq 1$, including non-unit active vectors. The argument-derivative terms vanish there as well.
  Let $L subset.double "int" Omega$ contain the fixed support. The limiting version of the right side of (4.227) is zero at every active ray, by the positive- and zero-density arguments. All its coefficients are bounded on $L$, uniformly for $\| v \| lt.eq 1$. Consequently
  $ sup_(cal(A) \|_L) \| C'_(H_j) \| lt.eq C_ell parallel upright(bold(G))_j - upright(bold(G))_0 parallel_(C_(upright("sp"))^1 \( L \)) arrow.r 0 \, $
  where the norm uses the common connection in spatial directions along the initial slice. This estimate contains no division by $mu$ and includes sequences of active rays approaching $Z$. The support remains strictly inside the open exterior throughout; no vanishing normal derivative of the densities at the original boundary is asserted. This proves the required uniform convergence with the derivative convention (4.204). ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 4.55.])[
  (Localization of the area multiplier).  #emph[For $pi.alt$-almost every element of $cal(T)$, its frontier equals $S$, where $pi.alt$ is the measure in Proposition 149. Consequently, for every variation $H = \( h \, p \)$ in $cal(V)$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.228)")
 $ integral_(cal(T)) a'_T \( h \) thin thin upright(d) pi.alt \( T \) = a'_S \( h \) = 1 / 2 integral_S "tr"_S h thin thin upright(d) A_g . $ <rig4:var:localized-area>]

 In particular the multiplier identity becomes 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.229)")
 $ 6 omega cal(E)' \( H \) - c thin a'_S \( h \) = chevron.l C'_H \, Lambda chevron.r - integral_S theta'_H thin thin upright(d) eta + z a . $ <rig4:var:localized-identity>]

]
] <rig4:var:area-localization>

#proof[
  Apply (4.219) to the variations in Lemma 150. Their charge, boundary, and end-coefficient terms vanish, and $Lambda$ is finite over their fixed compact support. The uniform constraint-derivative convergence therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.230)")
   $ integral_(cal(T)) integral_T ell thin "tr"_T K thin thin upright(d) A_g thin thin upright(d) pi.alt \( T \) = 0 #h(2em) upright("for every ") ell in C_c^oo \( "int" Omega \) . $ <rig4:var:trace-test>]
  Define the finite aggregate area measure $rho$ by
  $ integral f thin thin upright(d) rho = integral_(cal(T)) integral_T f thin thin upright(d) A_g thin thin upright(d) pi.alt \( T \) . $
  All frontiers lie in one compact set. By Proposition 104, off $S$ the value $"tr"_T K$ is a single continuous function $k \( x \)$ on their union. Thus (4.230) says $k rho = 0$ as a signed measure in the open exterior. It follows that $k = 0$ $rho$-almost everywhere. Fubini, followed by smoothness of each free part of a minimizing frontier, shows that for $pi.alt$-almost every $T$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.231)")
   $ "tr"_T K = 0 quad upright("everywhere on ") T \\ S . $ <rig4:var:trace-free-parts>]
  Indeed a nonzero value on a smooth free part would persist on an open piece of positive area. Such a $T$ is minimal away from $S$ by Proposition 102, and hence is a MOTS there.
  Fix one of these frontiers. At any contact point with $S$, it has the tangent plane and orientation of $S$, because its filled set contains the entire obstacle. In a local graph chart, let $f$ be its graph and $psi$ the smooth obstacle graph. The regularity in Proposition 102 gives $f in C^(1 \, alpha) ∩ W^(2 \, 2)$. On the coincidence set $f = psi$, one has $D f = D psi$. Locality of weak Sobolev derivatives, applied to the components of $D f - D psi$, gives $D^2 f = D^2 psi$ almost everywhere there. Since $S$ is a MOTS, the same MOTS equation holds almost everywhere on the contact set. By (4.231), it holds off that set as well.
  We spell out why no singular contact term remains. In these charts the MOTS equation has the form
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.232)")
   $ partial_i cal(F)^i \( x \, f \, D f \) = cal(B) \( x \, f \, D f \) \, $ <rig4:var:graph-equation>]
  where the coefficients are smooth after extending the one-sided smooth background across $S$, and the matrix $cal(F)_(p_j)^i$ is uniformly elliptic on the local bounded gradient range. As $f in W^(2 \, 2)$, its left side is an $L^2$ function and the almost-everywhere equation is also the weak divergence-form equation. For a local coordinate derivative $f_k$, distributional differentiation of (4.232) yields
  $ partial_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a^(i j) partial_j f_k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = partial_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] delta_(i k) cal(B) - cal(F)_(x_k)^i - cal(F)_z^i f_k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) a^(i j) = cal(F)_(p_j)^i \( x \, f \, D f \) . $
  All coefficients and the displayed right-hand vector field are $C^(0 \, alpha)$. The interior divergence-form Schauder estimate gives $f_k in C^(1 \, alpha)$ locally, hence $f in C^(2 \, alpha)$. Further differentiation bootstraps to smoothness #cite(label("GilbargTrudinger2001")). Thus $T$ is a smooth MOTS through contact.
  If $T$ touches $S$, strong comparison gives local coincidence of the two ordered smooth MOTS with the same orientation. To check the applicable maximum principle, their nonnegative graph difference solves a linear uniformly elliptic equation with bounded lower-order coefficients. Its zeroth-order coefficient can be replaced by a nonpositive one to obtain the inequality for the strong minimum principle; an interior zero then forces local vanishing. Therefore the intersection with $S$ is open and closed in $S$. Connectedness of $S$ implies that $S$ is a component of $T$. Since every minimizing frontier has total area $A = "Area"_g \( S \)$, there can be no additional component, and $T = S$.
  If $T$ does not touch $S$, it is a compact smooth embedded two-sided MOTS entirely in $"int" Omega$. Its connected exterior side and full enclosure of the obstacle were proved in Proposition 102. With all components and the orientation toward the end counted, it is exactly an enclosing hypersurface excluded by the outermostness hypothesis. This alternative is impossible. Thus $T = S$ for $pi.alt$-almost every $T$, proving (4.228) and (4.229). ◻
]

==== 4.10 A causal stationary field on the original exterior <rig4:adj:section>

We continue with the original equality data $\( Omega \, g \, K \)$ of Definition 99. In particular, $S = partial Omega$ is connected, $theta_(+) = 0$ on $S$, and $S$ is outer area-minimizing. The constants fixed in Section 9 are

$ r_0 = sqrt(2 m) \, #h(2em) b^0 = E / m \, #h(2em) b^i = - P_i / m \, #h(2em) c = 2 / r_0 \, #h(2em) kappa = c / 2 = 1 / r_0 . $

Thus $\( b^0 \)^2 - \| b \|^2 = 1$ and $b^0 > 0$. We fix the same exponent $1 < q_0 < min { q \, 2 }$ as in the strict variation constructed there. The purpose of this section is to extract a smooth field from the multiplier and to retain all information about the original second fundamental form.

