The proof occupies the remainder of this section. At no point do we assume that the original data are vacuum or that they lie in a prescribed stationary development. The metric in (4.237) is constructed from the multiplier.

===== 4.10.1 From a positive measure to the interior adjoint equations <rig4:adj:measures>

By Proposition 149 and the area localization in Proposition 151, the multiplier identity is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.241)")
 $ 6 omega thin cal(E)' \( H \) - c thin a'_S \( h \) = chevron.l C'_H \, Lambda chevron.r - integral_S theta'_H thin upright(d) eta + z a \, #h(2em) H = a Q + H_0 . $ <rig4:adj:localized-multiplier>]

The coefficient $a$ is the coefficient of the strict direction $Q$ in the variation space. We recall that

$ C \( x \, v \) = R_g + tau^2 - \| K \|^2 + 2 J \( v \) \, #h(2em) \| v \|_g lt.eq 1 \, $

that $Lambda$ is a positive locally finite measure on the active set $C = 0$, and that the test-vector identification for a metric variation $h$ has $dot(v) = - 1 / 2 h^sharp v$.

Let $u$ be the scalar pushforward of $Lambda$ to $Omega$, and let $X$ be its vector first moment. Initially these symbols denote measures: for a compactly supported scalar $f$ and covector $alpha$,

$ chevron.l u \, f chevron.r = integral f \( x \) thin upright(d) Lambda \( x \, v \) \, #h(2em) chevron.l X \, alpha chevron.r = integral alpha_x \( v \) thin upright(d) Lambda \( x \, v \) . $

The unit-ball condition and positivity give $\| X \|_g lt.eq u$ as measures, while activity gives $mu u + J \( X \) = 0$ as a measure. We use $upright(d) V_g$ as the reference density when expressing the resulting distributional equations. After establishing smoothness, we use the same letters for the smooth densities of these measures.

#thm([Lemma 4.57.])[
  (Interior adjoint equations).  #emph[The moment measures have smooth densities on $"int" Omega$. They satisfy (4.233)–(4.234) there, and $u gt.eq \| X \|_g$ there.]
] <rig4:adj:interior-equations>

#proof[
  For a variation supported in the open exterior, every term of (4.241) except the constraint pairing vanishes. For a pure tensor variation $p = dot(K)$, that pairing is
  $ 2 chevron.l u \, tau "tr"_g p - K : p #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[chevron.r] + 2 chevron.l X \, nabla^j \( p_(i j) - \( "tr"_g p \) g_(i j) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[chevron.r] . $
  Its distributional adjoint equation is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.242)")
   $ u \( tau g - K \) - nabla_(\( i) X_(j \)) + \( "div" X \) g = 0 . $ <rig4:adj:tensor-adjoint>]
  Taking the trace in four dimensions gives $3 u tau + 3 "div" X = 0$. Substitution proves (4.234).
  For the conformal variation $\( h \, p \) = \( 2 psi g \, psi K \)$, the constraint transformation already established in Section 9 gives, on active rays,
  $ C'_H \( x \, v \) = 6 { - Delta psi + K \( nabla psi \, v \) } . $
  Its adjoint is (4.234). This calculation is valid for measure moments, so both equations hold in distributions before any regularity assertion.
  Diverge (4.234) and substitute $"div" X = - u tau$. Commuting covariant derivatives gives a Laplace equation for $X$ whose right side consists of smooth coefficients times $u \, X \, nabla u$. In (4.234), the right side consists of smooth coefficients times $X \, nabla X$. Thus the coupled system has diagonal Laplace principal part on the scalar and vector bundles; all couplings have order at most one. Distributional interior elliptic regularity, iterated with the smooth coefficients, makes both densities smooth. The measure inequalities and complementarity now give the asserted pointwise statements.
  It remains to verify the full metric equation, including its matter term. We may now work with smooth $u \, X$. For a pure metric variation $h$, keep covariant $K$, scalar $u$, and contravariant $X$ fixed. The constraint pairing is the variation of
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.243)")
   $ integral #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] u \( R + tau^2 - \| K \|^2 \) + 2 X^i nabla^j \( K_(i j) - tau g_(i j) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] thin upright(d) V_g $ <rig4:adj:metric-functional>]
  minus $integral h \( X \, J^sharp \) thin upright(d) V_g$. The latter is precisely the contribution of $dot(v) = - 1 / 2 h^sharp v$. In using the varying volume form in (4.243), no term has been added: its original integrand is $2 u mu + 2 J \( X \) = 0$ pointwise.
  For completeness, put $pi^(i j) = K^(i j) - tau g^(i j)$. Integrating the shift term once by parts gives $- integral pi^(i j) \( cal(L)_X g \)_(i j) thin upright(d) V_g$, with unchanged boundary terms outside the variation support. At fixed covariant $K$,
  $ delta pi^(i j) = - h^i zws_a K^(a j) - h^j zws_a K^(i a) + \( K : h \) g^(i j) + tau h^(i j) . $
  Variation of the last integral, followed by integration of the term $- pi^(i j) \( cal(L)_X h \)_(i j)$, has coefficient
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.244)")
   $ cal(L)_X K - \( "div" X \) K - { X \( tau \) + K^(a b) nabla_a X_b } g . $ <rig4:adj:shift-coefficient>]
  One can see the cancellations directly: if $B = cal(L)_X g$, the variation of $pi$ contributes $2 "Sym" \( K B \) - \( "tr" B \) K - tau B$; integration of $cal(L)_X h$ contributes $\( cal(L)_X pi \)^(i j) + \( "div" X \) pi^(i j)$ with indices lowered; and the volume variation contributes $- 1 / 2 \( pi : B \) g$. Together they are (4.244).
  The scalar and quadratic terms in (4.243) have coefficient
  $ nabla^2 u - \( Delta u \) g - u "Ric"_g + 2 u \( K^2 - tau K \) + u mu g . $
  Adding (4.244) and the test-vector correction therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.245)")
   $ 0 =  & nabla^2 u - \( Delta u \) g - u "Ric"_g + 2 u \( K^2 - tau K \) + u mu g\
 & + cal(L)_X K - \( "div" X \) K - { X \( tau \) + K^(a b) nabla_a X_b } g - X_(\( i) J_(j \)) . $ <rig4:adj:metric-adjoint-expanded>]
  Equation (4.234) gives $K^(a b) nabla_a X_b = - u \| K \|^2$. Moreover, using $nabla^j K_(i j) = J_i + nabla_i tau$ in (4.234) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.246)")
   $ Delta u + X \( tau \) = u \| K \|^2 - J \( X \) = u \( \| K \|^2 + mu \) . $ <rig4:adj:trace-identity>]
  Together with $"div" X = - u tau$, these identities cancel the scalar multiples of $g$ in (4.245) and give (4.234). ◻
]

===== 4.10.2 Boundary regularity and the asymptotic constants <boundary-regularity-and-the-asymptotic-constants>

#thm([Lemma 4.58.])[
  (Prolongation and absence of boundary atoms).  #emph[The fields $u \, X$ extend smoothly to the one-sided boundary $S$. Their first jet at one interior point determines them on the connected interior. The original moment measures have no boundary-supported part, and hence are exactly $u thin upright(d) V_g$ and $X thin upright(d) V_g$ on all of $Omega$.]
] <rig4:adj:boundary-regularity>

#proof[
  Put $B_(i j) = - u K_(i j)$. Differentiate $nabla_(\( i) X_(j \)) = B_(i j)$ in three arrangements, add two of the resulting equations, and subtract the third. Commuting derivatives gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.247)")
   $ nabla_i nabla_j X_k & = nabla_i B_(j k) + nabla_j B_(i k) - nabla_k B_(i j) + cal(R)_(i j k) \( X \) \,\
cal(R)_(i j k) \( X \) & = - 1 / 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( \[ nabla_j \, nabla_i \] X \)_k + \( \[ nabla_i \, nabla_k \] X \)_j + \( \[ nabla_j \, nabla_k \] X \)_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rig4:adj:prolongation-x>]
  Every commutator is a curvature contraction with $X$. Thus this equation uses only $K nabla u$, $u nabla K$, and curvature times $X$. Expanding the Lie derivative in (4.234) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.248)")
   $ nabla_i nabla_j u =  & u \( "Ric"_(g \, i j) + tau K_(i j) - 2 \( K^2 \)_(i j) \) - X^a nabla_a K_(i j)\
 & - K_(a j) nabla_i X^a - K_(i a) nabla_j X^a + X_(\( i) J_(j \)) . $ <rig4:adj:prolongation-u>]
  Consequently
  $ cal(J) = \( u \, X \, nabla u \, nabla X \) $
  obeys a homogeneous linear first-order system, with smooth coefficients built from $g \, K$, their required derivatives, and $J$. Along any smooth curve, this is a linear ODE for $cal(J)$. Uniqueness along curves proves the first-jet assertion.
  In a compact collar of $S$, start on an interior collar section and solve this ODE along the normal segments down to $S$. Its coefficients are smooth up to the one-sided boundary. Smooth dependence on the initial point and on the normal parameter provides the smooth extension, agreeing with the original solution for positive collar parameter. The pointwise inequalities and complementarity extend by continuity.
  The original moments could still have parts supported on $S$; the interior regularity alone does not exclude them. Let $s$ be geodesic collar distance, let $f in C^oo \( S \)$ be arbitrary and extended constantly along the normal segments, and take a collar cutoff $chi$ equal to one near $S$. Choose the compact pure metric variation
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.249)")
   $ h = frac(s^2 chi \( s \) f, 6) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] g - upright(d) s ⊗ upright(d) s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) p = 0 . $ <rig4:adj:boundary-atom-test>]
  This is one of the allowed smooth compact variations, with strict-direction coefficient $a = 0$. Its value and first jet vanish on $S$. The trace along the three-dimensional collar slices is $s^2 chi f \/ 2$, so its second normal derivative at $S$ is $f$. At $S$, the scalar variation
  $ R'_g \( h \) = nabla^i nabla^j h_(i j) - Delta \( "tr"_g h \) - "Ric"_g : h $
  therefore equals $- f$: the only nonzero second jets are normal-normal derivatives of tangential components, whereas $h_(s s) = h_(s A) = 0$. The variation of $tau^2 - \| K \|^2$ uses only $h$, that of $J$ at fixed covariant $K$ uses only $h \, nabla h$, and the test-vector correction uses only $h$. Consequently $C'_H \|_S = - f$, independently of the active ray $v$.
  The area derivative vanishes because $h \|_S = 0$; the expansion derivative vanishes because $h \|_S = nabla h \|_S = p \|_S = 0$; and the charge derivative vanishes by compact support. Integrating the smooth interior densities by parts gives zero as well: the adjoint vanishes, and its scalar boundary terms involve only $h \, nabla h$, while its momentum boundary terms involve only $h$. This is ordinary integration up to $S$ using the one-sided smooth fields, not distributional differentiation of a zero extension across $S$. Thus (4.241) says that the scalar boundary measure annihilates every $f in C^oo \( S \)$, and that measure is zero. The inequality $\| X \|_g lt.eq u$ as measures eliminates the vector boundary part. Moreover, since $u$ is the pushforward of the positive measure $Lambda$, the entire measure $Lambda$ has zero mass over $S$, not merely zero first moment there. ◻
]

We first obtain constant limits from a Hessian estimate and causality. Related radial estimates for asymptotic Killing fields appear in #cite(label("BeigChrusciel1996")) Proposition 2.1 and Appendix C. The normalization of the limits will then follow from the end variations.

#thm([Lemma 4.59.])[
  (Asymptotics of causal fields).  #emph[Let $g$ be a smooth metric uniformly comparable to the Euclidean metric on an end $bb(R)^4 \\ overline(B)_R$. Let $u$ be a smooth function and $X$ a smooth vector field there with $u gt.eq \| X \|_g$. Write $Y$ for all coordinate components of $\( u \, X \)$. Suppose that, for some $q_0 > 1$ and a constant $C$, the coordinate derivatives satisfy 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.250)")
 $ \| partial^2 Y \| lt.eq C r^(- 1 - q_0) \| partial Y \| + C r^(- 2 - q_0) \| Y \| . $ <rig4:adj:jet-estimate>]

 Then there are constants $u_oo$ and $X_oo in bb(R)^4$ such that 
$ \( u \, X \) = \( u_oo \, X_oo \) + O_2 \( r^(- q_0) \) . $

]
] <rig4:adj:causal-jet-asymptotics>

#proof[
  Fix a sufficiently large coordinate sphere of radius $R$ and put
  $ D \( r \) = sup_(omega in S^3) \| partial Y \( r omega \) \| \, #h(2em) cal(M) \( r \) = sup_(R lt.eq t lt.eq r) D \( t \) . $
  Along each ray, $\| Y \( t omega \) \| lt.eq C + integral_R^t D \( a \) thin upright(d) a lt.eq C + t cal(M) \( t \)$, uniformly in $omega$. Integrating $partial_r \( partial Y \)$ using (4.250), and then taking the supremum over directions and radii at most $r$, gives
  $ cal(M) \( r \) lt.eq C + C integral_R^r t^(- 1 - q_0) cal(M) \( t \) thin upright(d) t . $
  The kernel is integrable. Gronwall therefore bounds $cal(M)$ uniformly, so $partial Y = O \( 1 \)$ and $Y = O \( r \)$. Equation (4.250) now gives $partial^2 Y = O \( r^(- 1 - q_0) \)$. Each coordinate derivative has a radial limit $L \( omega \)$, with error $O \( r^(- q_0) \)$. Any two points on the sphere of radius $r$ can be joined by a spherical arc of length at most $pi r$; integrating the Hessian along this arc gives an $O \( r^(- q_0) \)$ difference between their gradients. Letting $r arrow.r oo$ shows that $L \( omega \)$ is one constant matrix $L$. Radial integration of $partial Y - L$ then gives
  $ partial Y = L + O \( r^(- q_0) \) \, #h(2em) Y = L x + O \( 1 \) \, $
  where boundedness of the second error uses $q_0 > 1$. Since $u gt.eq 0$ in every direction, the linear part of $u$ vanishes. Uniform comparability of $g$ with the Euclidean metric and $\| X \|_g lt.eq u$ then force the linear part of $X$ to vanish as well. Thus $Y = O \( 1 \)$ and $partial Y = O \( r^(- q_0) \)$. Substitution in (4.250), using $q_0 > 1$, improves the Hessian bound to $O \( r^(- 2 - q_0) \)$. Integrating each gradient component radially toward its zero limit gives $partial Y = O \( r^(- 1 - q_0) \)$. A further radial integration gives $Y = B \( omega \) + O \( r^(- q_0) \)$; comparison of values along the same spherical arcs now gives an $O \( r^(- q_0) \)$ difference, so $B$ too is independent of direction. This proves the asserted constant limit and both differentiated estimates. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.60.])[
  (Asymptotic normalization and positive lapse).  #emph[Equation (4.235) holds, and $u > 0$ on $"int" Omega$.]
] <rig4:adj:asymptotics>

#proof[
  Let $Y$ denote all coordinate components of the pair $\( u \, X \)$. The prolonged system and the original $O_2$ metric and $O_1$ tensor decay imply Equation (4.250). Indeed curvature, $nabla K$, and $J$ have order $- 2 - q_0$, while $K$ and the Christoffel symbols have order $- 1 - q_0$. Equations (4.247) and (4.248) use no higher background derivatives. Converting their covariant Hessians to coordinate Hessians adds terms with coefficients $Gamma$, $partial Gamma$, and $Gamma^2$, controlled by the stated $O_2$ metric decay.
  Lemma 155 now gives constants with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.251)")
   $ \( u \, X \) = \( u_oo \, X_oo \) + O_2 \( r^(- q_0) \) . $ <rig4:adj:unnormalized-decay>]
  Use the five end prototypes of Definition 146, whose charge derivatives are computed in Lemma 147, in (4.241). Their strict-direction coefficient is $a = 0$, so the term $z a$ is absent; their boundary and area terms vanish because the prototypes are supported away from $S$. Integration by parts using the interior adjoint equations leaves only the outer boundary flux. By (4.251), its limit is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.252)")
   $ 6 omega #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] u_oo E' + X_oo^i P'_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rig4:adj:charge-flux>]
  For clarity, the leading integrand is
  $ u_oo \( partial_j h_(i j) - partial_i h_(j j) \) + 2 X_oo^j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p_(i j) - \( "tr"_delta p \) delta_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  This is exactly (4.252) with the energy and momentum normalizations of Equations (4.1)–(4.1). Every other boundary term contains a decaying background or adjoint coefficient, or its derivative, paired with $h = O_2 \( r^(- 2) \)$ or $p = O_1 \( r^(- 3) \)$ at the corresponding order. Multiplication by the sphere area $O \( r^3 \)$ makes each such flux tend to zero. The volume pairing converges as well: the prototype constraint derivatives are $O \( r^(- 4 - q) \)$ and the moment densities are bounded.
  The scalar prototype $h = r^(- 2) delta$, $p = 0$, has $\( E' \, P' \) = \( 1 \, 0 \)$. For the tensor prototype indexed by a constant vector $e$, its Euclidean trace reversal contracts with the sphere normal to $r^(- 3) e$, and thus has $\( E' \, P' \) = \( 0 \, e \/ 3 \)$. These independent charges identify $\( u_oo \, X_oo \) = \( b^0 \, b \)$ from (4.241) and (4.252). This proves (4.235).
  If $u$ vanished at an interior point, its nonnegativity would give $upright(d) u = 0$ there. Taylor’s theorem and $\| X \| lt.eq u$ would give $X = 0$ and $nabla X = 0$ at the same point. The whole first jet would vanish, contradicting Lemma 154 and $u_oo = b^0 > 0$. ◻
]

===== 4.10.3 Boundary fluxes and the two lapse cases <boundary-fluxes-and-the-two-lapse-cases>

#thm([Lemma 4.61.])[
  (Boundary equations).  #emph[The multiplier measure at $S$ is $upright(d) eta = 2 u thin upright(d) A_g$, and (4.236) holds.]
] <rig4:adj:boundary-data>

#proof[
  Take arbitrary compact pure tensor variations up to $S$. The actual outward normal of the exterior domain at its inner boundary is $- nu$. Integration by parts in (4.241) therefore gives
  $ - 2 integral_S #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] p \( X \, nu \) - \( "tr"_g p \) X_nu #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] thin upright(d) A_g - integral_S \( "tr"_S p \) thin upright(d) eta = 0 . $
  Arbitrary mixed components $p_(nu A)$ imply $X_(T S) = 0$, and arbitrary tangential trace implies $upright(d) eta = 2 X_nu thin upright(d) A_g$.
  Now take $\( h \, p \) = \( 2 psi g \, psi K \)$ with arbitrary compact support up to $S$. At a MOTS, its expansion derivative is $3 partial_nu psi$, while its area derivative is $3 integral_S psi thin upright(d) A_g$. Integrating its constraint pairing with (4.234) gives
  $ - 3 c integral_S psi thin upright(d) A_g =  & 6 integral_S [u partial_nu psi - { partial_nu u + K \( X \, nu \) } psi] thin upright(d) A_g - 3 integral_S partial_nu psi thin upright(d) eta . $
  The boundary value and normal derivative of $psi$ can be prescribed independently. Their coefficients give $upright(d) eta = 2 u thin upright(d) A_g$ and $partial_nu u + K \( X \, nu \) = c \/ 2$. Together with the tensor-variation conclusions these are exactly (4.236). ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.62.])[
  (Boundary lapse dichotomy).  #emph[Either $u > 0$ at every point of $S$ or $u = 0$ identically on $S$.]
] <rig4:adj:lapse-dichotomy>

#proof[
  Let $L_S$ be the expansion variation at $S$ under normal motion with speed $s nu$. Its principal part is $- Delta_S$. More explicitly, in a geodesic normal extension of $nu$, if $upright("II")$ is the second fundamental form of $S$ in $\( Omega \, g \)$,
  $ L_S s = - Delta_S s + 2 K \( nu \, nabla_S s \) + [- \| upright("II") \|^2 - "Ric"_g \( nu \, nu \) + "tr"_(T S) \( nabla_nu K \)] s . $
  Only the smoothness of the lower coefficients and the displayed principal part will be needed.
  Extend $s nu$ to a smooth compactly supported vector field $Y_1$ up to the boundary, and use $H = \( cal(L)_(Y_1) g \, cal(L)_(Y_1) K \)$ in (4.241). These are legitimate one-sided tensor variations; one may compute their jets using any smooth extension across $S$. Their area and expansion derivatives are respectively $integral_S H s thin upright(d) A_g$ and $L_S s$.
  We check the active-ray derivative with the specified vector identification. At a fixed interior point put $cal(A) \( v \) = nabla_v Y_1$, so $h^sharp = cal(A) + cal(A)^(*)$, where the adjoint uses $g$. Let $phi.alt_t$ be the local flow of $Y_1$ and let $v_t$ be the isometrically identified test vector for $g_t = phi.alt_t^(*) g$. Naturality gives
  $ C_(phi.alt_t^(*) g \, phi.alt_t^(*) K) \( x \, v_t \) = C_(g \, K) \( phi.alt_t x \, upright(d) phi.alt_t \( v_t \) \) . $
  At $t = 0$, the covariant velocity of the vector on the right, relative to parallel transport along $phi.alt_t x$, is
  $ cal(A) \( v \) + dot(v) = cal(A) \( v \) - 1 / 2 \( cal(A) + cal(A)^(*) \) v = 1 / 2 \( cal(A) - cal(A)^(*) \) v . $
  It is perpendicular to $v$. The base derivative of $C$, with $v$ parallel transported, vanishes at an interior active ray because it differentiates a nonnegative function at a two-sided interior minimum. The remaining vector derivative is $2 J \( 1 / 2 \( cal(A) - cal(A)^(*) \) v \)$, also zero: at an active unit ray $J = - mu v^flat$, and at an active ray with $\| v \| < 1$ we have $mu = J = 0$. Therefore $C'_H = 0$ at every interior active ray.
  The vector field $Y_1$ need not preserve $S$: its compact Lie derivatives are permitted tensor variations, independently of feasibility of a flow on the whole exterior. No normal derivative at a one-sided boundary minimum is being set equal to zero. Such boundary rays contribute no integral because $Lambda$ has zero mass over $S$ by Lemma 154. Thus the entire constraint pairing vanishes. The multiplier identity and $upright(d) eta = 2 u thin upright(d) A_g$ now read
  $ - c integral_S H s thin upright(d) A_g = - 2 integral_S u L_S s thin upright(d) A_g \, $
  and hence give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.253)")
   $ 2 L_S^(*) u = c H . $ <rig4:adj:boundary-stability-adjoint>]
  Here the adjoint is with respect to $upright(d) A_g$ on the closed manifold $S$.
  Outer area minimization, tested against every nonnegative outward normal speed, gives $H gt.eq 0$ pointwise. Hence $L_S^(*) u gt.eq 0$ and $u gt.eq 0$ on $S$. Increase the zeroth coefficient of $L_S^(*)$ by a constant until that coefficient is nonnegative. The inequality remains valid because $u gt.eq 0$. The strong minimum principle for this operator with principal part $- Delta_S$ shows that a zero of $u$ forces $u$ to vanish on the connected surface $S$. Otherwise it is everywhere positive. ◻
]

===== 4.10.4 The constructed spacetime and its null stress <the-constructed-spacetime-and-its-null-stress>

#thm([Lemma 4.63.])[
  (Killing development and timelike collars).  #emph[The metric in (4.237) has all the properties in parts (iv) and (v) of Theorem 152.]
] <rig4:adj:killing-development>

#proof[
  The interior positivity of $u$ makes (4.237) a smooth Lorentzian metric, with future normal $n = u^(- 1) \( partial_t - X \)$ after choosing the indicated time orientation. In the convention of the problem, the second fundamental form of a lapse-shift metric is
  $ frac(1, 2 u) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] partial_t g - cal(L)_X g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Its coefficients here are independent of $t$, and (4.234) identifies this tensor with the original $K$. The vector field $xi = partial_t$ is Killing by construction.
  We record the curvature computation to fix both the matter term and the sign convention. The Gauss and normal-variation identities for a general lapse-shift metric, with positive second fundamental form convention, are
  $ "Ric"_(upright(bold(g)) \, i j) & = "Ric"_(g \, i j) + tau K_(i j) - 2 \( K^2 \)_(i j) + u^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] partial_t K_(i j) - \( cal(L)_X K \)_(i j) - \( nabla^2 u \)_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
"Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \( n \, n \) & = - u^(- 1) \( partial_t tau - X tau \) - \| K \|^2 + u^(- 1) Delta u . $
  Tracing these equations uses
  $ g^(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] partial_t K_(i j) - \( cal(L)_X K \)_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = partial_t tau - X tau + 2 u \| K \|^2 . $
  In the stationary case this yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.254)")
   $ "Ric"_(upright(bold(g)) \, i j) & = "Ric"_(g \, i j) + tau K_(i j) - 2 \( K^2 \)_(i j) - u^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( cal(L)_X K \)_(i j) + \( nabla^2 u \)_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
R_(upright(bold(g))) & = R_g + tau^2 + \| K \|^2 - 2 u^(- 1) \( Delta u + X tau \) . $ <rig4:adj:spatial-ricci>]
  Equation (4.246) and complementarity show that
  $ R_(upright(bold(g))) = 2 mu + frac(2 J \( X \), u) = 0 . $
  Writing $upright(bold(G))$ for the Einstein tensor, the constraints and (4.234) consequently give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.255)")
   $ upright(bold(G)) \( n \, n \) = mu \, #h(2em) upright(bold(G)) \( n \, e_i \) = J_i \, #h(2em) u upright(bold(G))_(i j) = - X_(\( i) J_(j \)) . $ <rig4:adj:einstein-components>]
  These identities can also be compared with the transversal Killing construction in #cite(label("BeigChrusciel1997KID")); its momentum symbol has the opposite sign to the one used here. The null-fluid structure of a modified constraint adjoint is discussed in #cite(label("HuangLee2024Bartnik")) Section 6.1. We use the displayed direct calculation, in four spatial dimensions, rather than importing a stationarity or dimensional-transfer theorem.
  If $mu = 0$, the dominant energy condition gives $J = 0$, so all entries in (4.255) vanish. If $mu > 0$, the inequalities
  $ 0 = u mu + J \( X \) gt.eq u mu - \| J \| \| X \| gt.eq 0 $
  force $\| J \| = mu$, $\| X \| = u$, and $J = - mu X^flat \/ u$. Since $upright(bold(g)) \( xi \, n \) = - u$ and $upright(bold(g)) \( xi \, e_i \) = X_i$, the tensor with components (4.255) is exactly
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.256)")
   $ upright(bold(G)) = mu / u^2 thin xi^flat ⊗ xi^flat quad upright("where ") mu > 0 . $ <rig4:adj:null-stress>]
  The Killing one-form in this equation is the spacetime one-form. It is null wherever the displayed tensor is nonzero. Thus $upright(bold(G)) \( xi \, dot.op \) = 0$ everywhere. Since $R_(upright(bold(g))) = 0$, this is the first assertion of (4.238). If $N > 0$, then $\| X \| < u$, so complementarity forces $mu = J = 0$; all the Ricci components vanish. This proves the second assertion. An internal null region has not been excluded or assumed vacuum.
  It remains to examine the original boundary and the end. Equation (4.235) gives $N arrow.r \( b^0 \)^2 - \| b \|^2 = 1$. At $S$, Equation (4.236) gives $X = u nu$, so $N = 0$ and all tangential derivatives of $N$ vanish. If $u > 0$ on $S$, Equation (4.234) gives $chevron.l nabla_nu X \, nu chevron.r = - u K \( nu \, nu \)$. Hence
  $ partial_nu N = 2 u #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] partial_nu u + u K \( nu \, nu \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 2 u kappa \, $
  which is (4.239). Compactness of $S$ supplies a full collar on which $N > 0$.
  In the other case, $u \|_S = 0$ and $X \|_S = 0$. Tangential derivatives of $X$ vanish. The normal-normal and normal-tangential components of (4.234) then give $nabla_nu X = 0$ on $S$, so every first derivative of $X$ vanishes there. Smooth one-sided division by normal distance gives $u = s d$ and $X = s^2 Y_2$. The boundary condition gives $d \|_S = partial_nu u \|_S = kappa > 0$. This is (4.240), and once again compactness gives a full collar with $N > 0$. Together with positivity far out on the unique end, these collars place all interior zeros of $N$ in an interior compact set. ◻
]

#proof[
  Combine Lemmas 153, 154, 156, 157, 158, and 159. ◻
]

==== 4.11 Vanishing twist and the complete static base <rig4:sta:section>

We continue with the equality data and the fields supplied by Theorem 152. The stationary metric

$ upright(bold(g)) = - u^2 d t^2 + g_(i j) \( d x^i + X^i d t \) \( d x^j + X^j d t \) quad upright("on ") bb(R) times "int" Omega $

is smooth because $u > 0$ in the interior. Its Killing field is $xi = partial_t$. Recall that

$ N = u^2 - \| X \|_g^2 gt.eq 0 \, #h(2em) "Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \( xi \, dot.op \) = 0 \, #h(2em) "Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) = 0 quad upright("where ") N > 0 . $

Moreover, $N arrow.r 1$ at the end, and $N > 0$ on a full interior collar of $S$. Thus the interior zero set

$ Z = { x in "int" Omega : N \( x \) = 0 } $

is compactly contained in $"int" Omega$. No regularity of $Z$ as a subset is assumed. In particular, it may have interior or infinitely many components.

===== 4.11.1 The Killing current near a null set <the-killing-current-near-a-null-set>

On the open set $cal(P) = { N > 0 } subset "int" Omega$, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.257)")
 $ h_b & = g + N^(- 1) X^flat ⊗ X^flat \, & C_b & = g^(- 1) - u^(- 2) X ⊗ X = h_b^(- 1) \,\
A_b & = N^(- 1) X^flat \, & F & = d A_b \, #h(2em) lambda = sqrt(N) . $ <rig4:sta:base-variables>]

Here $X^flat$ is formed with $g$. Direct substitution gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.258)")
 $ upright(bold(g)) = - N \( d t - A_b \)^2 + h_b . $ <rig4:sta:stationary-decomposition>]

The tensor $C_b$ extends smoothly and nonnegatively through $Z$, although $h_b$ and $A_b$ need not extend there. For a covector $alpha$ write $\| alpha \|_(C_b)^2 = C_b^(i j) alpha_i alpha_j$.

#thm([Lemma 4.64.])[
  (Killing-current identities).  #emph[Let $cal(G)_(alpha beta) = nabla_alpha^(upright(bold(g))) xi_beta$. On the interior of $Omega$ one has 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.259)")
 $ u^(- 1) "div"_g \( u C_b thin d N \) = - 2 \| cal(G) \|_(upright(bold(g)))^2 . $ <rig4:sta:norm-equation>]

 On $cal(P)$ the antisymmetric tensor 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.260)")
 $ Q^(i j) = u N C_b^(i k) C_b^(j ell) F_(k ell) #h(2em) upright("satisfies") #h(2em) nabla_i^g Q^(i j) = 0 . $ <rig4:sta:killing-current>]

 The norm in (4.259) is the Lorentzian contraction of both tensor indices; it need not be nonnegative.]
] <rig4:sta:current-identities>

#proof[
  The Killing identities and $"Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \( xi \, dot.op \) = 0$ give
  $ nabla^(upright(bold(g)) \, alpha) cal(G)_(alpha beta) = 0 \, #h(2em) square.stroked.tiny_(upright(bold(g))) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] upright(bold(g)) \( xi \, xi \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 2 \| cal(G) \|_(upright(bold(g)))^2 . $
  For a stationary scalar function the wave operator is $u^(- 1) "div"_g \( u C_b thin d thin dot.op thin \)$: the spacetime volume density is $u sqrt(det g)$ and its spatial inverse-metric block is $C_b$. Since $upright(bold(g)) \( xi \, xi \) = - N$, this proves (4.259), including at $Z$ where all its entries are smooth.
  For the second identity set $theta = d t - A_b$. On $cal(P)$,
  $ xi^flat = - N theta \, #h(2em) cal(G) = 1 / 2 d xi^flat = 1 / 2 \( - d N and theta + N F \) . $
  The inverse-metric dual of $theta$ is proportional to $partial_t$, so the term containing $theta$ has zero spatial-spatial component after both indices are raised. Consequently
  $ cal(G)^(i j) = N / 2 C_b^(i k) C_b^(j ell) F_(k ell) . $
  The stationary divergence identity, with density $u sqrt(det g)$, now gives (4.260). In converting coordinate divergence to $g$-covariant divergence, the additional Christoffel term vanishes by antisymmetry. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.65.])[
  (A transverse logarithmic estimate).  #emph[Suppose $Z eq.not diameter$. On a neighborhood of $Z$ with compact closure in the interior, let 
$ B = \| X \|_g \, #h(2em) e = X \/ B \, #h(2em) D = e^perp \, $

 and denote by $d_D N$ the $g$-orthogonal projection of $d N$ to $D$. This neighborhood can be chosen so that $u$ and $B$ are bounded below by positive constants. For every smooth cutoff $zeta$ compactly supported there, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.261)")
 $ sup_(epsilon > 0) integral u zeta^2 frac(\| d N \|_(C_b)^2, \( N + epsilon \)^2) thin d V_g < oo . $ <rig4:sta:null-log-energy>]

 In particular, $d_D log N$ is square-integrable on the positive set in every smaller neighborhood of $Z$.]
] <rig4:sta:log-estimate>

#proof[
  At a point of $Z$, $B = u > 0$. Compactness provides the positive lower bounds after shrinking a neighborhood. On that neighborhood the eigenvalues of $C_b$ relative to $g$ are $1$ on $D$ and $N \/ u^2$ in the $e$ direction. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.262)")
   $ \| d N \|_(C_b)^2 = \| d_D N \|_g^2 + N / u^2 \( e N \)^2 . $ <rig4:sta:degenerate-norm>]
  Smoothness and nonnegativity of $N$ also give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.263)")
   $ \| d N \|_g^2 lt.eq C N . $ <rig4:sta:nonnegative-gradient>]
  For completeness, work in a slightly larger compact interior neighborhood, where the Hessian is bounded. Taylor’s inequality along a short geodesic in the direction $- nabla N$ gives $0 lt.eq N - t \| d N \| + C t^2 \/ 2$. Increasing $C$ so that $t = \| d N \| \/ C$ stays in this neighborhood proves (4.263).
  Let $n = u^(- 1) \( xi - X \)$ be the future unit normal to the original slice. In a local orthonormal frame $n \, e \, e_a$, with the $e_a$ in $D$, differentiating $upright(bold(g)) \( xi \, xi \) = - N$ gives
  $ cal(G)_(i n) = - frac(N_i \/ 2 + B cal(G)_(i e), u) quad upright("for each spatial index ") i . $
  As $\| cal(G) \|_(upright(bold(g)))^2 = 2 sum_(i < j) cal(G)_(i j)^2 - 2 sum_i cal(G)_(i n)^2$, we obtain the exact expansion
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.264)")
   $ - 2 \| cal(G) \|_(upright(bold(g)))^2 =  & frac(\| d N \|_g^2, u^2) + frac(4 B, u^2) sum_a N_a cal(G)_(a e) - frac(4 N, u^2) sum_a cal(G)_(a e)^2 - 4 sum_(a < b) cal(G)_(a b)^2\
lt.eq  & C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] N + \| d_D N \|_g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rig4:sta:killing-norm-bound>]
  All coefficients of $cal(G)$ are bounded here. This estimate is pointwise and does not require a global frame for $D$.
  Write $f = - 2 \| cal(G) \|_(upright(bold(g)))^2$ and test $"div"_g \( u C_b thin d N \) = u f$ against $zeta^2 \/ \( N + epsilon \)$. There is no singular boundary in this test: Equation (4.259) is smooth across $Z$, and $epsilon > 0$. Integration by parts gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.265)")
   $ integral u zeta^2 frac(\| d N \|_(C_b)^2, \( N + epsilon \)^2) thin d V_g =  & integral frac(u zeta^2 f, N + epsilon) thin d V_g\
 & + 2 integral frac(u zeta chevron.l d zeta \, d N chevron.r_(C_b), N + epsilon) thin d V_g . $ <rig4:sta:log-test>]
  In the first term use (4.264), $N \/ \( N + epsilon \) lt.eq 1$, and $\| d_D N \| lt.eq \| d N \|_(C_b)$. Young’s inequality absorbs its derivative term into, say, one quarter of the left side. The second integral is handled by Cauchy’s inequality for the nonnegative tensor $C_b$ and another quarter of that side. The remaining terms are bounded by a constant times $integral u \( zeta^2 + \| d zeta \|_(C_b)^2 \) thin d V_g$, uniformly in $epsilon$. This proves (4.261). Fatou’s lemma and (4.262) give the last assertion. ◻
]

===== 4.11.2 Removal of all twist boundary terms <removal-of-all-twist-boundary-terms>

#thm([Proposition 4.66.])[
  (Vanishing of the twist).  #emph[On all of $cal(P)$, including every component, $F = d A_b = 0$.]
] <rig4:sta:twist-vanishes>

#proof[
  On the asymptotic end the constant limit of $A_b$ is the covector $a_oo = b^i thin d x^i$, because $N arrow.r 1$ and $g arrow.r delta$. Choose a smooth function $f$ on $Omega$ which equals $b^i x^i$ sufficiently far out and is zero off an end neighborhood disjoint from $S$ and $Z$. Then
  $ beta = A_b - d f \, #h(2em) d beta = F \, #h(2em) beta = O \( r^(- q_0) \) quad upright("at infinity") . $
  For any nonnegative smooth function $chi$ compactly supported in $cal(P)$, multiply (4.260) by $chi beta_j$ and integrate. Antisymmetry gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.266)")
   $ 1 / 2 integral_(cal(P)) chi u N C_b^(i k) C_b^(j ell) F_(i j) F_(k ell) thin d V_g = - integral_(cal(P)) Q^(i j) \( partial_i chi \) beta_j thin d V_g . $ <rig4:sta:twist-test>]
  The integrand on the left is nonnegative, and its quadratic form in $F$ is positive definite at each point of $cal(P)$.
  The contraction in the right side has a cancellation that is needed at the null loci. Lowering its remaining index with $g$, one finds
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.267)")
   $ g_(i k) Q^(k j) \( A_b \)_j & = N / u F_(i j) X^j\
 & = 1 / u [X^j \( d X^flat \)_(i j) - frac(\| X \|^2 N_i - X \( N \) X_i, N)] . $ <rig4:sta:current-contraction>]
  Indeed $C_b A_b = X \/ u^2$, and the projection in the other raised index does not change $F_(i j) X^j$. This covector is orthogonal to $X$. Where $X eq.not 0$, its last numerator is $B^2 \( d_D N \)_i$. The coordinate-free numerator in (4.267) will also be used where $X = 0$.
  We choose cutoffs near the three possible escape loci with disjoint transition neighborhoods. If $Z = diameter$, the first cutoff below is identically one.
  ======= 4.11.2.1 The interior null set. <the-interior-null-set.>
  Choose nested relatively compact interior neighborhoods of $Z$ on which $u$ and $B$ are bounded below and Lemma 161 applies. A smooth cutoff $eta$ is one on the smaller neighborhood and supported in the larger. Let $theta.alt : \[ 0 \, oo \) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ be smooth, zero on $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ and one on $\[ 2 \, oo \)$, with bounded derivative, and set
  $ chi_(Z \, epsilon) = 1 - eta + eta theta.alt \( N \/ epsilon \) . $
  For all sufficiently small $epsilon$, $N$ has a positive lower bound on the support of $d eta$, so differentiation of $eta$ adds no term. The only transition is $epsilon < N < 2 epsilon$, and its derivative is bounded by $C \| d N \| \/ epsilon$. Here $d f = 0$. Orthogonality in (4.267) replaces $d N$ by $d_D N$ in the contraction. Since $N asymp epsilon$ on the band and the smooth term $X corner.r.b d X^flat$ is bounded, the absolute contribution to the right side of (4.266) is at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.268)")
   $ C integral_({ epsilon < N < 2 epsilon } ∩ "supp" eta) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + \| d_D log N \|_g^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] thin d V_g = o \( 1 \) . $ <rig4:sta:null-cutoff-cost>]
  The integrable majorant comes from Lemma 161. The characteristic functions of these bands tend pointwise to zero even at points of $Z$. Thus dominated convergence proves the last assertion without an assumption on the measure or structure of $Z$.
  ======= 4.11.2.2 The asymptotic end. <the-asymptotic-end.>
  Let $chi_(oo \, R)$ be one for $r lt.eq R$, zero for $r gt.eq 2 R$, with derivative bounded by $C \/ R$, and extend it by one over the compact part. From the adjoint end estimates,
  $ Q = O \( r^(- 1 - q_0) \) \, #h(2em) beta = O \( r^(- q_0) \) . $
  The transition annulus has volume $O \( R^4 \)$, so its contribution is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.269)")
   $ O \( R^(2 - 2 q_0) \) = o \( 1 \) \, $ <rig4:sta:end-cutoff-cost>]
  since $q_0 > 1$.
  ======= 4.11.2.3 The original boundary. <the-original-boundary.>
  Let $s$ be original $g$-normal distance from $S$, increasing into $Omega$, and let $chi_(S \, sigma)$ change from zero to one on $sigma < s < 2 sigma$, with derivative bounded by $C \/ sigma$. Again $d f = 0$ near this collar. If $u \|_S > 0$, the boundary conclusions of Theorem 152 give
  $ d N = 2 kappa X^flat quad upright("on ") S \, #h(2em) X = u nu quad upright("on ") S \, #h(2em) N asymp s . $
  In particular $d_D N = O \( s \)$, so (4.267) is bounded. Its contraction with the normal $nabla s$ is $O \( s \)$: it annihilates $X$, and $nabla s - X \/ u = O \( s \)$. Multiplication by $1 \/ sigma$ and integration over a collar of volume $O \( sigma \)$ therefore gives $O \( sigma \)$.
  If $u \|_S = 0$, the same theorem supplies smooth one-sided fields $d \, Y_2$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.270)")
   $ u = s d \, #h(2em) X = s^2 Y_2 \, #h(2em) d \|_S = kappa \, #h(2em) N = s^2 \( d^2 - s^2 \| Y_2 \|^2 \) . $ <rig4:sta:zero-lapse-collar>]
  Thus $u asymp s$, $N asymp s^2$, $d X^flat = O \( s \)$, and $d N = O \( s \)$. Both terms inside the brackets of (4.267) are $O \( s^3 \)$; its entire value is $O \( s^2 \)$. This gives a boundary cutoff cost $O \( sigma^2 \)$.
  Now take $chi = chi_(Z \, epsilon) chi_(S \, sigma) chi_(oo \, R)$. For positive parameters with sufficiently small $epsilon \, sigma$ and large $R$, it is a compactly supported smooth test on $cal(P)$. Multiplication by the other two factors does not increase any of the three estimates. Therefore the absolute value of the right side of (4.266) tends to zero as $epsilon \, sigma arrow.b 0$ and $R arrow.r oo$. Choose a sequence of such parameters; its cutoffs tend pointwise to one on $cal(P)$. Fatou’s lemma gives zero for the integral of the nonnegative density on the left. Its continuity and pointwise positive definiteness force $F = 0$ everywhere on $cal(P)$. This integration is on an open manifold with compactly supported tests and remains valid if $cal(P)$ has infinitely many components. ◻
]

===== 4.11.3 Static equations and the only finite-distance attachment <static-equations-and-the-only-finite-distance-attachment>

Let $cal(U)$ be the component of $cal(P)$ containing the distant asymptotic end. Since $F = 0$ on all of $cal(P)$, the Poincaré lemma supplies a local primitive of $A_b$ on every sufficiently small ball in any positive component. Replacing $t$ locally by $T = t - a$ with $d a = A_b$ turns (4.258) into $- lambda^2 d T^2 + h_b$. Its Ricci components are

$ \( "Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \)_(T T) = lambda Delta_(h_b) lambda \, #h(2em) \( "Ric"_(upright(bold(g))) \)_(i j) = \( "Ric"_(h_b) \)_(i j) - lambda^(- 1) \( "Hess"_(h_b) lambda \)_(i j) . $

These follow directly from the nonzero mixed Christoffel symbols $Gamma_(T i)^T = partial_i log lambda$ and $Gamma_(T T)^i = lambda h_b^(i j) partial_j lambda$. Vacuum on $cal(P)$ therefore gives, on every positive component,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.271)")
 $ lambda "Ric"_(h_b) = "Hess"_(h_b) lambda \, #h(2em) Delta_(h_b) lambda = 0 \, #h(2em) "Scal"_(h_b) = 0 . $ <rig4:sta:static-equations>]

This conclusion uses local primitives only; no global time primitive or topological conclusion has yet been used.

#thm([Lemma 4.67.])[
  (Connectedness of the positive set).  #emph[The set $cal(P)$ is connected and equals $cal(U)$. In particular, the positive collar of $S$ belongs to $cal(U)$.]
] <rig4:sta:connected-positive-set>

#proof[
  The unique asymptotic end lies in $cal(U)$ outside a compact set. Any other component $V$ of $cal(P)$ therefore has compact closure in the original exterior $Omega$, with its boundary included. Its frontier lies in $S union Z$: an interior frontier point with $N > 0$ would have a connected positive neighborhood meeting $V$ and hence belong to $V$. The frontier is nonempty, since otherwise $V$ would be both open and closed in the connected interior of $Omega$, which also contains $cal(U)$.
  The continuous function $lambda = sqrt(N)$ vanishes on this frontier and is positive in $V$. It therefore attains a positive maximum at an interior point of $V$. The strong maximum principle for $Delta_(h_b) lambda = 0$ makes $lambda$ constant on $V$, contradicting its zero frontier values. Thus there is no such component $V$. The collar supplied by Theorem 152 lies in $cal(P) = cal(U)$, as claimed. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 4.68.])[
  (Completeness and regular horizon attachment).  #emph[The component $cal(U)$ satisfies $0 < lambda < 1$. Every approach in $cal(U)$ to an interior zero of $N$ has infinite $h_b$-length. Adjoining $S$ gives a complete manifold with smooth one-sided metric $h_b$ and lapse $lambda$, in the boundary structure specified below, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.272)")
 $ h_b \|_(T S) = g \|_(T S) \, #h(2em) lambda \|_S = 0 \, #h(2em) partial_(nu_(h_b)) lambda = kappa \, #h(2em) upright("II")_(h_b) = 0 . $ <rig4:sta:boundary-data>]

 Here $nu_(h_b)$ points into the base exterior. The metric has an even reflection and the lapse an odd reflection of class at least $C^2$. More precisely:]
  + #emph[If $u \|_S > 0$, then $N$ is a defining function in the original smooth structure and $d N = 2 kappa X^flat$ on $S$. The base boundary structure uses $lambda = sqrt(N)$ in place of $N$. In that structure the reflected metric and signed lapse are smooth.]
  + #emph[If $u \|_S = 0$, the one-sided base metric and lapse are smooth in the original boundary structure. Their even and odd reflections in base normal distance are $C^2$.]
  #emph[In either case the base attachment is homeomorphic to the original attachment of $S$. There are no other finite-distance boundary attachments.]
] <rig4:sta:complete-base>

#proof[
  First consider an interior approach to $Z$. With the notation of Lemma 161, Equation (4.267) and $F = 0$ give
  $ B^2 d_D log N = X^j \( d X^flat \)_(i j) thin d x^i \, #h(2em) \| d_D log N \|_g lt.eq C . $
  Together with (4.263) this yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.273)")
   $ \| d log N \|_(h_b)^2 = \| d_D log N \|_g^2 + frac(\( e N \)^2, u^2 N) lt.eq C $ <rig4:sta:base-log-bound>]
  on the positive set near $Z$. Along any curve approaching $Z$, $log N arrow.r - oo$, which is incompatible with finite length and (4.273).
  The original space $\( Omega \, g \)$, with its boundary included, is a complete locally compact length space and hence proper. Since $h_b gt.eq g$ on $cal(U)$, a finite-length base curve is $g$-Cauchy and has a limit in $Omega$. A limit in the interior with $N > 0$ lies in $cal(U)$ and is a regular base point. A limit in $Z$ is excluded by the preceding bound. Escape to the asymptotic end also has infinite length. Thus the only possible finite-distance attachment is $S$, whose positive collar belongs to $cal(U)$ by Lemma 163.
  Next, $lambda$ tends to one at infinity, and it tends to zero at any finite limiting boundary of $cal(U)$ in the original manifold. If $lambda > 1$ somewhere, continuity on the compact part of the original space and its end limit give an attained interior maximum. The strong maximum principle in (4.271) would make $lambda$ constant on $cal(U)$, contradicting its limit one. Hence $lambda lt.eq 1$. If equality held at an interior point, the same principle would give $N equiv 1$ on $cal(U)$. Such a component has no interior frontier, because continuity would give $N = 1 > 0$ at each frontier point. It is therefore both open and closed in the connected $"int" Omega$, so it is the entire interior. This contradicts $N arrow.r 0$ at $S$. Thus $0 < lambda < 1$.
  It remains to construct and check the attachment of $S$.
  ======= 4.11.3.1 Positive boundary lapse. <positive-boundary-lapse.>
  Here $d N = 2 u kappa nu^flat$ on $S$, with $u > 0$, so $\( N \, y^A \)$ are original local boundary coordinates. The identity $d N = 2 kappa X^flat$ at $N = 0$ and smooth division give
  $ X^flat = a thin d N + N beta_A thin d y^A \, #h(2em) a \( 0 \, y \) = frac(1, 2 kappa) \, $
  where $a$ and $beta_A$ are smooth in $\( N \, y \)$. Passing to $\( lambda \, y \)$, with $N = lambda^2$, the base metric components are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.274)")
   $ \( h_b \)_(lambda lambda) & = 4 lambda^2 g_(N N) + 4 a^2 \,\
\( h_b \)_(lambda A) & = 2 lambda \( g_(N A) + a beta_A \) \,\
\( h_b \)_(A B) & = g_(A B) + lambda^2 beta_A beta_B . $ <rig4:sta:positive-lapse-base-collar>]
  All original coefficients on the right are evaluated at $\( lambda^2 \, y \)$. The first and third expressions are smooth even functions of signed $lambda$, and the middle one is smooth odd. They therefore define a smooth metric invariant under $\( lambda \, y \) mapsto \( - lambda \, y \)$. At the boundary the cross terms vanish and $\( h_b \)_(lambda lambda) = kappa^(- 2)$; the tangential metric is $g \|_(T S)$. Nondegeneracy holds on both sides after restricting the collar. The reflection fixes $S$, so its second fundamental form vanishes, and its inward unit normal is $kappa partial_lambda$ there. This proves (4.272). The lapse is the signed coordinate $lambda$, hence is smooth and odd. Changes between these boundary charts are smooth after the substitution $N = lambda^2$, so the construction is compatible on overlapping collars.
  ======= 4.11.3.2 Zero boundary lapse. <zero-boundary-lapse.>
  Equation (4.270) gives
  $ h_b = g + frac(s^2 Y_2^flat ⊗ Y_2^flat, d^2 - s^2 \| Y_2 \|^2) \, #h(2em) lambda = s sqrt(d^2 - s^2 \| Y_2 \|^2) . $
  Both are smooth one-sided in the original boundary structure, $h_b = g$ at $S$, and $partial_(nu_(h_b)) lambda = kappa$. Since $"Ric"_(h_b)$ is smooth up to $S$, the first equation in (4.271) extends continuously and gives $"Hess"_(h_b) lambda = 0$ there. Its tangential part equals $kappa upright("II")_(h_b)$, proving that the second form vanishes. Use base Gaussian coordinates $\( rho \, y \)$, so $h_b = d rho^2 + h_(A B) \( rho \, y \) d y^A d y^B$. At $rho = 0$ we have
  $ partial_rho h_(A B) = 0 \, #h(2em) lambda = 0 \, #h(2em) partial_rho^2 lambda = 0 . $
  Even reflection of $h_(A B)$ and odd reflection of $lambda$ thus match derivatives through order two. This proves the claimed $C^2$ reflection and all of (4.272).
  These constructions give a positive one-sided smooth base metric up to $S$. In the first case replacing $N gt.eq 0$ by $lambda = sqrt(N) gt.eq 0$ is a homeomorphism; in the second case the original structure is retained. The attachment therefore has the same underlying topology as the original one. If a base Cauchy sequence approaches $S$ in the original topology, the corresponding regular base coordinates converge, so it converges in the attached base metric as well. Combining this observation with the finite-length argument above proves completeness with $S$ included. ◻
]

==== 4.12 Classification of the four-dimensional static base <rig4:cla:section>

We now classify the static base obtained in Proposition 164. The positive set $cal(U) = { N > 0 }$ is connected and contains the boundary collar. On $cal(U)$ the metric $h_b$ and the lapse $lambda = sqrt(N)$ satisfy

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.275)")
 $ lambda "Ric"_(h_b) = "Hess"_(h_b) lambda \, #h(2em) Delta_(h_b) lambda = 0 \, #h(2em) "Scal"_(h_b) = 0 \, #h(2em) 0 < lambda < 1 . $ <rig4:cla:static-equations>]

The base is complete with $S$ attached; approaches to interior zeros of $N$ have infinite base length. Its boundary data are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.276)")
 $ h_b \|_(T S) = g \|_(T S) \, #h(2em) lambda \|_S = 0 \, #h(2em) partial_(nu_(h_b)) lambda = kappa \, #h(2em) upright("II")_(h_b) = 0 \, #h(2em) kappa = 1 / sqrt(2 m) . $ <rig4:cla:boundary-data>]

These are conclusions of the preceding argument, not hypotheses on the original data. In particular, neither simple connectivity nor absence of additional complete ends of the base is available yet.

Our goal is both the explicit static metric and the identity $cal(U) = "int" Omega$ in Proposition 169. The argument allows additional complete ends until Euclidean rigidity of the conformal double excludes interior null regions and identifies the whole exterior.

===== 4.12.1 Improvement of the static asymptotics <improvement-of-the-static-asymptotics>

After a constant linear change of the coordinates at infinity, the positive limiting matrix of $h_b$ becomes the identity. For a fixed $q_0 in \( 1 \, min { q \, 2 } \)$ we then have $h_b - delta = O_2 \( r^(- q_0) \)$ and $lambda - 1 = O_2 \( r^(- q_0) \)$. Introduce

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.277)")
 $ U = log lambda \, #h(2em) gamma = lambda h_b . $ <rig4:cla:reduced-variables>]

The conformal length factor from $h_b$ to $gamma$ is $e^(U \/ 2)$. The four-dimensional Ricci transformation and (4.275) give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.278)")
 $ "Ric"_gamma = 3 / 2 thin thin upright(d) U ⊗ thin upright(d) U \, #h(2em) Delta_gamma U = 0 . $ <rig4:cla:reduced-equations>]

Indeed, $"Ric"_(h_b) = "Hess"_(h_b) U + thin upright(d) U ⊗ thin upright(d) U$ and $Delta_(h_b) U = - \| thin upright(d) U \|_(h_b)^2$, which yield both identities directly.

#thm([Lemma 4.69.])[
  (Static end expansion).  #emph[There are coordinates harmonic for $gamma$ on a sufficiently distant part of the end, a number $epsilon.alt in \( 0 \, 1 \)$, a constant $M_1 > 0$, and homogeneous harmonic polynomials $H_1 \, H_2$ of degrees one and two such that, for every fixed nonnegative integer $k$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.279)")
 $ gamma - delta & = O_k \( r^(- 2 - epsilon.alt) \) \,\
U & = - M_1 / r^2 + frac(H_1 \( x \), r^4) + frac(H_2 \( x \), r^6) + O_k \( r^(- 4 - epsilon.alt) \) . $ <rig4:cla:gamma-decay>]

 In addition $gamma - delta = O_k \( r^(- 3) \)$.]
] <rig4:cla:asymptotics>

#proof[
  We give the coordinate and expansion arguments, since the initial falloff alone would not justify the compactification below.
  Extend $gamma$ from a sufficiently distant region to a smooth metric on $bb(R)^4$, using a cutoff interpolation with $delta$. Write its Laplacian as
  $ Delta_gamma = Delta_delta + D^(i j) partial_i partial_j + D^i partial_i . $
  By making the interpolation sufficiently far out, the scaled $C^(0 \, alpha)$ norms of $D^(i j)$ and $\( 1 + r \) D^i$ are as small as desired, for any fixed $alpha in \( 0 \, 1 \)$. Here a scaled norm means the ordinary norm after rescaling each annulus to fixed size. The initial differentiated falloff gives the needed Hölder estimates: second derivatives bound the scaled Hölder seminorm of first derivatives. Moreover $D^i = O \( r^(- 1 - q_0) \)$ in these scaled norms.
  Put $a = 1 - q_0 in \( - 1 \, 0 \)$. On all of $bb(R)^4$, with weight $1 + r$, the Euclidean Newton operator maps scaled $C^(0 \, alpha)$ functions of order $a - 2$ to scaled $C^(2 \, alpha)$ functions of order $a$. For completeness, the zeroth-order estimate follows from the kernel $C \| x - y \|^(- 2)$ by splitting the integral into $\| y \| < r \/ 2$, a comparable annulus, and $\| y \| > 2 r$; convergence uses $- 2 < a < 0$. Interior elliptic estimates after rescaling give the two derivative and Hölder bounds. The small coefficient norms therefore make the map obtained from
  $ Delta_delta v^i = - D^i - D^(a b) partial_a partial_b v^i - D^a partial_a v^i $
  a contraction in that weighted space. Its solution satisfies $v = O \( r^(1 - q_0) \)$ with the stated scaled $C^(2 \, alpha)$ bounds, and $x^i + v^i$ are $gamma$-harmonic coordinates on the far end. They are indeed coordinates there: their first derivative tends to the identity, and a cutoff of the correction can be chosen to have globally small first derivative. Local elliptic regularity makes the coordinates smooth wherever the original metric is smooth.
  In the harmonic chart, $gamma - delta$ and $U$ initially have order $- q_0$ in scaled $C^(1 \, alpha)$: one derivative of the transformed metric uses two derivatives of the coordinate correction. We do not assume a weighted second-derivative estimate at this stage. The chart and the fields are nevertheless smooth locally, so Equations (4.278) take the coupled elliptic form
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.280)")
   $ - 1 / 2 gamma^(a b) partial_a partial_b gamma_(i j) + Q_(i j) \( gamma \, partial gamma \) = 3 / 2 U_i U_j \, #h(2em) gamma^(a b) partial_a partial_b U = 0 \, $ <rig4:cla:harmonic-system>]
  where $Q$ is quadratic in first derivatives, with smooth coefficients depending on $gamma$. On an annulus rescaled to unit size, the initial $C^(1 \, alpha)$ norms of $gamma - delta$ and $U$ are $O \( R^(- q_0) \)$. Both quadratic right sides have $C^(0 \, alpha)$ norm $O \( R^(- 2 q_0) \)$, and the principal coefficients are uniformly elliptic with bounded $C^(1 \, alpha)$ norm. Interior Schauder estimates on a smaller annulus therefore restore $C^(2 \, alpha)$ bounds of order $R^(- q_0)$ for both unknowns. Differentiating this coupled system now inductively gives $gamma - delta \, U = O_k \( r^(- q_0) \)$ for every fixed $k$. Thus no higher derivative falloff is being assumed in the original coordinates. It follows in particular that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.281)")
   $ Delta_delta \( gamma - delta \) \, med Delta_delta U = O_k \( r^(- 2 - 2 q_0) \) . $ <rig4:cla:first-euclidean-source>]
  We use the following elementary multipole fact in dimension four. If a decaying smooth function $w$ on an end satisfies
  $ Delta_delta w = O_k \( r^(- 4 - j - eta) \) \, #h(2em) j in { 0 \, 1 \, 2 \, dots.h } \, quad 0 < eta < 1 \, $
  then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.282)")
   $ w = sum_(d = 0)^j frac(P_d \( x \), r^(2 + 2 d)) + O_k \( r^(- 2 - j - eta) \) \, $ <rig4:cla:multipole>]
  with $P_d$ homogeneous harmonic of degree $d$. To prove this, cut $w$ off to the end and represent the result by the whole-space Newton potential of its Laplacian. The difference is an entire harmonic function tending to zero, hence vanishes. The source has convergent moments through degree $j$. Taylor expansion of the kernel in the region $\| y \| < r \/ 2$ has remainder bounded by $C r^(- 3 - j) \| y \|^(j + 1)$; integration, and estimation of the complementary region after subtraction of the same moments, gives the error in (4.282). The local singularity of the Newton kernel is integrable. Rescaled elliptic estimates supply the differentiated error bounds. This also proves the statement simultaneously for each fixed finite number of derivatives.
  Choose $epsilon.alt in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ with $4 + epsilon.alt < 2 + 2 q_0$. Applying (4.282) first with $j = 0$ gives monopole expansions for $gamma - delta$ and $U$. Write the metric monopole as $D_(i j) \/ r^2$. The harmonic-coordinate condition is
  $ partial_j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] sqrt(det gamma) thin gamma^(j i) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 0 . $
  Its leading homogeneous term implies
  $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] D - 1 / 2 \( "tr" D \) "Id" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] x = 0 quad upright("for every ") x . $
  Taking the trace in dimension four gives $D = 0$. This proves (4.279). Since $U = O_k \( r^(- 2) \)$, its equation now improves to $Delta_delta U = O_k \( r^(- 6 - epsilon.alt) \)$. The case $j = 2$ of (4.282) gives (4.279), with the sign of its constant temporarily undetermined. In particular, the strict exponent in this source estimate excludes a logarithmic term at quadrupole order. The first use of (4.282) likewise accounts for the entire order $r^(- 2)$ tensor term, rather than only its spherical average. The metric equation in (4.280) has Euclidean source $O_k \( r^(- 6) \)$ at this stage. The case $j = 1$ of (4.282), with any smaller positive exponent if necessary, gives $gamma - delta = O_k \( r^(- 3) \)$. No expansion of the metric beyond this order is asserted or needed.
  Finally $- U$ is a positive $gamma$-harmonic function. On a sufficiently distant region the function $r^(- 2) + r^(- 2 - epsilon.alt)$ is positive and subharmonic for $gamma$; its favorable Euclidean Laplacian dominates the metric error. A small positive multiple lies below $- U$ on a fixed large sphere, and both tend to zero at infinity. The maximum principle therefore keeps it below $- U$ throughout the region. Taking the limit of $r^2 \( - U \)$ proves $M_1 > 0$. ◻
]

#prodef([Remark 4.70.])[
  The coefficient in Lemma 165 has the expected four-dimensional mass normalization:
  $ h_b = e^(- U) gamma = \( 1 + M_1 \/ r^2 \) delta + O_k \( r^(- 3) \) \, #h(2em) lambda = 1 - M_1 \/ r^2 + O_k \( r^(- 3) \) . $
  Direct substitution in the energy flux with factor $1 \/ \( 6 omega_3 \)$ gives base energy $M_1$. We have not identified that chart with the original ADM frame. The equality $M_1 = m$ will instead follow from the boundary constant $kappa$.
] <rig4:cla:base-mass>

===== 4.12.2 The complete conformal double <the-complete-conformal-double>

Define on $cal(U)$

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.283)")
 $ k_plus.minus = (frac(1 plus.minus lambda, 2))^2 h_b . $ <rig4:cla:conformal-metrics>]

The four-dimensional scalar conformal formula is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.284)")
 $ "Scal"_(f^2 h) = f^(- 3) \( - 6 Delta_h f + "Scal"_h f \) . $ <rig4:cla:scalar-transform>]

Thus both $k_(+)$ and $k_(-)$ are scalar flat. In the variables of (4.277), the useful exact identities are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.285)")
 $ k_(+) = cosh^2 \( U \/ 2 \) gamma \, #h(2em) k_(-) = sinh^2 \( U \/ 2 \) gamma . $ <rig4:cla:hyperbolic-factors>]

#thm([Lemma 4.71.])[
  (A zero-mass complete space).  #emph[Glue a plus and a minus copy along their common attached boundary $S$, and add one point at the minus asymptotic end. The resulting space $W$ is a connected orientable smooth boundaryless four-manifold with a complete $C^2$ scalar-flat metric $k_0$. This metric is smooth except possibly at the compact gluing hypersurface and the added point, and has a distinguished end satisfying 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.286)")
 $ k_0 - delta = O_k \( r^(- 3) \) quad upright("for every fixed ") k . $ <rig4:cla:zero-end>]

 No assertion about the other ends of $W$ is needed here.]
] <rig4:cla:double>

#proof[
  The smooth manifold structure is fixed before the metric is smoothed. At the seam use product charts from the regular base collar, with a signed normal coordinate on the double. In the positive boundary-lapse case this coordinate is signed $lambda$; in the zero boundary-lapse case one can use signed base normal distance. Tangential chart changes come from $S$, so these charts are smoothly compatible across the seam. Their overlaps with the original open base are smooth away from the seam. The added-point chart below is also smoothly compatible on every punctured overlap. Smoothness of this atlas does not assert smoothness of the reflected metric.
  By (4.285) and Lemma 165, the plus metric satisfies $k_(+) - delta = O_k \( r^(- 3) \)$; in particular its energy is zero.
  For the minus end use inversion $x = y \/ \| y \|^2$ and put $s = \| y \|$. Equation (4.279) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.287)")
   $ frac(U \( x \( y \) \), s^2) = - M_1 + H_1 \( y \) + H_2 \( y \) + O_k \( s^(2 + epsilon.alt) \) . $ <rig4:cla:inverted-lapse>]
  Differentiating the transformed remainder costs the corresponding powers of $s^(- 1)$. The Jacobian of inversion is $s^(- 2) A \( y \)$, where $A \( y \) = "Id" - 2 y y^t \/ \| y \|^2$ is orthogonal. Consequently the compactifying expression for $k_(-)$ is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.288)")
   $ (frac(sinh \( U \/ 2 \), s^2))^2 ["Id" + A \( y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] gamma \( x \( y \) \) - "Id" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] A \( y \)] . $ <rig4:cla:inverted-metric>]
  The first factor is the product
  $ 1 / 4 (U / s^2)^2 (frac(sinh \( U \/ 2 \), U \/ 2))^2 . $
  It has a $C^2$ extension with positive value $M_1^2 \/ 4$ at the origin. For the second factor, (4.279) gives $gamma \( x \( y \) \) - "Id" = O_k \( s^(2 + epsilon.alt) \)$. Each derivative of the angular matrix $A$ costs at most one power of $s^(- 1)$, so its conjugated error also has continuous derivatives through order two vanishing at the origin. This proves a nondegenerate $C^2$ extension of (4.288) across the added point. Scalar flatness extends there by continuity.
  Proposition 164 supplies the even $C^2$ reflection of $h_b$ and the odd $C^2$ reflection of $lambda$. The two conformal factors in (4.283) are one expression $\( \( 1 + lambda \) \/ 2 \)^2$ in that signed lapse. They therefore glue to a $C^2$ scalar-flat metric. Orient the second copy oppositely before gluing. The resulting manifold is orientable, and the punctured-ball chart above extends its orientation over the added point.
  It remains to check completeness, including possible escapes toward interior zeros of $N$. The plus length factor is at least $1 \/ 2$. On the minus copy its length factor is bounded below away from the chosen end: if a sequence there had $lambda arrow.r 1$, compactness in the original exterior would give either a positive-lapse limiting point with value one, contrary to the strict maximum principle, or a limiting boundary of the positive component, where $lambda arrow.r 0$. The latter possibility includes all interior zero loci and the attachment to $S$. Thus the complete base ends remain complete for both conformal metrics. The seam and the added point are regular metric neighborhoods. These observations exhaust the possible finite-distance escapes by Proposition 164 and the completeness of the original exterior. ◻
]

===== 4.12.3 Zero-mass rigidity without assumptions on the other ends <zero-mass-rigidity-without-assumptions-on-the-other-ends>

We use only the nonnegativity part of a smooth positive-mass theorem. The precise statement needed is Theorem 1.2 of Lesourd–Unger–Yau #cite(label("LesourdUngerYau2021")): a complete smooth orientable manifold of dimension $3 lt.eq n lt.eq 7$, with nonnegative scalar curvature and a distinguished asymptotically Schwarzschild end, has nonnegative mass at that end; its other ends are unrestricted. Definition 1.9 in that reference requires the remainder from the Schwarzschild metric to have weighted $C^(2 \, alpha)$ order $n - 1$. There is no spin hypothesis. The following argument reduces our $C^2$ zero-mass situation to exactly that smooth nonnegativity statement.

#thm([Lemma 4.72.])[
  (Zero-mass rigidity for the constructed space).  #emph[Let $\( W \, k_0 \)$ have the properties in Lemma 167. Then $\( W \, k_0 \)$ is isometric to Euclidean $bb(R)^4$. The isometry is smooth on every region where $k_0$ is smooth, including each smooth one-sided structure at the seam.]
] <rig4:cla:zero-mass>

#proof[
  Suppose first that $"Ric"_(k_0)$ is nonzero somewhere. By continuity it is nonzero at a point in the smooth part of the metric. Choose a smooth compactly supported symmetric tensor $p$ in that part for which
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.289)")
   $ I := integral_W "Scal"'_(k_0) \( p \) thin d V_(k_0) = - integral_W chevron.l "Ric"_(k_0) \, p chevron.r thin d V_(k_0) > 0 . $ <rig4:cla:positive-scalar-variation>]
  For example, a negative cutoff multiple of $"Ric"_(k_0)$ works. In this proof only, $s > 0$ denotes a small metric-variation parameter. Smooth the compact nonsmooth loci to obtain smooth metrics $ell_s$ equal to $k_0$ outside one fixed compact set, with
  $ ell_s = k_0 + s p + o \( s \) quad upright("in ") C^2 $
  on that compact set. More explicitly, choose fixed nested relatively compact neighborhoods of the attachment loci, and a smooth cutoff $chi$ equal to one on the smaller neighborhood and supported in the larger one. Finite-chart mollification and a partition of unity give a smooth tensor $t_s$ approximating $k_0 + s p$ in $C^2$ on the larger neighborhood with error at most $s^2$. Set $ell_s = chi t_s + \( 1 - chi \) \( k_0 + s p \)$ there and retain $k_0 + s p$ elsewhere. Where $chi$ is not one, the old metric is smooth, so this defines a globally smooth metric. The perturbation and all smoothing are confined to a fixed compact set; every other end is unchanged. For small $s$ the metrics are uniformly comparable to $k_0$ and therefore complete. Their scalar curvatures $R_s = "Scal"_(ell_s)$ have support in a fixed compact set $K$, satisfy $parallel R_s parallel_oo = O \( s \)$, and obey
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.290)")
   $ integral_W R_s thin d V_(ell_s) = s I + o \( s \) > 0 . $ <rig4:cla:scalar-integral>]
  We construct a positive smooth conformal factor satisfying
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.291)")
   $ - 6 Delta_(ell_s) f_s + R_s f_s & = 0 \, & f_s & = 1 + O \( s \) quad upright("uniformly on ") W \,\
f_s - 1 & = O_k \( r^(- 2) \) &  & upright("on the distinguished end") \,\
integral_(S_R) partial_(nu_(ell_s)) f_s thin d A_(ell_s) & = 1 / 6 integral_W R_s f_s thin d V_(ell_s) &  & upright("for every sufficiently large ") R . $ <rig4:cla:conformal-equation>]
  In particular, the flux in (4.291) is not being obtained by assuming zero flux at unspecified ends.
  Take connected smooth compact exhaustions $D_j$ whose boundary in the distinguished end is a large coordinate sphere $S_(R_j)$. The remaining boundary components lie outside each prescribed compact subset for sufficiently large $j$. Such an exhaustion is obtained by exhausting the complement of a fixed end neighborhood, joining to full coordinate annuli, and smoothing the compact joins. Impose $f = 1$ on $S_(R_j)$ and homogeneous Neumann conditions on every other boundary component. These boundary components are disjoint, so the mixed problem has no interface corners.
  We first record a uniform estimate for the compactly supported potential. If $v$ has zero Dirichlet value on $S_(R_j)$, then for every fixed compact set $K'$ in a fixed connected core neighborhood,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.292)")
   $ parallel v parallel_(L^2 \( K' \, ell_s \)) lt.eq C_(K') parallel nabla v parallel_(L^2 \( D_j \, ell_s \)) \, $ <rig4:cla:anchored-estimate>]
  with a constant independent of sufficiently large $j$ and small $s$. To see this first on a fixed end annulus, integrate along coordinate rays to the Dirichlet sphere. In Euclidean polar coordinates,
  $ \| v \( t \, theta \) \|^2 lt.eq (integral_t^(R_j) r^3 \| partial_r v \( r \, theta \) \|^2 thin d r) (integral_t^(R_j) r^(- 3) thin d r) \, #h(2em) integral_t^(R_j) r^(- 3) thin d r lt.eq frac(1, 2 t^2) . $
  Integration over the unit sphere and over the fixed annulus controls its $L^2$ norm by the full gradient energy. On a fixed connected neighborhood joining that annulus to $K'$, the Poincare inequality with the annulus as an anchor propagates the estimate. Uniform metric comparison on that neighborhood and the tail makes the constants uniform in $s$.
  For $v = f - 1$ the variational problem is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.293)")
   $ integral_(D_j) (chevron.l nabla v \, nabla w chevron.r + R_s / 6 v w) d V_(ell_s) = - 1 / 6 integral_(D_j) R_s w thin d V_(ell_s) \, $ <rig4:cla:mixed-weak>]
  for tests $w$ vanishing on the Dirichlet sphere. By (4.292) with a fixed neighborhood of $K$, the left quadratic form is bounded below by $\( 1 - C s \) parallel nabla v parallel_2^2$, and the right side is bounded in absolute value by $C s parallel nabla w parallel_2$. On each finite domain the gradient norm is a Hilbert norm on the Dirichlet test space. Lax–Milgram therefore solves (4.293) for small $s$, with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.294)")
   $ parallel nabla v parallel_(L^2 \( D_j \)) = O \( s \) \, #h(2em) parallel v parallel_(L^2 \( K' \)) = O \( s \) $ <rig4:cla:mixed-energy>]
  for every fixed compact $K'$ as above. Smooth elliptic regularity holds for the finite-domain solutions.
  These estimates also give a uniform supremum bound near $K$. First, interior $W^(2 \, 2)$ estimates for $Delta_(ell_s) v = \( R_s \/ 6 \) \( 1 + v \)$ give $O \( s \)$ on a slightly smaller fixed neighborhood. In dimension four this implies every finite local $L^p$ bound, though not yet an $L^oo$ bound. Applying the equation once more gives $W^(2 \, p) = O \( s \)$ for $p > 2$, and hence $parallel v parallel_oo = O \( s \)$ there. The constants are uniform because the metrics have uniform $C^2$ bounds and uniform ellipticity on those neighborhoods, and $R_s = O \( s \)$ in $L^oo$. Derivative bounds on the smoothing error beyond those needed here are unnecessary.
  Outside a fixed neighborhood of $K$, the function $v$ is harmonic. Its values at the inner boundary are bounded by $C s$, its outer Dirichlet values are zero, and its other outer normal derivatives are zero. Here is a global weak test that avoids assumptions on the remaining components. Take $\( v - C s \)_(+)$, which vanishes near $K$, and extend it by zero through that neighborhood. It is an admissible test in (4.293); both terms containing $R_s$ vanish, leaving the integral of its squared gradient equal to zero. Connectedness of $D_j$ and its zero Dirichlet trace imply $\( v - C s \)_(+) = 0$ everywhere. The negative excess gives the other bound. Thus $\| v \| lt.eq C s$ throughout $D_j$, with no geometry of the other ends used. On the distinguished end, a multiple of $r^(- 2) \( 1 - r^(- eta) \)$, with $0 < eta < 1$, is a positive strictly superharmonic barrier sufficiently far out for the metric (4.286). Comparison on the truncated annuli gives the uniform bound $\| v \| lt.eq C_s r^(- 2)$ there.
  For each fixed small $s$, pass to a subsequence converging smoothly on compact subsets as $j arrow.r oo$. The limit yields (4.291) and the zeroth-order decay in (4.291). Rescaled end estimates and differentiation give all its fixed differentiated orders. Positivity follows from the uniform bound $f_s = 1 + O \( s \)$. For a fixed sphere $S_R$ outside the support of $R_s$, integrate the finite-domain equation on
  $ B_(j \, R) = D_j \\ { x upright(" in the distinguished end") : r > R } . $
  Its boundary consists of $S_R$ and only artificial boundaries with homogeneous Neumann data; the Dirichlet sphere $S_(R_j)$ has been removed. For large $j$ it contains the whole support of $R_s$, so
  $ integral_(S_R) partial_(nu_(ell_s)) f_(s \, j) thin d A_(ell_s) = 1 / 6 integral_(D_j) R_s f_(s \, j) thin d V_(ell_s) . $
  The curvature has fixed compact support. Local convergence thus passes the derivative on the fixed sphere and the integral on that fixed support to the limit, proving (4.291) exactly. No integration over a limiting noncompact inner region is involved. The limiting factor may have harmonic behavior at the other ends; neither its limiting values nor individual end fluxes are required.
  The metric $f_s^2 ell_s$ is smooth, complete and scalar flat by (4.284) and the uniform positive bounds for $f_s$. Its distinguished end is asymptotically Schwarzschild in the precise sense required by the stated positive-mass theorem. Indeed, on that end $ell_s = k_0 = delta + O_k \( r^(- 3) \)$ and $Delta_(ell_s) f_s = 0$. Combining this with (4.291) gives $Delta_delta \( f_s - 1 \) = O_k \( r^(- 7) \)$. The multipole estimate (4.282) therefore implies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.295)")
   $ f_s = 1 + a_s / r^2 + O_k \( r^(- 3) \) \, #h(2em) f_s^2 ell_s = \( 1 + a_s \/ r^2 \)^2 delta + O_k \( r^(- 3) \) . $ <rig4:cla:factor-monopole>]
  The differentiated bounds supply the weighted $C^(2 \, alpha)$ remainder of order three, required in dimension four.
  On the other hand, (4.290) and the uniform bound in (4.291) give
  $ integral_W R_s f_s thin d V_(ell_s) = s I + o \( s \) > 0 . $
  Thus (4.291) has strictly positive outward flux. Physical and Euclidean fluxes have the same limit by the falloff. From (4.295), that limit is $- 2 omega_3 a_s$, so $a_s < 0$. Equivalently, direct computation with the prescribed energy normalization gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.296)")
   $ E \( f_s^2 ell_s \) = - 1 / omega_3 lim_(R arrow.r oo) integral_(S_R) partial_r f_s thin d A_delta = 2 a_s < 0 . $ <rig4:cla:negative-mass>]
  The background end contributes zero energy, and products of its error with the conformal error contribute zero limiting flux. This contradicts the smooth arbitrary-ends positive-mass theorem: the dimension is four, the manifold is orientable and boundaryless, the metric is complete and smooth, its scalar curvature vanishes, and (4.295) has exactly the required distinguished end. Hence $"Ric"_(k_0) = 0$.
  For clarity concerning regularity, a $C^2$ Ricci-flat metric becomes smooth in harmonic coordinates by the elliptic Ricci equation; this is the harmonic-coordinate regularity statement of DeTurck–Kazdan #cite(label("DeTurckKazdan1981")) Theorem 5.2 for $C^2$ Einstein metrics in dimension at least three. We may consequently apply Bishop–Gromov comparison and its rigidity statement #cite(label("Petersen2016")). The asymptotic volume ratio is at least the Euclidean value using the distinguished end alone: paths from a fixed base point to a fixed end sphere, followed by coordinate rays, have length $r + o \( r \)$ uniformly in angle, and the end volume form tends to its Euclidean value. Thus large metric balls contain asymptotically Euclidean coordinate annuli of the corresponding radius. Ricci nonnegativity gives the opposite inequality for the volume ratio. Its value is therefore one, and the equality case of Bishop–Gromov implies that $W$ is Euclidean space globally.
  The isometry is smooth wherever the original metric is smooth. Its smoothness in each one-sided seam structure can also be seen directly, without asserting that the reflected metric was smooth. The $C^2$ metric has $C^1$ connection coefficients in the original seam charts. A flat parallel coframe extends across the seam by parallel transport, with at least $C^1$ dependence on its base point. In coordinates each member $alpha$ satisfies
  $ partial_j alpha_i = Gamma_(j i)^k alpha_k . $
  On each closed one-sided chart the connection coefficients are smooth up to the boundary. This identity inductively makes the coframe smooth up to that boundary. Its closed forms integrate to Euclidean coordinates agreeing with the isometry on the open side after a fixed Euclidean motion, hence also at the seam by continuity. This proves the asserted smoothness separately in both one-sided structures. ◻
]

===== 4.12.4 The original positive component and its spherical boundary <the-original-positive-component-and-its-spherical-boundary>

#thm([Proposition 4.73.])[
  (Global static classification).  #emph[The positive component is the whole original open exterior: $cal(U) = "int" Omega$ and $N > 0$ there. Its attached boundary is $S$. There is a global isometry of the static base onto the Schwarzschild–Tangherlini spatial exterior of mass $m$, under which 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.297)")
 $ h_b = (1 + frac(m, 2 rho^2))^2 \( thin upright(d) rho^2 + rho^2 g_(bb(S)^3) \) \, #h(2em) lambda = frac(1 - m \/ \( 2 rho^2 \), 1 + m \/ \( 2 rho^2 \)) \, #h(2em) rho gt.eq sqrt(m \/ 2) . $ <rig4:cla:ST-base>]

 The isometry is smooth on the open base and in its one-sided regular boundary structure. The attachment is homeomorphic to the original attachment of $S$ in $Omega$ and restricts smoothly on $S$. In particular the open base is simply connected and $S$ is a round three-sphere in its induced metric. The base reflection is smooth in signed $lambda$ in the positive boundary-lapse case; its angular coordinates can be taken constant along base normal geodesics.]
] <rig4:cla:static-classification>

#proof[
  By Lemmas 167 and 168, the constructed space $W$ is Euclidean. Suppose a sequence in $cal(U)$ approaches an interior zero of $N$ in the original compact part of $Omega$. It escapes every compact subset of $W$: the zero locus is not a point of the plus copy, it cannot converge to $S$, and the only new point lies at the minus end in a neighborhood disjoint from that sequence. Yet the sequence remains outside a fixed distant tail of the distinguished end of $W$.
  This is impossible in Euclidean space. A coordinate cross-sphere in the distinguished end is a compact embedded sphere. Its tail has only that sphere as its frontier and is the unbounded component of its complement. Jordan–Brouwer separation makes the other side bounded; its closure is compact. Thus the complement of the tail is compact. This use of separation does not require a smooth Schoenflies theorem in dimension four.
  It follows that $cal(U)$ has no boundary in $"int" Omega$. The latter is connected, so $cal(U) = "int" Omega$. We now identify the plus and minus copies in the Euclidean double explicitly.
  At $S$, under the change $k_(+) = f^2 h_b$ with $f = \( 1 + lambda \) \/ 2$, the shape operator transforms by
  $ cal(S)_(k_(+)) = f^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(S)_(h_b) + nu_(h_b) \( log f \) "Id" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Using (4.276), its value is $2 kappa "Id"$ for the normal toward the plus side. In Euclidean coordinates write $z$ for position and $nu$ for that normal. With $d_(*) = \( 2 kappa \)^(- 1)$, the tangential derivative of $z - d_(*) nu$ vanishes. Connectedness of $S$ makes this vector a constant center. The image of $S$ lies on the sphere of radius $d_(*)$ about that center, and is both open there by local immersion and closed by compactness. It is the entire round sphere. Embeddedness follows from the global ambient isometry. The plus side, which contains the distinguished infinity, is its Euclidean exterior.
  Set $psi = 2 \/ \( 1 + lambda \)$. Then $h_b = psi^2 k_(+)$. Scalar flatness and (4.284) show that $psi$ is harmonic on that Euclidean exterior. It is equal to two on the sphere and tends to one at infinity. Uniqueness by the maximum principle therefore gives, in centered Euclidean radius $rho$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.298)")
   $ psi = 1 + d_(*)^2 / rho^2 \, #h(2em) lambda = frac(1 - d_(*)^2 \/ rho^2, 1 + d_(*)^2 \/ rho^2) . $ <rig4:cla:psi-and-lapse>]
  Since $kappa = 1 \/ sqrt(2 m)$, we have $d_(*)^2 = m \/ 2$, which proves (4.297). For an invariant check on the coefficient from Lemma 165, the flux of the $gamma$-harmonic function $U$ is independent of the end cross-section. Its limit is $2 omega_3 M_1$ in the harmonic coordinates and $2 omega_3 m$ in the explicit coordinates of (4.298). The divergence theorem between homologous cross-sections therefore gives $M_1 = m$, without identifying the two asymptotic charts. The area radius
  $ r = rho (1 + frac(m, 2 rho^2)) $
  satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.299)")
   $ N = 1 - frac(2 m, r^2) \, #h(2em) h_b = N^(- 1) thin upright(d) r^2 + r^2 g_(bb(S)^3) quad upright("on ") "int" Omega . $ <rig4:cla:area-radius-base>]
  The apparent degeneracy of the area-radius expression at the boundary is removed by the isotropic radius or the regular base collar. Its boundary radius is $r_0 = sqrt(2 m)$.
  The smooth one-sided boundary assertion follows from Lemma 168 and the regular base collar of Proposition 164. In the positive boundary-lapse case this collar is expressed in $lambda = sqrt(N)$, with a smooth reflection; replacing an original defining coordinate $N$ by $sqrt(N)$ changes the smooth boundary coordinate but not the underlying topology or the smooth structure on $S$ itself. In the zero boundary-lapse case the base collar is already smooth in the original one-sided structure. The spherical angular coordinates in (4.297) are constant along the base normal geodesics from $S$. In a reflected collar their normal projection to $S$ is smooth and invariant under reflection. These facts give precisely the regular boundary identification needed to recover the hypersurface in horizon coordinates. Finally the Euclidean exterior of a ball in dimension four is simply connected, proving the topological assertions. ◻
]

==== 4.13 Recovery of the original hypersurface and the converse <rig4:rec:section>

The static classification identifies a metric on the orbit space. We now recover the original spacelike hypersurface, including its second fundamental form and its smooth structure at the horizon. We then compute the invariant ADM mass of every admissible Tangherlini slice, allowing a nonzero asymptotic boost.

===== 4.13.1 The original graph in the open exterior <the-original-graph-in-the-open-exterior>

Throughout the forward implication, assume equality and all the horizon hypotheses of Definition 99. Retain

$ m = sqrt(E^2 - \| P \|^2) \, #h(2em) r_0 = sqrt(2 m) \, #h(2em) kappa = r_0^(- 1) . $

Proposition 169 identifies the whole open base $"int" Omega$ with the Tangherlini spatial exterior of mass $m$:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.300)")
 $ h_b = N^(- 1) thin upright(d) r^2 + r^2 g_(bb(S)^3) \, #h(2em) N = 1 - r_0^2 / r^2 \, #h(2em) r > r_0 . $ <rig4:rec:classified-base>]

In particular $N > 0$ throughout the original interior, the open base is simply connected, and its regular boundary attachment is homeomorphic to the original attachment of $S$. The adjoint field is the original field of Theorem 152; all occurrences of $X^flat$ below use the original metric $g$.

#thm([Lemma 4.74.])[
  (Recovery on the open exterior).  #emph[There is a smooth function $T_0$ on $"int" Omega$ such that the map 
$ x mapsto #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] T_0 \( x \) \, r \( x \) \, theta.alt \( x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $

 into the static Tangherlini exterior induces the original $g$ and $K$. Here $\( r \, theta.alt \)$ are the global base coordinates in Equation (4.300), with $theta.alt in bb(S)^3$.]
] <rig4:rec:interior-graph>

#proof[
  The one-form $A_b = N^(- 1) X^flat$ is closed by the staticity conclusion. Simple connectedness gives a global primitive with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.301)")
   $ thin upright(d) T_0 = - A_b . $ <rig4:rec:primitive>]
  On $bb(R) times "int" Omega$ put $T = t + T_0 \( x \)$. The definitions of $h_b$, $A_b$, and $N = u^2 - \| X \|_g^2$ give the exact identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.302)")
   $ - N thin upright(d) T^2 + h_b & = - N \( thin upright(d) t - A_b \)^2 + h_b\
 & = - u^2 thin upright(d) t^2 + g_(i j) \( thin upright(d) x^i + X^i thin upright(d) t \) \( thin upright(d) x^j + X^j thin upright(d) t \) . $ <rig4:rec:metric-identity>]
  Thus the graph $T = T_0$ is exactly the original $t = 0$ slice and induces $g$. Static time is future increasing, and its future unit normal is $n = u^(- 1) \( partial_t - X \)$. With the convention of the theorem, the lapse–shift identity is
  $ partial_t g = 2 u K + cal(L)_X g . $
  The metric on the right of Equation (4.302) is stationary, and Equation (4.234) therefore gives its second form as $- cal(L)_X g \/ \( 2 u \) = K$. This proves the claim for the original tensor, without a further deformation. These local sign conventions also agree with the Killing initial data identities in #cite(label("BeigChrusciel1997KID")) Equations (2.6) and (2.14). ◻
]

===== 4.13.2 Extension through the horizon in the original smooth structure <extension-through-the-horizon-in-the-original-smooth-structure>

The base has two possible regular boundary structures. When $u \|_S > 0$, $N$ is an original smooth defining function, whereas the regular base coordinate is $lambda = sqrt(N)$. When $u \|_S = 0$, the base is smooth in the original collar coordinate. The following lemma treats these cases before passing to regular spacetime coordinates.

#thm([Lemma 4.75.])[
  (Smooth horizon extension).  #emph[The graph in Lemma 170 extends smoothly, in the original smooth structure of $Omega$, to $S$ in the regular maximal Tangherlini extension. Its boundary is a smooth section of the corresponding future horizon if $u \|_S > 0$, and is the bifurcation sphere if $u \|_S = 0$.]
] <rig4:rec:horizon-extension>

#proof[
  Connectedness of $S$ and Theorem 152 leave exactly the two cases just described.
  ======= 4.13.2.1 Positive boundary lapse. <positive-boundary-lapse.-1>
  Equation (4.239) states that $thin upright(d) N = 2 kappa X^flat$ on $S$ and that $N$ is an original defining function. Consequently the smooth covector $X^flat - \( 2 kappa \)^(- 1) thin upright(d) N$ vanishes on $S$. Dividing its components by $N$ in an original collar gives a smooth one-form $beta_0$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.303)")
   $ A_b = frac(1, 2 kappa) thin upright(d) log N + beta_0 \, #h(2em) T_0 = - frac(1, 2 kappa) log N + B_0 . $ <rig4:rec:log-time>]
  Here $B_0$ is smooth up to $S$: its differential is $- beta_0$, and its values on an interior collar section determine its smooth extension by integration along collar segments. The primitive in Equation (4.301) is already single-valued, so this construction has no period ambiguity.
  We also need the angular coordinate $theta.alt$ to be smooth in the original coordinate $N$, not only in $lambda$. The specific reflection matters here. In the original-derived coordinates $\( lambda \, y \)$, with $N = lambda^2$, Equation (4.274) has even $lambda lambda$ and $A B$ coefficients and odd $lambda A$ coefficients, all original functions being evaluated at $\( lambda^2 \, y \)$. Thus the smooth isometric reflection is exactly
  $ cal(R) \( lambda \, y \) = \( - lambda \, y \) . $
  Let $p$ be normal projection to $S$ in this reflected base collar. Reflection fixes $S$ pointwise and reverses its unit normals, so $p compose cal(R) = p$. The smooth one-sided isometry in Proposition 169 sends these normal geodesics to the radial normal geodesics of the classified base. If $theta.alt_S$ denotes its smooth angular boundary identification, the original angular map on the positive collar is therefore $theta.alt = theta.alt_S compose p$. This formula extends it smoothly to the reflected collar and makes it invariant under the displayed $cal(R)$. In particular it is even in the specific $lambda$ coordinate obtained from original $N$, with its tangential parameters fixed. A smooth even function of $lambda$ is a smooth one-sided function of $lambda^2$: Taylor expansion has only even powers, and the differentiated remainder gives this assertion to every finite order. Applying this in angular charts proves that $theta.alt$ is smooth in the original $\( N \, y \)$ collar.
  ======= 4.13.2.2 Zero boundary lapse. <zero-boundary-lapse.-1>
  Let $s gt.eq 0$ be original outward collar distance. By Equation (4.240), there are smooth $d \, Y_2$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.304)")
   $ u = s d \, #h(2em) X = s^2 Y_2 \, #h(2em) d \|_S = kappa \, #h(2em) N = s^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] d^2 - s^2 \| Y_2 \|_g^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rig4:rec:zero-collar>]
  It follows that $A_b$, and hence $T_0$, extend smoothly and finitely to $S$. The positive square root on the exterior is
  $ lambda = s sqrt(d^2 - s^2 \| Y_2 \|_g^2) \, $
  which is smooth one-sided in $s$. In this case the original and regular one-sided base structures agree. The smooth one-sided base identification therefore makes $theta.alt$ smooth in the original collar as well.
  ======= 4.13.2.3 Regular spacetime coordinates. <regular-spacetime-coordinates.>
  Define the tortoise coordinate and Kruskal coordinates by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.305)")
   $ r_(*) & = r + r_0 / 2 log frac(r - r_0, r + r_0) \,\
sans(U) & = - exp #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] - kappa \( T - r_(*) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) sans(V) = exp #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] kappa \( T + r_(*) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rig4:rec:kruskal>]
  Differentiation gives $thin upright(d) r_(*) \/ thin upright(d) r = N^(- 1)$. More precisely,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.306)")
   $ e^(kappa r_(*)) = sqrt(N) thin D_(*) \( N \) \, #h(2em) D_(*) \( N \) = e^(r \/ r_0) frac(r, r + r_0) \, #h(2em) r = r_0 / sqrt(1 - N) . $ <rig4:rec:regular-factor>]
  Thus $D_(*)$ is smooth positive near $N = 0$, with $D_(*) \( 0 \) = e \/ 2$. The two-dimensional static part becomes
  $ - N thin upright(d) T^2 + N^(- 1) thin upright(d) r^2 = - frac(1, kappa^2 D_(*) \( N \)^2) thin thin upright(d) sans(U) thin thin upright(d) sans(V) . $
  Moreover $- sans(U) sans(V) = N D_(*) \( N \)^2$ has a smooth local inverse for $N$ near zero. These are regular horizon coordinates: the coefficient of $thin upright(d) sans(U) thin thin upright(d) sans(V)$ is smooth negative nonzero and $r$ is smooth across the horizon.
  In the positive boundary-lapse case, substituting Equation (4.303) into Equation (4.305) on the graph gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.307)")
   $ sans(U) = - N D_(*) \( N \) e^(- kappa B_0) \, #h(2em) sans(V) = D_(*) \( N \) e^(kappa B_0) . $ <rig4:rec:future-section>]
  Both functions are smooth in the original collar, and $S$ maps to $sans(U) = 0$, $sans(V) > 0$. Since its angular map identifies $S$ with $bb(S)^3$, this is a smooth section of the future horizon.
  In the zero boundary-lapse case, the same graph formulas are
  $ sans(U) = - lambda D_(*) \( N \) e^(- kappa T_0) \, #h(2em) sans(V) = lambda D_(*) \( N \) e^(kappa T_0) . $
  Equation (4.304) makes them smooth in $s$, and both vanish on $S$. Together with the angular identification, they map $S$ onto the bifurcation sphere. This proves the asserted smooth extension in both cases. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 4.76.])[
  (Global recovery of the original exterior).  #emph[Equality under the horizon hypotheses gives a global smooth spacelike embedding of the original $Omega$, including $S$, in the regular maximal Tangherlini extension of mass $m$. The embedding pulls back the spacetime metric and the future second fundamental form to the original $g$ and $K$. Its image lies in the closure of the corresponding exterior, its boundary has the form stated in Lemma 171, and its end approaches the corresponding spatial infinity.]
] <rig4:rec:embedding>

#proof[
  Combine the maps in Lemmas 170 and 171. Their pullback metric is $g$ on the open exterior and remains $g$ at $S$ by continuity. Since $g$ is positive definite, the extended map is a spacelike immersion there.
  It is injective on the interior by the global base identification, and on the boundary by the round-sphere identification. No interior point can have the same image as a boundary point, since their area radii are respectively $r > r_0$ and $r = r_0$. It is also proper. A compact set of the regular spacetime has bounded area radius; its preimage is a closed subset of a bounded annulus in the attached base. Such an annulus is compact, and the base attachment is homeomorphic to the original attachment. A proper injective immersion is an embedding.
  To obtain the normal at the boundary, take a smooth future timelike field in regular spacetime coordinates, project it to the normal line of the spacelike image, and normalize. Its normal projection is timelike and nonzero; the result is a smooth future unit normal. It agrees with the interior normal by uniqueness of the future choice. Hence the second-form identity from Lemma 170 extends to $S$. If desired, the smooth collar expressions extend locally across $S$ as a spacelike immersion, since immersion and spacelikeness are open conditions. No data beyond $S$ are prescribed or asserted.
  Finally the inverse-base identity gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.308)")
   $ \| thin upright(d) T_0 \|_(h_b)^2 = frac(\| X \|_g^2, u^2 N) arrow.r frac(\| b \|^2, \( b^0 \)^2) < 1 \, $ <rig4:rec:spatial-infinity>]
  using Equation (4.235) and $N arrow.r 1$. Choose $a < 1$ slightly larger than the limiting slope. Increasing a fixed base radius if necessary absorbs the radial length factor $N^(- 1 \/ 2)$, so integration along base radial rays, starting on a compact sphere, gives $\| T_0 \| lt.eq a r + C$ uniformly on the end. Thus the end remains in a spacelike cone of the static exterior and approaches its spatial infinity. ◻
]

===== 4.13.3 Asymptotic geometry of an admissible Tangherlini slice <asymptotic-geometry-of-an-admissible-tangherlini-slice>

For the converse write $M_0 > 0$ for the geometric mass parameter of the ambient Tangherlini spacetime, to distinguish it from the invariant ADM mass that we will compute. In isotropic spatial coordinates $y$ on its chosen exterior, with future static time $T$, its metric is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.309)")
 $ upright(bold(g))_(M_0) = - (frac(1 - M_0 \/ \( 2 \| y \|^2 \), 1 + M_0 \/ \( 2 \| y \|^2 \)))^2 thin upright(d) T^2 + (1 + frac(M_0, 2 \| y \|^2))^2 sum_(i = 1)^4 \( thin upright(d) y^i \)^2 . $ <rig4:rec:isotropic-spacetime>]

The following argument applies to the end of every slice in the converse class. It derives its asymptotic plane from the prescribed intrinsic decay.

#thm([Lemma 4.77.])[
  (Asymptotic affine plane).  #emph[Let a smooth spacelike hypersurface in the Tangherlini exterior of mass $M_0 > 0$ have an end with coordinates $x in bb(R)^4 \\ overline(B)$ and induced data satisfying 
$ g - delta = O_2 \( \| x \|^(- q) \) \, #h(2em) K = O_1 \( \| x \|^(- 1 - q) \) \, #h(2em) q > 1 . $

 Assume the hypersurface is smoothly embedded with a compact remaining part and boundary, if present, in the exterior closure. For any $1 < q_0 < min \( q \, 2 \)$ there are constants $L in "GL" \( 4 \, bb(R) \)$, $a in bb(R)^4$, $L_0 in bb(R)^4$, and $a_0 in bb(R)$ such that on the end 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.310)")
 $ y & = L x + L_0 + O_2 \( \| x \|^(1 - q_0) \) \,\
T & = a dot.op x + a_0 + O_2 \( \| x \|^(1 - q_0) \) \, #h(2em) L^t L - a ⊗ a = I . $ <rig4:rec:affine-plane>]

 In particular the limiting tangent plane is spacelike and $x$ gives Euclidean orthonormal coordinates on it.]
] <rig4:rec:asymptotic-plane>

#proof[
  We first control the spatial projection and then obtain its affine asymptotics.
  ======= 4.13.3.1 The end leaves every bounded area radius. <the-end-leaves-every-bounded-area-radius.>
  Gauss and Codazzi express the all-tangential and one-normal spacetime curvature components in a slice orthonormal frame in terms of $"Rm"_g$, $K^2$, and $nabla K$. All tend to zero by the stated decay. Vacuum $upright(bold(R i c))_(i j) = 0$ expresses the two-normal curvature components as sums of all-tangential ones. Thus every component tends to zero, and so does the spacetime curvature-square invariant.
  In the static orthonormal frame of Tangherlini, curvature components with one time index and three spatial indices vanish. All remaining squared components contribute nonnegatively to that invariant. The angular sectional curvature, computed from the static warped product, is
  $ frac(1 - \| nabla r \|_(h_(upright("static")))^2, r^2) = frac(2 M_0, r^4) . $
  The invariant is consequently bounded below by a positive constant times $M_0^2 \/ r^8$, including at the horizon by regularity. Hence the area radius $r$, and therefore $\| y \|$, tend to infinity as $\| x \| arrow.r oo$.
  ======= 4.13.3.2 A finite timelike limiting normal. <a-finite-timelike-limiting-normal.>
  For the estimates in original end coordinates, write $R = \| x \|$. Decompose the future static Killing field along the slice as $partial_T = u n + X$. On its exterior tail $u > \| X \|_g$. Local translates along this transverse field give stationary lapse–shift coordinates. The vacuum Killing initial data equations, with the present second-form convention, are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.311)")
   $ nabla_(\( i) X_(j \)) = - u K_(i j) \, #h(2em) nabla^2 u = u \( "Ric"_g + tau K - 2 K^2 \) - cal(L)_X K . $ <rig4:rec:vacuum-kid>]
  These follow respectively from the Killing equation and its normal derivative using the vacuum spatial Ricci equation. Their local form is dimension independent; we use them here in four spatial dimensions. The same local identities are recorded in #cite(label("BeigChrusciel1997KID")) Section 2.
  Commuting derivatives in the first equation expresses $nabla^2 X$ as curvature times $X$ and permutations of $nabla \( u K \)$. If $Y$ denotes the coordinate components of the pair $\( u \, X \)$, the prescribed decay therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.312)")
   $ \| partial^2 Y \| lt.eq C \| x \|^(- 1 - q_0) \| partial Y \| + C \| x \|^(- 2 - q_0) \| Y \| . $ <rig4:rec:jet-estimate>]
  Here only two derivatives of $g$ and one of $K$ are used.
  The metric $g$ is uniformly comparable to the Euclidean metric on the end, the lapse and shift are causal, and $q_0 > 1$. Applying Lemma 155 to Equation (4.312) gives finite constant limits with $O_2 \( R^(- q_0) \)$ errors. Their normalization follows separately from $u^2 - \| X \|_g^2 = - upright(bold(g))_(M_0) \( partial_T \, partial_T \) arrow.r 1$, using the already proved $\| y \| arrow.r oo$. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.313)")
   $ u = u_oo + O_2 \( R^(- q_0) \) \, #h(2em) X = X_oo + O_2 \( R^(- q_0) \) \, #h(2em) u_oo^2 - \| X_oo \|_delta^2 = 1 . $ <rig4:rec:limiting-normal>]
  ======= 4.13.3.3 Proper spatial projection and affine jets. <proper-spatial-projection-and-affine-jets.>
  Set $N = u^2 - \| X \|_g^2$. Pairing $partial_T$ with tangent vectors to the slice gives the exact identities
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.314)")
   $ thin upright(d) T = - X^flat / N \, #h(2em) y^(*) h_(upright("static")) = h_b = g + N^(- 1) X^flat ⊗ X^flat . $ <rig4:rec:projection-identities>]
  The spatial projection $y$ is a local diffeomorphism, since the static Killing field is timelike and transverse to the spacelike hypersurface. Equation (4.313) makes $h_b$ asymptotic to a positive constant matrix. Because $\| y \| arrow.r oo$, both its Jacobian and inverse Jacobian are uniformly bounded far out.
  We make the target coverage and path lifting explicit. Choose $R_(*)$ large enough that the preceding Jacobian bounds hold on the original end $E_(*) := { \| x \| > R_(*) }$. Choose $R_0$ larger than the maximum of $\| y \|$ on $partial E_(*)$, and put
  $ V_0 = { y in bb(R)^4 : \| y \| > R_0 } \, #h(2em) U_0 = E_(*) ∩ y^(- 1) \( V_0 \) . $
  The local diffeomorphism $y : U_0 arrow.r V_0$ is proper. Indeed a sequence whose images remain in a compact subset of $V_0$ cannot escape to original infinity, by $\| y \| arrow.r oo$, or approach $partial E_(*)$, by the choice of $R_0$. It therefore has a convergent subsequence in $U_0$. The image is open by local invertibility and closed by properness. It is nonempty, and $V_0$ is connected, so the image is all of $V_0$.
  A proper local diffeomorphism is a covering: inverse neighborhoods of its finite fibers give evenly covered neighborhoods, with properness excluding additional sheets arriving from outside those neighborhoods. In particular, radial paths down to a fixed sphere $\| y \| = R_1 > R_0$ lift. Their endpoints lie in the compact preimage of that sphere. The uniform inverse-Jacobian bound gives a lifted length at most $C \( \| y \| - R_1 \)$ and hence $\| x \| lt.eq C' \( 1 + \| y \| \)$. Conversely, integrating the Jacobian bound along original coordinate rays gives $\| y \| lt.eq C \( 1 + \| x \| \)$. Hence $\| y \| asymp \| x \|$.
  The connection transformation law for Equation (4.314) now yields $partial^2 y = O \( \| x \|^(- 1 - q_0) \)$: the connection of $h_b$ has that order, while the static connection is $O \( \| y \|^(- 3) \)$, and $q_0 < 2$. The first identity in Equation (4.314) similarly gives the required Hessian bound for $T$. Integration on radial rays and comparison on spheres first yield constant gradient limits and then constant translation limits. The latter comparison costs $O \( R^(1 - q_0) \)$, which tends to zero. This proves the two expansions in Equation (4.310). Finally the limit of the induced metric is $L^t L - a ⊗ a = I$. In particular $L$ is invertible and the affine plane is spacelike. ◻
]

===== 4.13.4 The invariant ADM mass of the slice <the-invariant-adm-mass-of-the-slice>

#thm([Proposition 4.78.])[
  (Mass of every admissible Tangherlini slice).  #emph[Every hypersurface in the converse class of Theorem 100, in Tangherlini spacetime of geometric mass $M_0 > 0$, has invariant ADM mass $M_0$. More precisely, in coordinates adapted to its asymptotic boost, its charges are $E = M_0 Gamma$ and $P = M_0 Gamma v thin e_1$, where $0 lt.eq v < 1$ and $Gamma = \( 1 - v^2 \)^(- 1 \/ 2)$.]
] <rig4:rec:converse-mass>

#proof[
  We compare the slice with its limiting plane and compute the flux on that plane.
  ======= 4.13.4.1 Comparison with the affine plane. <comparison-with-the-affine-plane.>
  Apply Lemma 173. Use the same parameter $x$ on the affine plane in Equation (4.310), and denote its induced data by $\( g^(*) \, K^(*) \)$. In affine Minkowski coordinates adapted to this plane, the residual displacement of the actual embedding is a spacetime vector $Z$, with time component $Z^0$, satisfying $Z = O_2 \( \| x \|^(1 - q_0) \)$. The ambient metric differs from Minkowski by $O_2 \( \| x \|^(- 2) \)$ near both embeddings. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.315)")
   $ g_(i j) - g_(i j)^(*) & = partial_i Z_j + partial_j Z_i + O_1 \( \| x \|^(- 2 q_0) \) \,\
K_(i j) - K_(i j)^(*) & = partial_i partial_j Z^0 + O \( \| x \|^(- 1 - 2 q_0) \) . $ <rig4:rec:gauge-comparison>]
  Here the indices on the spatial components of $Z$ are Euclidean. To check the error orders, the quadratic Minkowski metric term is $O_1 \( \| x \|^(- 2 q_0) \)$. Evaluation of the ambient perturbation at the displaced point, or its contraction with a displaced tangent vector, gives $O_1 \( \| x \|^(- 2 - q_0) \)$, which is smaller since $q_0 < 2$. For the second form use $- chevron.l n \, nabla_i partial_j iota chevron.r$. The normal differs from the constant future affine normal by $O \( \| x \|^(- q_0) \)$; multiplying this by $partial^2 Z$ gives $O \( \| x \|^(- 1 - 2 q_0) \)$. Ambient connection and evaluation errors have order $O \( \| x \|^(- 3 - q_0) \)$, again smaller. Euclidean trace reversal may be used in the difference with errors of the same allowed order.
  The only possible additional limiting fluxes in Equation (4.315) come from its displayed linear terms. For the metric term the energy vector is
  $ F_i = Delta Z_i - partial_i "div" Z \, #h(2em) partial_i F_i = 0 . $
  For the second-form term the momentum tensor is
  $ B_(i j) = partial_i partial_j Z^0 - delta_(i j) Delta Z^0 \, #h(2em) partial_j B_(i j) = 0 . $
  Extend $Z$ smoothly by a cutoff through the bounded Euclidean interior. The divergence theorem makes both linear fluxes exactly zero on every sufficiently large sphere. The remaining flux errors are $O \( R^(2 - 2 q_0) \)$, and hence tend to zero because $q_0 > 1$. It is therefore enough to compute the charges of the affine plane in the ambient metric (4.309).
  ======= 4.13.4.2 The four-dimensional boost calculation. <the-four-dimensional-boost-calculation.>
  Translations change the ambient leading mass terms only by $O \( \| x \|^(- 3) \)$ and do not affect the charges. After spatial rotations and an orthogonal choice of $x$ on the plane, write it as
